// notes/databases.js — TOPIC_CONTENT entries for the Databases phase
Object.assign(TOPIC_CONTENT, {
  "sql_fundamentals": "<main>\n  <h1>SQL Fundamentals</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-order\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Logical Query Order</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">FROM \u2192 \u2026 \u2192 LIMIT and what it explains</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-select\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">SELECT, WHERE &amp; Operators</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">IN, BETWEEN, LIKE/ILIKE, IS NULL</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-null\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">NULL &amp; Three-Valued Logic</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">UNKNOWN, NOT IN, COALESCE, sorting</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-sort\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Determinism, OFFSET cost, keyset paging</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-agg\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Aggregation &amp; GROUP BY</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">COUNT variants, HAVING, FILTER, int division</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-case\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">CASE Expressions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">First match wins, bucketing, pivots</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-crud\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">CRUD &amp; DDL</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">DELETE/TRUNCATE/DROP, UPSERT, constraints</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-dates\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Date &amp; String Functions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">DATE_TRUNC, EXTRACT, intervals, CAST</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sf-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Classic write-the-query questions</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Logical order explains almost every \"why can't I\u2026\" error:</strong> FROM/JOIN \u2192 WHERE \u2192 GROUP BY \u2192 HAVING \u2192 (window functions) \u2192 SELECT \u2192 DISTINCT \u2192 ORDER BY \u2192 LIMIT. WHERE can't see SELECT aliases or aggregates because neither exists yet; ORDER BY can use aliases because it runs after SELECT. The optimizer may physically reorder work, but the result must match this logical order.</li>\n    <li><strong>NULL means \"unknown\", so comparisons with it are UNKNOWN, not FALSE:</strong> <code>NULL = NULL</code> is UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only rows that are TRUE. Use <code>IS NULL</code> / <code>IS DISTINCT FROM</code>. <code>NOT IN (\u2026)</code> against a list containing a NULL can never be TRUE \u2192 returns zero rows. Prefer <code>NOT EXISTS</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) vs COUNT(DISTINCT col):</strong> COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values; COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts distinct non-NULL values. All aggregates except COUNT(*) skip NULLs, so <code>AVG(x)</code> = <code>SUM(x)/COUNT(x)</code>, not divided by row count. SUM over zero non-NULL rows returns NULL, COUNT returns 0.</li>\n    <li><strong>Integer division silently truncates:</strong> in PostgreSQL and SQL Server <code>7/2 = 3</code> and <code>COUNT(\u2026)/COUNT(*)</code> gives 0 for any rate below 100%. Cast one side (<code>::numeric</code>, <code>* 1.0</code>) and guard with <code>NULLIF(denominator, 0)</code>. MySQL and BigQuery's <code>/</code> return decimals.</li>\n    <li><strong>Conditional aggregation is the analytics workhorse:</strong> <code>SUM(CASE WHEN status='cancelled' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)</code> or Postgres <code>COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status='cancelled')</code> computes many metrics in one pass over the data, replacing several self-joins or subqueries. <code>AVG(CASE WHEN cond THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)</code> is a rate directly.</li>\n    <li><strong>LIMIT without ORDER BY is non-deterministic, and ORDER BY on a non-unique key has unstable ties:</strong> always add a unique tiebreaker (<code>ORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id DESC</code>). OFFSET pagination reads and discards all skipped rows (O(offset)) and drifts when rows are inserted; keyset pagination (<code>WHERE (order_date, order_id) &lt; (:d, :id)</code>) is O(page) with an index.</li>\n    <li><strong>DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP:</strong> DELETE removes rows one by one (WHERE allowed, row triggers fire, fully logged); TRUNCATE deallocates all rows at once (no WHERE, much faster, transactional in Postgres but implicit commit in MySQL); DROP removes the table definition itself. UPSERT = <code>INSERT \u2026 ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE SET col = EXCLUDED.col</code>, which needs a unique constraint on the key.</li>\n    <li><strong>Half-open date ranges beat BETWEEN on timestamps:</strong> <code>ts BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-01-31'</code> silently drops everything after midnight on Jan 31. Use <code>ts &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND ts &lt; '2024-02-01'</code>. Wrapping the column in a function (<code>DATE(ts) = \u2026</code>, <code>EXTRACT(YEAR FROM ts) = 2024</code>) also stops a plain index on ts being used.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. LOGICAL QUERY ORDER \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-order\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>Logical Query Processing Order</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 written order vs logical order</span>\n    <p>You <em>write</em>: SELECT \u2192 FROM \u2192 WHERE \u2192 GROUP BY \u2192 HAVING \u2192 ORDER BY \u2192 LIMIT.<br>The engine <em>logically evaluates</em>:<br><br>\n    <strong>1. FROM / JOIN</strong> \u2014 build the working row set (Cartesian product filtered by ON).<br>\n    <strong>2. WHERE</strong> \u2014 keep rows whose predicate is TRUE (FALSE and UNKNOWN are dropped).<br>\n    <strong>3. GROUP BY</strong> \u2014 collapse rows into one row per group key.<br>\n    <strong>4. HAVING</strong> \u2014 keep groups whose predicate is TRUE.<br>\n    <strong>5. SELECT</strong> \u2014 compute expressions, aggregates, window functions, assign aliases.<br>\n    <strong>6. DISTINCT</strong> \u2014 remove duplicate output rows.<br>\n    <strong>7. ORDER BY</strong> \u2014 sort.<br>\n    <strong>8. LIMIT / OFFSET</strong> \u2014 cut.<br><br>\n    Window functions are evaluated inside step 5, i.e. <em>after</em> WHERE/GROUP BY/HAVING and <em>before</em> DISTINCT/ORDER BY/LIMIT.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 each clause only sees what earlier steps produced</span>\n    <p>Think of a pipeline where each stage receives a table and hands a (smaller or reshaped) table to the next. A clause can only reference columns that exist in its input. Aliases are born in SELECT, so only ORDER BY (and LIMIT) downstream can see them. Aggregates are born at GROUP BY, so WHERE (upstream) can't use them, but HAVING can.<br><br>\n    This is a <em>logical</em> model, not a physical plan. The optimizer pushes predicates down, reorders joins and uses indexes freely, but it guarantees the same result as this order.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Consequences you will be asked about</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Why (from the order)</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE total &gt; 100</code> where <code>total</code> is a SELECT alias \u2192 error</td><td>WHERE (2) runs before SELECT (5) creates the alias</td><td>Repeat the expression, or wrap in a subquery/CTE</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 5</code> \u2192 \"aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE\"</td><td>Aggregates are computed at GROUP BY (3)</td><td>Move to <code>HAVING</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ORDER BY total</code> works</td><td>ORDER BY (7) runs after SELECT</td><td>\u2014</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\u2026) = 1</code> \u2192 error</td><td>Windows evaluate in SELECT, after WHERE</td><td>Compute in a CTE, filter outside (or <code>QUALIFY</code> in Snowflake/BigQuery/DuckDB)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>SELECT DISTINCT name \u2026 ORDER BY salary</code> \u2192 error in Postgres</td><td>After DISTINCT, salary no longer identifies a single row</td><td>Order by a selected column, or use GROUP BY + aggregate</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LIMIT 10 with a window function sees all rows</td><td>Windows (5) run before LIMIT (8)</td><td>Intentional: ranks are computed over the full set</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Fails: alias not visible in WHERE</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> total &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>          <span class=\"c\">-- ERROR: column \"total\" does not exist</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Correct: filter on the aggregate in HAVING</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>  <span class=\"c\">-- row filter: runs before grouping (cheap, shrinks input)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>    <span class=\"c\">-- group filter: runs after aggregation</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> total <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;        <span class=\"c\">-- alias OK here</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 dialect leniency hides the rule</span>\n    <p>PostgreSQL lets <code>GROUP BY</code> and <code>ORDER BY</code> reference output aliases or ordinals (<code>GROUP BY 1</code>); BigQuery and Snowflake allow aliases in GROUP BY too; MySQL even allows aliases in HAVING. None of them allow aliases in WHERE. In interviews, write the expression explicitly or use a CTE \u2014 it's portable and shows you know the order.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 a factory assembly line</span>\n    <p>FROM unloads raw parts from trucks, WHERE is quality control discarding defective parts, GROUP BY boxes parts by type, HAVING rejects boxes that are too light, SELECT puts labels on boxes, ORDER BY lines them up on the dock, LIMIT loads only the first N on the lorry. You cannot reject a box by its label at quality control \u2014 the label hasn't been printed yet.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. SELECT / WHERE / OPERATORS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-select\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>SELECT, WHERE &amp; Operators</h2>\n\n  <p>Running schema for this note: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id, manager_id, salary, hire_date)</code>, <code>departments(id, name)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)</code>, <code>users(user_id, signup_date, country)</code>, <code>events(user_id, event_time, event_type)</code>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Predicates and precedence</h3>\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 WHERE keeps only TRUE rows</span>\n    <p>WHERE evaluates a boolean predicate per row and keeps the row only when the result is <strong>TRUE</strong>. Rows evaluating to FALSE <em>or</em> UNKNOWN (anything touching NULL) are discarded. Operator precedence: <code>NOT</code> binds tighter than <code>AND</code>, which binds tighter than <code>OR</code>. So <code>a OR b AND c</code> means <code>a OR (b AND c)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Bug: returns ALL 'US' users regardless of signup date</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> country = <span class=\"g\">'US'</span> <span class=\"k\">OR</span> country = <span class=\"g\">'CA'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> signup_date &gt;= <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Intended: parenthesise, or better use IN</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> country <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"g\">'US'</span>, <span class=\"g\">'CA'</span>)\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> signup_date &gt;= <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span>;</div>\n\n  <h3>IN, BETWEEN, LIKE / ILIKE, IS NULL</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Semantics</th><th>Gotcha</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>x IN (a, b, c)</code></td><td>Shorthand for <code>x = a OR x = b OR x = c</code></td><td><code>x IN (1, NULL)</code> is TRUE for 1, UNKNOWN otherwise; <code>NOT IN</code> with a NULL is never TRUE</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>x BETWEEN a AND b</code></td><td><code>x &gt;= a AND x &lt;= b</code> \u2014 <strong>inclusive</strong> on both ends</td><td><code>BETWEEN 10 AND 1</code> returns nothing (not symmetric); timestamp end-day trap</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>LIKE 'ab%'</code></td><td><code>%</code> = any sequence (incl. empty), <code>_</code> = exactly one char</td><td>Case-sensitive in Postgres; case-insensitive under MySQL's default collation. Literal <code>%</code> needs <code>ESCAPE</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ILIKE</code></td><td>Case-insensitive LIKE</td><td>Postgres/Snowflake only; elsewhere use <code>LOWER(col) LIKE 'abc%'</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>IS NULL / IS NOT NULL</code></td><td>The only correct test for missingness</td><td><code>col = NULL</code> is always UNKNOWN \u2192 zero rows, no error</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>a IS DISTINCT FROM b</code></td><td>NULL-safe inequality (NULL vs NULL \u2192 FALSE)</td><td>MySQL spells NULL-safe equality <code>&lt;=&gt;</code></td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Leading-wildcard LIKE can't use a B-tree index: full scan</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> email <span class=\"k\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"g\">'%@gmail.com'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Prefix LIKE can use a B-tree index (with C collation or text_pattern_ops in Postgres)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> email <span class=\"k\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"g\">'aman%'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Matching a literal underscore</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> event_type <span class=\"k\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"g\">'page\\_%'</span> <span class=\"k\">ESCAPE</span> <span class=\"g\">'\\'</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 BETWEEN on timestamps</span>\n    <p><code>order_date BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-01-31'</code> on a <em>timestamp</em> column compares against <code>2024-01-31 00:00:00</code>, so every order placed later on Jan 31 is dropped. Always use half-open intervals: <code>order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2024-02-01'</code>. Works for dates and timestamps alike, and adjacent ranges never overlap or leave gaps.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Sargability \u2014 keep the column bare</h3>\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 an index is sorted by the raw column value</span>\n    <p>A B-tree on <code>order_date</code> is sorted by order_date, not by <code>EXTRACT(YEAR FROM order_date)</code>. Wrapping the column in a function forces the engine to compute the function on every row (full scan). Rewrite as a range on the bare column: <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-01-01'</code>. Same for <code>WHERE salary * 1.1 &gt; 100000</code> \u2192 <code>WHERE salary &gt; 100000 / 1.1</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. NULL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-null\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>NULL &amp; Three-Valued Logic</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 three-valued logic (3VL)</span>\n    <p>NULL is a marker for \"value unknown / not applicable\", not a value. Any comparison (<code>=, &lt;&gt;, &lt;, &gt;</code>) or arithmetic with NULL yields NULL / UNKNOWN. Boolean logic gains a third value:<br><br>\n    <code>TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN</code>, <code>FALSE AND UNKNOWN = FALSE</code><br>\n    <code>TRUE OR UNKNOWN = TRUE</code>, <code>FALSE OR UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN</code><br>\n    <code>NOT UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN</code><br><br>\n    WHERE, HAVING, ON and CASE WHEN treat UNKNOWN like FALSE. CHECK constraints treat UNKNOWN like TRUE (the row is accepted).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Expression</th><th>Result</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL = NULL</code></td><td>NULL (UNKNOWN)</td><td>Two unknowns aren't known to be equal</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL &lt;&gt; 5</code></td><td>NULL</td><td>Unknown might be 5</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL IS NULL</code></td><td>TRUE</td><td>IS tests the marker, never UNKNOWN</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL + 10</code>, <code>'a' || NULL</code></td><td>NULL</td><td>Propagates through arithmetic and <code>||</code> concatenation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL AND FALSE</code></td><td>FALSE</td><td>FALSE regardless of the unknown</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL OR TRUE</code></td><td>TRUE</td><td>TRUE regardless of the unknown</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL IS DISTINCT FROM NULL</code></td><td>FALSE</td><td>Null-safe comparison</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>The \"!=\" trap and the NOT IN trap (preview)</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Excludes cancelled orders... AND silently excludes orders with status NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status &lt;&gt; <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Null-safe version</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- or: WHERE status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled' OR status IS NULL</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- NOT IN trap: if ANY manager_id is NULL this returns zero rows</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> manager_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees);   <span class=\"c\">-- top-level CEO has manager_id NULL!</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Safe anti-join</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees r <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> r.manager_id = e.id);</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why NOT IN collapses</span>\n    <p><code>x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)</code> expands to <code>x &lt;&gt; 1 AND x &lt;&gt; 2 AND x &lt;&gt; NULL</code>. The last term is UNKNOWN for every x, so the conjunction is at best UNKNOWN \u2014 never TRUE \u2014 and WHERE drops every row. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> evaluates a row-level \"does a matching row exist?\", which is always TRUE or FALSE, so NULLs can't poison it. (Covered fully in the JOINs note.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>COALESCE and NULLIF</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- COALESCE: first non-NULL argument (short-circuits left to right)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount), <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;            <span class=\"c\">-- users with no orders get 0 instead of NULL</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- NULLIF(a, b): returns NULL if a = b, else a. Classic divide-by-zero guard:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(salary) / <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*), <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_sal\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Turn sentinel values into real NULLs</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(country), <span class=\"g\">''</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> country <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 COALESCE type and position matter</span>\n    <p>All COALESCE arguments must resolve to a common type: <code>COALESCE(amount, 'n/a')</code> fails in Postgres (numeric vs text). And <em>where</em> you put it matters: <code>COALESCE(SUM(x), 0)</code> turns an empty-group NULL into 0; <code>SUM(COALESCE(x, 0))</code> treats each missing value as 0 (same sum, but would change an <code>AVG</code>). SQL Server's <code>ISNULL</code>, MySQL's <code>IFNULL</code> and Oracle's <code>NVL</code> are 2-argument equivalents.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>NULLs in ORDER BY, GROUP BY, DISTINCT, UNIQUE</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Context</th><th>NULL behaviour</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>ORDER BY (Postgres, Oracle, Snowflake)</td><td>NULLs sort as <strong>larger</strong> than every value: last in ASC, first in DESC</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ORDER BY (MySQL, SQL Server, SQLite, BigQuery)</td><td>NULLs sort as <strong>smaller</strong>: first in ASC, last in DESC</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Override</td><td><code>ORDER BY x DESC NULLS LAST</code> (Postgres/Oracle/Snowflake/BigQuery). MySQL/SQL Server: <code>ORDER BY x IS NULL, x DESC</code> / <code>CASE WHEN x IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>GROUP BY, DISTINCT, UNION, PARTITION BY</td><td>All NULLs are treated as one group (\"not distinct\")</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>UNIQUE constraint</td><td>Postgres/MySQL/Oracle allow many NULLs (Postgres 15 adds <code>NULLS NOT DISTINCT</code>); SQL Server allows only one</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aggregates (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT(col))</td><td>Ignore NULL inputs; all-NULL / empty input \u2192 NULL (COUNT \u2192 0)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"top earners\" returning NULL salaries first</span>\n    <p><code>SELECT name, salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 3</code> in Postgres puts employees with NULL salary at the top. Add <code>NULLS LAST</code> or <code>WHERE salary IS NOT NULL</code>. Equally, <code>MAX(salary)</code> is unaffected (it ignores NULLs), so the two approaches can disagree.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. DISTINCT / ORDER BY / LIMIT \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-sort\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT/OFFSET &amp; Keyset Pagination</h2>\n\n  <h3>DISTINCT</h3>\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 DISTINCT applies to the whole output row</span>\n    <p><code>SELECT DISTINCT a, b</code> removes rows where <em>the combination</em> (a, b) repeats; it is not per-column. It's logically equivalent to <code>GROUP BY a, b</code> with no aggregates and usually planned identically (hash aggregate or sort + unique). <code>COUNT(DISTINCT a)</code> is different: it's an aggregate over one expression.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Postgres-only DISTINCT ON: one row per user \u2014 the latest order</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> (user_id) user_id, order_id, order_date, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;  <span class=\"c\">-- ORDER BY must start with the DISTINCT ON key</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Portable equivalent</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.*, <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n                                 <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 DISTINCT as a band-aid for a bad join</span>\n    <p>If a join duplicates rows and you slap <code>SELECT DISTINCT</code> on top, you hide the symptom, pay for a sort/hash of the whole result, and still get wrong SUMs if you aggregate before deduping. Interviewers notice. Find the fan-out (see the JOINs note) and fix the join grain instead.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>ORDER BY</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Without ORDER BY, row order is <strong>undefined</strong> \u2014 even if it \"looks sorted\" today (parallel scans, hash aggregates and updates change it).</li>\n    <li>Can sort by expressions, aliases, ordinals (<code>ORDER BY 2 DESC</code>) and columns not in SELECT (unless DISTINCT is used).</li>\n    <li>Sort is <strong>not stable</strong> across ties: rows with equal keys can come back in any order and differ between runs. Add a unique tiebreaker.</li>\n    <li>Text sorts by collation: <code>'a' &lt; 'B'</code> under most linguistic collations but not under C/binary collation.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>LIMIT / OFFSET and why OFFSET hurts</h3>\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 dialect spellings</span>\n    <p>Postgres/MySQL/SQLite/Snowflake/BigQuery: <code>LIMIT n OFFSET k</code>. SQL Server: <code>SELECT TOP n</code> or <code>OFFSET k ROWS FETCH NEXT n ROWS ONLY</code> (requires ORDER BY). ANSI: <code>FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY</code>; <code>FETCH FIRST n ROWS WITH TIES</code> (Postgres 13+) also returns rows tied with the last one.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 OFFSET still reads the rows it skips</span>\n    <p><code>OFFSET 100000 LIMIT 20</code> must produce rows 1\u2026100,020 in order and throw away 100,000 of them. Page 5,000 costs 5,000\u00d7 page 1. And if a row is inserted at the top between page requests, everything shifts by one: users see a duplicate or miss a row.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Keyset (seek) pagination</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Page 1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, order_date, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">20</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Next page: pass the last row's (order_date, order_id) from the previous page</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, order_date, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> (order_date, order_id) &lt; (<span class=\"g\">'2024-03-14 09:12:00'</span>, <span class=\"y\">88231</span>)   <span class=\"c\">-- row-value comparison</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">20</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Supporting index makes each page an index range scan of ~20 rows</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">INDEX</span> idx_orders_date_id <span class=\"k\">ON</span> orders (order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>OFFSET pagination</th><th>Keyset pagination</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cost of page p</td><td>O(p \u00d7 page size)</td><td>O(page size) with an index</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Stable under inserts</td><td>No \u2014 rows shift</td><td>Yes \u2014 anchored to a key</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Jump to page 37</td><td>Easy</td><td>Not directly (only next/prev)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Requirement</td><td>Any ORDER BY</td><td>A unique, indexed sort key (add PK as tiebreaker)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 row-value comparison support</span>\n    <p><code>(a, b) &lt; (x, y)</code> means <code>a &lt; x OR (a = x AND b &lt; y)</code>. Postgres and MySQL support it (Postgres uses the composite index directly). SQL Server doesn't support row-value comparison \u2014 write the expanded OR form. If the sort column can be NULL, keyset breaks (NULL comparisons are UNKNOWN), so coalesce or exclude NULLs.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. AGGREGATION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-agg\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>Aggregations, GROUP BY &amp; HAVING</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 GROUP BY changes the grain</span>\n    <p>GROUP BY collapses input rows to one output row per distinct key combination. After grouping, every SELECT/HAVING/ORDER BY expression must be either a grouping key, an aggregate, or a constant. Postgres relaxes this when the key is the table's primary key (other columns of that table are functionally dependent); MySQL with <code>ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY</code> off picks an arbitrary value \u2014 a silent-bug generator.<br><br>\n    Without GROUP BY, an aggregate makes the <em>whole table</em> one group: <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE 1=0</code> returns one row (0), while the same with <code>GROUP BY user_id</code> returns zero rows.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) vs COUNT(DISTINCT col)</h3>\n  <p>Input: <code>orders.status</code> = 'paid', 'paid', NULL, 'cancelled', NULL</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Expression</th><th>Result</th><th>Counts</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>COUNT(*)</code></td><td>5</td><td>Rows (NULL or not)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>COUNT(1)</code></td><td>5</td><td>Same as COUNT(*) \u2014 no performance difference in modern engines</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>COUNT(status)</code></td><td>3</td><td>Non-NULL values</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>COUNT(DISTINCT status)</code></td><td>2</td><td>Distinct non-NULL values (paid, cancelled)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>COUNT(DISTINCT COALESCE(status,'?'))</code></td><td>3</td><td>Treat NULL as its own category</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 COUNT(*) after a LEFT JOIN</span>\n    <p>Counting orders per user with <code>users LEFT JOIN orders</code> and <code>COUNT(*)</code> gives 1 (not 0) for users without orders, because the NULL-extended row is still a row. Use <code>COUNT(o.order_id)</code> \u2014 a column from the right side that is never NULL in a real match.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>AVG and NULLs</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- salaries: 100, 200, NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary)                 <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_known,     <span class=\"c\">-- 150  (300 / 2)</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(salary) / <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)      <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_all_rows,  <span class=\"c\">-- 100  (300 / 3)</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(<span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(salary, <span class=\"y\">0</span>))    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_null_as_0  <span class=\"c\">-- 100</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees;</div>\n  <p>Which is \"right\" depends on what NULL means: unknown salary (exclude, use AVG) vs no revenue (zero, coalesce first). Say this out loud in interviews.</p>\n\n  <h3>The integer division trap</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Postgres / SQL Server: both operands integer -&gt; integer result, truncated toward zero</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">7</span> / <span class=\"y\">2</span>;                         <span class=\"c\">-- 3</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span>) / <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;  <span class=\"c\">-- 0 (!)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Fixes</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">7</span> / <span class=\"y\">2.0</span>;                       <span class=\"c\">-- 3.5000000000000000</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">7</span>::numeric / <span class=\"y\">2</span>;                <span class=\"c\">-- 3.5000000000000000</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">ROUND</span>(<span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span>)\n             / <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*), <span class=\"y\">0</span>), <span class=\"y\">2</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cancel_pct\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;</div>\n  <p>MySQL's <code>/</code> always returns a decimal (<code>DIV</code> is integer division). BigQuery's <code>/</code> returns FLOAT64 and errors on divide-by-zero (use <code>SAFE_DIVIDE</code>). Postgres <code>AVG(int_col)</code> returns numeric, so AVG itself is safe.</p>\n\n  <h3>Conditional aggregation: CASE inside aggregates and FILTER</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- One pass, many metrics: per-month order funnel</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, order_date)                         <span class=\"k\">AS</span> month,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)                                                <span class=\"k\">AS</span> orders,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>)            <span class=\"k\">AS</span> completed,   <span class=\"c\">-- Postgres/SQLite</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>)   <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cancelled,   <span class=\"c\">-- portable</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> amount <span class=\"k\">END</span>)     <span class=\"k\">AS</span> gmv,         <span class=\"c\">-- no ELSE -&gt; NULL, ignored by SUM</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1.0</span> <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cancel_rate,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> user_id)                                 <span class=\"k\">AS</span> buyers\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 CASE as a per-row switch feeding the aggregate</span>\n    <p>The CASE runs for every row before aggregation, producing a value that is either \"counted\" (1 / amount) or \"not counted\" (0 / NULL). The aggregate then sees a column that already encodes your condition. This replaces N separate <code>WHERE</code>-filtered queries joined together \u2014 one scan instead of N.<br><br>\n    Note the subtle difference: <code>SUM(CASE \u2026 ELSE 0 END)</code> returns 0 for a group with no matches; <code>SUM(CASE \u2026 END)</code> (no ELSE) returns NULL. For <code>AVG</code>, the ELSE matters even more: <code>AVG(CASE WHEN c THEN 1.0 END)</code> is always 1 or NULL (the non-matching rows are NULLs and get ignored); you need <code>ELSE 0</code>. Dialect shortcuts: BigQuery <code>COUNTIF(cond)</code>, Snowflake <code>COUNT_IF(cond)</code>, Postgres <code>AVG((cond)::int)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>WHERE vs HAVING</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>WHERE</th><th>HAVING</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Filters</td><td>Rows, before grouping</td><td>Groups, after grouping</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Can use aggregates</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Performance</td><td>Shrinks input to GROUP BY \u2014 prefer for non-aggregate conditions</td><td>Non-aggregate conditions here still work but are pushed down by most optimizers anyway</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Example</td><td><code>WHERE amount &gt; 100</code> \u2192 keep big orders, then sum</td><td><code>HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 100</code> \u2192 keep users whose total &gt; 100</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 HAVING without GROUP BY</span>\n    <p><code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1000</code> is legal: the whole table is one group, so you get one row or zero rows. Useful for \"assert\" queries in data-quality checks.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Grouping extensions (quick reference)</h3>\n  <p><code>GROUP BY ROLLUP (country, month)</code> produces (country, month), (country), and grand-total rows; <code>CUBE</code> produces every combination; <code>GROUPING SETS ((country), (month), ())</code> lists them explicitly. Subtotal rows have NULL in rolled-up columns \u2014 use <code>GROUPING(col)</code> to tell a subtotal NULL from a data NULL.</p>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. CASE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-case\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>CASE Expressions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 searched vs simple CASE</span>\n    <p><strong>Searched:</strong> <code>CASE WHEN cond1 THEN r1 WHEN cond2 THEN r2 ELSE r3 END</code> \u2014 conditions are tested top to bottom and the <strong>first TRUE wins</strong>. <strong>Simple:</strong> <code>CASE expr WHEN v1 THEN r1 \u2026 END</code> \u2014 compares with <code>=</code>, so <code>WHEN NULL</code> never matches. Missing ELSE \u2192 NULL. All branches must share a common type. CASE is an <em>expression</em>: usable in SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY, inside aggregates and in UPDATE SET.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Bucketing: order matters \u2014 first TRUE branch wins</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, amount,\n       <span class=\"k\">CASE</span>\n         <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> amount <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'unknown'</span>\n         <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> amount &gt;= <span class=\"y\">500</span>  <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'large'</span>\n         <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> amount &gt;= <span class=\"y\">100</span>  <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'medium'</span>    <span class=\"c\">-- only reached if amount &lt; 500</span>\n         <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"g\">'small'</span>\n       <span class=\"k\">END</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> size_bucket\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Custom sort order</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"k\">CASE</span> status <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'pending'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">2</span> <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">3</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>,\n         order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Pivot rows into columns (country x year revenue)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(YEAR <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> o.order_date) = <span class=\"y\">2023</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> o.amount <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rev_2023,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(YEAR <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> o.order_date) = <span class=\"y\">2024</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> o.amount <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rev_2024\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 overlapping or mis-ordered branches</span>\n    <p>Writing <code>WHEN amount &gt;= 100 THEN 'medium' WHEN amount &gt;= 500 THEN 'large'</code> means 'large' is unreachable. Also: <code>CASE x WHEN NULL THEN \u2026</code> never fires \u2014 use the searched form with <code>WHEN x IS NULL</code>. And CASE does <em>not</em> fully guarantee short-circuiting when constant folding or aggregates are involved (e.g. Postgres may evaluate <code>1/0</code> in a branch at plan time if it's a constant), so guard divisions with NULLIF rather than relying on CASE order.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. CRUD & DDL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-crud\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>CRUD &amp; DDL</h2>\n\n  <h3>Creating tables, types and constraints</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">TABLE</span> departments (\n  id    <span class=\"k\">INT</span> <span class=\"k\">PRIMARY</span> <span class=\"k\">KEY</span>,\n  name  <span class=\"k\">TEXT</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> <span class=\"k\">UNIQUE</span>\n);\n\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">TABLE</span> employees (\n  id          <span class=\"k\">BIGINT</span> <span class=\"k\">GENERATED</span> <span class=\"k\">ALWAYS</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> <span class=\"k\">IDENTITY</span> <span class=\"k\">PRIMARY</span> <span class=\"k\">KEY</span>,\n  name        <span class=\"k\">TEXT</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>,\n  dept_id     <span class=\"k\">INT</span> <span class=\"k\">REFERENCES</span> departments(id) <span class=\"k\">ON</span> <span class=\"k\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"k\">SET</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>,\n  manager_id  <span class=\"k\">BIGINT</span> <span class=\"k\">REFERENCES</span> employees(id),\n  salary      <span class=\"k\">NUMERIC</span>(<span class=\"y\">12</span>,<span class=\"y\">2</span>) <span class=\"k\">CHECK</span> (salary &gt; <span class=\"y\">0</span>),\n  hire_date   <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> <span class=\"k\">DEFAULT</span> CURRENT_DATE,\n  email       <span class=\"k\">TEXT</span>,\n  <span class=\"k\">CONSTRAINT</span> uq_emp_email <span class=\"k\">UNIQUE</span> (email)\n);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Guarantees</th><th>NULL behaviour / notes</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>PRIMARY KEY</td><td>Unique + NOT NULL; one per table; creates a unique index</td><td>NULLs forbidden</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>UNIQUE</td><td>No two rows share the value(s); creates a unique index</td><td>Multiple NULLs allowed in Postgres/MySQL (NULL \u2260 NULL); one in SQL Server</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>FOREIGN KEY</td><td>Value must exist in the referenced PK/UNIQUE column</td><td>NULL FK is allowed (no reference). Actions: <code>ON DELETE CASCADE / SET NULL / RESTRICT</code>. Postgres does not auto-index the FK column \u2014 add one for join/delete speed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CHECK</td><td>Row-level predicate must not be FALSE</td><td>NULL passes (UNKNOWN is accepted): <code>CHECK (salary &gt; 0)</code> allows NULL salary</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Column must have a value</td><td>Combine with CHECK to fully constrain</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Data types worth knowing</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Need</th><th>Use</th><th>Avoid / note</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Money</td><td><code>NUMERIC(p,s)</code> / <code>DECIMAL</code> \u2014 exact</td><td><code>FLOAT</code>/<code>REAL</code>: 0.1 + 0.2 \u2260 0.3</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IDs, counts</td><td><code>INT</code> (\u00b12.1B), <code>BIGINT</code> for event tables</td><td>INT overflow on high-volume IDs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Text</td><td><code>TEXT</code> / <code>VARCHAR(n)</code> (same performance in Postgres)</td><td><code>CHAR(n)</code> pads with spaces</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Points in time</td><td><code>TIMESTAMPTZ</code> (stored as UTC, displayed in session zone)</td><td><code>TIMESTAMP</code> without zone is ambiguous across regions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Calendar day</td><td><code>DATE</code></td><td>Storing dates as strings breaks sorting/arithmetic</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Flags</td><td><code>BOOLEAN</code></td><td>MySQL BOOLEAN is TINYINT(1)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Semi-structured</td><td><code>JSONB</code> (Postgres), <code>VARIANT</code> (Snowflake), <code>JSON</code>/<code>STRUCT</code> (BigQuery)</td><td>Don't hide relational data in JSON</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">INSERT</span> <span class=\"k\">INTO</span> departments (id, name) <span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"g\">'Data'</span>), (<span class=\"y\">2</span>, <span class=\"g\">'Infra'</span>);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Insert from a query (backfills, ETL)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">INSERT</span> <span class=\"k\">INTO</span> daily_revenue (day, revenue)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date::date, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- UPDATE with a join (Postgres: UPDATE ... FROM; MySQL: UPDATE t JOIN s ... SET)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">SET</span> salary = e.salary * <span class=\"y\">1.10</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> departments d\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> d.id = e.dept_id <span class=\"k\">AND</span> d.name = <span class=\"g\">'Data'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">RETURNING</span> e.id, e.salary;                <span class=\"c\">-- Postgres: see what changed</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- DELETE with a join (Postgres: DELETE ... USING)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">USING</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> u.user_id = o.user_id <span class=\"k\">AND</span> u.country = <span class=\"g\">'XX'</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 UPDATE/DELETE without WHERE, and UPDATE \u2026 FROM fan-out</span>\n    <p>A missing WHERE updates every row. Habit: write the statement as a SELECT first, check the count, then convert; or run it in <code>BEGIN; \u2026 ROLLBACK;</code>. In Postgres, if the FROM table matches a target row more than once, the row is updated with an <em>arbitrary</em> one of the matches \u2014 no error. Make sure the join is on a unique key.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>DELETE</th><th>TRUNCATE</th><th>DROP</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Removes</td><td>Rows matching WHERE (or all)</td><td>All rows</td><td>Rows + table definition, indexes, constraints, grants</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Category</td><td>DML</td><td>DDL</td><td>DDL</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Row by row, each logged; leaves dead tuples (Postgres needs VACUUM)</td><td>Deallocates data pages \u2014 near-instant</td><td>Instant</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Triggers</td><td>Row DELETE triggers fire</td><td>Row triggers don't fire</td><td>\u2014</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Rollback</td><td>Yes</td><td>Postgres/SQL Server: yes inside a transaction; MySQL/Oracle: implicit commit</td><td>Postgres: yes; MySQL/Oracle: implicit commit</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Identity / auto-increment</td><td>Unchanged</td><td>Reset in MySQL/SQL Server; Postgres only with <code>RESTART IDENTITY</code></td><td>Gone</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>FK-referenced table</td><td>Allowed if no referencing rows (or cascade)</td><td>Fails unless <code>CASCADE</code> (Postgres) / not allowed (SQL Server)</td><td>Fails unless <code>CASCADE</code></td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>UPSERT: ON CONFLICT and MERGE</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Postgres / SQLite: needs a UNIQUE or PK constraint on (user_id, day)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">INSERT</span> <span class=\"k\">INTO</span> user_daily_stats (user_id, day, events)\n<span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"y\">42</span>, <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-01'</span>, <span class=\"y\">7</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">ON</span> <span class=\"k\">CONFLICT</span> (user_id, day)\n<span class=\"k\">DO</span> <span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> <span class=\"k\">SET</span> events = user_daily_stats.events + <span class=\"k\">EXCLUDED</span>.events;   <span class=\"c\">-- EXCLUDED = the row we tried to insert</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Ignore duplicates instead</span>\n<span class=\"k\">INSERT</span> <span class=\"k\">INTO</span> users (user_id, signup_date, country)\n<span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"y\">42</span>, <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-05'</span>, <span class=\"g\">'IN'</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">ON</span> <span class=\"k\">CONFLICT</span> (user_id) <span class=\"k\">DO</span> <span class=\"k\">NOTHING</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- MySQL equivalent</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE events = events + VALUES(events);</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- ANSI MERGE: Postgres 15+, SQL Server, Oracle, Snowflake, BigQuery</span>\n<span class=\"k\">MERGE</span> <span class=\"k\">INTO</span> user_daily_stats t\n<span class=\"k\">USING</span> staging_stats s\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> t.user_id = s.user_id <span class=\"k\">AND</span> t.day = s.day\n<span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"k\">MATCHED</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> <span class=\"k\">SET</span> events = t.events + s.events\n<span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">MATCHED</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">INSERT</span> (user_id, day, events) <span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (s.user_id, s.day, s.events);</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why ON CONFLICT beats \"SELECT then INSERT\"</span>\n    <p>Check-then-insert in application code is a race: two workers both see \"not exists\" and both insert (duplicate) or one fails. ON CONFLICT relies on the unique index to detect the clash atomically, so it's concurrency-safe. MERGE is more flexible (DELETE branches, arbitrary source queries) but in Postgres/SQL Server it can still hit unique violations under concurrent inserts unless run at a strict isolation level. A MERGE source with duplicate keys errors in most engines (\"row matched more than once\") \u2014 dedupe the source first.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. DATE & STRING FUNCTIONS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-dates\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>Date &amp; String Functions for Analytics</h2>\n\n  <h3>DATE_TRUNC, EXTRACT, intervals</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, <span class=\"k\">TIMESTAMP</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17 14:05'</span>)   <span class=\"c\">-- 2024-03-01 00:00:00</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'week'</span>,  <span class=\"k\">TIMESTAMP</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17 14:05'</span>)   <span class=\"c\">-- 2024-03-11 00:00:00 (ISO weeks start Monday)</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(YEAR  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"k\">TIMESTAMP</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17'</span>)          <span class=\"c\">-- 2024</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(DOW   <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17'</span>)               <span class=\"c\">-- 0 (Sunday = 0 ... Saturday = 6)</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(ISODOW <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17'</span>)              <span class=\"c\">-- 7 (Monday = 1 ... Sunday = 7)</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(EPOCH <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'2 hours'</span>)              <span class=\"c\">-- 7200 (seconds)</span>\n     , <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-31'</span> - <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-01'</span>               <span class=\"c\">-- 30 (integer days)</span>\n     , <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-31'</span> + <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 month'</span>              <span class=\"c\">-- 2024-02-29 00:00:00 (clamped to month end)</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">NOW</span>() - <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'7 days'</span>                           <span class=\"c\">-- rolling 7-day window start</span>\n     , <span class=\"o\">AGE</span>(<span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17'</span>, <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2020-01-01'</span>);          <span class=\"c\">-- 4 years 2 mons 16 days</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Monthly active users and revenue: the canonical analytics shape</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, o.order_date)::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> month,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> o.user_id)               <span class=\"k\">AS</span> buyers,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)                           <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.order_date &gt;= <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.order_date &lt;  <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2025-01-01'</span>        <span class=\"c\">-- sargable range, not EXTRACT(YEAR ...) = 2024</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Days from signup to first order</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id,\n       <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(o.order_date)::date - u.signup_date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> days_to_first_order\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id, u.signup_date;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Task</th><th>PostgreSQL</th><th>BigQuery</th><th>Snowflake</th><th>MySQL</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Truncate to month</td><td><code>DATE_TRUNC('month', ts)</code></td><td><code>DATE_TRUNC(d, MONTH)</code> / <code>TIMESTAMP_TRUNC</code></td><td><code>DATE_TRUNC('month', ts)</code></td><td><code>DATE_FORMAT(ts, '%Y-%m-01')</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Add 7 days</td><td><code>d + INTERVAL '7 days'</code></td><td><code>DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 7 DAY)</code></td><td><code>DATEADD(day, 7, d)</code></td><td><code>DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 7 DAY)</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Day difference</td><td><code>d2 - d1</code></td><td><code>DATE_DIFF(d2, d1, DAY)</code></td><td><code>DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)</code></td><td><code>DATEDIFF(d2, d1)</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Day of week</td><td><code>EXTRACT(DOW \u2026)</code> 0=Sun</td><td><code>EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK \u2026)</code> 1=Sun</td><td><code>DAYOFWEEK(d)</code> 0=Sun (default)</td><td><code>DAYOFWEEK(d)</code> 1=Sun</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 time zones and DATEDIFF boundaries</span>\n    <p><code>DATE_TRUNC('day', ts)</code> on a TIMESTAMPTZ truncates in the <em>session</em> time zone, so the same query gives different daily numbers for a server in UTC vs PST. Be explicit: <code>DATE_TRUNC('day', ts AT TIME ZONE 'America/Los_Angeles')</code>. Also, Snowflake/SQL Server <code>DATEDIFF(month, '2024-01-31', '2024-02-01')</code> = 1 because it counts <em>boundaries crossed</em>, not elapsed time.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>CAST and type coercion</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">CAST</span>(<span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17'</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> <span class=\"k\">DATE</span>),      <span class=\"c\">-- ANSI</span>\n       <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-17'</span>::date,                <span class=\"c\">-- Postgres shorthand</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">CAST</span>(amount <span class=\"k\">AS</span> <span class=\"k\">INT</span>),               <span class=\"c\">-- rounds in Postgres (2.5 -&gt; 3), truncates in some engines</span>\n       order_date::date,                  <span class=\"c\">-- strip time from a timestamp</span>\n       <span class=\"g\">'42'</span>::int + <span class=\"y\">1</span>;                     <span class=\"c\">-- 43</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Failing casts raise an error; BigQuery SAFE_CAST / Snowflake TRY_CAST return NULL instead</span></div>\n\n  <h3>String operations</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">LOWER</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(email))                      <span class=\"k\">AS</span> email_norm,   <span class=\"c\">-- normalise before joining/deduping</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SPLIT_PART</span>(email, <span class=\"g\">'@'</span>, <span class=\"y\">2</span>)                  <span class=\"k\">AS</span> domain,       <span class=\"c\">-- Postgres/Snowflake</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SUBSTRING</span>(name <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> FOR <span class=\"y\">3</span>)               <span class=\"k\">AS</span> first3,       <span class=\"c\">-- also SUBSTRING(name, 1, 3)</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">LENGTH</span>(name)                               <span class=\"k\">AS</span> len,\n       <span class=\"o\">POSITION</span>(<span class=\"g\">'@'</span> <span class=\"k\">IN</span> email)                     <span class=\"k\">AS</span> at_pos,\n       <span class=\"o\">REPLACE</span>(phone, <span class=\"g\">'-'</span>, <span class=\"g\">''</span>)                    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> phone_digits,\n       name || <span class=\"g\">' ('</span> || country || <span class=\"g\">')'</span>             <span class=\"k\">AS</span> label,        <span class=\"c\">-- NULL if any part is NULL</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">CONCAT</span>(name, <span class=\"g\">' ('</span>, country, <span class=\"g\">')'</span>)           <span class=\"k\">AS</span> label_safe,   <span class=\"c\">-- CONCAT treats NULL as '' in Postgres</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">REGEXP_REPLACE</span>(phone, <span class=\"g\">'[^0-9]'</span>, <span class=\"g\">''</span>, <span class=\"g\">'g'</span>)   <span class=\"k\">AS</span> digits_only,\n       <span class=\"o\">LPAD</span>(<span class=\"o\">CAST</span>(id <span class=\"k\">AS</span> <span class=\"k\">TEXT</span>), <span class=\"y\">6</span>, <span class=\"g\">'0'</span>)             <span class=\"k\">AS</span> padded_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Aggregate strings</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">STRING_AGG</span>(name, <span class=\"g\">', '</span> <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> name) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> members   <span class=\"c\">-- MySQL: GROUP_CONCAT</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 dirty join keys</span>\n    <p>Joins on text keys fail silently on trailing spaces, case differences and invisible characters: <code>'Alice@x.com'</code> \u2260 <code>'alice@x.com '</code>. Normalise with <code>LOWER(TRIM(...))</code> before joining or deduping, and note that MySQL's default collations are case-insensitive (and older PAD SPACE collations also ignore trailing spaces), while Postgres compares exactly \u2014 the same query can return different counts across engines.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. INTERVIEW Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sf-qa\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Walk through the logical order of a SELECT. Why can't you use a SELECT alias in WHERE, but you can in ORDER BY?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Logical order: FROM/JOIN \u2192 WHERE \u2192 GROUP BY \u2192 HAVING \u2192 SELECT (incl. window functions) \u2192 DISTINCT \u2192 ORDER BY \u2192 LIMIT/OFFSET. Each step only sees what earlier steps produced. Aliases are created in SELECT, so WHERE (which runs earlier) can't reference them, while ORDER BY (which runs later) can. The same logic explains why aggregates can't appear in WHERE (they don't exist before GROUP BY) and why window functions can't be filtered in WHERE (they're computed in SELECT) \u2014 you wrap them in a CTE or use QUALIFY in Snowflake/BigQuery. It's a logical model; the optimizer physically reorders (predicate pushdown, join reordering) but must return the same result.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What's the difference between COUNT(*), COUNT(col), COUNT(DISTINCT col)? What does AVG do with NULLs?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">COUNT(*) counts rows, including rows that are all NULL. COUNT(col) counts rows where col is not NULL. COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts distinct non-NULL values. COUNT(1) is identical to COUNT(*). AVG, SUM, MIN, MAX ignore NULLs, so AVG(x) = SUM(x)/COUNT(x), dividing by non-NULL count only. If NULL should mean zero (e.g., no revenue) use AVG(COALESCE(x, 0)). On an empty input SUM/AVG/MAX return NULL while COUNT returns 0. After a LEFT JOIN, count a right-side key column (COUNT(o.order_id)) not COUNT(*), or unmatched rows count as 1.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query returning the second-highest distinct salary, or NULL if it doesn't exist.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Option 1: MAX below MAX. Returns NULL automatically when there's no second value</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> second_highest\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &lt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Option 2: OFFSET, wrapped in a scalar subquery so an empty result becomes NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> salary\n        <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n        <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">OFFSET</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> second_highest;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Option 3 (generalises to Nth): DENSE_RANK</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> nth_highest\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> salary, <span class=\"o\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dr\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dr = <span class=\"y\">2</span>;</div>Key points: DISTINCT/DENSE_RANK handle ties (two people at the top salary shouldn't make the top salary \"second\"); wrapping in MAX or a scalar subquery turns \"no rows\" into a single NULL row; exclude NULL salaries because Postgres sorts NULLs first in DESC.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why does <code>WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT manager_id FROM employees)</code> return no rows? How do you fix it?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">The subquery contains a NULL (the CEO has no manager). <code>x NOT IN (a, b, NULL)</code> expands to <code>x &lt;&gt; a AND x &lt;&gt; b AND x &lt;&gt; NULL</code>; the last term is UNKNOWN, so the whole predicate is never TRUE and WHERE drops every row. Fixes: (1) <code>NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees r WHERE r.manager_id = e.id)</code> \u2014 null-safe and usually planned as an anti-join; (2) add <code>WHERE manager_id IS NOT NULL</code> inside the subquery; (3) <code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE r.id IS NULL</code>. NOT EXISTS is the habit to show.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Compute the monthly cancellation rate (as a percentage) of orders. What bug do people hit?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, order_date)::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> month,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> orders,\n       <span class=\"o\">ROUND</span>(<span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FILTER</span> (<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'cancelled'</span>)\n             / <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*), <span class=\"y\">0</span>), <span class=\"y\">2</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cancel_pct\n       <span class=\"c\">-- portable: 100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'cancelled' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>The classic bug is integer division: <code>COUNT(\u2026)/COUNT(*)</code> is integer/integer in Postgres and SQL Server, truncating every rate below 100% to 0. Multiply by <code>100.0</code> (or cast to numeric) before dividing. NULLIF guards division by zero. Also decide whether NULL status counts in the denominator \u2014 COUNT(*) includes it, COUNT(status) doesn't.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Find emails that appear more than once, then delete duplicates keeping the lowest id.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Find duplicates</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">LOWER</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(email)) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> email, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"o\">LOWER</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(email))\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) &gt; <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Delete all but the lowest id per email (Postgres self-join delete)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users a\n<span class=\"k\">USING</span> users b\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"o\">LOWER</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(a.email)) = <span class=\"o\">LOWER</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(b.email))\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> a.user_id &gt; b.user_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Alternative with ROW_NUMBER (works in most engines)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"o\">LOWER</span>(<span class=\"o\">TRIM</span>(email)) <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n  ) t <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn &gt; <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n);</div>HAVING is required because the filter is on an aggregate. Normalising the key avoids missing case/whitespace duplicates. Afterwards add a unique index on the normalised expression to stop recurrence. Run the SELECT version first and wrap the DELETE in a transaction.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP \u2014 when would you use each?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">DELETE is DML: removes rows matching a WHERE, logs each row, fires row triggers, is rollback-able, and leaves dead tuples in Postgres until VACUUM \u2014 use for selective removal. TRUNCATE removes all rows by deallocating pages: no WHERE, no row triggers, extremely fast, transactional in Postgres/SQL Server but an implicit commit in MySQL/Oracle; resets auto-increment in MySQL/SQL Server (Postgres needs RESTART IDENTITY); fails on FK-referenced tables unless CASCADE. Use it to empty staging tables in ETL. DROP removes the table itself \u2014 schema, indexes, constraints, permissions. Use when the table is no longer needed.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>How would you paginate through 50M orders for an API? Why not OFFSET?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">OFFSET k reads and discards k rows, so deep pages get linearly slower, and concurrent inserts shift pages, causing duplicates/skips. Use keyset pagination: order by a unique composite key and seek past the last seen value.<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, order_date, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> (order_date, order_id) &lt; (:last_date, :last_id)\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">50</span>;</div>With an index on (order_date DESC, order_id DESC), each page is an index range scan of 50 rows regardless of depth. order_id is the tiebreaker that makes ordering total and deterministic. Trade-off: no random \"jump to page N\".</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>WHERE vs HAVING: given \"users who placed more than 3 orders over $100 each in 2024\", where does each condition go?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> big_orders\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> amount &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>                              <span class=\"c\">-- per-row condition</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> order_date &gt;= <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> order_date &lt;  <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2025-01-01'</span>           <span class=\"c\">-- per-row, sargable</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) &gt; <span class=\"y\">3</span>;                            <span class=\"c\">-- per-group condition</span></div>\"Over $100 each\" and \"in 2024\" are properties of individual rows \u2192 WHERE (also shrinks the data before grouping). \"More than 3 orders\" is a property of the group \u2192 HAVING. If the question were \"total spend over $100\", it would be HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 100 instead.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write an UPSERT that increments a daily counter. What does it require, and why is it better than check-then-insert?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">INSERT</span> <span class=\"k\">INTO</span> user_daily_stats (user_id, day, events)\n<span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"y\">42</span>, CURRENT_DATE, <span class=\"y\">1</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">ON</span> <span class=\"k\">CONFLICT</span> (user_id, day)\n<span class=\"k\">DO</span> <span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> <span class=\"k\">SET</span> events = user_daily_stats.events + <span class=\"k\">EXCLUDED</span>.events;</div>Requires a UNIQUE constraint or index exactly matching the conflict target (user_id, day). EXCLUDED refers to the proposed row. It's atomic: the unique index detects the conflict, so two concurrent writers can't both insert. Application-side SELECT-then-INSERT races. MySQL: ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE; SQL Server/Snowflake/BigQuery/Postgres 15+: MERGE (dedupe the source first, or it errors on multiple matches).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What does this return in Postgres, and why? <code>SELECT name FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 3</code></summary><div class=\"qa-body\">The three rows with the \"largest\" salaries \u2014 but Postgres treats NULL as larger than any value, so employees with NULL salary appear <em>first</em> in DESC order. In MySQL/SQL Server NULLs sort first in ASC instead, so the same query would put them last. Also, if two employees tie for 3rd place, which one appears is non-deterministic. Fix: <code>ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS LAST, id</code> (or filter NULLs), and use <code>FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS WITH TIES</code> or DENSE_RANK if ties should all be included.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why might <code>WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM order_date) = 2024</code> be slow, and how do you rewrite it?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">It is not sargable: the B-tree index on order_date is ordered by raw order_date, not by EXTRACT(YEAR \u2026), so the engine must compute the expression for every row (sequential scan). Rewrite as a half-open range on the bare column: <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= DATE '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; DATE '2025-01-01'</code>, which becomes an index range scan. Alternatives: an expression index on EXTRACT(YEAR FROM order_date), or partition pruning on a date-partitioned table (BigQuery/Snowflake rely on pruning rather than indexes, and the same \"bare column\" rule applies).</div></details>\n</main>",
  "sql_joins": "<main>\n  <h1>SQL JOINs</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-model\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">What a Join Is</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Cartesian product + filter mental model</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-inner\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">INNER JOIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Only matched pairs, NULL keys never match</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-left\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">LEFT / RIGHT JOIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Preserve one side, NULL-extend the other</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-full\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">FULL OUTER JOIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Reconciliation, MySQL emulation</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-cross\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">CROSS JOIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Date spines and zero-filled metrics</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-self\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">SELF JOIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Hierarchies, pairs, a.id &lt; b.id</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-semi\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Semi &amp; Anti Joins</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">EXISTS, NOT EXISTS, the NOT IN trap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-onwhere\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">ON vs WHERE</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">How a filter turns LEFT into INNER</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-fanout\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Fan-out &amp; Double Counting</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Pre-aggregate, then join</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-nonequi\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Non-equi &amp; Range Joins</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Validity periods, buckets, as-of joins</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-using\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">USING / NATURAL Pitfalls</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Implicit columns that break silently</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-algos\"><span class=\"rm-num\">12</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Join Algorithms</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Nested loop, hash, merge \u2014 when each wins</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sj-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">13</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Row counts, anti-joins, write-the-query</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>A join is a filtered Cartesian product:</strong> conceptually, pair every left row with every right row and keep pairs where ON is TRUE; outer joins then add back unmatched rows padded with NULLs. This model predicts row counts exactly: each left row appears once per matching right row, so a key that repeats m times on the left and n times on the right yields m\u00d7n rows.</li>\n    <li><strong>NULL keys never match:</strong> <code>ON a.k = b.k</code> is UNKNOWN when either side is NULL, so NULL-keyed rows drop out of INNER joins and appear unmatched in OUTER joins. To match NULLs to NULLs use <code>IS NOT DISTINCT FROM</code> (usually prevents hash joins, so coalesce to a sentinel if performance matters).</li>\n    <li><strong>ON vs WHERE in a LEFT JOIN:</strong> conditions on the right table belong in ON; putting them in WHERE (<code>WHERE o.status = 'paid'</code>) discards the NULL-extended rows and silently turns the LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN. Conversely, conditions on the <em>left</em> table in ON don't filter left rows at all \u2014 they only control which rows match.</li>\n    <li><strong>Anti-join: NOT EXISTS, not NOT IN:</strong> <code>NOT IN (subquery)</code> returns zero rows if the subquery yields any NULL. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> and <code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE right.pk IS NULL</code> are null-safe; most planners turn NOT EXISTS into a hash anti-join. Semi-joins (EXISTS / IN) return each left row at most once, unlike an INNER JOIN, which duplicates per match.</li>\n    <li><strong>Fan-out double counts:</strong> joining users \u2192 orders \u2192 events multiplies each user's orders by their events, so <code>SUM(amount)</code> is inflated. COUNT(DISTINCT) patches counts but not sums. Fix: aggregate each child table to the join grain first (one row per user), then join the aggregates.</li>\n    <li><strong>CROSS JOIN builds the grid you want to report on:</strong> dates \u00d7 countries (via <code>generate_series</code>) LEFT JOINed to aggregated facts gives zero-filled time series with no missing days \u2014 essential for charts, moving averages and LAG().</li>\n    <li><strong>Engines pick one of three algorithms:</strong> nested loop (any predicate; great when the outer side is small and the inner is indexed), hash join (equi-joins only; builds a hash table on the smaller input, O(n+m), may spill to disk), merge join (equi-joins on sorted inputs; great when both sides are already ordered by the key). Non-equi joins usually force nested loops \u2014 that's why range joins on big tables are slow.</li>\n    <li><strong>Prefer explicit ON over USING/NATURAL:</strong> NATURAL JOIN joins on every same-named column, so adding a column like <code>created_at</code> to both tables silently changes results (often to zero rows). USING is fine for clean key names but merges the columns into one output column.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. WHAT A JOIN IS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-model\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>What a Join Is \u2014 Cartesian Product + Filter</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 logical semantics of every join</span>\n    <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> form the Cartesian product L \u00d7 R (every left row paired with every right row; |L|\u00b7|R| rows).<br>\n    <strong>Step 2:</strong> keep only pairs for which the ON predicate is TRUE (this is the INNER JOIN).<br>\n    <strong>Step 3 (outer joins only):</strong> add back each preserved-side row that matched nothing, with the other side's columns filled with NULL.<br><br>\n    No engine actually materialises the product \u2014 it's a <em>specification</em> of the result, which the optimizer satisfies with nested loops, hashing or sorting.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why this model is worth memorising</span>\n    <p>Every \"why did my row count change?\" question is answered by it. Each left row survives once <em>per matching right row</em>. If the right key is unique (a PK), the join can't increase the left row count. If it isn't unique, rows multiply. If nothing matches, an INNER JOIN drops the row and a LEFT JOIN keeps one NULL-padded copy.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Worked row-count example (the classic interview puzzle)</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">A.k : <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">2</span>, <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>          B.k : <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">3</span>, <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">INNER</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> A.k = B.k   -&gt; <span class=\"y\">4</span> rows   (two 1s x two 1s; <span class=\"y\">2</span>, <span class=\"y\">3</span> unmatched; <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> = <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> is <span class=\"k\">UNKNOWN</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span>  <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span>                -&gt; <span class=\"y\">6</span> rows   (<span class=\"y\">4</span> matched + A<span class=\"g\">'s 2 + A'</span>s <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">RIGHT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span>                -&gt; <span class=\"y\">6</span> rows   (<span class=\"y\">4</span> matched + B<span class=\"g\">'s 3 + B'</span>s <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">FULL</span>  <span class=\"k\">OUTER</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span>          -&gt; <span class=\"y\">8</span> rows   (<span class=\"y\">4</span> matched + <span class=\"y\">2</span> from A + <span class=\"y\">2</span> from B)\n<span class=\"k\">CROSS</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span>                -&gt; <span class=\"y\">16</span> rows  (<span class=\"y\">4</span> x <span class=\"y\">4</span>)</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Join</th><th>Row count bounds (|L| = n, |R| = m)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>INNER</td><td>0 \u2026 n\u00b7m</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LEFT</td><td>n \u2026 n\u00b7m (exactly n if R's key is unique)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>FULL OUTER</td><td>max(n, m) \u2026 n\u00b7m (when n, m \u2265 1)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CROSS</td><td>exactly n\u00b7m</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>SEMI (EXISTS)</td><td>0 \u2026 n \u2014 never duplicates</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ANTI (NOT EXISTS)</td><td>0 \u2026 n</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 a speed-dating event</span>\n    <p>CROSS JOIN: everyone on the left meets everyone on the right. INNER JOIN: only pairs that \"click\" (ON is TRUE) leave as couples \u2014 someone who clicks with three people leaves in three couples. LEFT JOIN: every left-side guest leaves with a partner card, and those who clicked with nobody get a blank card (NULLs). FULL OUTER: both sides get blank cards if unmatched.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>Running schema: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id, manager_id, salary, hire_date)</code>, <code>departments(id, name)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)</code>, <code>users(user_id, signup_date, country)</code>, <code>events(user_id, event_time, event_type)</code>.</p>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. INNER \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-inner\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>INNER JOIN</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 INNER JOIN</span>\n    <p>Returns only the pairs where the ON predicate is TRUE. <code>JOIN</code> alone means INNER JOIN. The old comma syntax <code>FROM a, b WHERE a.k = b.k</code> is equivalent but mixes join and filter conditions and makes an accidental Cartesian product one forgotten predicate away. For inner joins, a predicate in ON or in WHERE gives the same result.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Employees with their department name; employees with NULL or dangling dept_id disappear</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, d.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> departments d <span class=\"k\">ON</span> d.id = e.dept_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Multi-column join: always include every key column</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, s.shipped_at\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> shipments s\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> s.order_id = o.order_id\n <span class=\"k\">AND</span> s.user_id  = o.user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 inner joins silently drop rows</span>\n    <p>\"Revenue by department\" via INNER JOIN excludes every employee with a NULL dept_id or a dept_id missing from departments \u2014 totals won't reconcile with the source table. Before trusting an inner join, compare <code>COUNT(*)</code> before and after, or use LEFT JOIN and inspect the NULLs. Data type mismatch is another silent killer: joining a TEXT key to an INT key forces casts (and kills index use) or fails outright in Postgres.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. LEFT / RIGHT \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-left\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>LEFT / RIGHT JOIN</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 LEFT OUTER JOIN</span>\n    <p>All rows from the left (preserved) table; for each, every matching right row, or one row with right-side columns NULL if there's no match. <code>RIGHT JOIN</code> is the mirror image \u2014 <code>A RIGHT JOIN B</code> \u2261 <code>B LEFT JOIN A</code> (column order aside). In practice teams standardise on LEFT JOIN so the \"driving\" table is always first and readable top-down.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Every user with their order count and revenue, including users with zero orders</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id)          <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders,     <span class=\"c\">-- not COUNT(*): would count 1 for no-order users</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount), <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">users                    orders                         users <span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders\nuser_id | country       order_id | user_id | amount     user_id | order_id | amount\n<span class=\"y\">1</span>       | US            <span class=\"y\">10</span>       | <span class=\"y\">1</span>       | <span class=\"y\">50</span>         <span class=\"y\">1</span>       | <span class=\"y\">10</span>       | <span class=\"y\">50</span>\n<span class=\"y\">2</span>       | <span class=\"k\">IN</span>            <span class=\"y\">11</span>       | <span class=\"y\">1</span>       | <span class=\"y\">20</span>         <span class=\"y\">1</span>       | <span class=\"y\">11</span>       | <span class=\"y\">20</span>\n<span class=\"y\">3</span>       | US            <span class=\"y\">12</span>       | <span class=\"y\">9</span>       | <span class=\"y\">99</span>         <span class=\"y\">2</span>       | <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>     | <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n                                                        <span class=\"y\">3</span>       | <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>     | <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n                                                        (order <span class=\"y\">12</span> with user <span class=\"y\">9</span> is dropped: right-side only)</div>\n\n  <h3>Chaining LEFT JOINs</h3>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 an INNER JOIN after a LEFT JOIN undoes it</span>\n    <p><code>users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON \u2026 JOIN payments p ON p.order_id = o.order_id</code>: for users without orders, o.order_id is NULL, the inner join to payments fails, and those users vanish \u2014 the LEFT JOIN is effectively INNER. Either make every downstream join LEFT, or group the inner part first: <code>users u LEFT JOIN (orders o JOIN payments p ON p.order_id = o.order_id) ON o.user_id = u.user_id</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. FULL OUTER \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-full\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>FULL OUTER JOIN</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 FULL OUTER JOIN</span>\n    <p>Matched pairs, plus unmatched left rows (right side NULL), plus unmatched right rows (left side NULL). Its natural use is <strong>reconciliation</strong>: comparing two sources that should agree (yesterday vs today snapshot, finance ledger vs product events, budget vs actuals).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Reconcile daily revenue between the orders table and the finance ledger</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(o.day, f.day)            <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day,\n       o.revenue                         <span class=\"k\">AS</span> product_rev,\n       f.revenue                         <span class=\"k\">AS</span> finance_rev,\n       <span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> o.day <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'missing_in_product'</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> f.day <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'missing_in_finance'</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> o.revenue &lt;&gt; f.revenue <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"g\">'mismatch'</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"g\">'ok'</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>                <span class=\"k\">AS</span> status\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span> <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>) o\n<span class=\"k\">FULL</span> <span class=\"k\">OUTER</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> finance_daily f <span class=\"k\">ON</span> f.day = o.day\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 MySQL has no FULL OUTER JOIN; and keys need COALESCE</span>\n    <p>Emulate with LEFT JOIN plus the anti-join half of the RIGHT JOIN, combined with <code>UNION ALL</code> (plain <code>UNION</code> would also collapse legitimate duplicate rows):</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> a.k, a.v, b.v <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> a <span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> b <span class=\"k\">ON</span> b.k = a.k\n<span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> b.k, <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>, b.v <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> b\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> a <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> a.k = b.k);</div>\n  <p>And after any full join, the key lives in either side: use <code>COALESCE(a.k, b.k)</code> for grouping/ordering, or rows from one side will show a NULL key. Filtering a full join in WHERE on one side's column turns it into a LEFT or RIGHT join (same mechanism as \u00a708).</p>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. CROSS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-cross\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>CROSS JOIN \u2014 Date Spines &amp; Zero-Filling</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 CROSS JOIN</span>\n    <p>The full Cartesian product, no predicate. Equivalent to <code>JOIN \u2026 ON TRUE</code> or <code>FROM a, b</code>. Accidental cross joins (a forgotten ON) are a classic way to bring down a warehouse; deliberate ones build <em>scaffolds</em>: every (date, segment) combination you want to report on, whether or not data exists.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 \"GROUP BY day\" can't produce days that have no rows</span>\n    <p>Aggregating events by day only returns days on which something happened. A chart then skips days, a 7-day moving average over \"the previous 6 rows\" spans more than 7 days, and <code>LAG()</code> compares against the wrong day. A date spine provides the rows that don't exist in the data; LEFT JOIN the facts onto it and COALESCE to 0.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Daily orders per country for March 2024, with zeros for empty days</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> days <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"o\">GENERATE_SERIES</span>(<span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-01'</span>, <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-31'</span>, <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 day'</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> g(d)\n),\ncountries <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> country <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n),\ndaily <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_date::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day, u.country, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n  <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.order_date &gt;= <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-03-01'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.order_date &lt; <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-04-01'</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">2</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d.day, c.country, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(x.n_orders, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> days d\n<span class=\"k\">CROSS</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> countries c                       <span class=\"c\">-- 31 x (#countries) scaffold</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> daily x <span class=\"k\">ON</span> x.day = d.day <span class=\"k\">AND</span> x.country = c.country\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> c.country, d.day;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Date spine generator</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>generate_series(start, end, interval '1 day')</code> (returns timestamps for date inputs \u2014 cast to date)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>BigQuery</td><td><code>UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY('2024-03-01', '2024-03-31'))</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Snowflake</td><td><code>TABLE(GENERATOR(ROWCOUNT =&gt; 31))</code> + <code>DATEADD(day, ROW_NUMBER() \u2026 - 1, start)</code> (SEQ4 alone may have gaps)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>MySQL 8 / SQL Server</td><td>Recursive CTE, or a permanent calendar dimension table (best practice in warehouses)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 put the spine on the LEFT and filter facts in the CTE</span>\n    <p>If you write <code>daily LEFT JOIN days</code> the scaffold does nothing. And if you filter the fact table in the outer WHERE (e.g. <code>WHERE x.n_orders &gt; 0</code> or a WHERE on a fact column), you drop exactly the zero rows you built the spine for \u2014 see ON vs WHERE.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. SELF \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-self\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>SELF JOIN \u2014 Hierarchies and Pairs</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 self join</span>\n    <p>A table joined to itself under two aliases, so each row can be compared with other rows of the same table. Used for parent/child relationships stored in one table (manager_id \u2192 id), comparing pairs, and \"previous event\" lookups (though window functions like LAG usually do the latter better).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Manager hierarchy</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Each employee with their manager's name (LEFT keeps the CEO, whose manager_id is NULL)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> employee, m.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> manager\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees m <span class=\"k\">ON</span> m.id = e.manager_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Employees earning more than their manager</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.salary, m.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> manager, m.salary <span class=\"k\">AS</span> manager_salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees m <span class=\"k\">ON</span> m.id = e.manager_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; m.salary;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Managers with at least 3 direct reports</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> m.id, m.name, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> reports\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees m\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.manager_id = m.id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> m.id, m.name\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) &gt;= <span class=\"y\">3</span>;</div>\n\n  <p>A fixed number of self-joins only reaches a fixed depth (e \u2192 manager \u2192 manager's manager). For arbitrary depth (full chain of command, org size under a VP) use a recursive CTE: <code>WITH RECURSIVE chain AS (anchor UNION ALL SELECT \u2026 FROM employees e JOIN chain c ON e.manager_id = c.id)</code>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Pairs and the a.id &lt; b.id dedupe</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Pairs of employees in the same department</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> a.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> emp_1, b.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> emp_2, a.dept_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees a\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees b\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span>  a.dept_id = b.dept_id\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> a.id &lt; b.id;          <span class=\"c\">-- not &lt;&gt; : that would return (Ann, Bob) AND (Bob, Ann)</span></div>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Condition</th><th>Rows for a dept of k employees</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>(none beyond dept)</td><td>k\u00b2</td><td>Includes self-pairs (Ann, Ann) and both orders</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>a.id &lt;&gt; b.id</code></td><td>k(k\u22121)</td><td>Ordered pairs \u2014 each pair twice</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>a.id &lt; b.id</code></td><td>k(k\u22121)/2</td><td>Unordered pairs \u2014 each exactly once</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 self joins are quadratic</span>\n    <p>A pairs query over a department of 10,000 people produces ~50M rows. Range self-joins like \"running total = sum of all earlier rows\" (<code>JOIN t b ON b.day &lt;= a.day</code>) are O(n\u00b2); window functions (<code>SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY day)</code>) do it in one sorted pass. Mention this trade-off when you reach for a self join.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. SEMI & ANTI \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-semi\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>Semi &amp; Anti Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 semi-join and anti-join</span>\n    <p><strong>Semi-join:</strong> return left rows that have <em>at least one</em> match on the right, each left row at most once, with no right columns. SQL spellings: <code>EXISTS</code>, <code>IN (subquery)</code>.<br>\n    <strong>Anti-join:</strong> return left rows that have <em>no</em> match. Spellings: <code>NOT EXISTS</code>, <code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE right.key IS NULL</code>, <code>NOT IN</code> (dangerous). Neither has its own keyword in standard SQL (Spark SQL has <code>LEFT SEMI JOIN</code> / <code>LEFT ANTI JOIN</code>), but planners show them as \"Hash Semi Join\" / \"Hash Anti Join\".</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Semi-join: EXISTS vs INNER JOIN</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Users who placed at least one order: each user once</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, u.country\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Same with IN (fine: NULLs are harmless for IN, only for NOT IN)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, u.country\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- WRONG for \"users who ordered\": one row per ORDER, users with 5 orders appear 5 times</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, u.country\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 EXISTS asks a yes/no question</span>\n    <p>EXISTS can stop at the first matching row (no need to find all of them) and never multiplies the left side. The <code>SELECT 1</code> inside is irrelevant \u2014 the select list of an EXISTS subquery is never evaluated. Modern Postgres/SQL Server/Snowflake optimizers plan <code>EXISTS</code> and <code>IN (subquery)</code> identically as a semi-join, so the \"EXISTS is faster than IN\" folklore is mostly from old MySQL versions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Anti-join: three spellings, one trap</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- 1. NOT EXISTS: null-safe, clear intent, planned as an anti-join. Default choice.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- 2. LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL: test a column that can't be NULL in a real match (the join key or PK)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.order_id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- 3. NOT IN: returns ZERO rows if orders.user_id contains even one NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o);   <span class=\"c\">-- guest checkouts with NULL user_id break this</span></div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>NULL in subquery/right key</th><th>Duplicates left rows?</th><th>Typical plan</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NOT EXISTS</code></td><td>Safe</td><td>No</td><td>Hash/merge anti-join</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 IS NULL</code></td><td>Safe (if you test a non-nullable column)</td><td>No (unmatched rows appear once)</td><td>Anti-join or outer join + filter</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NOT IN</code></td><td><strong>Returns nothing</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Postgres can't turn it into an anti-join; runs a (hashed) subplan per row, which degrades badly if the subquery result doesn't fit in memory</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 testing the wrong column for IS NULL</span>\n    <p>In the LEFT JOIN anti pattern, <code>WHERE o.amount IS NULL</code> also returns users whose matched order simply has a NULL amount. Always test the join key or the right table's primary key, which is NULL only for the padding rows.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. ON vs WHERE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-onwhere\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>ON vs WHERE in Outer Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 when each predicate applies</span>\n    <p><strong>ON</strong> decides which right rows <em>match</em> during the join; unmatched left rows are still preserved and NULL-padded. <strong>WHERE</strong> runs <em>after</em> the join on the finished rows, including the NULL-padded ones. For INNER joins the distinction doesn't matter; for OUTER joins it changes the answer.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Goal: every user, with their count of COMPLETED orders (0 if none)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- WRONG: the WHERE removes users with no orders (o.status is NULL -&gt; UNKNOWN)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- and users with only cancelled orders. The LEFT JOIN has become an INNER JOIN.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> completed\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- RIGHT: filter the right table inside ON</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> completed\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.status  = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">users: <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">2</span>, <span class=\"y\">3</span>        orders: (u1, completed), (u1, cancelled), (u2, cancelled)\n\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> version            <span class=\"k\">ON</span> version\nuser_id | completed       user_id | completed\n<span class=\"y\">1</span>       | <span class=\"y\">1</span>               <span class=\"y\">1</span>       | <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n                          <span class=\"y\">2</span>       | <span class=\"y\">0</span>\n                          <span class=\"y\">3</span>       | <span class=\"y\">0</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 WHERE sees NULL padding as data</span>\n    <p>After the LEFT JOIN, user 3's row is <code>(3, NULL, NULL, \u2026)</code>. <code>WHERE o.status = 'completed'</code> evaluates <code>NULL = 'completed'</code> \u2192 UNKNOWN \u2192 dropped. Any WHERE predicate on a right-table column that rejects NULL (\"null-rejecting\") does this, and optimizers actually rewrite such LEFT JOINs to INNER JOINs. The only WHERE condition on the right table that keeps outer-ness is one that accepts NULL, like the anti-join's <code>IS NULL</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>The mirror trap: left-table filters in ON</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Intended: only US users. Actual: ALL users returned; non-US users just get no orders matched.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n <span class=\"k\">AND</span> u.country = <span class=\"g\">'US'</span>          <span class=\"c\">-- restricts matching, NOT the preserved side</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Correct: preserved-side filters go in WHERE</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> u.country = <span class=\"g\">'US'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Predicate on\u2026</th><th>In ON (LEFT JOIN)</th><th>In WHERE</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Right (optional) table</td><td>Restricts matches; all left rows kept \u2714</td><td>Drops unmatched left rows \u2192 acts as INNER \u2718</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Left (preserved) table</td><td>Doesn't remove left rows; just blocks their matches \u2718</td><td>Filters left rows \u2714</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 guest list vs bouncer</span>\n    <p>The LEFT JOIN guarantees every invited guest (left row) gets into the party; ON decides who they're seated with. WHERE is the bouncer at the exit who throws out anyone not meeting a rule. If the rule is \"must be seated with a completed order\", every guest sitting alone (NULLs) gets thrown out \u2014 the guarantee the LEFT JOIN made is broken.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. FAN-OUT \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-fanout\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>Join Fan-out &amp; Double Counting</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 fan-out</span>\n    <p>Fan-out happens when you join on a key that isn't unique on the joined side, so each row multiplies. Joining a parent to two independent child tables (one-to-many each) produces, per parent, <em>orders \u00d7 events</em> rows \u2014 a \"chasm trap\". Any SUM or COUNT over those rows is inflated by the other child's multiplicity.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- user 1: orders 50, 20 (2 rows)   events: 3 rows</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- WRONG: 2 x 3 = 6 joined rows -&gt; revenue = (50+20) x 3 = 210, events = 6</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)   <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue,     <span class=\"c\">-- 210, should be 70</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(e.user_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_events    <span class=\"c\">-- 6, should be 3</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> events e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- RIGHT: pre-aggregate each child to the user grain, then join 1:1:1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> o <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n),\ne <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_events\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id,\n       <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(o.revenue, <span class=\"y\">0</span>)  <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue,\n       <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(o.n_orders, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders,\n       <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(e.n_events, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_events\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.user_id = u.user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 COUNT(DISTINCT) is a partial fix</span>\n    <p><code>COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id)</code> repairs the order count in the fanned-out query, but there is no equivalent for sums: <code>SUM(DISTINCT o.amount)</code> collapses two genuinely different orders of $20 into one. Pre-aggregating (or a correlated scalar subquery per child) is the correct fix. It's also faster: joining two small aggregated tables beats aggregating a multiplied one.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 always know the grain</span>\n    <p>Before writing a join, state the grain of every input (\"one row per order\", \"one row per user-day\") and of the output. Join keys must be unique on at least one side for the grain to be preserved. A quick check: <code>SELECT key, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY key HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code> on the side you think is unique \u2014 dimension tables with accidental duplicates (SCD history, re-ingested rows) are the most common cause of inflated dashboards.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. NON-EQUI \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-nonequi\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>Non-equi &amp; Range Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 non-equi join</span>\n    <p>Any join whose ON uses something other than equality: <code>&lt;, &gt;, BETWEEN, &lt;&gt;</code>, overlaps, LIKE. Typical uses: attach the price/exchange rate valid at the time of a transaction, bucket values into ranges, find events within N minutes of each other, interval overlaps.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>For these examples assume orders also carries <code>product_id</code> and <code>currency</code>.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Price valid at order time: price_history(product_id, price, valid_from, valid_to)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Half-open interval: valid_to is exclusive; current price has valid_to NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, o.order_date, p.price\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> price_history p\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span>  p.product_id  = o.product_id\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.order_date &gt;= p.valid_from\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> (o.order_date &lt; p.valid_to <span class=\"k\">OR</span> p.valid_to <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Bucketing with a ranges table: salary_bands(band, min_sal, max_sal)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, b.band\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> salary_bands b\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.salary &gt;= b.min_sal <span class=\"k\">AND</span> e.salary &lt; b.max_sal;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Sessions: events from the same user within 30 minutes after a signup event</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> s.user_id, s.event_time <span class=\"k\">AS</span> signup_time, x.event_type, x.event_time\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events s\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> events x\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span>  x.user_id = s.user_id\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> x.event_time &gt;  s.event_time\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> x.event_time &lt;= s.event_time + <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'30 minutes'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> s.event_type = <span class=\"g\">'signup'</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 overlapping ranges fan out; inclusive ends double-match</span>\n    <p>If ranges overlap (or both ends are inclusive, so a value equal to a boundary matches two bands), a row joins multiple ranges and sums inflate. Use half-open intervals <code>[from, to)</code> and validate that ranges per key don't overlap. Also note the first example has an equality component (product_id) \u2014 engines hash on that and apply the range as a residual filter. A <em>pure</em> range join with no equality gets a nested loop: O(n\u00b7m).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>\"Latest value as of\" (as-of join)</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- For each order, the user's most recent event before the order (Postgres LATERAL)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, o.order_date, last_e.event_type\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> <span class=\"k\">LATERAL</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.event_type\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events e\n  <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.user_id = o.user_id <span class=\"k\">AND</span> e.event_time &lt;= o.order_date\n  <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> e.event_time <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n) last_e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> <span class=\"k\">TRUE</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- Index on events(user_id, event_time) makes each lookup a single index probe.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Snowflake / DuckDB have a native ASOF JOIN; SQL Server uses CROSS/OUTER APPLY.</span></div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. USING / NATURAL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-using\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>USING / NATURAL Pitfalls</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 USING and NATURAL</span>\n    <p><code>JOIN orders USING (user_id)</code> \u2261 <code>ON users.user_id = orders.user_id</code>, but the output contains a single merged <code>user_id</code> column. For INNER/LEFT it equals the left value; for FULL JOIN it's <code>COALESCE(left.user_id, right.user_id)</code>. <code>NATURAL JOIN</code> is USING over <em>every</em> column name the two tables share.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>What happens</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>NATURAL JOIN after a schema change</td><td>Someone adds <code>created_at</code> or <code>name</code> to both tables \u2192 the join now also requires those to be equal \u2192 result shrinks to (nearly) zero rows, no error</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>NATURAL JOIN with no shared columns</td><td>Silently becomes a CROSS JOIN</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>employees NATURAL JOIN departments</code></td><td>Both have <code>id</code> and <code>name</code> \u2192 joins employee.id = department.id AND employee.name = department.name \u2014 nonsense</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Qualifying a USING column</td><td><code>SELECT o.user_id</code> works in Postgres but Oracle rejects qualified USING columns; unqualified <code>user_id</code> means the merged column</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Different key names</td><td>USING requires identical names (<code>orders.user_id</code> vs <code>users.id</code> can't use it)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 reviewers can't see the join condition</span>\n    <p>NATURAL JOIN hides the predicate inside the schema, so code review can't catch a wrong join and a migration can change query results. Treat it as banned in production. USING is acceptable for consistently named keys (some style guides prefer it), but explicit <code>ON</code> is always safe and works with differently named or multi-column keys.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 12. ALGORITHMS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-algos\"><span class=\"num\">12</span>How Engines Execute Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the logical join is fixed; the physical algorithm is a cost decision</span>\n    <p>The optimizer estimates row counts (from table statistics), checks available indexes and sort orders, and picks the cheapest algorithm per join, plus the join order. Bad estimates (stale stats, correlated predicates, skew) are the main reason a join suddenly gets slow \u2014 e.g. a nested loop chosen because the planner thought the outer side had 10 rows when it had 10 million.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Nested loop join</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">for each row l in outer:\n    for each row r in inner matching l      <span class=\"c\">-- full scan, or an index lookup on inner</span>\n        emit (l, r)</div>\n  <p>Cost: O(n\u00b7m) with scans; O(n\u00b7log m) with an index on the inner join key. The only algorithm that handles <strong>any predicate</strong> (range, LIKE, inequality, OR). Chosen when the outer side is small (after filters) and the inner side has an index on the join key \u2014 the classic OLTP lookup (\"this user's orders\"). Also the basis of LATERAL / APPLY. Disastrous when both sides are large and there's no index.</p>\n\n  <h3>Hash join</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">build:  hash table on join key from the SMALLER input (build side)\nprobe:  stream the larger input, look up each key in the hash table, emit matches</div>\n  <p>Cost: O(n + m), one pass each. <strong>Equality predicates only</strong> (it hashes the key). The default for large analytical joins with no useful ordering. Memory-bound: if the build side exceeds memory (<code>work_mem</code> in Postgres), it partitions both inputs into batches and spills to disk (grace hash join), which is slower but still linear. Building on the wrong (large) side due to bad estimates is a common performance bug. Supports inner, outer, semi and anti variants.</p>\n\n  <h3>Merge (sort-merge) join</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">sort both inputs by join key (skip if already sorted, e.g. via index or upstream <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span>)\nwalk both sorted lists in lockstep with two pointers, emitting matches</div>\n  <p>Cost: O(n log n + m log m) if sorting is needed, O(n + m) if inputs are pre-sorted. Equality joins (Postgres merge join only supports merge-joinable equality operators). Chosen when both inputs are large and already ordered by the key (clustered index, index scan, or the output must be sorted anyway), or when the hash table wouldn't fit in memory. Handles FULL OUTER joins naturally and never needs a big in-memory table.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>Nested loop</th><th>Hash join</th><th>Merge join</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Join predicate</td><td>Any</td><td>Equality only</td><td>Equality (some engines: ranges)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Complexity</td><td>O(n\u00b7m), or O(n log m) with index</td><td>O(n + m)</td><td>O(n + m) if sorted, else + sort cost</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Memory</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Hash table of build side (spills if too big)</td><td>Sort memory (or none if pre-sorted)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Best when</td><td>Small outer, indexed inner; non-equi joins; LIMIT queries</td><td>Large unsorted inputs, one side fits in memory</td><td>Both large and pre-sorted on the key</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Startup</td><td>Immediate first row (good with LIMIT)</td><td>Must build the whole hash table first</td><td>Must sort first (unless pre-sorted)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Distributed engines (Spark, BigQuery, Snowflake)</h3>\n  <p><strong>Broadcast join:</strong> copy the small table to every worker, hash-join locally \u2014 no shuffle of the large table. <strong>Shuffle (partitioned) hash join:</strong> repartition both sides by join key so matching keys land on the same worker. <strong>Skew:</strong> a hot key (e.g. NULL or a test user with millions of events) sends most rows to one worker; mitigate by filtering NULL keys before joining, salting the hot key, or adaptive skew handling (Spark AQE).</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">EXPLAIN</span> <span class=\"k\">ANALYZE</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country;\n<span class=\"c\">-- Look for: \"Hash Join\" with \"Hash\" on users (smaller build side),</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- \"Batches: 1\" (fits in memory) vs &gt; 1 (spilled), and estimated vs actual rows.</span></div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 13. INTERVIEW Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sj-qa\"><span class=\"num\">13</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Table A has keys (1, 1, 2, NULL) and B has (1, 1, 3, NULL). How many rows do INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL and CROSS joins on A.k = B.k return?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">INNER = 4: each of A's two 1s matches each of B's two 1s (2\u00d72); 2 and 3 have no partner; NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN so NULLs never match. LEFT = 6: the 4 matches plus A's unmatched 2 and NULL. RIGHT = 6: 4 plus B's 3 and NULL. FULL = 8: 4 + 2 + 2. CROSS = 16 (4\u00d74). The principle: each row appears once per matching row on the other side, and outer joins add one NULL-padded row per unmatched preserved row.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Find users who have never placed an order. Give two correct ways and one wrong way.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Correct 1: NOT EXISTS (null-safe, planned as an anti-join)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Correct 2: LEFT JOIN + IS NULL on a non-nullable right column</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.order_id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Wrong: NOT IN returns zero rows if any orders.user_id is NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders);</div><code>x NOT IN (\u2026, NULL)</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN or FALSE for every x, never TRUE. NOT IN is only safe if the subquery column is declared NOT NULL (or you filter <code>WHERE user_id IS NOT NULL</code>).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>A query meant to list all users with their count of completed orders (LEFT JOIN orders, WHERE o.status = 'completed') is missing users with no completed orders. Why?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id;</div>WHERE runs after the join. For users with no orders, o.status is NULL, and <code>NULL = 'completed'</code> is UNKNOWN, so those rows are dropped; users with only non-completed orders are dropped too. A null-rejecting WHERE predicate on the right table converts the LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN (optimizers literally rewrite it). Fix: move the condition into ON \u2014 <code>LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.user_id AND o.status = 'completed'</code> \u2014 so it restricts which orders match while preserving every user, and COUNT(o.order_id) gives 0 for them.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>A dashboard joins users to orders and to events and reports revenue 3\u00d7 too high. What's going on and how do you fix it?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Fan-out (chasm trap): orders and events are both one-to-many from users, so per user the join produces orders \u00d7 events rows, and each order amount is repeated once per event. COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) would fix counts but nothing fixes SUM on the multiplied rows. Fix by aggregating each child to the user grain first, then joining:<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> o <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id),\n     e <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_events <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(o.revenue, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(e.n_events, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_events\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.user_id = u.user_id;</div>Also check that \"dimension\" tables really have unique keys \u2014 duplicate rows in a lookup table cause the same inflation.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query for employees who earn more than their manager.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> employee, e.salary, m.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> manager, m.salary <span class=\"k\">AS</span> manager_salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees m <span class=\"k\">ON</span> m.id = e.manager_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; m.salary;</div>A self join with two aliases: e is the employee row, m is the manager row found via e.manager_id = m.id. INNER JOIN is right here \u2014 employees without a manager (CEO) can't satisfy the comparison anyway. If either salary is NULL the comparison is UNKNOWN and the row is excluded, which is usually what you want; mention it.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Produce daily active users for the last 30 days, including days with zero activity.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> days <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"o\">GENERATE_SERIES</span>(CURRENT_DATE - <span class=\"y\">29</span>, CURRENT_DATE, <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 day'</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> g(d)\n),\ndau <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> event_time::date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> user_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dau\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events\n  <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> event_time &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - <span class=\"y\">29</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">AND</span> event_time &lt;  CURRENT_DATE + <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d.day, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(x.dau, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dau\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> days d\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> dau x <span class=\"k\">ON</span> x.day = d.day\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d.day;</div>GROUP BY alone can't emit days with no rows, so a generated date spine is the left table and the aggregated facts are LEFT JOINed onto it. The fact filter lives inside the CTE (not the outer WHERE) so zero days survive. For a per-segment version, CROSS JOIN the spine with the list of segments first. BigQuery: <code>UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY(\u2026))</code>; warehouses usually have a calendar table.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What's the difference between a semi-join and an inner join? When would an INNER JOIN give wrong results for \"customers who ordered\"?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">An inner join returns one row per matching pair, so a customer with five orders appears five times; any aggregate over customer attributes (e.g. AVG age, SUM of credit limit) is weighted by order count. A semi-join (<code>WHERE EXISTS (\u2026)</code> or <code>IN (subquery)</code>) returns each customer at most once and can stop searching at the first match. Patching an inner join with DISTINCT works for listing but costs a sort/hash and is easy to forget inside aggregates. Use EXISTS when you need a filter, a join when you need columns from the other side.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Explain nested loop, hash and merge joins. Which would the planner pick for (a) one user's orders, (b) joining two 500M-row tables on user_id, (c) a range join on timestamps?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Nested loop: for each outer row, find matching inner rows (scan or index probe) \u2014 O(n\u00b7m) or O(n log m) with an index; supports any predicate. Hash join: build a hash table on the smaller input's key, stream the larger one and probe \u2014 O(n+m), equality only, needs memory (spills in batches). Merge join: sort both inputs on the key (or use existing order) and walk them together \u2014 linear once sorted, equality joins, good when inputs are pre-sorted or too big for a hash table.<br><br>(a) Nested loop with an index lookup on orders(user_id): tiny outer side, first row immediately. (b) Hash join (or merge join if both are already sorted/clustered by user_id); in a distributed engine, a shuffle hash join since neither side is small enough to broadcast. (c) Nested loop, since hash and (Postgres) merge joins need equality; if there's also an equality key (same user_id), it hashes on that and applies the range as a residual filter. Specialised engines offer ASOF/IEJoin for pure range joins.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>List all unique pairs of users from the same country, without self-pairs or mirrored duplicates.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> a.user_id <span class=\"k\">AS</span> user_a, b.user_id <span class=\"k\">AS</span> user_b, a.country\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users a\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users b\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span>  a.country = b.country\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> a.user_id &lt; b.user_id;</div><code>a.user_id &lt;&gt; b.user_id</code> would remove self-pairs but still return both (1,2) and (2,1); <code>&lt;</code> imposes an order so each unordered pair appears once, giving k(k\u22121)/2 rows per country of size k. Warn that this is quadratic \u2014 for big groups, consider whether you really need the pairs or just a count (which is k(k\u22121)/2 computed from a GROUP BY).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why is NATURAL JOIN considered dangerous, and how does USING differ from ON?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">NATURAL JOIN joins on every column with the same name in both tables. Adding a shared column later (created_at, name, status) silently adds an equality condition, typically shrinking results to nothing with no error; if no columns are shared, it becomes a CROSS JOIN. With the running schema, employees NATURAL JOIN departments would match on id and name \u2014 meaningless. USING (col) names the join columns explicitly but requires identical names on both sides and merges them into one output column (COALESCE of both sides in a FULL JOIN). ON is the most explicit and flexible: different names, multiple columns, non-equi conditions.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>For each order, attach the exchange rate in effect on the order date. Rates are in fx_rates(currency, rate, valid_from) with no end date.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Derive the end of each validity window with LEAD, then do a half-open range join:<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> r <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> currency, rate, valid_from,\n         <span class=\"o\">LEAD</span>(valid_from) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> currency <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> valid_from) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> valid_to\n  <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> fx_rates\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, o.amount, r.rate, o.amount * r.rate <span class=\"k\">AS</span> amount_usd\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> r\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span>  r.currency = o.currency\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.order_date &gt;= r.valid_from\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> (o.order_date &lt; r.valid_to <span class=\"k\">OR</span> r.valid_to <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>);</div>Half-open windows mean each order matches exactly one rate (no double counting at boundaries); the NULL valid_to covers the current rate; LEFT JOIN keeps orders with no known rate so you can detect gaps rather than silently dropping revenue. Alternative: LEFT JOIN LATERAL (\u2026 WHERE valid_from &lt;= o.order_date ORDER BY valid_from DESC LIMIT 1), or a native ASOF JOIN in Snowflake/DuckDB.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>You LEFT JOIN users to orders and then INNER JOIN payments on the order. Some users disappear. Why?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">For users without orders, the LEFT JOIN produces NULL order columns; the subsequent INNER JOIN on <code>p.order_id = o.order_id</code> compares to NULL, is UNKNOWN, and drops those rows \u2014 the outer join is undone by a later inner join. Fix by making the payments join a LEFT JOIN too, or by nesting the inner join so it happens before the outer one: <code>users u LEFT JOIN (orders o JOIN payments p ON p.order_id = o.order_id) ON o.user_id = u.user_id</code>. Same family of bug as filtering the right table in WHERE.</div></details>\n</main>",
  "sql_subqueries": "<main>\n  <h1>Subqueries, CTEs &amp; Views</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-types\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Subquery Types</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">By position (SELECT / FROM / WHERE) and by return shape</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-correlated\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Correlated vs Non-correlated</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Execution cost, how optimizers decorrelate</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-exists\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">EXISTS vs IN vs JOIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Semi-joins, duplicates, the NOT IN + NULL trap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-anyall\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">ANY / ALL</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Quantified comparisons and empty-set behaviour</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-cte\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">CTEs (WITH)</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Chaining, inlining vs materialization</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-recursive\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Recursive CTEs</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Hierarchies, series, graph traversal with cycle guard</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-views\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Views vs Materialized Views</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Stored queries vs stored results, refresh</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-temp\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Temp Tables vs CTEs</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Statistics, indexes, reuse across statements</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-lateral\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">LATERAL Joins</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Per-row subqueries, top-N per group</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-procs\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Stored Procedures &amp; Functions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">When to use, why DS rarely should</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sq-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Anti-joins, hierarchies, calendars, top-N</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>NOT IN + NULL returns nothing:</strong> <code>x NOT IN (a, b, NULL)</code> expands to <code>x &lt;&gt; a AND x &lt;&gt; b AND x &lt;&gt; NULL</code>. The last term is UNKNOWN, so the whole predicate can never be TRUE and every row is filtered out. Use <code>NOT EXISTS</code> (NULL-safe) or a <code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE right.key IS NULL</code> anti-join for \"rows with no match\".</li>\n    <li><strong>IN / EXISTS are semi-joins, JOIN is not:</strong> a semi-join returns each outer row at most once no matter how many inner matches exist. An inner JOIN returns one row per match, so a user with 5 orders appears 5 times. Reaching for <code>DISTINCT</code> after a JOIN to \"fix\" duplicates is a smell \u2014 you wanted EXISTS.</li>\n    <li><strong>Scalar subquery contract:</strong> must return at most one row and one column. Zero rows \u2192 NULL (silently); more than one row \u2192 runtime error (\"more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression\"). The error only fires when the data actually produces two rows, so it can pass tests and break in production.</li>\n    <li><strong>Correlated subqueries are logically per-row:</strong> semantically re-evaluated for every outer row. Modern optimizers rewrite EXISTS/IN into semi/anti hash joins, but Postgres generally does not decorrelate scalar subqueries in SELECT or aggregates in correlated WHERE \u2014 they run as a SubPlan per row. Rewrite as a JOIN to a pre-aggregated derived table or use a window function.</li>\n    <li><strong>CTE materialization (Postgres 12+):</strong> a non-recursive, side-effect-free CTE referenced once is inlined like a subquery (predicates push down). Referenced two or more times it is materialized once by default. Force with <code>AS MATERIALIZED</code> (optimization fence) or <code>AS NOT MATERIALIZED</code>. Before PG12 every CTE was a fence \u2014 old advice \"CTEs are slow\" comes from that.</li>\n    <li><strong>Recursive CTE anatomy:</strong> anchor member (seed rows) <code>UNION ALL</code> recursive member (references the CTE once, joins to the previous iteration's rows only). Iteration stops when the recursive member yields zero rows. Always add a termination guard (depth limit or visited-path check) for graphs that may contain cycles.</li>\n    <li><strong>View vs materialized view:</strong> a view is a saved query \u2014 always fresh, costs the full query each read. A materialized view stores the result \u2014 fast reads, stale until <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code>; <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> avoids blocking readers but needs a unique index.</li>\n    <li><strong>LATERAL = correlated subquery in FROM:</strong> lets a derived table reference columns from tables to its left, so you can write <code>ORDER BY \u2026 LIMIT N</code> per outer row. With an index on (user_id, amount DESC) it beats ROW_NUMBER for top-N per group when groups are large and N is small. SQL Server spells it <code>CROSS APPLY / OUTER APPLY</code>.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. SUBQUERY TYPES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-types\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>Subquery Types \u2014 by Position and by Shape</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 subquery</span>\n    <p>A subquery (inner query, nested query) is a complete <code>SELECT</code> enclosed in parentheses and used where the outer query expects a value, a list, or a table. Two independent ways to classify one:<br><br>\n    <strong>By position:</strong> in <code>SELECT</code> (scalar expression), in <code>FROM</code> (derived table / inline view), in <code>WHERE</code>/<code>HAVING</code> (predicate input).<br>\n    <strong>By return shape:</strong> scalar (1\u00d71), column / list (N\u00d71), row (1\u00d7N), table (N\u00d7M). The shape must match the context \u2014 a list where a scalar is expected is an error.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>Running schema for the whole note: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id, manager_id, salary, hire_date)</code>, <code>departments(id, name)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)</code>, <code>users(user_id, signup_date, country)</code>, <code>events(user_id, event_time, event_type)</code>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Scalar subquery in SELECT</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Each employee next to the company-wide average (non-correlated: computed once)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name,\n       salary,\n       (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> company_avg,\n       salary - (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> diff_from_avg\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Correlated scalar: department name per row (a JOIN is usually clearer)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name,\n       (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d.name <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> departments d <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> d.id = e.dept_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept_name\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 scalar subqueries that are not scalar</span>\n    <p>If <code>departments</code> had two rows with the same <code>id</code> (no PK), the second query errors at runtime: <em>more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression</em>. If an employee's <code>dept_id</code> matches nothing, the value is NULL \u2014 the row is kept, unlike an INNER JOIN which would drop it. So the correlated scalar subquery behaves like a LEFT JOIN that asserts uniqueness.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Derived table in FROM</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Departments whose average salary exceeds 100k</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, avg_sal\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_sal\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id\n) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept_stats            <span class=\"c\">-- alias required in MySQL / SQL Server / Postgres &lt; 16</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> avg_sal &gt; <span class=\"y\">100000</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why derived tables exist at all</span>\n    <p>SQL's logical order is FROM \u2192 WHERE \u2192 GROUP BY \u2192 HAVING \u2192 SELECT \u2192 ORDER BY. You cannot filter on a SELECT alias or a window function in the same level's WHERE, because WHERE logically runs first. A derived table (or CTE) adds a <em>new level</em>: its SELECT finishes before the outer WHERE begins, so the outer query can filter on anything computed inside. That is the single most common reason to nest.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Subquery in WHERE / HAVING</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Scalar comparison</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- List membership (column subquery)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> country = <span class=\"g\">'DE'</span>);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Row subquery (row constructor comparison) \u2014 Postgres / MySQL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> (dept_id, salary) = (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n                           <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dept_id = <span class=\"y\">10</span> <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- In HAVING: departments larger than the average department size</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> headcount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) &gt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(cnt) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cnt\n                                         <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) t);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Shape</th><th>Returns</th><th>Valid contexts</th><th>Failure mode</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Scalar</td><td>1 row \u00d7 1 col</td><td>SELECT list, <code>=</code> / <code>&gt;</code> comparisons, arithmetic</td><td>0 rows \u2192 NULL; 2+ rows \u2192 runtime error</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Column (list)</td><td>N rows \u00d7 1 col</td><td><code>IN</code>, <code>NOT IN</code>, <code>= ANY</code>, <code>&gt; ALL</code></td><td>NULLs inside the list poison NOT IN / ALL</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Row</td><td>1 row \u00d7 N cols</td><td>Row-constructor comparison <code>(a,b) = (\u2026)</code></td><td>2+ rows \u2192 error; not supported in SQL Server</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Table</td><td>N \u00d7 M</td><td>FROM (derived table), <code>EXISTS</code>, <code>(a,b) IN (\u2026)</code></td><td>Missing alias error in some dialects</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. CORRELATED \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-correlated\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>Correlated vs Non-correlated</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 correlation</span>\n    <p>A <strong>non-correlated</strong> subquery references only its own tables; it can be evaluated once and its result reused. A <strong>correlated</strong> subquery references a column of the outer query (e.g. <code>e.dept_id</code>), so its result depends on the current outer row \u2014 logically it is re-evaluated for every outer row.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Correlated: employees paid above THEIR department's average</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.dept_id, e.salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(e2.salary)\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e2\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e2.dept_id = e.dept_id      <span class=\"c\">-- reference to outer row \u2192 correlated</span>\n);</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the naive cost and how the optimizer escapes it</span>\n    <p>Naively: for each of N employees, scan employees again to compute an average \u2192 O(N\u00b2). With an index on <code>dept_id</code> it becomes N index lookups, still repeating the same average for every employee in the same department.<br><br>\n    <strong>Decorrelation</strong> rewrites the query so the inner part runs once as a set operation: compute <code>AVG(salary) GROUP BY dept_id</code> once, then hash-join it back. Optimizers do this reliably for <code>EXISTS</code>/<code>IN</code> (\u2192 semi-join) and <code>NOT EXISTS</code> (\u2192 anti-join). For correlated <em>scalar aggregates</em>, SQL Server, Oracle, Snowflake and BigQuery usually unnest them; <strong>Postgres generally does not</strong> \u2014 EXPLAIN shows a <code>SubPlan</code> executed per outer row. Do it by hand.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Manual decorrelation #1: join to a pre-aggregated derived table</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.dept_id, e.salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_sal\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) d\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> d.dept_id = e.dept_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; d.avg_sal;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Manual decorrelation #2: window function (single scan, one sort/hash by dept)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary,\n             <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_sal\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; avg_sal;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>What the planner typically does (Postgres)</th><th>Cost shape</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Non-correlated scalar <code>(SELECT AVG(..))</code></td><td>InitPlan \u2014 run once, result cached</td><td>O(N) total</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE x IN (SELECT \u2026)</code></td><td>Hash/merge semi-join (pulled up)</td><td>O(N + M)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE EXISTS (correlated)</code></td><td>Semi-join</td><td>O(N + M)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE NOT EXISTS (correlated)</code></td><td>Anti-join</td><td>O(N + M)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE x NOT IN (SELECT \u2026)</code></td><td>Hashed SubPlan (cannot become anti-join because of NULL semantics); can degrade to per-row scan if the list doesn't fit in work_mem</td><td>O(N + M) best, O(N\u00b7M) worst</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Correlated scalar aggregate in SELECT/WHERE</td><td>SubPlan per outer row</td><td>O(N \u00b7 inner cost)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"correlated subqueries are always slow\"</span>\n    <p>Not true. A correlated <code>EXISTS</code> with an index on the inner join key is often the <em>fastest</em> form because it can stop at the first match. The real performance risk is correlated <em>scalar aggregates</em> and <code>NOT IN</code> on large lists. In an interview say: \"logically per-row; physically depends on whether the optimizer can turn it into a join \u2014 I'd check EXPLAIN.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. EXISTS / IN / JOIN \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-exists\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>EXISTS vs IN vs JOIN</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 semi-join and anti-join</span>\n    <p><strong>Semi-join:</strong> return outer rows that have <em>at least one</em> match, each outer row at most once, with no inner columns. <code>IN (subquery)</code> and <code>EXISTS</code> are semi-joins.<br>\n    <strong>Anti-join:</strong> return outer rows that have <em>no</em> match. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> and <code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE inner.key IS NULL</code> are anti-joins. <code>NOT IN</code> is <em>almost</em> one \u2014 except for NULLs.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Duplicates: JOIN multiplies, semi-joins don't</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- users: (1,'US'), (2,'DE')   orders: user 1 has 3 orders, user 2 has 0</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.* <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id;\n<span class=\"c\">-- \u2192 user 1 three times</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.* <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);\n<span class=\"c\">-- \u2192 user 1 once</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.* <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders);\n<span class=\"c\">-- \u2192 user 1 once (IN deduplicates logically)</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 what EXISTS actually checks</span>\n    <p>EXISTS only asks \"does the inner query produce any row?\" \u2014 the SELECT list is ignored, so <code>SELECT 1</code>, <code>SELECT *</code> and <code>SELECT NULL</code> are identical. The engine can stop at the first matching row. EXISTS is always TRUE or FALSE, never UNKNOWN \u2014 that is why it is NULL-safe.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>NULLs: the NOT IN catastrophe</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Find users who never ordered. Suppose orders contains a row with user_id = NULL</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- (e.g. guest checkout).</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders);\n<span class=\"c\">-- \u2192 ZERO rows, always, as soon as one NULL user_id exists in orders</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Why: 3 NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   = 3 &lt;&gt; 1 AND 3 &lt;&gt; 2 AND 3 &lt;&gt; NULL</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   = TRUE  AND TRUE   AND UNKNOWN</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   = UNKNOWN            \u2192 WHERE keeps only TRUE \u2192 row dropped</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Correct anti-join, three equivalent ways:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.* <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.* <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>;     <span class=\"c\">-- test the JOIN key (or a NOT NULL column) of the right side</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Expression</th><th>Inner list</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>3 IN (1, 3, NULL)</code></td><td>contains match</td><td>TRUE</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>3 IN (1, 2, NULL)</code></td><td>no match, has NULL</td><td>UNKNOWN (filtered like FALSE \u2014 harmless)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>3 NOT IN (1, 2, NULL)</code></td><td>no match, has NULL</td><td>UNKNOWN \u2192 row dropped (the bug)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL IN (1, 2)</code></td><td>outer value NULL</td><td>UNKNOWN</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NULL NOT IN (SELECT \u2026 empty)</code></td><td>empty set</td><td>TRUE (empty list \u2192 vacuously true)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>NOT EXISTS (\u2026)</code></td><td>any</td><td>TRUE or FALSE, never UNKNOWN</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 the LEFT JOIN anti-join on the wrong column</span>\n    <p><code>LEFT JOIN orders o \u2026 WHERE o.amount IS NULL</code> is wrong if <code>amount</code> can be NULL on real orders \u2014 those users get reported as \"never ordered\". Always test the join key or a column declared NOT NULL. And never put the right-table filter in WHERE when you want to keep unmatched rows: <code>WHERE o.status = 'paid'</code> turns the LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN; put it in the <code>ON</code> clause.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>IN (subquery)</th><th>EXISTS</th><th>JOIN</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Duplicates of outer row</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes \u2014 one per match</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Can return inner columns</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Negated form NULL-safe?</td><td><code>NOT IN</code>: no</td><td><code>NOT EXISTS</code>: yes</td><td>LEFT JOIN + IS NULL: yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Multi-column match</td><td><code>(a,b) IN (SELECT a,b \u2026)</code> (not SQL Server)</td><td>Any predicate, incl. ranges</td><td>Any predicate</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Performance (modern optimizers)</td><td>Semi-join</td><td>Semi-join</td><td>Join (+ DISTINCT cost if you dedupe)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 guest list vs seating chart</span>\n    <p>EXISTS/IN is the bouncer with a guest list: each person is either let in or not, once. A JOIN is the seating chart: if you're invited to three tables, you appear on the chart three times. NOT IN with a NULL in the list is a guest list with a smudged, unreadable name \u2014 the bouncer can't prove you're <em>not</em> that person, so he turns everyone away.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. ANY / ALL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-anyall\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>ANY / ALL \u2014 Quantified Comparisons</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 ANY (SOME) and ALL</span>\n    <p><code>x op ANY (subquery)</code> is TRUE if the comparison is TRUE for <em>at least one</em> value. <code>x op ALL (subquery)</code> is TRUE if it is TRUE for <em>every</em> value. <code>SOME</code> is a synonym for <code>ANY</code>.<br><br>\n    Equivalences: <code>= ANY</code> \u2261 <code>IN</code>; <code>&lt;&gt; ALL</code> \u2261 <code>NOT IN</code> (same NULL problem); <code>&gt; ALL (\u2026)</code> \u2248 <code>&gt; (SELECT MAX(\u2026))</code>; <code>&gt; ANY (\u2026)</code> \u2248 <code>&gt; (SELECT MIN(\u2026))</code> \u2014 but the MAX/MIN forms differ on empty sets and NULLs.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Employees who earn more than EVERY employee in dept 20</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; <span class=\"k\">ALL</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> salary <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dept_id = <span class=\"y\">20</span>);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Employees who earn more than AT LEAST ONE employee in dept 20</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; <span class=\"k\">ANY</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> salary <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dept_id = <span class=\"y\">20</span>);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Postgres also accepts arrays:  WHERE status = ANY (ARRAY['paid','shipped'])</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--                                WHERE status = ANY ($1)   -- great for parameterised lists</span></div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Inner set</th><th><code>x &gt; ALL (set)</code></th><th><code>x &gt; (SELECT MAX \u2026)</code></th><th><code>x &gt; ANY (set)</code></th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Empty (dept 20 has no rows)</td><td>TRUE for every x</td><td>MAX = NULL \u2192 UNKNOWN \u2192 no rows</td><td>FALSE</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>{50, NULL}, x = 60</td><td>UNKNOWN (60 &gt; NULL unknown)</td><td>MAX ignores NULL \u2192 60 &gt; 50 \u2192 TRUE</td><td>TRUE (one TRUE is enough)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>{50, 70}, x = 60</td><td>FALSE</td><td>FALSE</td><td>TRUE</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 ALL over an empty set is vacuously TRUE</span>\n    <p>\"Employees earning more than everyone in department 99\" returns <em>all employees</em> if department 99 doesn't exist. The MAX rewrite returns none. Neither is wrong \u2014 they answer different questions \u2014 but you must know which one you wrote. ANY/ALL are rare in analytics code; interviewers ask them mainly to probe empty-set and NULL logic.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. CTEs \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-cte\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>CTEs (WITH) \u2014 Readability and Materialization</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 Common Table Expression</span>\n    <p>A CTE is a named subquery defined in a <code>WITH</code> clause at the top of a statement, visible to the rest of that one statement only. Multiple CTEs are comma-separated, and each can reference the ones defined before it. Semantically a non-recursive CTE is the same as a derived table; its value is readability, reuse within the statement, and (in some engines) control over materialization.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Chaining CTEs \u2014 a pipeline you can read top-down</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Monthly revenue per country, month-over-month growth, top country each month</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> paid_orders <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, o.user_id, o.amount,\n           <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, o.order_date) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> month\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>\n),\ncountry_month <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, p.month, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(p.amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> paid_orders p\n    <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = p.user_id\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country, p.month\n),\nranked <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> country, month, revenue,\n           <span class=\"o\">RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> revenue <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rnk\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> country_month\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> month, country, revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> ranked\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rnk = <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 CTEs are named steps, not temp tables</span>\n    <p>Write each CTE as one transformation with a name that says what the rows <em>are</em> (<code>paid_orders</code>, <code>country_month</code>). Interviewers read top-down; debugging is easy because you can swap the final SELECT for <code>SELECT * FROM country_month</code>. Whether the engine actually stores the intermediate rows is a separate, engine-specific question.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Materialization behaviour by engine</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Default behaviour</th><th>Control</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Postgres \u2264 11</td><td>Always materialized \u2014 an <em>optimization fence</em>: outer predicates are not pushed in, indexes on base tables can't be used through it</td><td>None (rewrite as subquery)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Postgres 12+</td><td>Inlined if non-recursive, no side effects (no data-modifying statement, no volatile functions) and referenced <strong>once</strong>; materialized if referenced 2+ times</td><td><code>WITH x AS MATERIALIZED (\u2026)</code>, <code>AS NOT MATERIALIZED (\u2026)</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>Always inlined (re-executed per reference)</td><td>Use a temp table to materialize</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>MySQL 8</td><td>Optimizer chooses: merge into outer query or materialize</td><td>Optimizer hints (<code>MERGE</code> / <code>NO_MERGE</code>)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Snowflake / BigQuery</td><td>Optimizer decides; BigQuery generally re-evaluates a CTE per reference (non-recursive)</td><td>Temp table / materialized intermediate</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Postgres 12+: inlined, so the outer filter pushes down to the index on orders(user_id)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> o <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class=\"y\">42</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Forced fence: scans + stores ALL orders first, then filters. Usually worse...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> o <span class=\"k\">AS</span> <span class=\"k\">MATERIALIZED</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class=\"y\">42</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- ...but MATERIALIZED helps when an expensive CTE is referenced many times</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- or when you want to stop the planner from making a bad inlining decision.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> heavy <span class=\"k\">AS</span> <span class=\"k\">MATERIALIZED</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, expensive_score(user_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> s <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> a.user_id, b.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> heavy a <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> heavy b <span class=\"k\">ON</span> a.s = b.s <span class=\"k\">AND</span> a.user_id &lt; b.user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"CTEs are just for readability, zero performance impact\"</span>\n    <p>True in Postgres 12+ for single-reference CTEs; false elsewhere. In SQL Server a CTE referenced 3 times executes 3 times. In Postgres 11 a CTE prevented predicate pushdown. Safe interview answer: \"Logically equivalent to a subquery; physically, depends on the engine \u2014 Postgres 12+ inlines single-use CTEs, and I can force behaviour with MATERIALIZED.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. RECURSIVE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-recursive\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>Recursive CTEs</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 recursive CTE</span>\n    <p><code>WITH RECURSIVE name AS (anchor UNION [ALL] recursive_member)</code>. Evaluation is iterative, not truly recursive:<br><br>\n    1. Run the <strong>anchor</strong> \u2192 these rows form the result and the <em>working table</em>.<br>\n    2. Run the <strong>recursive member</strong> with <code>name</code> referring <em>only to the working table</em> (the previous iteration's new rows).<br>\n    3. Append its output to the result; it becomes the new working table.<br>\n    4. Repeat until an iteration produces zero rows.<br><br>\n    <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps everything; <code>UNION</code> discards rows already seen (a cheap cycle guard when whole rows repeat). Dialects: Postgres/MySQL 8/SQLite require <code>RECURSIVE</code>; SQL Server omits it and caps depth with <code>OPTION (MAXRECURSION n)</code> (default 100); MySQL caps at <code>cte_max_recursion_depth</code> (default 1000).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Org chart: hierarchy with depth and path</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> org <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"c\">-- anchor: the root(s) \u2014 the CEO has no manager</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, name, manager_id,\n           <span class=\"y\">1</span>             <span class=\"k\">AS</span> depth,\n           name::<span class=\"k\">text</span>    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> path\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> manager_id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n\n    <span class=\"c\">-- recursive member: direct reports of the rows found in the previous step</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.id, e.name, e.manager_id,\n           o.depth + <span class=\"y\">1</span>,\n           o.path || <span class=\"g\">' &gt; '</span> || e.name\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n    <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> org o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.manager_id = o.id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, depth, <span class=\"o\">repeat</span>(<span class=\"g\">'  '</span>, depth - <span class=\"y\">1</span>) || name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> indented, path\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> org\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> path;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">id | depth | indented        | path\n---+-------+-----------------+--------------------------\n 1 |     1 | Ana             | Ana\n 2 |     2 |   Ben           | Ana &gt; Ben\n 4 |     3 |     Dee         | Ana &gt; Ben &gt; Dee\n 3 |     2 |   Cal           | Ana &gt; Cal</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 column types in the anchor fix the types for every iteration</span>\n    <p>In Postgres, if the anchor emits <code>name</code> as <code>varchar(50)</code> and the recursive member emits a longer concatenation, you get \"recursive query column has type varchar(50) in non-recursive term but type text overall\". Cast in the anchor (<code>name::text</code>, <code>1::bigint</code>). SQL Server is even stricter \u2014 types must match exactly, so <code>CAST(name AS varchar(4000))</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- All direct + indirect reports of employee 2 (subtree), with distance</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> reports <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, name, <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> lvl <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">2</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.id, e.name, r.lvl + <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> reports r <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.manager_id = r.id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> reports <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> lvl &gt; <span class=\"y\">0</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Walking UP: the management chain above employee 4</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> chain <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, name, manager_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">4</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> m.id, m.name, m.manager_id\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees m <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> chain c <span class=\"k\">ON</span> m.id = c.manager_id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> chain;</div>\n\n  <h3>Generating a series (calendar spine)</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Every day in January 2024, with revenue (0 for days with no orders)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> days <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> day + <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> days <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> day &lt; <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-31'</span>   <span class=\"c\">-- date + int = date in Postgres</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d.day, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount), <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> days d\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.order_date = d.day\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d.day\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d.day;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Postgres shortcut (not recursive): generate_series</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> gs::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"o\">generate_series</span>(<span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class=\"k\">DATE</span> <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-31'</span>, <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 day'</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> gs;\n<span class=\"c\">-- BigQuery: UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY('2024-01-01','2024-01-31'))</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why you need a spine</span>\n    <p>GROUP BY can only produce groups that exist in the data. A day with zero orders has no row, so it silently vanishes from charts, and later window functions like \"7-row moving average\" average over the wrong days. Generate the complete domain first, then LEFT JOIN facts onto it. If <code>order_date</code> is a timestamp, join on <code>o.order_date &gt;= d.day AND o.order_date &lt; d.day + 1</code> to keep the index usable.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Graph traversal with a cycle guard</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- edges(src, dst): follower graph, may contain cycles (A\u2192B\u2192C\u2192A)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- All nodes reachable from node 1 within 5 hops, with the path taken</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> reach <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.dst <span class=\"k\">AS</span> node, <span class=\"k\">ARRAY</span>[e.src, e.dst] <span class=\"k\">AS</span> path, <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> hops\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> edges e\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.src = <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.dst, r.path || e.dst, r.hops + <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> reach r\n    <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> edges e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.src = r.node\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.dst &lt;&gt; <span class=\"k\">ALL</span> (r.path)      <span class=\"c\">-- don't revisit a node already on this path</span>\n      <span class=\"k\">AND</span> r.hops &lt; <span class=\"y\">5</span>                  <span class=\"c\">-- hard depth cap as a second safety net</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> node, <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(hops) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> shortest_hops\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> reach\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> node;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Postgres 14+: built-in CYCLE clause does the path bookkeeping for you</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> reach(node) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dst <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> edges <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> src = <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.dst <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> reach r <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> edges e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.src = r.node\n) <span class=\"k\">CYCLE</span> node <span class=\"k\">SET</span> is_cycle <span class=\"k\">USING</span> visited\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> node <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> reach <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> is_cycle;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 UNION is not a cycle guard when rows carry a path or depth</span>\n    <p><code>UNION</code> only removes rows that are <em>entirely</em> identical. Once each row carries <code>depth</code> or <code>path</code>, revisiting a node yields a different row (depth 4 vs depth 1), so UNION never deduplicates and the query loops forever (or until a recursion limit). Use an explicit visited-path check. Also note the path-per-row approach enumerates <em>all simple paths</em>, which explodes combinatorially on dense graphs \u2014 SQL is a poor graph engine beyond small hierarchies.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 a phone tree</span>\n    <p>The CEO (anchor) calls her direct reports. Each of them calls their own reports \u2014 but only the people who were <em>just</em> called make calls in the next round (the working table). The tree finishes when a round makes no calls. A cycle is someone whose report is also their boss: without a \"have I already been called?\" list, the phones never stop ringing.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. VIEWS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-views\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>Views vs Materialized Views</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 view and materialized view</span>\n    <p>A <strong>view</strong> is a named, stored query. Selecting from it substitutes the definition into your query (like an inlined CTE), so it is always current and costs whatever the underlying query costs. A <strong>materialized view</strong> stores the query's result as a physical table; reads are fast and indexable but the data is a snapshot as of the last refresh.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Plain view: access layer / reusable logic</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">VIEW</span> active_users <span class=\"k\">AS</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, u.country, <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(e.event_time) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> last_seen\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> events e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.user_id = u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.user_id, u.country\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(e.event_time) &gt; <span class=\"o\">now</span>() - <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'30 days'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Materialized view: precomputed daily KPIs for a dashboard</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">MATERIALIZED</span> <span class=\"k\">VIEW</span> daily_revenue <span class=\"k\">AS</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date;\n\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> UNIQUE <span class=\"k\">INDEX</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> daily_revenue (order_date);   <span class=\"c\">-- required for CONCURRENTLY</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">REFRESH</span> <span class=\"k\">MATERIALIZED</span> <span class=\"k\">VIEW</span> daily_revenue;               <span class=\"c\">-- full rebuild, blocks reads</span>\n<span class=\"k\">REFRESH</span> <span class=\"k\">MATERIALIZED</span> <span class=\"k\">VIEW</span> <span class=\"k\">CONCURRENTLY</span> daily_revenue;  <span class=\"c\">-- diff-based, readers not blocked</span></div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>View</th><th>Materialized view</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Stores</td><td>Query text</td><td>Result rows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Always current</td><td>Stale until refresh</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Read cost</td><td>Full underlying query</td><td>Table scan / index lookup</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Indexes</td><td>No (uses base-table indexes)</td><td>Yes, on the MV itself</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Refresh (Postgres)</td><td>N/A</td><td>Manual / cron, full recompute; no built-in incremental refresh</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Refresh (Snowflake, BigQuery, Oracle)</td><td>N/A</td><td>Automatic / incremental for supported query shapes (restrictions on joins, window functions, non-deterministic functions)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Use for</td><td>Security (expose a subset of columns), stable interface over changing tables, shared business logic</td><td>Expensive aggregations read often, dashboards, feature tables that tolerate lag</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 views stacked on views</span>\n    <p>Views are cheap to create and easy to stack: <code>v_revenue</code> on <code>v_orders_clean</code> on <code>v_orders_dedup</code>. Each SELECT re-expands the whole chain, the planner may fail to push filters through aggregates or DISTINCT in lower layers, and nobody can tell what the final query does. In dbt-style analytics stacks, the fix is to materialize key layers as tables. Also: <code>SELECT *</code> in a Postgres view is expanded at creation time \u2014 new columns on the base table don't appear until you recreate it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. TEMP TABLES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-temp\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>Temp Tables vs CTEs</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Temp table: lives until end of session (or transaction with ON COMMIT DROP)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">TEMP</span> <span class=\"k\">TABLE</span> recent_buyers <span class=\"k\">AS</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> spend_30d\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> order_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - <span class=\"y\">30</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id;\n\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">INDEX</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> recent_buyers (user_id);\n<span class=\"k\">ANALYZE</span> recent_buyers;           <span class=\"c\">-- give the planner real row counts</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- reused by several later statements in the same session</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> recent_buyers <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> spend_30d &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(r.spend_30d) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> recent_buyers r <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">USING</span> (user_id) <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>CTE</th><th>Temp table</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Scope</td><td>One statement</td><td>Session (visible across statements)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Statistics</td><td>Planner estimates from the expression (often badly after several joins/aggregates)</td><td>Real stats after ANALYZE \u2192 better downstream join choices</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Indexes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Materialized?</td><td>Engine-dependent (see \u00a705)</td><td>Always</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Overhead</td><td>None</td><td>Write cost, catalog churn; not visible to other sessions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Readable single queries, recursion</td><td>Multi-step scripts, reusing an expensive intermediate many times, fixing bad cardinality estimates</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the estimate problem</span>\n    <p>After three joins and a GROUP BY, the planner's row-count estimate can be off by 100\u00d7, causing a nested loop where a hash join was needed. Breaking the pipeline with a temp table + ANALYZE resets estimates to the truth. That is the main legitimate performance reason to prefer temp tables in Postgres \u2014 not \"CTEs are slow\".</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. LATERAL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-lateral\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>LATERAL Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 LATERAL</span>\n    <p>Normally a derived table in FROM cannot see the other FROM items. <code>LATERAL</code> lets a subquery (or set-returning function) reference columns from items <em>to its left</em>, evaluating it once per left row \u2014 a correlated subquery that can return many rows and many columns. <code>CROSS JOIN LATERAL</code> drops left rows with no output; <code>LEFT JOIN LATERAL \u2026 ON TRUE</code> keeps them with NULLs. SQL Server / Oracle: <code>CROSS APPLY</code> / <code>OUTER APPLY</code>. MySQL 8.0.14+ supports LATERAL. Snowflake supports LATERAL mainly with FLATTEN; BigQuery has no general LATERAL (use window functions or correlated ARRAY subqueries).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Top-N per group</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- 3 largest orders per user, keeping users with no orders</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, t.order_id, t.amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> <span class=\"k\">LATERAL</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, o.amount\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id        <span class=\"c\">-- references the left table</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> o.amount <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">3</span>\n) t <span class=\"k\">ON</span> <span class=\"k\">TRUE</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Supporting index: each lateral call becomes an index range scan that stops after 3 rows</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">INDEX</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> orders (user_id, amount <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Window-function equivalent (scans/sorts ALL orders)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, amount,\n             <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> amount <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn &lt;= <span class=\"y\">3</span>;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>LATERAL + LIMIT</th><th>ROW_NUMBER filter</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Work done</td><td>#groups \u00d7 (index seek + N rows)</td><td>Sort/scan all rows, then filter</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Wins when</td><td>Few groups relative to rows, large groups, small N, good index</td><td>Most rows needed anyway, no index, many tiny groups</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Keeps groups with 0 rows</td><td>Yes with LEFT JOIN LATERAL</td><td>No (need a join from users)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Portability</td><td>Postgres, MySQL 8, SQL Server (APPLY), Oracle</td><td>Everywhere</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- LATERAL also avoids repeating expressions: compute once, reuse by name</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.order_id, calc.net, calc.net * <span class=\"y\">0.2</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> tax\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">CROSS</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> <span class=\"k\">LATERAL</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> o.amount - <span class=\"y\">5</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> net) calc\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> calc.net &gt; <span class=\"y\">0</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- And unnests per-row arrays/JSON (set-returning functions are implicitly lateral)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.user_id, tag\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events e, <span class=\"o\">jsonb_array_elements_text</span>(e.payload -&gt; <span class=\"g\">'tags'</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> tag;</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. PROCS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-procs\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>Stored Procedures &amp; Functions \u2014 Briefly</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 function vs procedure (Postgres)</span>\n    <p>A <strong>function</strong> (<code>CREATE FUNCTION</code>, in SQL or PL/pgSQL) returns a value or a set of rows and is called inside a query; it runs inside the caller's transaction. A <strong>procedure</strong> (<code>CREATE PROCEDURE</code>, PG11+) is invoked with <code>CALL</code>, returns nothing (or OUT params), and can <code>COMMIT</code>/<code>ROLLBACK</code> mid-body \u2014 useful for batch maintenance jobs. Functions declare volatility (<code>IMMUTABLE</code> / <code>STABLE</code> / <code>VOLATILE</code>), which tells the planner whether it may cache, inline, or use them in indexes.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Simple SQL function: inlinable by the planner, usable in queries</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"k\">FUNCTION</span> fiscal_quarter(d <span class=\"k\">date</span>) <span class=\"k\">RETURNS</span> <span class=\"k\">int</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LANGUAGE</span> <span class=\"k\">sql</span> <span class=\"k\">IMMUTABLE</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> $$\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> ((<span class=\"o\">EXTRACT</span>(<span class=\"k\">MONTH</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> d)::<span class=\"k\">int</span> + <span class=\"y\">2</span>) % <span class=\"y\">12</span>) / <span class=\"y\">3</span> + <span class=\"y\">1</span>   <span class=\"c\">-- FY starting in October</span>\n$$;\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> fiscal_quarter(order_date) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> fq, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Good uses</th><th>Why data scientists rarely should</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Enforcing invariants close to data (triggers, constraints too complex for CHECK)</td><td>Logic hidden in the database is invisible to code review, version control and CI unless you have migration tooling</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Reducing round-trips for OLTP application logic</td><td>Analytics work belongs in versioned, testable transformations (dbt models, Python pipelines)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Batch maintenance jobs (procedures with COMMIT)</td><td>PL/pgSQL row-by-row loops are far slower than set-based SQL</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Small IMMUTABLE helpers reused across queries / expression indexes</td><td>Not portable across warehouses; Snowflake/BigQuery UDFs differ completely</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 mislabelled volatility</span>\n    <p>Declaring a function <code>IMMUTABLE</code> when it reads a table or depends on <code>now()</code> or timezone settings lets Postgres constant-fold or index its result \u2014 producing silently wrong answers later. When in doubt, <code>STABLE</code> (same result within one statement) is the honest choice for functions that read data.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sq-qa\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query for users who have never placed an order. Why might the NOT IN version return zero rows?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Preferred: NOT EXISTS, which is NULL-safe and planned as an anti-join.\n<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n);</div>\nEquivalent: <code>LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.user_id WHERE o.user_id IS NULL</code>. The <code>NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders)</code> version returns zero rows if any <code>orders.user_id</code> is NULL: <code>x NOT IN (\u2026, NULL)</code> becomes <code>\u2026 AND x &lt;&gt; NULL</code>, which is UNKNOWN, never TRUE. It is also hard for Postgres to optimize (no anti-join), so it is worse on both correctness and speed. If forced to use NOT IN, add <code>WHERE user_id IS NOT NULL</code> to the subquery.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query for employees who earn more than the average of their own department \u2014 two ways.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Correlated subquery (clear, but Postgres runs it as a per-row SubPlan):\n<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.dept_id, e.salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees x <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> x.dept_id = e.dept_id);</div>\nWindow function (one pass, scales):\n<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary,\n             <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept_avg\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; dept_avg;</div>\nMention that the window version must be wrapped because a window function cannot appear in WHERE. A third option is joining to a GROUP BY derived table. Employees with NULL dept_id: the correlated version compares against AVG over <code>dept_id = NULL</code> (no rows \u2192 NULL) so they're excluded; the window version puts all NULL-dept employees in one partition and compares them to each other \u2014 a subtle difference worth calling out.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>EXISTS vs IN vs JOIN \u2014 which do you use and when?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Semantics first: use a JOIN when you need columns from the other table and want one row per match. Use EXISTS / IN when you only need to filter the outer table by \"has a match\" \u2014 they're semi-joins and never duplicate outer rows, so you don't need DISTINCT. For \"has no match\", use NOT EXISTS (or LEFT JOIN \u2026 IS NULL), never NOT IN on a nullable column. Performance: modern optimizers (Postgres, SQL Server, Snowflake) plan IN and EXISTS identically as semi-joins, so \"EXISTS is faster than IN\" is mostly folklore from old MySQL/Oracle. EXISTS is more flexible (multi-column, range or inequality conditions). A JOIN followed by DISTINCT to remove duplicates is both slower (extra sort/hash) and fragile (DISTINCT may collapse rows that legitimately repeat).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Find the second-highest distinct salary using only subqueries. What does it return if there's only one distinct salary?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> second_highest\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &lt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees);</div>\nIf all employees earn the same, the WHERE removes every row and <code>MAX</code> over zero rows returns a single row containing NULL \u2014 usually the desired behaviour (\"no second-highest\"). Compare with <code>SELECT DISTINCT salary \u2026 ORDER BY salary DESC OFFSET 1 LIMIT 1</code>, which returns <em>zero rows</em> instead of NULL; wrap it as a scalar subquery <code>SELECT (SELECT \u2026) AS second_highest</code> to get NULL. For Nth-highest generally, use <code>DENSE_RANK() = N</code>.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query listing every employee under manager 7 (direct and indirect), their level below 7, and the reporting path.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> sub <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, name, <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> lvl, name::<span class=\"k\">text</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> path\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> manager_id = <span class=\"y\">7</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.id, e.name, s.lvl + <span class=\"y\">1</span>, s.path || <span class=\"g\">' &gt; '</span> || e.name\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n    <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> sub s <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.manager_id = s.id\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> s.lvl &lt; <span class=\"y\">20</span>                        <span class=\"c\">-- guard against bad data cycles</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, name, lvl, path <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sub <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> path;</div>\nAnchor = direct reports of 7; the recursive member joins employees whose manager is any row from the previous iteration. Use UNION ALL (a tree has no duplicates; UNION would add a dedup cost). The depth guard protects against corrupted data where someone is their own indirect manager. Counting team size per manager is then <code>SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sub</code>.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Daily revenue for the last 30 days including days with zero orders \u2014 write it.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> days <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> gs::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"o\">generate_series</span>(CURRENT_DATE - <span class=\"y\">29</span>, CURRENT_DATE, <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 day'</span>) gs\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> d.day, <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount), <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(o.order_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_orders\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> days d\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> orders o\n       <span class=\"k\">ON</span> o.order_date = d.day\n      <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>              <span class=\"c\">-- filter in ON, not WHERE, to keep empty days</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d.day\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d.day;</div>\nKey points: build a date spine (generate_series or a recursive CTE where it's unavailable), LEFT JOIN facts onto it, put right-table filters in ON, and use <code>COUNT(o.order_id)</code> not <code>COUNT(*)</code> \u2014 COUNT(*) would count the NULL-extended row and report 1 order on empty days.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Is a CTE always materialized? How does that affect performance?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Depends on the engine. Postgres \u2264 11: always materialized, acting as an optimization fence \u2014 outer WHERE clauses don't push into it, so <code>WITH o AS (SELECT * FROM orders) SELECT \u2026 WHERE user_id = 42</code> scanned all orders. Postgres 12+: inlined when non-recursive, side-effect free and referenced once; materialized when referenced more than once; overridable with <code>MATERIALIZED</code> / <code>NOT MATERIALIZED</code>. SQL Server never materializes \u2014 a CTE referenced three times runs three times, so use a temp table. MySQL 8 and the cloud warehouses decide per query. Materialization helps when an expensive result is reused or you want to freeze a plan; it hurts when it blocks predicate pushdown or index use.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write the top 2 orders by amount per user using LATERAL, then explain when it beats ROW_NUMBER.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id, t.order_id, t.amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">CROSS</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> <span class=\"k\">LATERAL</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, amount\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id\n    <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> amount <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id      <span class=\"c\">-- tie-breaker for determinism</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">2</span>\n) t;</div>\nWith an index on <code>orders(user_id, amount DESC)</code> each lateral call is an index seek that reads 2 rows and stops, so cost \u221d users \u00d7 2. ROW_NUMBER must read and sort/partition every order before filtering, so cost \u221d total orders \u00d7 log. LATERAL wins with many orders per user and small N; ROW_NUMBER wins when there's no index, when groups are tiny, or on warehouses (BigQuery) without general LATERAL. Use <code>LEFT JOIN LATERAL \u2026 ON TRUE</code> to include users with no orders. Note <code>LIMIT 2</code> cuts ties arbitrarily unless you add a tie-breaker \u2014 the equivalent of ROW_NUMBER, not RANK.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What happens when a scalar subquery returns no rows, or more than one?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">No rows \u2192 the expression evaluates to NULL, the outer row is kept (NULL in a SELECT column; in WHERE, a comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN so the row is filtered). More than one row \u2192 runtime error in Postgres/SQL Server/MySQL/Snowflake (\"more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression\"); BigQuery errors similarly. The danger is that this is data-dependent: the query passes in dev and fails the day a duplicate appears. Defend with a primary/unique key on the lookup column, an aggregate (<code>MAX</code>), or <code>LIMIT 1</code> with an explicit ORDER BY if any row will do.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>View, materialized view, temp table, or CTE \u2014 a dashboard runs a 40-second aggregation every page load. What do you do?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">A plain view doesn't help \u2014 it still runs the 40-second query on every read. A CTE only organizes the query. A temp table is session-scoped, so the dashboard's connections can't share it. A materialized view (or a scheduled summary table) is the fit: precompute the aggregate, index it on the dashboard's filter columns, and refresh on a schedule matching the freshness requirement (<code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY</code> with a unique index so reads aren't blocked). Discuss the trade-off explicitly: staleness window vs compute cost; whether incremental refresh exists (Snowflake/BigQuery MVs refresh incrementally; Postgres recomputes fully \u2014 for large data, an incremental table updated by a job is better).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Rewrite \"users whose total spend exceeds the average user's total spend\" without a correlated subquery.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> spend <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, total\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> spend\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> total &gt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(total) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> spend);</div>\nThe inner <code>(SELECT AVG(total) FROM spend)</code> is non-correlated \u2014 computed once as an InitPlan. Because <code>spend</code> is referenced twice, Postgres 12+ materializes it once by default, which is what we want. Note that the \"average user\" here is the average among users who have at least one paid order; if non-buyers should count as 0, build <code>spend</code> from <code>users LEFT JOIN orders</code> with <code>COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)</code> \u2014 clarifying the denominator is exactly what interviewers look for.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>How would you detect a cycle in a manager hierarchy with SQL?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Carry the visited path and flag when the next node is already in it:\n<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> <span class=\"k\">RECURSIVE</span> walk <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id <span class=\"k\">AS</span> start_id, id, manager_id, <span class=\"k\">ARRAY</span>[id] <span class=\"k\">AS</span> path, <span class=\"k\">FALSE</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> is_cycle\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n    <span class=\"k\">UNION</span> <span class=\"k\">ALL</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> w.start_id, e.id, e.manager_id, w.path || e.id, e.id = <span class=\"k\">ANY</span> (w.path)\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> walk w\n    <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees e <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.id = w.manager_id\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> w.is_cycle\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> start_id, path <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> walk <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> is_cycle;</div>\nEach walk goes upward from every employee; the step that revisits a node is emitted with <code>is_cycle = TRUE</code> and the <code>WHERE NOT w.is_cycle</code> condition stops further expansion, guaranteeing termination. In Postgres 14+ the <code>CYCLE id SET is_cycle USING path</code> clause does the same bookkeeping.</div></details>\n</main>",
  "sql_windows": "<main>\n  <h1>Window Functions</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-what\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">What Windows Are</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Rows don't collapse; where they sit in logical order</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-anatomy\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">OVER() Anatomy</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">PARTITION BY, ORDER BY, frame</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-ranking\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Ranking Functions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK, NTILE, PERCENT_RANK, CUME_DIST</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-topn\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Top-N per Group &amp; Dedup</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Keep the latest row per key</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-offset\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">LAG / LEAD</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Defaults, day-over-day, YoY</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-value\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">FIRST / LAST / NTH_VALUE</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">The LAST_VALUE default-frame trap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-agg\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Aggregate Windows</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Running totals, moving averages, % of total</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-frames\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Frames: ROWS / RANGE / GROUPS</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Default frame and the duplicate-timestamp trap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-named\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Named WINDOW &amp; QUALIFY</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Reuse specs; filter on windows by dialect</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-patterns\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Classic Patterns</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Gaps &amp; islands, sessionization, medians</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-perf\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Performance</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Sort cost, shared partitions, indexes</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sw-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">12</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Write-the-query drills</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Windows don't collapse rows:</strong> GROUP BY turns N rows into one per group; a window function computes a value over a set of related rows and attaches it to <em>every</em> row. That is why you can show each order next to the user's total, rank, or previous order in one pass.</li>\n    <li><strong>Logical position:</strong> FROM \u2192 WHERE \u2192 GROUP BY \u2192 HAVING \u2192 <em>window functions</em> \u2192 SELECT list \u2192 DISTINCT \u2192 ORDER BY \u2192 LIMIT. So a window can't be referenced in WHERE, GROUP BY or HAVING of the same level \u2014 wrap it in a subquery/CTE (or use QUALIFY in Snowflake/BigQuery/Databricks). It can be used in ORDER BY, and it can window over aggregates: <code>SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER ()</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Ties decide the ranking function:</strong> for values 100, 90, 90, 80 \u2014 ROW_NUMBER 1,2,3,4 (arbitrary between ties); RANK 1,2,2,4 (gaps); DENSE_RANK 1,2,2,3 (no gaps). \"Nth highest distinct salary\" \u2192 DENSE_RANK. \"Exactly one row per group\" \u2192 ROW_NUMBER with a deterministic tie-breaker.</li>\n    <li><strong>Default frame trap:</strong> with ORDER BY and no frame clause, the frame is <code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> \u2014 which includes all <em>peers</em> (rows with equal ORDER BY values). Running totals over duplicate timestamps therefore jump by the whole tie group, and LAST_VALUE returns the current row (or its last peer), not the partition's last row. Write <code>ROWS BETWEEN \u2026</code> explicitly.</li>\n    <li><strong>Without ORDER BY, the frame is the whole partition:</strong> <code>SUM(x) OVER (PARTITION BY g)</code> = group total on every row. Adding ORDER BY silently turns it into a running total. Percent-of-total must not have ORDER BY inside OVER.</li>\n    <li><strong>ROWS vs RANGE vs GROUPS:</strong> ROWS counts physical rows; RANGE uses value distance on the single ORDER BY key (<code>RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL '6 days' PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> = true 7-calendar-day window even with missing days, PG11+); GROUPS counts peer groups. A \"7-day moving average\" with ROWS 6 PRECEDING is only correct if there is exactly one row per day.</li>\n    <li><strong>Gaps &amp; islands:</strong> for consecutive dates, <code>date \u2212 ROW_NUMBER()</code> is constant within a run of consecutive days and changes after a gap. Group by that key to get streaks. Deduplicate dates first, or duplicates break the arithmetic.</li>\n    <li><strong>Sessionization:</strong> flag a new session when <code>LAG(event_time)</code> is NULL or the gap exceeds 30 minutes; a running <code>SUM(flag)</code> over the same ordering yields a session number per user. Two window passes, no self-join.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. WHAT \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-what\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>What Window Functions Are</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 window function</span>\n    <p>A window function computes a value for each row using a set of rows related to it \u2014 the <strong>window</strong> \u2014 defined by the <code>OVER (\u2026)</code> clause. Unlike an aggregate with GROUP BY, the input rows are not collapsed: the output has exactly as many rows as the input to the window step. Any aggregate (<code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>COUNT</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>) becomes a window function when followed by OVER; there are also window-only functions (ranking, offset, value).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- GROUP BY: one row per department</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept_avg\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Window: every employee row kept, with its department's average attached</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, dept_id, salary,\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept_avg,\n       salary - <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> diff\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 the class photo vs the report card</span>\n    <p>GROUP BY is the school report: \"Class 3B average: 74\" \u2014 the individual students are gone. A window function is the class photo with a caption under each student: \"Alice \u2014 81, class average 74, rank 3 of 28\". Everyone is still in the picture; each just gets extra context about the group they belong to.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Where windows sit in logical query order</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Step</th><th>Clause</th><th>Can reference a window function?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>1</td><td>FROM / JOIN</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>2</td><td>WHERE</td><td>No \u2014 error \"window functions are not allowed in WHERE\"</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3</td><td>GROUP BY</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>4</td><td>HAVING</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Window functions evaluated</strong></td><td>\u2014</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>6</td><td>SELECT list (expressions, aliases)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>7</td><td>DISTINCT</td><td>Applied after windows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>8</td><td>ORDER BY</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>9</td><td>LIMIT / OFFSET</td><td>Applied after windows (windows see all rows, not just the LIMITed ones)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 consequences of running after GROUP BY</span>\n    <p>Because windows run after aggregation, their input is the <em>grouped</em> rows. So in a grouped query you can window over aggregates: <code>SUM(amount)</code> is the group total, <code>SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER ()</code> is the total across all groups. And because WHERE runs first, filters shrink the window: <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01'</code> means LAG on the first 2024 row returns NULL even if a 2023 row exists. To compute over all history but display a subset, window in an inner query and filter in the outer one.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Revenue per country and its share of the grand total, in one grouped query</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)                                        <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue,\n       <span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount) / <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> ()   <span class=\"k\">AS</span> pct_of_total\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> revenue <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 DISTINCT with windows</span>\n    <p><code>SELECT DISTINCT dept_id, COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id) FROM employees</code> works (the window runs first, then DISTINCT collapses duplicates), but it computes N window values to throw most away \u2014 a GROUP BY is cheaper and clearer. Conversely <code>COUNT(DISTINCT x) OVER (\u2026)</code> is not supported in Postgres or SQL Server (BigQuery and Snowflake allow it); see \u00a707 for the workaround.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. ANATOMY \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-anatomy\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>OVER (PARTITION BY \u2026 ORDER BY \u2026 frame) Anatomy</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">function_name(args) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> partition_expr, ...        <span class=\"c\">-- which rows form the group (optional)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> sort_expr [<span class=\"k\">ASC</span>|<span class=\"k\">DESC</span>] [<span class=\"k\">NULLS</span> <span class=\"k\">FIRST</span>|<span class=\"k\">LAST</span>], ...   <span class=\"c\">-- order inside each partition</span>\n    frame_clause                             <span class=\"c\">-- which subset of the partition, relative to the current row</span>\n)\n<span class=\"c\">-- frame_clause:</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   { ROWS | RANGE | GROUPS } BETWEEN frame_start AND frame_end [EXCLUDE ...]</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   frame_start/end: UNBOUNDED PRECEDING | n PRECEDING | CURRENT ROW | n FOLLOWING | UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</span></div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Part</th><th>Effect</th><th>If omitted</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>PARTITION BY</code></td><td>Splits rows into independent groups; the function resets per partition</td><td>Whole result set is one partition</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ORDER BY</code></td><td>Order within partition; required by ranking and offset functions; defines \"peers\" (equal sort values)</td><td>Rows unordered; all rows are peers of each other</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Frame</td><td>Subset of the partition used by aggregate and value functions (ignored by ranking/LAG/LEAD)</td><td>With ORDER BY: <code>RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING \u2026 CURRENT ROW</code>. Without: whole partition</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)    <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> ()                                         <span class=\"k\">AS</span> all_orders,       <span class=\"c\">-- whole table</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id)                     <span class=\"k\">AS</span> user_total,       <span class=\"c\">-- per-user total</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date,\n                         order_id <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>)        <span class=\"k\">AS</span> user_running,     <span class=\"c\">-- running total</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date, order_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> nth_order\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 three questions per window</span>\n    <p>For every window, answer: (1) <em>Who is in my group?</em> \u2014 PARTITION BY. (2) <em>In what order do we line up?</em> \u2014 ORDER BY. (3) <em>Which of them do I look at from where I stand?</em> \u2014 the frame. Ranking and LAG/LEAD only need (1) and (2); aggregates and FIRST/LAST_VALUE depend on all three.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. RANKING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-ranking\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>Ranking Functions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 the six ranking functions</span>\n    <p><strong>ROW_NUMBER()</strong> \u2014 1, 2, 3\u2026 unique per row; ties broken arbitrarily (non-deterministic unless ORDER BY is unique).<br>\n    <strong>RANK()</strong> \u2014 ties share a rank; next rank skips (1, 2, 2, 4). = 1 + number of rows strictly before.<br>\n    <strong>DENSE_RANK()</strong> \u2014 ties share a rank; no gaps (1, 2, 2, 3). = number of distinct values up to and including this one.<br>\n    <strong>NTILE(n)</strong> \u2014 splits the ordered partition into n buckets as equal as possible; earlier buckets get the extra rows (10 rows into 3 \u2192 4, 3, 3). Ties can straddle buckets.<br>\n    <strong>PERCENT_RANK()</strong> \u2014 (RANK \u2212 1) / (rows \u2212 1), in [0, 1]; 0 for a single-row partition.<br>\n    <strong>CUME_DIST()</strong> \u2014 (rows with value \u2264 current, peers included) / rows, in (0, 1].</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n       <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>()   <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> row_num,\n       <span class=\"o\">RANK</span>()         <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rnk,\n       <span class=\"o\">DENSE_RANK</span>()   <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dense_rnk,\n       <span class=\"o\">NTILE</span>(<span class=\"y\">2</span>)       <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> half,\n       <span class=\"o\">PERCENT_RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> pct_rank,\n       <span class=\"o\">CUME_DIST</span>()    <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cume\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dept_id = <span class=\"y\">10</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WINDOW</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>name</th><th>salary</th><th>ROW_NUMBER</th><th>RANK</th><th>DENSE_RANK</th><th>NTILE(2)</th><th>PERCENT_RANK</th><th>CUME_DIST</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ana</td><td>100</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0.000</td><td>0.25</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ben</td><td>90</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>0.333</td><td>0.75</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cal</td><td>90</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>0.333</td><td>0.75</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dee</td><td>80</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>1.000</td><td>1.00</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 reading the tie row</span>\n    <p>Ben and Cal tie at 90. ROW_NUMBER must give them different numbers, so which one gets 2 is up to the engine and can change between runs. RANK says \"one person is ahead of you\" \u2192 2 for both, then Dee has three people ahead \u2192 4. DENSE_RANK says \"you have the 2nd-highest distinct salary\" \u2192 2, Dee has the 3rd \u2192 3. CUME_DIST for Ben counts himself <em>and his peer</em> Cal: 3 of 4 rows \u2264 90 in this ordering \u2192 0.75. NTILE(2) splits Ben and Cal into different halves despite their tie \u2014 a reason not to use NTILE for \"top 50%\" cut-offs when ties matter.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Question</th><th>Function</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Exactly one row per group (dedup, latest record)</td><td>ROW_NUMBER with a unique tie-breaker</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Top 3 including everyone tied for 3rd place</td><td>RANK \u2264 3 (may return more than 3 rows)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Nth highest <em>distinct</em> value</td><td>DENSE_RANK = N</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Deciles / quartiles for bucketing users</td><td>NTILE(10) / NTILE(4)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>\"What percentile is this user's spend?\"</td><td>PERCENT_RANK or CUME_DIST</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 NULLs sort first or last depending on dialect</span>\n    <p>Postgres and Oracle treat NULL as larger than any value: last in ASC, <strong>first in DESC</strong>. MySQL, SQL Server and BigQuery treat NULL as smallest: first in ASC, last in DESC. So <code>RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC)</code> in Postgres ranks NULL-salary employees #1. Add <code>NULLS LAST</code> (Postgres, Oracle, Snowflake, BigQuery) or filter NULLs out.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. TOP-N \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-topn\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>Top-N per Group &amp; Deduplication</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Top 3 earners per department (ties at the boundary all included)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, name, salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, name, salary,\n           <span class=\"o\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dr\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dr &lt;= <span class=\"y\">3</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id, salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;</div>\n\n  <h3>Keep the latest row per key</h3>\n  <p>Typical case: a CDC / snapshot table <code>user_profiles(user_id, email, country, updated_at, ingest_id)</code> with many versions per user. Keep the latest.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> ranked <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> p.*,\n           <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (\n               <span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n               <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> updated_at <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, ingest_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>   <span class=\"c\">-- tie-breaker: deterministic result</span>\n           ) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> user_profiles p\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, email, country, updated_at\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> ranked\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Postgres-only shortcut: DISTINCT ON keeps the first row of each user_id group</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> (user_id) user_id, email, country, updated_at\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> user_profiles\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, updated_at <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, ingest_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Deleting the duplicates in place (Postgres), keeping the latest</span>\n<span class=\"k\">DELETE</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> user_profiles p\n<span class=\"k\">USING</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> ctid, <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n                                       <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> updated_at <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, ingest_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n       <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> user_profiles) d\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> p.ctid = d.ctid <span class=\"k\">AND</span> d.rn &gt; <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 non-deterministic dedup</span>\n    <p>If two versions of a user share the same <code>updated_at</code> and you order only by <code>updated_at</code>, ROW_NUMBER picks one arbitrarily. Re-running the pipeline can flip which email \"wins\", so downstream tables change without any data changing \u2014 very hard to debug. Always end the ORDER BY with a unique column. Also, <code>updated_at DESC</code> puts NULL timestamps <em>first</em> in Postgres; use <code>DESC NULLS LAST</code> if NULLs are possible.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why not GROUP BY + MAX?</span>\n    <p><code>SELECT user_id, MAX(updated_at) \u2026 GROUP BY user_id</code> gives the latest timestamp but not the other columns of that row. Joining back on (user_id, updated_at) works but returns two rows when timestamps tie, and costs a second scan. ROW_NUMBER keeps the whole row in a single pass.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. OFFSET \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-offset\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>Offset Functions \u2014 LAG / LEAD</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 LAG and LEAD</span>\n    <p><code>LAG(expr [, offset [, default]])</code> returns <code>expr</code> from the row <code>offset</code> rows <em>before</em> the current one in the partition's order (offset defaults to 1); <code>LEAD</code> looks <em>after</em>. When that row doesn't exist, it returns <code>default</code> (NULL if omitted). They ignore the frame clause. Postgres has no <code>IGNORE NULLS</code> option for LAG/LEAD (Oracle, Snowflake, BigQuery, SQL Server 2022 do).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Day-over-day change</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> daily <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> day,\n       revenue,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue)    <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day)                    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> prev_revenue,\n       revenue - <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day)             <span class=\"k\">AS</span> abs_change,\n       <span class=\"o\">ROUND</span>(<span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * (revenue - <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day))\n             / <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day), <span class=\"y\">0</span>), <span class=\"y\">1</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> pct_change,\n       <span class=\"o\">LEAD</span>(revenue)   <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day)                    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> next_revenue,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue, <span class=\"y\">1</span>, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day)                 <span class=\"k\">AS</span> prev_or_zero\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> daily\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">day        | revenue | prev_revenue | abs_change | pct_change | prev_or_zero\n-----------+---------+--------------+------------+------------+-------------\n2024-01-01 |     500 |         NULL |       NULL |       NULL |            0\n2024-01-02 |     600 |          500 |        100 |       20.0 |          500\n2024-01-04 |     300 |          600 |       -300 |      -50.0 |          600   \u2190 Jan 3 missing!</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 LAG means \"previous row\", not \"previous day\"</span>\n    <p>Jan 3 had no orders, so there's no row, and Jan 4's \"previous\" is Jan 2. The pct_change mislabels a two-day change as day-over-day. Fix: build a date spine and LEFT JOIN so every day exists (with 0 revenue), or compute the true previous day explicitly with a self-join on <code>day - 1</code>. Also: the default value of <code>LAG(x, 1, 0)</code> is only used when the previous <em>row</em> is missing \u2014 if the previous row exists with x = NULL, you still get NULL.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Year-over-year</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> monthly <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, order_date)::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> month, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n)\n<span class=\"c\">-- Option A: LAG 12 rows \u2014 correct ONLY if every month is present</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> month, revenue,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue, <span class=\"y\">12</span>) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue_ly\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> monthly;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Option B: robust to missing months \u2014 RANGE frame on a date offset (PG11+)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> month, revenue,\n       <span class=\"o\">FIRST_VALUE</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (\n           <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month\n           <span class=\"k\">RANGE</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'12 months'</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'12 months'</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>\n       ) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue_ly                    <span class=\"c\">-- NULL when last year's month is absent</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> monthly;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Option C: self-join on the date \u2014 simplest to reason about, portable</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> m.month, m.revenue, ly.revenue <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue_ly,\n       <span class=\"o\">ROUND</span>(<span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * (m.revenue - ly.revenue) / <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(ly.revenue, <span class=\"y\">0</span>), <span class=\"y\">1</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> yoy_pct\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> monthly m\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> monthly ly <span class=\"k\">ON</span> ly.month = m.month - <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 year'</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 when to reach for LAG vs a self-join</span>\n    <p>LAG is a single sorted pass, cheapest when the series is dense (one row per period, guaranteed by a spine). A self-join on a date key is immune to gaps and expresses \"same period last year\" literally. In interviews, use LAG and then say out loud: \"this assumes no missing periods \u2014 otherwise I'd join on a calendar.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. VALUE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-value\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>FIRST_VALUE / LAST_VALUE / NTH_VALUE</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 value functions</span>\n    <p><code>FIRST_VALUE(expr)</code>, <code>LAST_VALUE(expr)</code> and <code>NTH_VALUE(expr, n)</code> return the value from the first, last, or n-th row <em>of the frame</em> \u2014 not of the partition. Unlike LAG/LEAD, they respect the frame clause, which is exactly where the trap lives.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Each order alongside the user's first and (attempted) last order amount</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">FIRST_VALUE</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> first_amt,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAST_VALUE</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> last_amt_WRONG,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAST_VALUE</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date\n                                 <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>\n                                          <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">FOLLOWING</span>)      <span class=\"k\">AS</span> last_amt,\n       <span class=\"o\">NTH_VALUE</span>(amount, <span class=\"y\">2</span>) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date\n                                  <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>\n                                           <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">FOLLOWING</span>)     <span class=\"k\">AS</span> second_amt\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">user | order_date | amount | first_amt | last_amt_WRONG | last_amt | second_amt\n-----+------------+--------+-----------+----------------+----------+-----------\n  1  | 2024-01-05 |     20 |        20 |             20 |       70 |        35\n  1  | 2024-02-10 |     35 |        20 |             35 |       70 |        35\n  1  | 2024-03-01 |     70 |        20 |             70 |       70 |        35</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 LAST_VALUE returns the current row</span>\n    <p>With ORDER BY and no frame, the frame ends at <code>CURRENT ROW</code> (plus its peers). The last row of that frame <em>is the current row</em>, so LAST_VALUE just echoes the current value. Fixes: (1) explicit <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code>; (2) <code>FIRST_VALUE(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date DESC)</code> \u2014 reverse the order and take the first, which works with the default frame. The same applies to NTH_VALUE: on row 1 with the default frame, <code>NTH_VALUE(amount, 2)</code> is NULL because the frame only has 1 row. FIRST_VALUE is safe with the default frame because the frame always starts at the partition's first row.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. AGGREGATE WINDOWS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-agg\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>Aggregate Windows \u2014 Running Totals, Moving Averages, % of Total</h2>\n\n  <h3>Running totals</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date, order_id, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n                         <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date, order_id\n                         <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> lifetime_spend,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)    <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n                         <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date, order_id\n                         <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>)                         <span class=\"k\">AS</span> order_number\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;\n<span class=\"c\">-- \"ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING\" is shorthand for \"ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\"</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Moving averages</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> daily <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> day, revenue,\n       <span class=\"c\">-- 7 ROWS: correct only if every day has a row</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">6</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> ma7_rows,\n       <span class=\"c\">-- 7 CALENDAR DAYS: correct even with missing days (PG11+, MySQL 8, Oracle; not SQL Server \u2014 see \u00a708)</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day\n                          <span class=\"k\">RANGE</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'6 days'</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> ma7_range,\n       <span class=\"c\">-- how many rows actually went into the average (early rows have fewer than 7)</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)     <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">6</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_in_window\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> daily;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 two subtle moving-average bugs</span>\n    <p><strong>Missing days:</strong> with <code>ROWS 6 PRECEDING</code>, if 3 days have no orders the window silently spans 10 calendar days. <strong>Average of what?</strong> <code>ma7_range</code> averages the <em>days that exist</em>; a day with no orders contributes nothing instead of 0, inflating the average. The fully correct 7-day average of daily revenue is: spine of all days \u2192 LEFT JOIN \u2192 COALESCE(revenue, 0) \u2192 <code>AVG \u2026 ROWS 6 PRECEDING</code>. And decide whether the first 6 days (partial windows) should be reported or nulled out with <code>CASE WHEN n_in_window = 7</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Percent of total and share within group</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, o.user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> spend,\n       <span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount) / <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> pct_of_country,\n       <span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount) / <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> ()                      <span class=\"k\">AS</span> pct_of_world\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">USING</span> (user_id)\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> u.country, o.user_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Cumulative share (Pareto): which products make up the first 80% of revenue?</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> p <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> product_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rev <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> order_items <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> product_id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> product_id, rev,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> rev <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, product_id <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>)\n         / <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> () <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cum_share\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> p\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> rev <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 integer division and stray ORDER BY</span>\n    <p>If amounts are integers, <code>SUM(x) / SUM(SUM(x)) OVER ()</code> is integer division \u2192 0 for everything but the top row. Multiply by <code>100.0</code> or cast to numeric first. And never put ORDER BY inside the OVER for a denominator \u2014 <code>SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY \u2026)</code> is a running total, so each row's \"share\" would be computed against a different, growing total.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>COUNT(DISTINCT) over a window \u2014 the workaround</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Postgres / SQL Server reject COUNT(DISTINCT x) OVER (...). Trick using two DENSE_RANKs:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_type,\n       <span class=\"o\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> event_type <span class=\"k\">ASC</span>)\n     + <span class=\"o\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> event_type <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) - <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n       <span class=\"k\">AS</span> distinct_event_types       <span class=\"c\">-- exact when event_type has no NULLs</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why the DENSE_RANK trick works</span>\n    <p>If a partition has k distinct values, a value at ascending dense rank r has descending dense rank k \u2212 r + 1. Their sum is always k + 1, so subtracting 1 gives k on every row. NULLs count as an extra distinct value (they get their own rank), so filter or COALESCE them if they shouldn't count.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. FRAMES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-frames\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>Frames: ROWS vs RANGE vs GROUPS</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 the three frame units</span>\n    <p><strong>ROWS</strong> \u2014 offsets count physical rows. <code>ROWS 2 PRECEDING</code> = this row and the two rows before it, regardless of values.<br>\n    <strong>RANGE</strong> \u2014 offsets are distances in the ORDER BY <em>value</em>; requires exactly one ORDER BY column for numeric/interval offsets. <code>CURRENT ROW</code> in RANGE mode means \"the current row and all its peers\".<br>\n    <strong>GROUPS</strong> \u2014 offsets count peer groups (sets of rows with equal ORDER BY values). <code>GROUPS 1 PRECEDING</code> = the current tie group and the previous one. Postgres 11+, SQLite, Oracle 21c; not SQL Server/MySQL/BigQuery/Snowflake.<br><br>\n    Optional <code>EXCLUDE CURRENT ROW | GROUP | TIES | NO OTHERS</code> (Postgres 11+) removes rows from the frame \u2014 e.g. \"average of my peers excluding me\".</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 the default frame</span>\n    <p>With ORDER BY and no frame: <code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> \u2014 everything from the partition start through the current row <em>and all its peers</em>. Without ORDER BY: the whole partition (<code>\u2026 AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code>, all rows are peers). This is the SQL standard and the default in Postgres, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle and BigQuery. Snowflake differs slightly for some functions \u2014 always write the frame if it matters.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>The duplicate-timestamp trap, concretely</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date)                                <span class=\"k\">AS</span> default_range,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>)       <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rows_frame,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date, order_id <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rows_tiebroken\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>order_id</th><th>order_date</th><th>amount</th><th>default (RANGE)</th><th>ROWS</th><th>ROWS + tie-breaker</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>1</td><td>2024-01-01</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>2</td><td>2024-01-02</td><td>20</td><td><strong>50</strong></td><td>30 or 60*</td><td>30</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3</td><td>2024-01-02</td><td>30</td><td><strong>50</strong></td><td>60 or 40*</td><td>60</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>4</td><td>2024-01-03</td><td>5</td><td>65</td><td>65</td><td>65</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>* Order between the two Jan 2 rows is undefined, so plain ROWS can give either sequence across runs.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 which one is \"right\"?</span>\n    <p>RANGE gives both Jan 2 rows the same value 50 = total through end of Jan 2 \u2014 perfect if you want \"cumulative revenue as of that date\" and will dedupe to one row per date. ROWS gives a per-order running balance, but it's only deterministic with a unique tie-breaker. Bugs arise when people expect per-row behaviour and get RANGE silently: a \"running count\" that jumps 1 \u2192 3 \u2192 3 \u2192 4, or LAST_VALUE returning the last peer. Rule: <strong>write ROWS explicitly and add a unique tie-breaker</strong> unless you deliberately want peer semantics.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>RANGE with value offsets</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Revenue in the trailing 30 days for each order (timestamps, gaps OK)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date\n                         <span class=\"k\">RANGE</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'29 days'</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rev_30d\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Numeric RANGE: all employees within \u00b15000 of my salary (incl. me)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">RANGE</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">5000</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"y\">5000</span> <span class=\"k\">FOLLOWING</span>) - <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n         <span class=\"k\">AS</span> near_peers\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- GROUPS: sum of the current date's orders and the previous distinct date's orders</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">GROUPS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders;</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Dialect</th><th>ROWS</th><th>RANGE with UNBOUNDED / CURRENT ROW</th><th>RANGE with value offsets</th><th>GROUPS</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Postgres 11+</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (numeric, date/timestamp + interval)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>MySQL 8</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (incl. INTERVAL)</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>BigQuery</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Numeric only (convert dates with UNIX_DATE)</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Snowflake</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes in current versions (numeric / INTERVAL offsets); older releases did not support it</td><td>No</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. NAMED WINDOW & QUALIFY \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-named\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>Named WINDOW Clause &amp; QUALIFY</h2>\n\n  <h3>Named windows</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w                                       <span class=\"k\">AS</span> nth,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w                                       <span class=\"k\">AS</span> prev_amt,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (w <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>)            <span class=\"k\">AS</span> running,  <span class=\"c\">-- extend with a frame</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id)                  <span class=\"k\">AS</span> user_avg\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WINDOW</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date, order_id)   <span class=\"c\">-- after HAVING, before ORDER BY</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, order_date;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 WINDOW clause rules</span>\n    <p>A named window can be referenced as <code>OVER w</code> or extended as <code>OVER (w frame)</code> / <code>OVER (w ORDER BY \u2026)</code> if w has no ORDER BY. You cannot override a PARTITION BY, or add ORDER BY to a window that already has one. Supported in Postgres, MySQL 8, SQLite, BigQuery, SQL Server 2022+, DuckDB. Beyond readability, identical window specs let the planner reuse one sort for all functions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>QUALIFY \u2014 filter on window results</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Snowflake / BigQuery / Databricks / DuckDB / Teradata</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">QUALIFY</span> <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- Logical order: ... HAVING \u2192 windows \u2192 QUALIFY \u2192 DISTINCT \u2192 ORDER BY \u2192 LIMIT</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Postgres / MySQL / SQL Server equivalent: wrap and filter</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date,\n           <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 writing QUALIFY in a Postgres interview</span>\n    <p>QUALIFY is not in the SQL standard and doesn't exist in Postgres, MySQL or SQL Server. If the interviewer said \"Postgres\", use the subquery form; if you know the shop runs Snowflake/BigQuery, QUALIFY shows fluency \u2014 but mention the portable version. (Older BigQuery required a WHERE, GROUP BY or HAVING alongside QUALIFY, hence the idiom <code>WHERE TRUE QUALIFY \u2026</code>.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. PATTERNS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-patterns\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>Classic Patterns</h2>\n\n  <h3>Gaps &amp; islands \u2014 consecutive login days</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- logins(user_id, login_ts). Longest streak of consecutive login days per user.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> days <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (                                   <span class=\"c\">-- 1. one row per user per day</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, login_ts::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> d\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> logins\n),\ngrp <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (                                         <span class=\"c\">-- 2. date minus row number = island key</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, d,\n           d - (<span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d))::<span class=\"k\">int</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> island\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> days\n),\nstreaks <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (                                     <span class=\"c\">-- 3. collapse each island</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, island,\n           <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(d) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> start_day, <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(d) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> end_day, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> streak_len\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> grp\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, island\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(streak_len) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> longest_streak\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> streaks\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">d          | row_number | d - rn      (island key)\n-----------+------------+-----------\n2024-03-01 |          1 | 2024-02-29\n2024-03-02 |          2 | 2024-02-29   \u2190 same key: consecutive\n2024-03-03 |          3 | 2024-02-29\n2024-03-07 |          4 | 2024-03-03   \u2190 gap \u2192 key jumps\n2024-03-08 |          5 | 2024-03-03</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why date \u2212 ROW_NUMBER is constant on an island</span>\n    <p>Inside a run of consecutive days, both the date and the row number increase by exactly 1 per row, so their difference stays fixed. A gap of g missing days increases the date by g + 1 while the row number increases by 1, shifting the difference by g. The key itself is meaningless \u2014 it just labels the run. The DISTINCT in step 1 is essential: two logins on the same day would advance ROW_NUMBER without advancing the date and split one streak into two. (MySQL: <code>DATE_SUB(d, INTERVAL rn DAY)</code>; SQL Server: <code>DATEADD(day, -rn, d)</code>; BigQuery: <code>DATE_SUB(d, INTERVAL rn DAY)</code>.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 runners and a pace car</span>\n    <p>Picture the dates as runners and ROW_NUMBER as a pace car that moves one step per runner. While runners arrive one day apart, they stay the same distance behind the pace car. When a runner arrives late (a gap), every later runner is now further behind \u2014 a new \"distance\" value, i.e. a new island.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Alternative island method (works for any \"change of state\", not just consecutive integers):</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- flag the start of each island with LAG, then running-SUM the flags.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> f <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, d,\n           <span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> d - <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(d) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d) = <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n                <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> is_start\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, login_ts::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> d <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> logins) x\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, d,\n       <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(is_start) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> d <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> island_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> f;</div>\n\n  <h3>Sessionization \u2014 30-minute inactivity gap</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> flagged <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_time, event_type,\n           <span class=\"k\">CASE</span>\n             <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>                                <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n             <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> event_time - <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> w &gt; <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'30 minutes'</span>  <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n             <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span>\n           <span class=\"k\">END</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> new_session\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events\n    <span class=\"k\">WINDOW</span> w <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> event_time)\n),\nsessions <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> *,\n           <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(new_session) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> event_time\n                                  <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> session_num\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> flagged\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, session_num,\n       <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(event_time)                    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> session_start,\n       <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(event_time)                    <span class=\"k\">AS</span> session_end,\n       <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(event_time) - <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(event_time)  <span class=\"k\">AS</span> duration,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)                           <span class=\"k\">AS</span> n_events\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sessions\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, session_num\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, session_num;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 the running SUM must use ROWS (or a unique order)</span>\n    <p>If two events of a user share the same <code>event_time</code> and one of them starts a session, the default RANGE frame adds that flag to <em>both</em> peers \u2014 fine here because peers share the same timestamp and thus belong to the same session. But it's fragile; ROWS with a deterministic order is the habit. Also note: <code>timestamp \u2212 timestamp</code> yields an interval in Postgres; in BigQuery use <code>TIMESTAMP_DIFF(event_time, prev, MINUTE) &gt; 30</code>, in Snowflake <code>DATEDIFF('minute', prev, event_time)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Median and percentiles</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Postgres: PERCENTILE_CONT is an ordered-set AGGREGATE (needs GROUP BY, not OVER)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id,\n       <span class=\"o\">PERCENTILE_CONT</span>(<span class=\"y\">0.5</span>)  <span class=\"k\">WITHIN</span> <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> median_interp,  <span class=\"c\">-- interpolates</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">PERCENTILE_DISC</span>(<span class=\"y\">0.5</span>)  <span class=\"k\">WITHIN</span> <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> median_actual,  <span class=\"c\">-- an actual value</span>\n       <span class=\"o\">PERCENTILE_CONT</span>(<span class=\"k\">ARRAY</span>[<span class=\"y\">0.25</span>, <span class=\"y\">0.5</span>, <span class=\"y\">0.75</span>]) <span class=\"k\">WITHIN</span> <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> quartiles\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Attach the dept median to every employee in Postgres: aggregate then join back</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.salary, m.median_sal\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">PERCENTILE_CONT</span>(<span class=\"y\">0.5</span>) <span class=\"k\">WITHIN</span> <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> median_sal\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id) m <span class=\"k\">USING</span> (dept_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Snowflake / SQL Server / Oracle allow it as a window:</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- BigQuery (analytic only):  PERCENTILE_CONT(salary, 0.5) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- MySQL: no PERCENTILE_CONT \u2014 use the ROW_NUMBER/COUNT method below</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Portable median with window functions (average of the middle one or two values)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> median_sal\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, salary,\n           <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn,\n           <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*)     <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id)                  <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cnt\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn <span class=\"k\">IN</span> ((cnt + <span class=\"y\">1</span>) / <span class=\"y\">2</span>, (cnt + <span class=\"y\">2</span>) / <span class=\"y\">2</span>)      <span class=\"c\">-- integer division: odd \u2192 same row twice, even \u2192 two middles</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 checking the middle-row arithmetic</span>\n    <p>cnt = 5: (6)/2 = 3 and (7)/2 = 3 \u2192 row 3 only. cnt = 4: (5)/2 = 2 and (6)/2 = 3 \u2192 rows 2 and 3, averaged. Integer division in Postgres/SQL Server makes this work; in MySQL use <code>FLOOR</code> or <code>DIV</code> because <code>/</code> returns a decimal. PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) gives the same answer as this method; PERCENTILE_DISC returns the lower middle value for even counts.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. PERFORMANCE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-perf\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>Performance</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 how the engine executes a window</span>\n    <p>Postgres: sort the input by (PARTITION BY keys, ORDER BY keys), then stream through it once in a WindowAgg node, keeping only the current frame in memory. The dominant cost is the <strong>sort</strong> \u2014 O(N log N), spilling to disk if it exceeds <code>work_mem</code>. Each distinct (partition, order) specification needs its own sort; specs that are prefix-compatible can share one. Distributed warehouses (BigQuery, Snowflake, Spark) first <em>shuffle</em> rows so each partition lands on one worker \u2014 a skewed partition (one giant user, or no PARTITION BY at all) funnels everything to a single worker.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Technique</th><th>Why it helps</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Reuse identical OVER specs (or a named WINDOW)</td><td>One sort feeds many functions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Index on (partition_key, order_key)</td><td>Postgres can read pre-sorted rows from the index and skip the Sort node</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Filter in WHERE before windowing (when semantics allow)</td><td>Fewer rows to sort \u2014 but filtering changes what LAG/running totals see</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Postgres 15+: <code>WHERE rn &lt;= N</code> on ROW_NUMBER/RANK in an outer query</td><td>Run-condition pushdown stops WindowAgg early per partition</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Avoid <code>OVER ()</code> / no PARTITION BY on huge tables in distributed engines</td><td>Forces all rows through one worker; compute the grand total with a separate aggregate and CROSS JOIN instead</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Prefer ROWS over RANGE when equivalent</td><td>ROWS frames have fixed boundaries; RANGE must locate peer/value boundaries for every row</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LATERAL + LIMIT for top-N with a good index</td><td>Avoids sorting all rows when groups are large and N is small</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 different ORDER BYs multiply sorts</span>\n    <p><code>RANK() OVER (ORDER BY revenue DESC)</code> + <code>LAG(x) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY day)</code> + <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY day)</code> = three full sorts of the data. In EXPLAIN you'll see a stack of Sort \u2192 WindowAgg nodes. When a query is slow, count distinct window specs first.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 12. Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sw-qa\"><span class=\"num\">12</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why can't you use a window function in WHERE, and how do you filter on one?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Logically, WHERE is evaluated before GROUP BY, HAVING and window functions \u2014 at WHERE time the window values don't exist yet, and the window's input depends on which rows WHERE keeps. So <code>WHERE ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\u2026) = 1</code> is an error. Compute the window in a subquery or CTE and filter in the outer query; in Snowflake/BigQuery/Databricks use <code>QUALIFY</code>, which is evaluated right after windows. Same reason you can't use them in GROUP BY or HAVING \u2014 but you can in ORDER BY, which runs after.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query for the second-highest salary in each department, handling ties.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, name, salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, name, salary,\n           <span class=\"o\">DENSE_RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> dept_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dr\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dr = <span class=\"y\">2</span>;</div>\nDENSE_RANK so that if two people tie for the top salary, the next distinct salary is still rank 2 (RANK would give it 3 and return nothing for dr = 2). All employees earning the second-highest salary are returned. Departments with a single distinct salary produce no row \u2014 if the interviewer wants a NULL row for them, LEFT JOIN from departments. The NULL filter matters in Postgres because NULLs sort first in DESC.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Deduplicate a table to keep only the most recent record per user.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> *\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> p.*,\n           <span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id\n                              <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> updated_at <span class=\"k\">DESC</span> <span class=\"k\">NULLS</span> <span class=\"k\">LAST</span>, ingest_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> user_profiles p\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rn = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;</div>\nROW_NUMBER (not RANK) guarantees exactly one row per user; RANK would keep both rows on a timestamp tie. The unique tie-breaker makes the result deterministic across runs. <code>NULLS LAST</code> prevents a NULL timestamp being treated as newest in Postgres. Postgres alternative: <code>SELECT DISTINCT ON (user_id) * \u2026 ORDER BY user_id, updated_at DESC, ingest_id DESC</code>. Snowflake/BigQuery: <code>QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\u2026) = 1</code>. Note <code>t.*</code> will include the <code>rn</code> column \u2014 list columns explicitly in production.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query for each user's longest streak of consecutive active days.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> d <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> user_id, event_time::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events\n), g <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, day,\n           day - (<span class=\"o\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> day))::<span class=\"k\">int</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> grp\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> d\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(cnt) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> longest_streak\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, grp, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cnt <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> g <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, grp) s\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id;</div>\nGaps-and-islands: within a run of consecutive days, day \u2212 row_number is constant; a gap shifts it. Dedup to one row per user-day first, otherwise same-day events break the arithmetic. The <code>::int</code> cast is needed because ROW_NUMBER returns bigint and Postgres defines <code>date \u2212 integer</code>. Follow-ups: \"current streak\" = the island whose MAX(day) is today/yesterday; \"users with a 7+ day streak\" = HAVING COUNT(*) \u2265 7 on the island grouping.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Sessionize the events table with a 30-minute inactivity timeout and report average session length.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> f <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_time,\n           <span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> event_time - <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(event_time) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> event_time)\n                     &lt;= <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'30 minutes'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> new_sess\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events\n), s <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_time,\n           <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(new_sess) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> event_time\n                               <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> sess\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> f\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(sess_len) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_session_length\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, sess, <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(event_time) - <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(event_time) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> sess_len\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> s <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id, sess) x;</div>\nThe CASE is written so that the first event (LAG is NULL \u2192 comparison is UNKNOWN \u2192 falls to ELSE) automatically starts a session \u2014 a neat NULL trick worth explaining. The running SUM of flags numbers sessions per user. Discuss: single-event sessions have length 0 (some products add a fixed tail or drop them); whether the timeout should be measured from the last event or session start; and timezone/day boundaries if sessions should also split at midnight.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Compute month-over-month revenue growth percentage.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> m <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">DATE_TRUNC</span>(<span class=\"g\">'month'</span>, order_date) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> month, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> revenue\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n    <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'paid'</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> month, revenue,\n       <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> prev,\n       <span class=\"o\">ROUND</span>(<span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * (revenue - <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month))\n             / <span class=\"o\">NULLIF</span>(<span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(revenue) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month), <span class=\"y\">0</span>), <span class=\"y\">2</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> mom_pct\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> m\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> month;</div>\n<code>NULLIF(\u2026, 0)</code> avoids division by zero; <code>100.0</code> avoids integer division. The first month has NULL growth. Caveat to state: LAG takes the previous <em>row</em>, so a month with no sales would make the next month compare against two months back \u2014 join to a month spine (generate_series) if that's possible. Partial current month should usually be excluded or flagged.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why does LAST_VALUE return the current row's value, and how do you fix it?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">With ORDER BY in OVER and no explicit frame, the frame is <code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>. The frame ends at the current row (plus its peers), so the \"last value of the frame\" is the current row \u2014 or its last tie peer. Fix with <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code>, or flip the ordering and use FIRST_VALUE, which is unaffected because every default frame starts at the partition's first row. Same issue hits NTH_VALUE and any aggregate where you expected a partition-wide value but added ORDER BY.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Running total per user vs per day \u2014 what goes wrong with duplicate timestamps?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><code>SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date)</code> uses the default RANGE frame, so all orders on the same date are peers and each gets the cumulative total <em>through the end of that date</em> \u2014 two same-day orders of 20 and 30 after a prior 10 both show 60 instead of 30 and 60. If you want a per-order running balance, use <code>ROWS UNBOUNDED PRECEDING</code> and order by a unique key too (<code>order_date, order_id</code>), otherwise ROWS is non-deterministic between peers. If you want \"cumulative as of each date\", RANGE is actually what you want \u2014 but then aggregate to one row per date first to make it explicit.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a 7-day rolling average of daily active users that is correct when some days have no data.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> spine <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> gs::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> <span class=\"o\">generate_series</span>((<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MIN</span>(event_time)::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events),\n                         (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">MAX</span>(event_time)::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events),\n                         <span class=\"k\">INTERVAL</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 day'</span>) gs\n), dau <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> event_time::<span class=\"k\">date</span> <span class=\"k\">AS</span> day, <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> user_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dau\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> s.day,\n       <span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(d.dau, <span class=\"y\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dau,\n       <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(<span class=\"o\">COALESCE</span>(d.dau, <span class=\"y\">0</span>)) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> s.day\n                                     <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">6</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dau_7d_avg,\n       <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> s.day <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">6</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT</span> <span class=\"k\">ROW</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> days_in_window\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> spine s\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT</span> <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> dau d <span class=\"k\">USING</span> (day)\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> s.day;</div>\nThe spine guarantees exactly one row per day, so ROWS 6 PRECEDING = 7 calendar days and missing days count as 0 rather than being skipped. <code>days_in_window</code> lets you blank out the first 6 partial windows. Distinguish this from \"7-day active users\" (distinct users over the trailing 7 days), which is <em>not</em> an average of DAU and can't be computed from daily counts \u2014 it needs a self-join or a range join on raw events.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Find products that together make up the top 80% of revenue (Pareto).</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> p <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> product_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rev\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> order_items <span class=\"k\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> product_id\n), c <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> product_id, rev,\n           <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> rev <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, product_id <span class=\"k\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"k\">UNBOUNDED</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cum_rev,\n           <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(rev) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> ()                                                        <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total_rev\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> p\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> product_id, rev, <span class=\"o\">ROUND</span>(<span class=\"y\">100.0</span> * cum_rev / total_rev, <span class=\"y\">2</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> cum_pct\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> c\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> cum_rev - rev &lt; <span class=\"y\">0.8</span> * total_rev      <span class=\"c\">-- include the product that crosses 80%</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> rev <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>;</div>\nRunning total in descending revenue order over a grand total. <code>cum_rev \u2212 rev</code> is the cumulative share <em>before</em> this product, so the condition keeps every product needed to reach 80%, including the one that crosses the line (filtering <code>cum_rev \u2264 0.8 * total</code> would stop just short). ROWS + a tie-breaker avoids equal-revenue products being lumped together by the default RANGE frame.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>For each user, find orders placed within 7 days of their previous order.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date, prev_date, order_date - prev_date <span class=\"k\">AS</span> days_since_prev\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, order_id, order_date,\n           <span class=\"o\">LAG</span>(order_date) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"k\">BY</span> order_date, order_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> prev_date\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> order_date - prev_date &lt;= <span class=\"y\">7</span>;</div>\nWith <code>order_date</code> as a date, <code>date \u2212 date</code> returns an integer number of days in Postgres (use DATEDIFF in MySQL/SQL Server/Snowflake, DATE_DIFF in BigQuery). First orders have NULL prev_date and are filtered automatically because the comparison is UNKNOWN. The LAG must be computed in an inner query because WHERE runs before windows. Extensions: repeat-purchase rate = share of users with at least one such order; time-to-second-order = <code>NTH_VALUE</code> or <code>ROW_NUMBER = 2</code>.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK \u2014 \"top 3 salaries per department\". Which do you choose?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Clarify the requirement, because each gives a different answer with ties. With salaries 100, 90, 90, 80, 70: ROW_NUMBER \u2264 3 returns exactly 3 people (100, 90, 90) \u2014 but if four people tied at 100 it would keep an arbitrary three of them, so add a tie-breaker. RANK \u2264 3 returns 100, 90, 90 (80 has rank 4) \u2014 \"top 3 positions, ties share\". DENSE_RANK \u2264 3 returns 100, 90, 90, 80 \u2014 \"people earning one of the top 3 distinct salaries\". Default interview answer: DENSE_RANK if they say \"top 3 salaries\", ROW_NUMBER with a tie-breaker if they say \"exactly 3 employees\", and say the trade-off out loud.</div></details>\n</main>",
  "sql_optimization": "<main>\n  <h1>Indexes &amp; Query Optimization</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-storage\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">How Data Is Stored</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Pages, the heap, why full scans cost what they cost</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-btree\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">B-tree Internals</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">O(log n), range scans, free ordering</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-types\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Other Index Types</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Hash, GIN, GiST, BRIN, bitmap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-composite\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Composite Indexes</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Leftmost prefix, equality before range</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-covering\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Covering Indexes</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">INCLUDE and index-only scans</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-partial\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Partial &amp; Expression Indexes</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Index a subset, index a computation</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-sarg\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">The Sargability Trap</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Functions, wildcards, casts, OR \u2014 and rewrites</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-cost\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">The Cost of Indexes</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Write amplification, when NOT to index</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-planner\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Planner &amp; Statistics</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">ANALYZE, cardinality, why estimates go wrong</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-explain\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Reading EXPLAIN</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Scan &amp; join nodes, estimated vs actual, buffers</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-rewrites\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Common Rewrites</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">EXISTS, UNION ALL, keyset pagination, pre-aggregation</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-partition\"><span class=\"rm-num\">12</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Partitioning</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Range / list / hash and partition pruning</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-olap\"><span class=\"rm-num\">13</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">OLTP vs Columnar Warehouses</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Sort/cluster keys, paying per byte scanned</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"so-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">14</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Diagnose, rewrite, defend</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Cost is measured in pages, not rows:</strong> a row store reads fixed-size pages (8 KB in Postgres). A sequential scan reads every page once, in order, which is cheap per page; an index lookup costs a few random page reads per matching row. That is why an index wins for a handful of rows but a full scan wins once a query touches more than a few percent of the table \u2014 the planner is comparing page counts, not \"index good, scan bad\".</li>\n    <li><strong>A B-tree is a sorted, shallow tree:</strong> with hundreds of keys per page, three or four levels cover billions of rows, so a lookup is ~4 page reads. Because leaves are sorted and linked, the same index serves equality, ranges, prefix <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code> and <code>MIN/MAX</code> without a sort.</li>\n    <li><strong>Composite index = sort by col1, then col2:</strong> an index on <code>(a, b, c)</code> is usable for predicates on <code>a</code>, <code>a,b</code>, <code>a,b,c</code> \u2014 the leftmost prefix. Put equality columns first and the range column last: once you hit a range, columns after it can't narrow the seek.</li>\n    <li><strong>Sargability:</strong> a predicate is index-friendly only if the bare column is compared against something constant. <code>WHERE date(created_at) = '2024-01-01'</code>, <code>LIKE '%abc'</code>, a column-side implicit cast, or an OR across different columns all force a scan. Rewrite to a half-open range on the raw column, or build an expression index.</li>\n    <li><strong>Covering indexes skip the heap:</strong> if every column the query needs is in the index (key or <code>INCLUDE</code>), Postgres can do an Index Only Scan \u2014 no heap visit, provided the visibility map says the pages are all-visible (so VACUUM matters).</li>\n    <li><strong>Every index taxes writes:</strong> each INSERT updates every index; an UPDATE to an indexed column rewrites index entries; indexes consume disk and buffer cache. Don't index low-selectivity columns (booleans, status with 3 values) unless a partial index targets the rare value.</li>\n    <li><strong>The planner is only as good as its row estimates:</strong> it uses statistics (MCVs, histograms, n_distinct) gathered by ANALYZE and assumes columns are independent. Stale stats, correlated columns, and functions on columns produce wrong cardinalities, which pick the wrong join algorithm. In EXPLAIN ANALYZE, a 100x gap between <code>rows=</code> estimated and actual is the first thing to chase.</li>\n    <li><strong>Warehouses don't use indexes:</strong> columnar engines (BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift) read only the referenced columns and skip blocks via min/max metadata. You optimise with partitioning and clustering/sort keys, and you pay (in BigQuery on-demand) for bytes scanned \u2014 so <code>SELECT *</code> costs money and <code>LIMIT</code> does not reduce it.</li>\n  </ol>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. HOW DATA IS STORED \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-storage\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>How data is stored \u2014 pages, heaps and the cost of a scan</h2>\n\n  <p>Every optimisation decision makes sense once you know what the disk actually holds. Running schema for the whole note: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id, manager_id, salary, hire_date)</code>, <code>departments(id, name)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)</code>, <code>users(user_id, signup_date, country)</code> (plus <code>email</code> and <code>phone</code> where an example needs a text column), <code>events(user_id, event_time, event_type)</code>.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 page, heap, tuple</span>\n    <p>A <strong>page</strong> (block) is the unit of I/O: the database never reads one row, it reads the whole page that contains it (8 KB in Postgres, 16 KB in MySQL InnoDB). A Postgres table is a <strong>heap</strong>: an unordered pile of pages, rows appended wherever there is free space. Each row version is a <strong>tuple</strong> addressed by a <strong>TID / ctid</strong> = (page number, slot), e.g. <code>(4217, 3)</code>. Large values (long text, jsonb) are moved out of line into a TOAST table.<br><br>\n    MySQL InnoDB is different: the table <em>is</em> a B-tree clustered on the primary key (a \"clustered index\"), and secondary indexes store the primary key instead of a physical address. SQL Server lets you choose a clustered index; Postgres has none (the <code>CLUSTER</code> command reorders once but is not maintained).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Why a full scan costs what it costs</h3>\n  <p>A <strong>sequential scan</strong> reads pages 0..N in order. Disks and the OS read-ahead love that: consecutive reads, often served in large chunks. An index lookup produces TIDs pointing to scattered pages \u2014 <strong>random I/O</strong>. Postgres encodes this asymmetry in its cost model: <code>seq_page_cost = 1.0</code>, <code>random_page_cost = 4.0</code> (often lowered to ~1.1 on SSDs).</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- How big is the table, in pages?</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> relpages, reltuples\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> pg_class <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> relname = <span class=\"g\">'orders'</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">--  relpages | reltuples</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   125000  | 10000000      \u2192 ~80 rows per 8 KB page</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Seq scan cost  \u2248 relpages * seq_page_cost + rows * cpu_tuple_cost</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--               \u2248 125000 * 1.0 + 10M * 0.01  = 225,000</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Index fetch of k scattered rows \u2248 k * random_page_cost (worst case)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   k = 1,000   \u2192 ~4,000      index wins easily</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   k = 200,000 \u2192 ~800,000    seq scan wins (2% of rows!)</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the crossover point</span>\n    <p>For a 10M-row table, fetching 2% of rows through an index can already cost more than reading the whole table, because each matching row may live on a different page and each page may be read more than once. This is why \"the planner ignored my index\" is often the <em>correct</em> decision: the predicate wasn't selective enough. The exact crossover depends on how well the heap order correlates with the index order (<code>pg_stats.correlation</code>): if rows were inserted in <code>order_date</code> order, consecutive index entries point to the same pages, and the index stays cheap for much larger fractions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 a library without and with a catalogue</span>\n    <p>The heap is a library where books are shelved in the order they arrived. To find every book by one author you either walk every shelf (seq scan \u2014 tiring but you never backtrack) or look the author up in a card catalogue that tells you \"shelf 812, slot 3; shelf 14, slot 9; \u2026\" (index scan). For three books the catalogue wins. For 20,000 books spread across the building, you will walk more running back and forth than if you'd just swept the shelves once.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. B-TREE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-btree\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>B-tree index internals</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 B-tree (B+tree)</span>\n    <p>A balanced tree of pages. <strong>Internal pages</strong> hold separator keys and child pointers; <strong>leaf pages</strong> hold every indexed key in sorted order together with the TID of its heap row, and each leaf links to its neighbours. All leaves are at the same depth. It is the default index type in every major RDBMS (<code>CREATE INDEX</code> with no <code>USING</code> clause builds a B-tree).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">                   [ root:  \u2026 | 5000 | 9000 | \u2026 ]\n                   /             |              \\\n     [ 1000 | 2500 | 4000 ]  [ 6000 | 7500 ]  [ 9500 | \u2026 ]       internal\n       /    |    |    \\\n  [leaf: 2500 2501 2502 \u2026 3999] \u21c4 [leaf: 4000 4001 \u2026] \u21c4 \u2026        leaves (sorted, linked)\n      each entry \u2192 (page, slot) in the heap</div>\n\n  <h3>Why O(log n) is really \"about 3\u20134 reads\"</h3>\n  <p>The branching factor (fan-out) is the number of keys per page \u2014 for an 8-byte integer key that's several hundred. With fan-out 300: level 1 addresses 300 leaves, level 2 \u2192 90,000, level 3 \u2192 27 million, level 4 \u2192 8 billion. The top levels are hot and stay cached, so a point lookup on a billion-row table is typically 1\u20132 physical reads for the index plus 1 for the heap.</p>\n\n  <h3>What the sorted order buys you</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Operation</th><th>How the B-tree serves it</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE id = 42</code></td><td>Descend root \u2192 leaf, one entry</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE order_date BETWEEN a AND b</code></td><td>Descend to <code>a</code>, then walk leaves rightwards until <code>&gt; b</code> (range scan)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE name LIKE 'Ali%'</code></td><td>A prefix is a range <code>['Ali', 'Alj')</code>. In Postgres needs C collation or a <code>text_pattern_ops</code> index</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ORDER BY order_date LIMIT 10</code></td><td>Read the first 10 leaf entries \u2014 no sort, no full read</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>MIN(order_date)</code> / <code>MAX(...)</code></td><td>Leftmost / rightmost leaf entry</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ORDER BY order_date DESC</code></td><td>Walk leaves backwards (B-trees scan both directions)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE x IS NULL</code></td><td>Postgres B-trees store NULLs (sorted last by default), so yes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Uniqueness</td><td><code>UNIQUE</code> and <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> constraints are enforced by a unique B-tree</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"an index scan returns rows in order, so I don't need ORDER BY\"</span>\n    <p>Without <code>ORDER BY</code>, SQL guarantees no order at all. The planner may switch to a seq scan, a bitmap scan or a parallel plan tomorrow and your \"ordered\" output silently changes. Always write <code>ORDER BY</code>; the index just makes it free.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. OTHER TYPES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-types\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>Other index types \u2014 and when each wins</h2>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Structure</th><th>Supports</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Hash</strong></td><td>Hash of key \u2192 bucket</td><td><code>=</code> only. No range, no ordering, no uniqueness</td><td>Rarely worth it over B-tree; possibly very long keys (UUID strings, URLs) where only equality is ever used. Crash-safe in Postgres only since v10</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>GIN</strong> (Generalized Inverted)</td><td>Each element/token \u2192 posting list of rows</td><td>Containment: <code>@&gt;</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, full-text <code>@@</code>; with <code>pg_trgm</code>, <code>LIKE '%abc%'</code></td><td>jsonb documents, arrays/tags, full-text search, substring search. Slow-ish to update, fast to read</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>GiST</strong> (Generalized Search Tree)</td><td>Balanced tree of bounding \"regions\"</td><td>Overlap, containment, distance (<code>&lt;-&gt;</code> nearest-neighbour), range types</td><td>Geospatial (PostGIS), time ranges, exclusion constraints (\"no two bookings overlap\"), KNN queries</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>SP-GiST</strong></td><td>Space-partitioned trees (quad-tree, radix)</td><td>Points, IP prefixes, text prefixes</td><td>Non-balanced data like phone prefixes or 2-D points</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>BRIN</strong> (Block Range)</td><td>Min/max per range of (default 128) pages</td><td>Range predicates on physically ordered data</td><td>Huge append-only tables where the column correlates with insert order (<code>events.event_time</code>). Kilobytes instead of gigabytes; useless if data is randomly ordered</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Bitmap</strong></td><td>One bit-vector per distinct value</td><td>Fast AND/OR of low-cardinality predicates</td><td>Persistent bitmap indexes exist in Oracle and warehouses for low-cardinality columns. Postgres has none on disk \u2014 it builds bitmaps <em>at query time</em> (Bitmap Index Scan, BitmapAnd/BitmapOr) from ordinary indexes</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- GIN on jsonb: \"events whose payload contains device=ios\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_events_payload <span class=\"k\">ON</span> events <span class=\"k\">USING</span> gin (payload);\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> payload @&gt; <span class=\"g\">'{\"device\": \"ios\"}'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Trigram GIN: makes LIKE '%gmail%' indexable</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS</span> pg_trgm;\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_users_email_trgm <span class=\"k\">ON</span> users <span class=\"k\">USING</span> gin (email gin_trgm_ops);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- BRIN on an append-only log: tiny, great for time-range filters</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_events_time_brin <span class=\"k\">ON</span> events <span class=\"k\">USING</span> brin (event_time);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- GiST exclusion constraint: no overlapping bookings per room</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS</span> btree_gist;\n<span class=\"k\">ALTER TABLE</span> bookings <span class=\"k\">ADD CONSTRAINT</span> no_overlap\n  <span class=\"k\">EXCLUDE USING</span> gist (room_id <span class=\"k\">WITH</span> =, during <span class=\"k\">WITH</span> &amp;&amp;);</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 pick by the operator, not the column</span>\n    <p>An index type is a promise to accelerate certain <em>operators</em>. B-tree: <code>&lt; \u2264 = \u2265 &gt;</code>. Hash: <code>=</code>. GIN: \"contains element\". GiST: \"overlaps / is near\". BRIN: \"could this block range contain the value\". Ask \"what operator does my WHERE use?\" and the index type follows.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. COMPOSITE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-composite\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>Composite indexes \u2014 leftmost prefix and column order</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 composite (multi-column) index</span>\n    <p>A B-tree on <code>(a, b, c)</code> sorts entries by <code>a</code>, ties broken by <code>b</code>, then by <code>c</code> \u2014 exactly like a phone book sorted by (last name, first name). The <strong>leftmost-prefix rule</strong>: the index can seek on <code>a</code>, on <code>(a, b)</code>, or on <code>(a, b, c)</code>, but not efficiently on <code>b</code> or <code>c</code> alone, because entries for a given <code>b</code> are scattered across every value of <code>a</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Predicate (index on <code>orders(user_id, status, order_date)</code>)</th><th>Seekable?</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>user_id = 7</code></td><td>Yes \u2014 prefix</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>user_id = 7 AND status = 'paid'</code></td><td>Yes \u2014 two-column prefix</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>user_id = 7 AND status = 'paid' AND order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01'</code></td><td>Yes \u2014 all three, tight range</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>user_id = 7 AND order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01'</code></td><td>Seek on <code>user_id</code> only; <code>order_date</code> is checked as a filter within the index (status is skipped)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>status = 'paid'</code></td><td>No seek (full index scan at best). MySQL 8.0.13+, Oracle and Postgres 18 can \"skip scan\" when the leading column has few distinct values</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>ORDER BY user_id, status</code></td><td>Yes \u2014 same order, no sort</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>user_id = 7 ORDER BY status, order_date</code></td><td>Yes \u2014 within one user, entries are already in that order</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Equality first, range last</h3>\n  <p>Query: <code>WHERE dept_id = 3 AND hire_date &gt;= '2023-01-01'</code>.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- GOOD: (dept_id, hire_date)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- All dept 3 entries are contiguous AND sorted by hire_date inside that block:</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- seek to (3, '2023-01-01'), read forward until dept_id changes. Every entry read is a hit.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_emp_dept_hire <span class=\"k\">ON</span> employees (dept_id, hire_date);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- WORSE: (hire_date, dept_id)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Seek to '2023-01-01', then walk EVERY hire since then across all depts,</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- discarding the ones not in dept 3. dept_id cannot narrow the seek because</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- after a range on the first column, the second column is no longer sorted.</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the range \"breaks\" the sort</span>\n    <p>Within <code>dept_id = 3</code>, hire dates are sorted. But across <code>hire_date \u2265 2023</code>, dept_ids go 1,4,3,2,3,\u2026 \u2014 unsorted. So anything after the first range column can only be used as a filter, not a seek. Rule of thumb for column order: (1) equality columns, (2) the column you sort by or range on, (3) extra columns only to make the index covering. Among equality columns, selectivity order matters much less than people think \u2014 prefer the order that lets the index serve other queries' prefixes too.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 redundant indexes</span>\n    <p>If you have <code>(user_id, order_date)</code>, a separate index on <code>(user_id)</code> is almost always redundant \u2014 the composite already serves every <code>user_id</code> lookup. Redundant indexes cost writes and memory for nothing. The reverse is not true: <code>(order_date)</code> alone is not covered by <code>(user_id, order_date)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. COVERING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-covering\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>Covering indexes, INCLUDE and index-only scans</h2>\n\n  <p>A normal Index Scan does two hops per row: read the index entry, then fetch the heap tuple to get the other columns (and, in Postgres, to check visibility). If the index already holds every column the query references, the second hop can be skipped.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Query: a user's recent order totals</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_date, amount\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class=\"y\">42</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">20</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Covering index (Postgres 11+, SQL Server): amount rides along in the leaves,</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- but is NOT part of the sort key, so it doesn't bloat internal pages.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_orders_user_date\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> orders (user_id, order_date) <span class=\"k\">INCLUDE</span> (amount);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Plan becomes:</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Limit</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   -&gt; Index Only Scan Backward using idx_orders_user_date on orders</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--        Index Cond: (user_id = 42)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--        Heap Fetches: 0</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 Index Only Scan and the visibility map</span>\n    <p>Postgres index entries carry no transaction visibility information, so it normally must visit the heap to know whether a row version is visible to your snapshot. The <strong>visibility map</strong> marks pages where every tuple is visible to all transactions; for those pages the heap visit is skipped. <code>Heap Fetches: N</code> in EXPLAIN ANALYZE counts the pages it still had to visit. A freshly bulk-loaded or heavily updated table that hasn't been VACUUMed will show many heap fetches and gain little. MySQL InnoDB secondary indexes always contain the primary key, so <code>(user_id)</code> implicitly \"covers\" <code>order_id</code> too.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Key column vs INCLUDE column</th><th>Key column</th><th>INCLUDE column</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Used for seek / ordering</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Stored in internal pages</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (leaves only)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Part of UNIQUE check</td><td>Yes</td><td>No \u2014 lets you enforce uniqueness on <code>(a)</code> while still covering <code>b</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Can filter in index</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes for index-only scans (as a filter, not a seek)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. PARTIAL / EXPRESSION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-partial\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>Partial and expression indexes</h2>\n\n  <h3>Partial index \u2014 index only the rows you query</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- 99% of orders are 'delivered'; the app constantly polls the few 'pending' ones.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_orders_pending\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> orders (order_date)\n  <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'pending'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Used, because the query's WHERE implies the index predicate:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'pending'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> order_date &lt; now() - <span class=\"k\">interval</span> <span class=\"g\">'1 day'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Partial UNIQUE: one active subscription per user, unlimited cancelled ones</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE UNIQUE INDEX</span> one_active_sub\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> subscriptions (user_id) <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> cancelled_at <span class=\"k\">IS NULL</span>;</div>\n  <p>Tiny index, cheap to maintain (rows outside the predicate don't touch it), and it solves the low-selectivity problem: an index on <code>status</code> is useless, an index on <em>only the rare status</em> is excellent. Caveat: the planner must prove the query's WHERE implies the index's WHERE at plan time \u2014 a parameter <code>status = $1</code> in a generic prepared plan can't use it. Partial indexes are Postgres/SQLite (SQL Server calls them filtered indexes; MySQL lacks them).</p>\n\n  <h3>Expression (functional) index \u2014 index a computed value</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Case-insensitive login lookup</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_users_email_lower <span class=\"k\">ON</span> users (<span class=\"o\">lower</span>(email));\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"o\">lower</span>(email) = <span class=\"g\">'ann@x.com'</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- must match the expression exactly</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Daily rollups on a timestamp (expression must be IMMUTABLE;</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- date_trunc on timestamptz depends on TimeZone so it is not \u2014 use timestamp or AT TIME ZONE)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_events_day <span class=\"k\">ON</span> events (<span class=\"o\">date_trunc</span>(<span class=\"g\">'day'</span>, event_time));   <span class=\"c\">-- event_time is timestamp</span></div>\n  <p>The planner keeps separate statistics for indexed expressions after ANALYZE, which also fixes row estimates for that expression. MySQL 8.0.13+ supports functional key parts; older MySQL used an indexed generated column.</p>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. SARGABILITY \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-sarg\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>The sargability trap \u2014 and the rewrites</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 sargable (Search ARGument ABLE)</span>\n    <p>A predicate is sargable when it has the form <code>indexed_column &lt;op&gt; value</code>, where the value can be computed once before scanning. Then the engine can translate it into a seek on the sorted index. If the column is wrapped in anything \u2014 function, arithmetic, cast \u2014 the engine would have to compute that expression for every entry, so the sort order of the raw column no longer helps.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Non-sargable</th><th>Why it breaks</th><th>Sargable rewrite</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM order_date) = 2024</code></td><td>Function on column</td><td><code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-01-01'</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE order_date::date = '2024-03-05'</code> (timestamp column)</td><td>Cast on column</td><td><code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-03-05' AND order_date &lt; '2024-03-06'</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE amount * 1.2 &gt; 120</code></td><td>Arithmetic on column</td><td><code>WHERE amount &gt; 100</code> (move math to the constant side)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE name LIKE '%son'</code></td><td>Leading wildcard: no known prefix to seek to</td><td>Trigram GIN index; or index <code>reverse(name)</code> and query <code>reverse(name) LIKE 'nos%'</code>; or full-text search</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE lower(email) = 'a@x.com'</code></td><td>Function on column</td><td>Expression index on <code>lower(email)</code>, or a <code>citext</code> column</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE COALESCE(status, 'none') = 'paid'</code></td><td>Function on column</td><td><code>WHERE status = 'paid'</code> (NULL \u2260 'paid' anyway)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>WHERE user_id = 5 OR country = 'IN'</code></td><td>OR across different columns: no single index range covers both</td><td>Separate indexes \u2192 Postgres BitmapOr; or <code>UNION</code> of two indexed queries</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Implicit casts \u2014 the invisible function</h3>\n  <p>When types don't match, the engine inserts a conversion. If it converts the <em>literal</em>, fine; if it converts the <em>column</em>, the index dies silently.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- MySQL: phone is VARCHAR, compared to a number \u2192 every row's phone is cast to a number</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> phone = <span class=\"y\">5551234</span>;      <span class=\"c\">-- full scan</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> phone = <span class=\"g\">'5551234'</span>;    <span class=\"c\">-- index seek</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Postgres: bigint column vs numeric literal \u2192 column is promoted to numeric</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> order_id = <span class=\"y\">42.0</span>;     <span class=\"c\">-- order_id::numeric = 42.0 \u2192 seq scan</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> order_id = <span class=\"y\">42</span>;       <span class=\"c\">-- index scan</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Also classic: an ORM binding a Java String as NVARCHAR against a VARCHAR column</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- in SQL Server, or joining int to text keys across two tables.</span></div>\n\n  <h3>OR \u2192 UNION rewrite</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- One plan must satisfy both branches \u2192 often a seq scan</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class=\"y\">5</span> <span class=\"k\">OR</span> country = <span class=\"g\">'IN'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Each branch uses its own index; UNION (not ALL) removes the row matching both</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class=\"y\">5</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UNION</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> country = <span class=\"g\">'IN'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Note: OR on the SAME column is fine: status = 'a' OR status = 'b'</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- is equivalent to status IN ('a','b') \u2192 one index, multiple seeks.</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 BETWEEN on timestamps</span>\n    <p><code>order_date BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-01-31'</code> on a timestamp column is sargable but <em>wrong</em>: <code>'2024-01-31'</code> means midnight, so all of Jan 31 after 00:00:00 is excluded. Always use half-open ranges: <code>&gt;= '2024-01-01' AND &lt; '2024-02-01'</code>. Correct and sargable.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. COST \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-cost\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>The cost of indexes \u2014 and when NOT to index</h2>\n\n  <h3>Write amplification</h3>\n  <p>A table with 6 indexes turns one INSERT into 7 structure modifications (heap + 6 B-tree inserts, each possibly splitting a page), each WAL-logged. In Postgres, an UPDATE writes a whole new tuple version; if any indexed column changed, or the new version doesn't fit on the same page, every index gets a new entry. If no indexed column changed and there's room on the page, Postgres does a <strong>HOT update</strong> (Heap-Only Tuple) and skips index maintenance entirely \u2014 one reason not to index frequently-updated columns like <code>last_seen_at</code>.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Cost</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Write latency</td><td>Every INSERT/DELETE/indexed-UPDATE touches every index. Bulk loads are often faster with indexes dropped and rebuilt</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Indexes can rival the table size; a 5-index table may be 2\u20133\u00d7 its heap on disk</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Memory</td><td>Indexes compete with data for shared_buffers / buffer pool; hot index pages evict hot data pages</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Bloat &amp; maintenance</td><td>Dead entries accumulate; VACUUM and occasional <code>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> are needed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Lock during creation</td><td>Plain <code>CREATE INDEX</code> blocks writes; use <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> in production (slower, can't run in a transaction)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Planner time</td><td>More candidate indexes = more plans to cost (minor, but real for OLTP)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>When NOT to index</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Low selectivity:</strong> <code>is_active</code> (2 values), <code>country</code> where 60% are 'US'. A lookup matching half the table is cheaper as a seq scan. Exception: a partial index on the rare value.</li>\n    <li><strong>Small tables:</strong> a few pages fit in one read; the seq scan is already optimal.</li>\n    <li><strong>Write-heavy, rarely-read tables:</strong> logs and event sinks \u2014 every index slows ingestion. Consider BRIN or none.</li>\n    <li><strong>Columns only ever wrapped in functions</strong> you can't express as an expression index.</li>\n    <li><strong>Duplicates:</strong> an index that is a prefix of another. Find unused ones via <code>pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan = 0</code> over a representative period.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 forgetting foreign-key indexes</span>\n    <p>Postgres automatically indexes PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE columns but <strong>not</strong> foreign-key columns. <code>orders.user_id</code> referencing <code>users</code> has no index unless you create one \u2014 so joins on it scan, and every <code>DELETE FROM users</code> must scan <code>orders</code> to check the constraint. (MySQL InnoDB does create an index for FKs automatically.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. PLANNER \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-planner\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>The query planner and statistics</h2>\n\n  <p>SQL is declarative: you say <em>what</em>, the optimizer decides <em>how</em>. Postgres's planner enumerates candidate plans (scan method per table, join order, join algorithm), estimates the cost of each from <strong>row-count estimates</strong> times per-operation cost constants, and picks the cheapest. (For more than <code>geqo_threshold</code> = 12 FROM items it switches to a genetic search.) Cost is in arbitrary units anchored at one sequential page read = 1.0.</p>\n\n  <h3>Where row estimates come from</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">ANALYZE</span> orders;   <span class=\"c\">-- samples ~300 \u00d7 default_statistics_target (100) = 30,000 rows</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> attname, n_distinct, most_common_vals, most_common_freqs, histogram_bounds, correlation\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> pg_stats <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> tablename = <span class=\"g\">'orders'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- status:     n_distinct=4, MCV {delivered,pending,cancelled,refunded}, freqs {0.91,0.05,0.03,0.01}</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--             \u2192 WHERE status='pending' \u2248 0.05 \u00d7 10M = 500,000 rows</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- amount:     histogram of 100 equal-population buckets \u2192 range selectivity by interpolation</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- order_date: correlation \u2248 0.99 (inserted in date order \u2192 index range scans are cheap)</span></div>\n  <p>Autovacuum runs ANALYZE automatically after enough rows change (by default ~10% of the table plus 50 rows), but after a bulk load or a big delete you should run it yourself.</p>\n\n  <h3>Why estimates go wrong</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Cause</th><th>Example</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Stale statistics</td><td>Loaded 5M rows of today's data; histogram still ends yesterday \u2192 planner thinks <code>order_date = today</code> returns ~1 row, picks nested loop</td><td><code>ANALYZE</code> after loads</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Correlated columns (independence assumption)</td><td><code>country='IN' AND city='Mumbai'</code>: planner multiplies selectivities 0.1 \u00d7 0.01, but every Mumbai row is in IN \u2192 underestimates 10\u00d7</td><td><code>CREATE STATISTICS s (dependencies, mcv) ON country, city FROM users;</code> then ANALYZE</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Functions / expressions</td><td><code>WHERE lower(email) = \u2026</code> or <code>amount * 2 &gt; x</code>: no stats on the expression \u2192 default guess (0.5% for equality, 33% for inequality)</td><td>Expression index (gets its own stats) or rewrite</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Skew beyond the MCV list</td><td>A long tail of heavy users not in the top-100 MCVs</td><td><code>ALTER TABLE \u2026 ALTER COLUMN \u2026 SET STATISTICS 1000</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Error compounding through joins</td><td>A 10\u00d7 miss at the scan becomes 1000\u00d7 after three joins</td><td>Fix the leaf estimate; break query into materialised steps</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Generic prepared plans</td><td>After 5 executions Postgres may switch to a generic plan that ignores the actual parameter (bad for skewed values)</td><td><code>plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan</code> for that workload</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>CTE / table variable / temp table without stats</td><td>Temp tables aren't auto-analyzed</td><td><code>ANALYZE temp_table</code> after filling it</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 wrong rows \u2192 wrong join</span>\n    <p>Almost every \"the query was fast yesterday\" story is a cardinality misestimate. If the planner thinks the outer side of a join has 10 rows, a Nested Loop (10 index probes) is ideal. If it really has 2 million, that's 2 million probes \u2014 a Hash Join would have been 100\u00d7 faster. The plan is a function of the estimates; fix the estimates and the plan fixes itself.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. EXPLAIN \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-explain\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>Reading EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE</h2>\n\n  <p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the chosen plan and estimates without running it. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> actually executes the query (careful with INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE \u2014 wrap in <code>BEGIN \u2026 ROLLBACK</code>) and adds real timings and row counts. Add <code>BUFFERS</code> to see page I/O. Read the tree <strong>bottom-up, innermost first</strong>; each node's time includes its children.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">EXPLAIN</span> (<span class=\"k\">ANALYZE</span>, <span class=\"k\">BUFFERS</span>)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.order_date &gt;= <span class=\"g\">'2024-06-01'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> o.order_date &lt; <span class=\"g\">'2024-07-01'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> u.country;\n\nHashAggregate  (cost=48210.55..48212.95 rows=192 width=40)\n               (actual time=412.8..413.0 rows=187 loops=1)\n  Group Key: u.country\n  Buffers: shared hit=21890 read=9120\n  -&gt;  Hash Join  (cost=3185.00..46710.55 rows=300000 width=14)\n                 (actual time=31.2..340.6 rows=842311 loops=1)\n        Hash Cond: (o.user_id = u.user_id)\n        -&gt;  Bitmap Heap Scan on orders o  (cost=4210.1..41892.3 rows=300000 width=14)\n                                          (actual time=24.9..198.3 rows=842311 loops=1)\n              Recheck Cond: ((order_date &gt;= '2024-06-01') AND (order_date &lt; '2024-07-01'))\n              Heap Blocks: exact=10522\n              -&gt;  Bitmap Index Scan on idx_orders_date  (rows=300000)\n                                          (actual rows=842311 loops=1)\n        -&gt;  Hash  (cost=1935.00..1935.00 rows=100000 width=8) (actual rows=100000 loops=1)\n              Buckets: 131072  Memory Usage: 4931kB\n              -&gt;  Seq Scan on users u  (rows=100000) (actual rows=100000 loops=1)\nPlanning Time: 0.4 ms\nExecution Time: 414.1 ms</div>\n\n  <h3>How to read each field</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>cost=A..B</code></td><td>Estimated startup cost (before first row) .. total cost. Arbitrary units</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>rows=</code> (in cost)</td><td>Estimated rows output by this node</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>actual time=A..B</code></td><td>Real ms to first row .. last row, <strong>per loop</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>actual rows= \u2026 loops=N</code></td><td>Real rows <strong>per loop</strong>; multiply by loops for the total (crucial for inner side of nested loops)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>Buffers: shared hit / read</code></td><td>Pages found in cache vs read from disk (OS cache or storage). The truest measure of work</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>Rows Removed by Filter</code></td><td>Rows read then discarded \u2014 a big number means a missing or unusable index</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>Heap Fetches</code></td><td>Index-only scan pages that still needed the heap (vacuum!)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>Sort Method: external merge Disk: \u2026</code></td><td>Sort spilled past <code>work_mem</code></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>In the plan above, the date range was estimated at 300k rows but returned 842k (stale stats after a load) \u2014 not harmful here since Hash Join was chosen anyway, but the kind of gap you'd ANALYZE away.</p>\n\n  <h3>Scan nodes</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Node</th><th>What it does</th><th>Chosen when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Seq Scan</td><td>Reads every page of the heap</td><td>Low selectivity, small table, or no usable index</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Index Scan</td><td>Walks index, fetches each heap tuple in index order</td><td>Few rows, or ordered output needed (ORDER BY \u2026 LIMIT)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Index Only Scan</td><td>Answers from the index alone</td><td>Covering index + mostly all-visible pages</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Bitmap Index Scan \u2192 Bitmap Heap Scan</td><td>Collects all matching TIDs into a bitmap sorted by page, then reads each heap page once in physical order. \"Recheck Cond\" re-applies the predicate when the bitmap went lossy (page-level)</td><td>Medium selectivity (too many rows for random index fetches, too few for a seq scan); combining indexes with BitmapAnd / BitmapOr</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Join nodes</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Join</th><th>Algorithm</th><th>Cost</th><th>Best when</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Nested Loop</td><td>For each outer row, probe the inner (ideally via index)</td><td>O(outer \u00d7 cost of probe)</td><td>Small outer side + index on inner join key. Only algorithm for non-equality joins. Disaster if outer is misestimated</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hash Join</td><td>Build hash table on smaller input, stream the larger and probe</td><td>O(N + M), memory for the build side (spills in batches past work_mem)</td><td>Large unsorted inputs with equality join \u2014 the analytics workhorse</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Merge Join</td><td>Both inputs sorted on the key, walk in lockstep</td><td>O(N + M) + sorting if not already sorted</td><td>Inputs already ordered (from indexes), very large inputs, or range-friendly merges</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 reading total time on a looped node</span>\n    <p>An inner Index Scan showing <code>actual time=0.02..0.03 rows=1 loops=500000</code> looks instant but costs 500,000 \u00d7 0.03 ms = 15 s. Always multiply per-loop time and rows by <code>loops</code>. Tools like explain.depesz.com or explain.dalibo.com do this for you.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. REWRITES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-rewrites\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>Common query rewrites</h2>\n\n  <h3>Avoid SELECT *</h3>\n  <p>It defeats index-only scans (the index can't cover \"every column\"), drags TOASTed blobs across the network, breaks when columns are added, and in columnar warehouses reads (and bills) every column. Name the columns you need.</p>\n\n  <h3>Correlated subquery \u2192 JOIN or window</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Employees earning above their department average.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Correlated: logically re-evaluates the AVG per outer row</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees x <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> x.dept_id = e.dept_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Set-based: compute each average once, then join</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> dept_avg <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> dept_id, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_sal <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> dept_id\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name, e.salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> dept_avg d <span class=\"k\">ON</span> d.dept_id = e.dept_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; d.avg_sal;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Or one pass with a window function</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary, <span class=\"o\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> dept_id) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_sal\n      <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; avg_sal;</div>\n  <p>Edge case worth stating in an interview: employees with NULL <code>dept_id</code> drop out of the correlated and JOIN versions (<code>NULL = NULL</code> is not true), but the window version puts all NULL-dept employees into one partition and compares them against each other. The versions are equivalent only if <code>dept_id</code> is NOT NULL. Modern planners often decorrelate simple subqueries automatically; the rewrite matters most when they can't (complex correlation, LIMIT inside, MySQL 5.x).</p>\n\n  <h3>EXISTS instead of COUNT(*) &gt; 0</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Counts every order of the user just to compare with zero</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id) &gt; <span class=\"y\">0</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Stops at the first match; planner turns it into a semi-join</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Anti-join: prefer NOT EXISTS over NOT IN.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- NOT IN returns ZERO rows if the subquery yields a single NULL, and Postgres</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- can't plan NOT IN as an anti-join (it uses a hashed or per-row SubPlan instead).</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);</div>\n\n  <h3>UNION ALL vs UNION</h3>\n  <p><code>UNION</code> deduplicates \u2014 that's a sort or hash over the whole combined result. If the branches are disjoint by construction (e.g. different partitions, different years) or duplicates are acceptable, <code>UNION ALL</code> just concatenates and can stream rows immediately.</p>\n\n  <h3>Pagination: OFFSET vs keyset</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- OFFSET: reads and throws away 100,000 rows to return 20. Gets slower every page,</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- and rows shift between pages if new rows arrive.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, order_date, amount <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">20</span> <span class=\"k\">OFFSET</span> <span class=\"y\">100000</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Keyset (seek) pagination: remember the last row of the previous page.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- With an index on (order_date DESC, order_id DESC), each page is one index seek.</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> order_id, order_date, amount <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> (order_date, order_id) &lt; (<span class=\"g\">'2024-06-14 10:22:01'</span>, <span class=\"y\">983311</span>)   <span class=\"c\">-- row-value comparison</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, order_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">20</span>;</div>\n  <p>The tiebreaker (<code>order_id</code>) is mandatory: without a unique total order, rows with equal timestamps can be skipped or repeated. Trade-off: no \"jump to page 500\".</p>\n\n  <h3>Pre-aggregate before joining</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Join 10M orders to users, THEN group: hashes 10M wide rows</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.amount)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> u.country;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Aggregate orders per user first (10M \u2192 100k rows), then join the small result</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.country, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(o.user_total)\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"o\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> user_total <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> user_id) o\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> users u <span class=\"k\">ON</span> u.user_id = o.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> u.country;</div>\n  <p>Equivalent results because SUM is decomposable. Also prevents the <strong>fan-out bug</strong>: joining two one-to-many children (orders and events) to users and then summing double-counts \u2014 aggregate each child to the user grain first. For dashboards hit repeatedly, persist the aggregate as a materialized view or summary table.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 CTEs as optimisation fences</span>\n    <p>Before Postgres 12, every CTE was materialised: a filter outside the CTE couldn't be pushed into it. Since 12, a non-recursive, side-effect-free CTE referenced once is inlined. You can force either with <code>WITH x AS MATERIALIZED (\u2026)</code> / <code>NOT MATERIALIZED</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 12. PARTITIONING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-partition\"><span class=\"num\">12</span>Partitioning</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 table partitioning</span>\n    <p>Splitting one logical table into multiple physical child tables by the value of a <strong>partition key</strong>, within one database server. Queries hit the parent; the planner routes them. (Distinct from <em>sharding</em>, which splits data across servers.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Rule</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Range</td><td><code>FROM ('2024-01-01') TO ('2024-02-01')</code> (upper bound exclusive)</td><td>Time series: events, orders, logs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>List</td><td><code>IN ('US','CA')</code></td><td>Region, tenant tier, category</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hash</td><td><code>MODULUS 8, REMAINDER 3</code></td><td>Spread evenly when there's no natural range; parallel maintenance</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">CREATE TABLE</span> events (\n  user_id    <span class=\"k\">bigint</span>,\n  event_time <span class=\"k\">timestamptz</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT NULL</span>,\n  event_type <span class=\"k\">text</span>\n) <span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY RANGE</span> (event_time);\n\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE TABLE</span> events_2024_06 <span class=\"k\">PARTITION OF</span> events\n  <span class=\"k\">FOR VALUES FROM</span> (<span class=\"g\">'2024-06-01'</span>) <span class=\"k\">TO</span> (<span class=\"g\">'2024-07-01'</span>);\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE TABLE</span> events_2024_07 <span class=\"k\">PARTITION OF</span> events\n  <span class=\"k\">FOR VALUES FROM</span> (<span class=\"g\">'2024-07-01'</span>) <span class=\"k\">TO</span> (<span class=\"g\">'2024-08-01'</span>);\n\n<span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> events (user_id, event_time);   <span class=\"c\">-- cascades to every partition</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Partition pruning: only events_2024_06 is scanned</span>\n<span class=\"k\">EXPLAIN SELECT</span> <span class=\"o\">count</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> event_time &gt;= <span class=\"g\">'2024-06-10'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> event_time &lt; <span class=\"g\">'2024-06-20'</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Retention: dropping a month is instant metadata, not a 50M-row DELETE</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ALTER TABLE</span> events <span class=\"k\">DETACH PARTITION</span> events_2024_06;\n<span class=\"k\">DROP TABLE</span> events_2024_06;</div>\n\n  <h3>Partition pruning</h3>\n  <p>The planner eliminates partitions whose bounds can't match the WHERE clause \u2014 at plan time for constants, and (Postgres 11+) at execution time for parameters and values from a join/subquery. Pruning only works if the predicate is on the partition key and is sargable: <code>WHERE date(event_time) = '2024-06-10'</code> prunes nothing.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 partitioning is not a speed button</span>\n    <p>It helps when queries filter on the partition key (pruning), when you need cheap bulk deletion/archival, and when per-partition indexes stay small enough to cache. It <em>hurts</em> queries that don't filter on the key (they scan every partition's index), adds planning overhead with thousands of partitions, and in Postgres every PRIMARY KEY / UNIQUE constraint must include the partition key. A well-indexed 50M-row table usually doesn't need partitioning; a 5B-row append-only event log usually does.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 13. OLAP \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-olap\"><span class=\"num\">13</span>OLTP row stores vs OLAP columnar warehouses</h2>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>OLTP row store (Postgres, MySQL)</th><th>OLAP column store (BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Workload</td><td>Many small reads/writes by key; low latency</td><td>Few large scans + aggregations over billions of rows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Physical layout</td><td>All columns of a row together in a page</td><td>Each column stored separately, heavily compressed (run-length, dictionary)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>SELECT AVG(amount)</code> over 1B rows</td><td>Reads every column of every row</td><td>Reads only the <code>amount</code> column</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Main access accelerator</td><td>B-tree indexes</td><td>Block/zone min-max metadata, partitioning, clustering/sort keys</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Point lookups / single-row updates</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Poor \u2014 updates rewrite whole compressed blocks</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Constraints</td><td>Enforced PK/FK/UNIQUE</td><td>Often declared but <em>not enforced</em> (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift) \u2014 duplicates are your problem</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>How each warehouse prunes</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Knobs</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>BigQuery</td><td><code>PARTITION BY DATE(event_time)</code>, <code>CLUSTER BY user_id, event_type</code> (up to 4 cols)</td><td>Partitions are skipped entirely; within a partition, storage blocks are sorted by cluster columns and skipped by min/max. <code>require_partition_filter</code> forces users to filter</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Snowflake</td><td>Automatic micro-partitions (~50\u2013500 MB uncompressed); optional <code>CLUSTER BY</code></td><td>Per-micro-partition min/max metadata prunes; clustering keeps ranges tight as data churns (background reclustering costs credits). Search Optimization Service for selective point lookups</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Redshift</td><td><code>SORTKEY</code> (compound or interleaved), <code>DISTKEY</code> / <code>DISTSTYLE</code></td><td>Zone maps (min/max per 1 MB block) prune via the sort key; the dist key co-locates rows across nodes so joins on it avoid network shuffles</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Paying by bytes scanned</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- BigQuery on-demand bills bytes read from the referenced columns (after partition pruning).</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Scans every column of the whole table; LIMIT does NOT reduce bytes billed:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> analytics.events <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">10</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Two columns, one week of partitions:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> user_id, event_type\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> analytics.events\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> event_time &gt;= <span class=\"k\">TIMESTAMP</span>(<span class=\"g\">'2024-06-01'</span>) <span class=\"k\">AND</span> event_time &lt; <span class=\"k\">TIMESTAMP</span>(<span class=\"g\">'2024-06-08'</span>);</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 same principles, different unit</span>\n    <p>In both worlds the goal is \"touch less data\". OLTP measures it in pages and avoids reading rows via indexes. OLAP measures it in bytes/blocks and avoids reading columns (projection) and blocks (pruning). Sargability still matters: <code>WHERE DATE(event_time) = \u2026</code> can still prune in BigQuery when partitioned on <code>DATE(event_time)</code>, but wrapping a clustering column in an arbitrary function generally defeats block pruning. Warehouse-specific wins: approximate aggregates (<code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code>), materialized views, and avoiding join explosions that shuffle data between nodes.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 14. Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"so-qa\"><span class=\"num\">14</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>A query filtering on an indexed column is doing a Seq Scan. Walk through how you'd diagnose it.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">1) Run <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>. 2) Check sargability: is the column wrapped in a function, cast (implicit type mismatch), or a leading-wildcard LIKE? Is it an OR across columns? 3) Check selectivity: how many rows actually match? If it's more than a few percent of the table, the seq scan is the right call. 4) Compare estimated vs actual rows: a big gap means stale or insufficient statistics \u2192 <code>ANALYZE</code>, raise the statistics target, or add extended statistics for correlated columns. 5) For composite indexes, check the leftmost-prefix rule. 6) For a partial index, check the query's predicate implies the index's. 7) Check for a generic prepared plan ignoring a skewed parameter. 8) As a diagnostic only, <code>SET enable_seqscan = off</code> in the session to see the index plan's cost and actual runtime \u2014 if it's slower, the planner was right.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Design the index for: <code>SELECT order_id, amount FROM orders WHERE user_id = ? AND status = 'paid' ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 10</code></summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Equality columns first, then the sort column, then the payload: <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> idx_orders_user_status_date\n  <span class=\"k\">ON</span> orders (user_id, status, order_date <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">INCLUDE</span> (amount);</div>The seek lands on (user_id, 'paid'), entries are already ordered by order_date descending, so the engine reads exactly 10 entries and stops \u2014 no sort node. <code>order_id</code>: in Postgres include it too (<code>INCLUDE (amount, order_id)</code>) for an index-only scan; in InnoDB the PK is already in every secondary index. If almost every query is for 'paid', a partial index <code>ON orders (user_id, order_date DESC) WHERE status = 'paid'</code> is smaller. (<code>DESC</code> is optional \u2014 a B-tree can scan backwards \u2014 but matters when mixing directions, e.g. <code>ORDER BY a ASC, b DESC</code>.)</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Rewrite to be sargable: <code>WHERE EXTRACT(MONTH FROM order_date) = 6 AND EXTRACT(YEAR FROM order_date) = 2024</code></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> order_date &gt;= <span class=\"g\">'2024-06-01'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> order_date &lt; <span class=\"g\">'2024-07-01'</span></div>Half-open range on the raw column maps to one contiguous slice of the B-tree and works for both date and timestamp types (no lost last-day rows as with BETWEEN). If the query is genuinely \"June of every year\", a range can't express it: use an expression index on <code>EXTRACT(MONTH FROM order_date)</code> (fine for date/timestamp columns, which make it immutable) \u2014 or accept the scan, since one month in 12 is ~8% selectivity anyway.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why might adding an index make the system slower?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Every INSERT and DELETE must update it, and UPDATEs to its columns must too \u2014 and in Postgres, indexing a column that is updated frequently disables HOT updates for those updates, so every update now writes to <em>all</em> indexes. It costs disk, WAL volume (which slows replication), and buffer-cache space that hot data pages wanted. Building it without <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> blocks writes. And it can mislead the planner: a new index with poor real selectivity but optimistic estimates can get chosen for a nested-loop plan that is slower than the old hash join.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Explain Index Scan vs Bitmap Heap Scan vs Seq Scan.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><strong>Index Scan:</strong> walk the index, fetch each heap tuple immediately in index order \u2014 random I/O per row, but returns ordered rows and can stop early with LIMIT. Best for few rows. <strong>Bitmap Index Scan + Bitmap Heap Scan:</strong> first collect all matching TIDs into an in-memory bitmap ordered by page, then read each needed heap page once in physical order. Converts random I/O into near-sequential, and can AND/OR several indexes. Loses ordering; if the bitmap exceeds work_mem it becomes lossy (page-level) and needs a Recheck. Best for medium selectivity. <strong>Seq Scan:</strong> read all pages, filter each row. Best when a large fraction matches or the table is small.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows a Nested Loop with estimated rows=12, actual rows=480000. What's going on and what do you do?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">A cardinality underestimate made the planner think the outer input was tiny, so it chose a nested loop that now runs 480k inner probes; a hash join would scale linearly. Find the leaf node where the estimate first diverges. Causes: stale stats (run ANALYZE), correlated predicates multiplied as if independent (<code>CREATE STATISTICS \u2026 (dependencies, mcv)</code>), a function on a column with no stats (expression index or rewrite), skew outside the MCV list (raise the column's statistics target), or a generic prepared plan. Fix the estimate rather than forcing a join type; as an emergency only, session-level <code>SET enable_nestloop = off</code> (Postgres has no built-in hints; the pg_hint_plan extension adds them).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query: users who signed up in 2024 but have never placed an order. Make it efficient.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> u.user_id\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> users u\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> u.signup_date &gt;= <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> u.signup_date &lt; <span class=\"g\">'2025-01-01'</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.user_id = u.user_id);</div>Sargable range on <code>signup_date</code>; <code>NOT EXISTS</code> compiles to a Hash Anti Join (or a nested-loop anti join probing an index on <code>orders(user_id)</code>, which you should have since it's an FK). Avoid <code>NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders)</code>: if any <code>orders.user_id</code> is NULL, it returns no rows, and Postgres can't plan it as a true anti-join. <code>LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE o.user_id IS NULL</code> is an equivalent alternative.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>How do you paginate a 50M-row feed sorted by time efficiently?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Keyset pagination with a unique tiebreaker and a matching index: <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">CREATE INDEX</span> <span class=\"k\">ON</span> events (event_time <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, event_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>);\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> event_id, event_time, event_type <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> events\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> (event_time, event_id) &lt; (:last_time, :last_id)\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> event_time <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>, event_id <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">50</span>;</div>Each page costs one index seek plus 50 entries regardless of depth, whereas <code>OFFSET n</code> must produce and discard n rows, so page 10,000 is 10,000\u00d7 the work. Keyset is also stable under concurrent inserts (no duplicates/skips). Cost: no random page jumps; the cursor (last_time, last_id) is passed to the client.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>When would you choose BRIN over B-tree?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">For very large, append-mostly tables where the column's values correlate with physical row order \u2014 timestamps on an event log, auto-increment IDs. BRIN stores only min/max per block range (default 128 pages), so it's thousands of times smaller than a B-tree and nearly free to maintain on insert. A range query skips block ranges whose min/max can't match. It's useless for random data (every range spans everything), only gives \"maybe\" answers (heap rows are rechecked), and is poor for point lookups. Check <code>pg_stats.correlation</code> near \u00b11 before choosing it.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>A BigQuery query costs too much. What do you change?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Billing (on-demand) is bytes scanned, so: 1) select only needed columns \u2014 never <code>SELECT *</code>; 2) partition the table by date and always filter on the partition column with a direct predicate (enable <code>require_partition_filter</code>); 3) cluster by frequently filtered/joined columns (user_id, event_type) so blocks are pruned; 4) note that <code>LIMIT</code> doesn't reduce bytes; 5) pre-aggregate into summary tables or materialized views for dashboards; 6) use <code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code> where exactness isn't needed; 7) dry-run (<code>--dry_run</code>) to see bytes before running. Indexes aren't the lever here \u2014 layout is.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Should you put an index on a boolean <code>is_deleted</code> column?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Not a plain one. If 98% of rows are <code>false</code>, a query for <code>is_deleted = false</code> matches almost everything and a seq scan wins; the index only costs writes. Two better options: (1) a partial index on the column you actually search, restricted to live rows \u2014 <code>CREATE INDEX ON orders (user_id) WHERE NOT is_deleted</code> \u2014 so every live-row query gets a smaller index; (2) if you query the rare <code>true</code> rows (e.g. a purge job), <code>CREATE INDEX ON orders (order_id) WHERE is_deleted</code>, which is tiny.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>When does partitioning help, and when does it hurt?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Helps: queries filtering on the partition key (pruning skips partitions entirely), time-based retention (<code>DETACH/DROP PARTITION</code> instead of massive DELETEs that bloat and generate WAL), smaller per-partition indexes that fit in memory, and per-partition maintenance (VACUUM, REINDEX). Hurts: queries not filtering on the key probe every partition's index; many partitions inflate planning time and lock overhead; unique constraints must include the partition key, so global uniqueness on another column can't be enforced; cross-partition row moves on key updates are expensive. Choose the key from the dominant query filter (almost always time for event data).</div></details>\n</main>",
  "sql_transactions": "<main>\n  <h1>Transactions, Concurrency &amp; Scaling</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-txn\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Transactions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">BEGIN / COMMIT / ROLLBACK, savepoints, autocommit</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-acid\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">ACID &amp; Its Mechanisms</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">WAL, constraints, locks and MVCC</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-anomalies\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Concurrency Anomalies</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Dirty, non-repeatable, phantom, lost update, write skew</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-isolation\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Isolation Levels</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">The table, Postgres vs MySQL InnoDB</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-mvcc\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">MVCC</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Row versions, xmin/xmax, VACUUM</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-locking\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Locking</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">FOR UPDATE, SKIP LOCKED, optimistic locking</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-deadlock\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Deadlocks</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">How they arise, detection, prevention</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-replication\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Replication</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Sync vs async, lag, read-your-writes</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-sharding\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Sharding</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Shard keys, hot spots, cross-shard joins</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-cap\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">CAP, PACELC &amp; NoSQL</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">When MongoDB or DynamoDB instead</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"st-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Timelines, fixes, trade-offs</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>A transaction is all-or-nothing and isolated:</strong> statements between <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code> become visible together or not at all. Without an explicit BEGIN, most clients run in autocommit \u2014 every statement is its own transaction. In Postgres, any error aborts the whole transaction until ROLLBACK (or ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT).</li>\n    <li><strong>ACID is implemented, not magic:</strong> atomicity and durability come from the write-ahead log (WAL / redo log): changes are logged and fsynced at COMMIT before data pages are written, and crash recovery replays or discards. Consistency comes from constraints and your application invariants. Isolation comes from locks and/or MVCC snapshots.</li>\n    <li><strong>Five anomalies to name:</strong> dirty read (see uncommitted data), non-repeatable read (same row, different value on re-read), phantom (same predicate, new rows appear), lost update (read-modify-write overwrites another's write), write skew (two transactions read overlapping data, write different rows, jointly break an invariant).</li>\n    <li><strong>Defaults differ:</strong> Postgres, Oracle and SQL Server default to Read Committed; MySQL InnoDB defaults to Repeatable Read. Postgres's Repeatable Read is snapshot isolation (no phantoms, but write skew is possible); its Serializable (SSI) prevents write skew by aborting one transaction with SQLSTATE 40001 \u2014 the app must retry.</li>\n    <li><strong>MVCC = readers don't block writers:</strong> an UPDATE writes a new row version; readers keep seeing the version visible in their snapshot. Postgres tags versions with <code>xmin</code>/<code>xmax</code> and leaves dead versions in the table until VACUUM; long-running transactions prevent cleanup and cause bloat.</li>\n    <li><strong>Fix lost updates three ways:</strong> atomic SQL (<code>SET balance = balance - 10</code>), pessimistic locking (<code>SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE</code>), or optimistic locking (a <code>version</code> column checked in the UPDATE's WHERE). Job queues use <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> so workers don't block each other.</li>\n    <li><strong>Deadlocks are cycles in the wait-for graph:</strong> the database detects them and kills one transaction. Prevent them by acquiring locks in a consistent order (e.g. by ascending id), keeping transactions short, and retrying on deadlock errors.</li>\n    <li><strong>Scaling trade-offs:</strong> replicas scale reads but async replication lags, breaking read-your-writes; sharding scales writes but makes the shard key decisive \u2014 cross-shard joins and transactions become expensive. CAP says during a network partition you choose consistency or availability; PACELC adds that even without one you trade latency for consistency.</li>\n  </ol>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. TRANSACTIONS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-txn\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>Transactions \u2014 BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT</h2>\n\n  <p>Running schema for this note: <code>accounts(id, owner, balance, version)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)</code>, <code>users(user_id, signup_date, country)</code>, <code>doctors(id, name, on_call)</code>, <code>jobs(id, payload, status, created_at)</code>.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 transaction</span>\n    <p>A sequence of reads and writes treated as one logical unit: it either <strong>commits</strong> (all its effects become durable and visible to others) or <strong>aborts</strong> (none of them ever happened). Other concurrent transactions see it according to the isolation level.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Transfer 100 from account 1 to account 2</span>\n<span class=\"k\">BEGIN</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = balance - <span class=\"y\">100</span> <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = balance + <span class=\"y\">100</span> <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">2</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">COMMIT</span>;    <span class=\"c\">-- both or neither; a crash between the UPDATEs leaves no trace</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Savepoints: partial rollback inside a transaction</span>\n<span class=\"k\">BEGIN</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">INSERT INTO</span> orders (order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)\n  <span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"y\">9001</span>, <span class=\"y\">42</span>, now(), <span class=\"y\">59.90</span>, <span class=\"g\">'pending'</span>);\n<span class=\"k\">SAVEPOINT</span> before_bonus;\n<span class=\"k\">INSERT INTO</span> loyalty_points (user_id, points) <span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"y\">42</span>, <span class=\"y\">60</span>);   <span class=\"c\">-- fails: FK violation</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT</span> before_bonus;   <span class=\"c\">-- undo only the failed part</span>\n<span class=\"k\">COMMIT</span>;                              <span class=\"c\">-- the order is kept</span></div>\n\n  <h3>Autocommit and the \"aborted transaction\" state</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Autocommit</strong> is the default in psql, the MySQL client and JDBC: each statement commits on its own, so a multi-step change without BEGIN is <em>not</em> atomic. Python DB-API drivers (psycopg2/psycopg 3) do the opposite: they open a transaction implicitly and nothing is saved until <code>conn.commit()</code> \u2014 forgetting it silently discards writes.</li>\n    <li><strong>Postgres:</strong> after any error inside a transaction, every following statement fails with \"current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block\" until ROLLBACK. Savepoints are how you recover mid-transaction (ORMs use them for nested \"transactions\").</li>\n    <li><strong>MySQL:</strong> a failed statement is rolled back but the transaction continues; DDL (<code>CREATE/ALTER TABLE</code>) causes an <strong>implicit commit</strong>. Postgres DDL is transactional \u2014 you can roll back an <code>ALTER TABLE</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 long or idle transactions</span>\n    <p>A transaction that stays open (a notebook that ran BEGIN and went to lunch, an app holding a connection across an HTTP call) keeps its locks and, in Postgres, pins its snapshot so VACUUM can't clean any row version newer than it \u2014 across the whole database. Keep transactions short; never wait on user input or network calls inside one. Postgres shows these as <code>idle in transaction</code> in <code>pg_stat_activity</code>; <code>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout</code> kills them.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. ACID \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-acid\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>ACID \u2014 each letter and the mechanism behind it</h2>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Property</th><th>Promise</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Atomicity</strong></td><td>All of a transaction's writes happen, or none do</td><td>WAL + commit record: a transaction is committed iff its commit record reached the log. On crash, recovery redoes logged changes and ignores (Postgres: marks aborted in the commit log, pg_xact) or rolls back (InnoDB: undo log) anything without a commit record</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Consistency</strong></td><td>Each transaction moves the DB from one valid state to another</td><td>Declared constraints (PK, FK, UNIQUE, CHECK, NOT NULL, exclusion), triggers \u2014 plus application logic. Mostly <em>your</em> job; the DB enforces only what you declare</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Isolation</strong></td><td>Concurrent transactions don't see each other's partial work; ideally behave as if run one at a time</td><td>Locks (two-phase locking) and/or MVCC snapshots; level chosen per transaction</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Durability</strong></td><td>Once COMMIT returns, the data survives crashes</td><td>WAL flushed (fsync) to stable storage before COMMIT returns; data pages written lazily later by checkpoints. Replication extends it to survive losing the machine</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Write-ahead logging in one picture</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">UPDATE accounts ...      \u2192 modify page in shared buffers (memory), mark dirty\n                         \u2192 append redo record to WAL buffer\nCOMMIT                   \u2192 append commit record, fsync WAL to disk   \u2190 COMMIT returns here\n... later ...            \u2192 checkpoint writes dirty data pages to the table files\n\nCrash after COMMIT, before checkpoint?  Recovery replays WAL from last checkpoint \u2192 change restored.\nCrash before COMMIT?                    No commit record \u2192 transaction treated as aborted.</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why log first?</span>\n    <p>Writing the scattered data pages for a transaction would mean many random writes; appending to the log is one sequential write. So the log is the source of truth and the tables are a cache of it that can be rebuilt. This same log is what streaming replication ships to replicas and what CDC tools (Debezium) read to stream changes to Kafka or a warehouse.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"C\" in ACID is not \"C\" in CAP</span>\n    <p>ACID consistency means \"invariants hold\". CAP consistency means linearizability \u2014 every read sees the latest write across replicas. Different concepts sharing a letter; interviewers like to check you know the difference. Also: <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> in Postgres trades durability of the last few hundred ms of commits for latency (no corruption, just possible loss of recent commits on crash).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. ANOMALIES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-anomalies\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>Concurrency anomalies \u2014 with two-session timelines</h2>\n\n  <p>Each timeline below shows two sessions, A and B, executing interleaved; time flows downward. Account 1 starts with balance 100.</p>\n\n  <h3>Dirty read \u2014 reading uncommitted data</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">   Session A                                      Session B\n   BEGIN;\n   UPDATE accounts SET balance = 0 WHERE id = 1;\n                                                  BEGIN;\n                                                  SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;\n                                                  \u2192 0   (dirty! A hasn't committed)\n   ROLLBACK;\n                                                  -- B acted on a value that never existed</div>\n  <p>Only possible at Read Uncommitted \u2014 and Postgres doesn't implement that (it silently gives you Read Committed).</p>\n\n  <h3>Non-repeatable read \u2014 same row, different answer</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">   Session A                                      Session B\n   BEGIN;\n   SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;  \u2192 100\n                                                  UPDATE accounts SET balance = 50 WHERE id = 1;\n                                                  -- (autocommit: committed)\n   SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1;  \u2192 50   (changed inside my transaction)\n   COMMIT;</div>\n  <p>Allowed at Read Committed: each <em>statement</em> takes a fresh snapshot. Report queries that compute a total and then the details in separate statements can disagree with themselves.</p>\n\n  <h3>Phantom read \u2014 same predicate, new rows</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">   Session A                                      Session B\n   BEGIN;\n   SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42;  \u2192 3\n                                                  INSERT INTO orders (order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)\n                                                    VALUES (9002, 42, now(), 10, 'paid');   -- committed\n   SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42;  \u2192 4   (a phantom row appeared)\n   COMMIT;</div>\n  <p>Different from non-repeatable read: no existing row changed; the <em>set</em> matching the predicate changed. Row locks can't prevent it (the new row didn't exist to lock) \u2014 you need predicate/gap locks or a snapshot.</p>\n\n  <h3>Lost update \u2014 read-modify-write race</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">   Session A                                      Session B\n   BEGIN;                                         BEGIN;\n   SELECT balance ... WHERE id = 1;  \u2192 100\n                                                  SELECT balance ... WHERE id = 1;  \u2192 100\n   -- app computes 100 + 50\n   UPDATE accounts SET balance = 150 WHERE id = 1;\n   COMMIT;\n                                                  -- app computes 100 - 30\n                                                  UPDATE accounts SET balance = 70 WHERE id = 1;\n                                                  COMMIT;\n   Final: 70.  Expected: 120.  A's deposit is lost.</div>\n  <p>Happens at Read Committed in every engine, and at InnoDB's Repeatable Read too (its UPDATE reads the latest committed row and doesn't complain). Postgres Repeatable Read catches it: B's UPDATE fails with <em>could not serialize access due to concurrent update</em>.</p>\n\n  <h3>Write skew \u2014 the subtle one</h3>\n  <p>Invariant: at least one doctor must be on call. Alice and Bob are both on call.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\">   Session A (Alice)                              Session B (Bob)\n   BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;         BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;\n   SELECT COUNT(*) FROM doctors WHERE on_call;  \u2192 2\n                                                  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM doctors WHERE on_call;  \u2192 2\n   -- 2 &gt; 1, safe for me to leave\n   UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false\n     WHERE name = 'Alice';\n                                                  -- 2 &gt; 1, safe for me to leave\n                                                  UPDATE doctors SET on_call = false\n                                                    WHERE name = 'Bob';\n   COMMIT;                                        COMMIT;\n   Result: zero doctors on call. Invariant broken.</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why snapshots don't catch write skew</span>\n    <p>Both transactions read the same consistent snapshot and each wrote a <em>different</em> row, so there's no write-write conflict for snapshot isolation to detect. The bug lives in the read: each decision was based on data the other transaction invalidated. Fixes: run at Serializable (Postgres SSI tracks read-write dependencies and aborts one), or materialise the conflict by locking the rows you read: <code>SELECT \u2026 FROM doctors WHERE on_call FOR UPDATE</code>. Real cases: double-booking a meeting room, two withdrawals from accounts that share a combined minimum balance, a username claimed twice when uniqueness is checked in code rather than with a UNIQUE constraint.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 two flatmates and one shared shopping list</span>\n    <p>Both glance at the fridge (the snapshot), both see two cartons of milk, both independently decide \"we have plenty, I'll use one for baking.\" Neither touched the other's carton, so nobody \"conflicted\" \u2014 yet the fridge ends up empty. The problem wasn't simultaneous writes to the same thing; it was decisions based on a read that became stale. Locking the fridge while you decide (FOR UPDATE) or a referee who checks \"did someone change what you looked at?\" (SSI) prevents it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. ISOLATION LEVELS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-isolation\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>Isolation levels</h2>\n\n  <p>The SQL standard defines four levels by which anomalies they forbid. Real engines implement them differently \u2014 the table below shows what <em>actually</em> happens.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Level</th><th>Dirty read</th><th>Non-repeatable</th><th>Phantom</th><th>Lost update</th><th>Write skew</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Read Uncommitted (standard)</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Read Committed (standard)</td><td>No</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td><td>Possible</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Repeatable Read (standard)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Possible</td><td>No</td><td>Possible</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Serializable (standard)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Postgres RR</strong> (= snapshot isolation)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No (aborts with 40001)</td><td><strong>Possible</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Postgres Serializable</strong> (SSI)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No (aborts with 40001)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>InnoDB RR</strong> (default)</td><td>No</td><td>No (plain reads)</td><td>No for plain reads (snapshot); locking reads use next-key locks</td><td><strong>Possible</strong> with plain SELECT then UPDATE</td><td>Possible</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>InnoDB Serializable</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No (plain SELECTs become <code>FOR SHARE</code> locking reads when autocommit is off)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Engine defaults and quirks</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Default</th><th>Notable behaviour</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Read Committed</td><td>Read Uncommitted = Read Committed. RR is a transaction-wide snapshot taken at the first statement. Serializable uses SSI (since 9.1): no extra blocking, but some transactions abort and must be retried</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>MySQL InnoDB</td><td>Repeatable Read</td><td>Consistent (plain) reads use a snapshot from the first read; <em>locking</em> reads and UPDATE/DELETE read the latest committed version and take next-key (record + gap) locks \u2014 so within one transaction a SELECT and an UPDATE can see different data</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>Read Committed (locking)</td><td>Readers take shared locks and block on writers unless <code>READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT</code> is on (default in Azure SQL). Has a separate SNAPSHOT level</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Oracle</td><td>Read Committed</td><td>Its \"SERIALIZABLE\" is really snapshot isolation (write skew possible)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Set per transaction (Postgres)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- ... reads and writes ...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">COMMIT</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- may fail: ERROR: could not serialize access ... (SQLSTATE 40001)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- MySQL: applies to the NEXT transaction</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL</span> <span class=\"k\">READ COMMITTED</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">START TRANSACTION</span>;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 Serializable needs a retry loop</span>\n    <p>Higher isolation in MVCC engines doesn't make transactions wait \u2014 it makes some <em>fail</em>. Any code that runs at Postgres RR or Serializable must catch SQLSTATE <code>40001</code> (serialization_failure) and <code>40P01</code> (deadlock) and rerun the <em>whole</em> transaction, including its reads. Without a retry loop, raising the isolation level just converts silent data bugs into user-visible errors.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. MVCC \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-mvcc\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>MVCC \u2014 multi-version concurrency control</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 MVCC</span>\n    <p>Instead of overwriting a row in place and making readers wait, the database keeps multiple <strong>versions</strong> of the row. Each transaction reads from a <strong>snapshot</strong> \u2014 the set of transactions committed as of a point in time \u2014 and sees, for each row, the newest version committed in that snapshot. Writers create new versions; readers read old ones. Result: <strong>readers never block writers and writers never block readers</strong>. Writers still block writers on the same row.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Postgres: xmin and xmax</h3>\n  <p>Every tuple carries hidden system columns: <code>xmin</code> = ID of the transaction that created this version, <code>xmax</code> = ID of the transaction that deleted or superseded it (0 if live). An UPDATE is an insert of a new version plus setting <code>xmax</code> on the old one.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> xmin, xmax, id, balance <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">--  xmin | xmax | id | balance</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   700 |    0 |  1 |    100</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Transaction 812 runs: UPDATE accounts SET balance = 80 WHERE id = 1;  (not yet committed)</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- On disk now:</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   version 1: xmin=700 xmax=812 balance=100   \u2190 still visible to everyone else</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   version 2: xmin=812 xmax=0   balance=80    \u2190 visible only to txn 812 until it commits</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Visibility rule (simplified): a version is visible to my snapshot if</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   xmin is committed and in my snapshot, AND</span>\n<span class=\"c\">--   xmax is 0, or aborted, or not yet committed as of my snapshot.</span></div>\n\n  <h3>VACUUM \u2014 garbage collection for row versions</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Once no running snapshot can see an old version (it's \"dead\"), <strong>VACUUM</strong> marks its space reusable, removes index entries pointing to it, and updates the visibility map (enabling index-only scans). <strong>Autovacuum</strong> does this in the background.</li>\n    <li><strong>Bloat:</strong> heavy-update tables accumulate dead tuples faster than vacuum reclaims them; table and indexes grow. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites the table compactly but takes an exclusive lock (tools like pg_repack avoid that).</li>\n    <li><strong>The oldest snapshot rules:</strong> a single long-running transaction or an abandoned replication slot holds back cleanup database-wide.</li>\n    <li><strong>XID wraparound:</strong> transaction IDs are 32-bit; vacuum must \"freeze\" old tuples so IDs can be reused. If it can't keep up, Postgres eventually refuses new writes to protect data \u2014 a real production outage pattern.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>PostgreSQL</th><th>MySQL InnoDB / Oracle</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Where old versions live</td><td>In the table itself (new tuple per update)</td><td>In the undo log/segment; the table holds the newest version</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cleanup</td><td>VACUUM / autovacuum</td><td>Purge thread (InnoDB) discards undo no longer needed</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cost of long transactions</td><td>Table/index bloat</td><td>Undo log growth (\"history list length\"), slower reads reconstructing old versions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Rollback cost</td><td>Cheap (just mark aborted)</td><td>Expensive (apply undo)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 a wiki with page history</span>\n    <p>Editing a wiki page doesn't erase it; it saves a new revision. Someone who opened the page a minute ago keeps reading their revision undisturbed while you edit. Two people editing at once still conflict (writer vs writer). Periodically an admin deletes revisions nobody can possibly be looking at \u2014 that's VACUUM. If one reader leaves a tab open for a week, no revision newer than their view can be cleaned up.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. LOCKING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-locking\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>Locking \u2014 pessimistic and optimistic</h2>\n\n  <h3>Lock granularity and modes</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Shared (S) lock</td><td>\"I'm reading this, don't change it.\" Many holders allowed; conflicts with exclusive</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Exclusive (X) lock</td><td>\"I'm changing this.\" One holder; conflicts with everything that isn't plain MVCC reading</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Row-level locks</td><td>Taken by UPDATE/DELETE and <code>SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE / FOR NO KEY UPDATE / FOR SHARE / FOR KEY SHARE</code>. Postgres stores them in the tuple header (no lock-table memory limit); InnoDB locks index records</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Table-level locks</td><td>Every statement takes one. SELECT takes ACCESS SHARE; INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE take ROW EXCLUSIVE (compatible with each other); most <code>ALTER TABLE</code> forms take ACCESS EXCLUSIVE, which blocks even SELECTs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Gap / next-key locks (InnoDB)</td><td>Lock the range between index records to stop phantom inserts under RR</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Advisory locks (Postgres)</td><td><code>pg_advisory_xact_lock(key)</code>: app-defined mutex on an integer, e.g. \"only one cron run at a time\"</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>Locks are held until the transaction ends (strict two-phase locking): you can't release a row lock early.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 the migration that took the site down</span>\n    <p><code>ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN \u2026</code> needs ACCESS EXCLUSIVE. If one long-running SELECT holds ACCESS SHARE, the ALTER waits \u2014 and every new query on <code>orders</code> queues behind the waiting ALTER. A 1 ms metadata change becomes a full outage. Always run DDL with <code>SET lock_timeout = '3s'</code> and retry.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Pessimistic: SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">BEGIN</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> balance <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">FOR UPDATE</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- row locked; others' FOR UPDATE/UPDATE wait</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- app checks balance &gt;= 30</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = balance - <span class=\"y\">30</span> <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">COMMIT</span>;                                                  <span class=\"c\">-- lock released</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Even simpler when the logic fits in SQL: one atomic statement, no race</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = balance - <span class=\"y\">30</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> balance &gt;= <span class=\"y\">30</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- 0 rows updated = insufficient funds</span></div>\n  <p>In Postgres Read Committed, a blocked UPDATE re-checks its WHERE against the newly committed row version after the lock is released (\"EvalPlanQual\"), which is why the atomic form is safe at the default level.</p>\n\n  <h3>Job queues: FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Each worker claims one pending job; workers never wait on each other's rows</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WITH</span> next_job <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n  <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> jobs\n  <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'pending'</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> created_at\n  <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> jobs j <span class=\"k\">SET</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'running'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> next_job\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> j.id = next_job.id\n<span class=\"k\">RETURNING</span> j.id, j.payload;</div>\n  <p>Without SKIP LOCKED, all workers pick the same oldest job and queue behind one lock; with it, each worker skips rows already claimed. (Postgres 9.5+, MySQL 8.0+.) <code>NOWAIT</code> is the sibling that errors immediately instead of waiting. A partial index <code>ON jobs (created_at) WHERE status = 'pending'</code> keeps the claim query fast.</p>\n\n  <h3>Optimistic locking with a version column</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- 1. Read without locking (can be in a separate request, e.g. an edit form)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, balance, version <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;    <span class=\"c\">-- balance=100, version=7</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- 2. Write only if nobody changed it since</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts\n<span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = <span class=\"y\">150</span>, version = version + <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> version = <span class=\"y\">7</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- 1 row updated \u2192 success.  0 rows \u2192 conflict: re-read, re-apply, or tell the user.</span></div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>Pessimistic (FOR UPDATE)</th><th>Optimistic (version check)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Assumes</td><td>Conflicts are likely</td><td>Conflicts are rare</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Cost on conflict</td><td>Waiting (and deadlock risk)</td><td>Retry work</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Holds locks across user think-time?</td><td>Would have to \u2014 unacceptable</td><td>No \u2014 works across HTTP requests</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Inventory decrement, money movement, queue claims</td><td>Editing a profile/document, ORMs (JPA <code>@Version</code>, Rails <code>lock_version</code>)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. DEADLOCKS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-deadlock\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>Deadlocks</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 deadlock</span>\n    <p>Two or more transactions each hold a lock the other needs, forming a cycle in the <strong>wait-for graph</strong>. None can proceed, ever, without outside intervention.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">   Session A                                      Session B\n   BEGIN;                                         BEGIN;\n   UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10\n     WHERE id = 1;          -- locks row 1\n                                                  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10\n                                                    WHERE id = 2;          -- locks row 2\n   UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10\n     WHERE id = 2;          -- waits for B\n                                                  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10\n                                                    WHERE id = 1;          -- waits for A \u2192 cycle\n   ERROR: deadlock detected (SQLSTATE 40P01)     -- one of them is chosen as victim and rolled back</div>\n\n  <h3>Detection</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Postgres:</strong> a waiting backend sleeps for <code>deadlock_timeout</code> (default 1 s), then builds the wait-for graph; if it finds a cycle it aborts itself. Log line shows both queries.</li>\n    <li><strong>InnoDB:</strong> checks immediately on each lock wait and rolls back the transaction that modified the fewest rows; <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code> shows the latest deadlock. <code>innodb_lock_wait_timeout</code> (50 s) separately caps plain waits.</li>\n    <li><strong>SQL Server:</strong> background monitor picks a victim by <code>DEADLOCK_PRIORITY</code> and rollback cost (error 1205).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>Prevention</h3>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Consistent lock ordering:</strong> always lock rows in the same global order. For a transfer, update the lower id first regardless of direction: <code>SELECT \u2026 FROM accounts WHERE id IN (1, 2) ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE</code>. No cycle can form if everyone acquires in ascending order.</li>\n    <li><strong>Keep transactions short</strong> and touch as few rows as possible; do slow computation before BEGIN.</li>\n    <li><strong>Lock up front:</strong> take all locks you'll need at the start (FOR UPDATE on everything) rather than upgrading later. Upgrading a shared lock to exclusive is a classic deadlock source.</li>\n    <li><strong>Index foreign keys and the WHERE columns of updates</strong> (especially in InnoDB, where an unindexed UPDATE locks every row/gap it scans).</li>\n    <li><strong>Retry:</strong> deadlocks can't be eliminated in general; treat 40P01 like 40001 and retry with backoff.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. REPLICATION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-replication\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>Replication</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 primary\u2013replica replication</span>\n    <p>One <strong>primary</strong> accepts writes and streams its change log to one or more <strong>replicas</strong> (standbys), which replay it and serve reads. Postgres streaming replication ships WAL (physical: byte-identical copy); logical replication ships row changes per table (can feed different versions/schemas). MySQL ships the binlog (row-based by default).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>Asynchronous</th><th>Synchronous / semi-synchronous</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>COMMIT returns when</td><td>Primary's WAL is flushed locally</td><td>At least one replica confirms receipt/flush (Postgres <code>synchronous_standby_names</code>; MySQL semi-sync)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Write latency</td><td>Lowest</td><td>+ one network round trip</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Data loss on primary failure</td><td>Commits not yet shipped are lost on failover</td><td>None (RPO = 0) for acknowledged commits</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Availability risk</td><td>None from replicas</td><td>If the sync replica dies, writes stall unless another can take over (use \"ANY 1 (r1, r2)\")</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Replica freshness</td><td>Lags by ms to minutes</td><td>Received \u2260 applied: reads can still be stale unless <code>synchronous_commit = remote_apply</code></td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Replication lag and its user-visible bugs</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">  t=0   user updates profile name \u2192 write goes to PRIMARY, commits\n  t=5ms page reloads \u2192 read routed to REPLICA (still 200 ms behind)\n        \u2192 shows OLD name.  \"My save didn't work!\"  (read-your-writes violated)\n\n  Also: refresh twice, hit two replicas with different lag \u2192 value goes new \u2192 old\n        (monotonic reads violated)</div>\n\n  <h3>Fixes for read-your-writes</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Read from primary after a write:</strong> for N seconds after a user writes, or always for that user's own data (profile, settings).</li>\n    <li><strong>Track positions:</strong> remember the primary's WAL LSN at commit (<code>pg_current_wal_lsn()</code>) in the session; only read from a replica whose <code>pg_last_wal_replay_lsn()</code> has caught up, otherwise wait or go to primary.</li>\n    <li><strong>Sticky sessions:</strong> pin a user to one replica \u2014 gives monotonic reads (time never goes backwards), though not read-your-writes alone.</li>\n    <li><strong>Monitor lag:</strong> <code>pg_stat_replication</code> (write/flush/replay lag) on the primary; in MySQL, <code>Seconds_Behind_Source</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 replicas are not backups, and long queries on replicas</span>\n    <p>A replica faithfully replays your accidental <code>DROP TABLE</code> within milliseconds; you still need point-in-time recovery (base backup + archived WAL). And a long analytics query on a Postgres hot standby conflicts with VACUUM cleanup arriving from the primary: either the query gets cancelled (\"canceling statement due to conflict with recovery\") or, with <code>hot_standby_feedback = on</code>, the primary keeps the dead rows and bloats. Heavy analytics belongs in a warehouse fed by CDC, not on the OLTP replica.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. SHARDING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-sharding\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>Sharding and partitioning for scale</h2>\n\n  <p>Replicas scale <em>reads</em>; every write still lands on one primary. When write throughput or data size outgrows one machine (after vertical scaling, connection pooling, caching and archival are exhausted), you <strong>shard</strong>: split rows across independent databases, each owning a subset.</p>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Approach</th><th>How rows map to shards</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Hash sharding</td><td><code>hash(user_id) mod N</code>, or consistent hashing / fixed virtual buckets mapped to nodes</td><td>Even spread, no hot ranges</td><td>Range queries hit every shard; naive mod-N reshuffles almost everything when N changes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Range sharding</td><td>user_id 1\u20131M \u2192 shard 1, \u2026; or by date</td><td>Efficient range scans; easy to split a range</td><td>Monotonic keys (time, auto-increment) send all new writes to the last shard</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Directory / lookup</td><td>A mapping table: tenant \u2192 shard</td><td>Flexible; move a big tenant on its own</td><td>Extra lookup and a critical metadata service</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Geo / tenant</td><td>By region or customer</td><td>Data residency, isolation</td><td>Uneven sizes</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Choosing the shard key</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>High cardinality and even distribution</strong> \u2014 <code>country</code> is a terrible key (a few countries hold most users).</li>\n    <li><strong>Matches the dominant access path</strong> \u2014 most queries should include it so they route to one shard. For a consumer app, <code>user_id</code>: a user's orders, events and settings all co-locate.</li>\n    <li><strong>Co-location:</strong> shard child tables by the same key (orders by <code>user_id</code>, not <code>order_id</code>) so joins for one user stay local. Small reference tables (countries, products) are replicated to every shard.</li>\n    <li><strong>Hot spots:</strong> a celebrity account or one huge tenant overloads its shard regardless of hashing. Mitigations: split hot keys with a suffix (<code>user_id#0..9</code>) and fan-in on read, give big tenants dedicated shards, cache hot reads.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3>What you give up</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Single-node feature</th><th>After sharding</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>JOIN across any rows</td><td>Cross-shard joins = scatter-gather in the app or a coordinator (Citus, Vitess); slow and costly. Denormalise or co-locate instead</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ACID transaction across rows</td><td>Cross-shard needs two-phase commit (blocking if the coordinator dies) or sagas with compensating actions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Global UNIQUE / auto-increment</td><td>Needs a global ID scheme: UUIDv7, Snowflake-style IDs (timestamp + machine + sequence)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aggregates (<code>COUNT(*)</code> over everything)</td><td>Fan out to all shards, merge; push analytics to a warehouse</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Rebalancing</td><td>Moving data while live; easier with many small virtual shards mapped onto fewer physical nodes</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 partitioning vs sharding</span>\n    <p><strong>Partitioning</strong> (Postgres declarative partitioning) splits a table into pieces on <em>one</em> server: it helps pruning and maintenance, but not CPU/disk/write limits of that server. <strong>Sharding</strong> is horizontal partitioning across <em>servers</em>: it raises total capacity, at the cost of everything in the table above. Systems like Citus (Postgres), Vitess (MySQL), and distributed SQL (CockroachDB, Spanner, YugabyteDB) automate sharding and even provide distributed transactions \u2014 with higher per-transaction latency.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. CAP / NOSQL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-cap\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>CAP, PACELC and SQL vs NoSQL</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 CAP and PACELC</span>\n    <p><strong>CAP:</strong> in a replicated system, when a network <strong>P</strong>artition separates nodes, you must choose between <strong>C</strong>onsistency (linearizable: every read sees the latest write, so the minority side refuses requests) and <strong>A</strong>vailability (every node keeps answering, possibly with stale data). Partitions aren't optional, so it's really \"CP or AP during a partition\".<br><br>\n    <strong>PACELC:</strong> if <strong>P</strong>artitioned, choose <strong>A</strong> or <strong>C</strong>; <strong>E</strong>lse (normal operation), choose <strong>L</strong>atency or <strong>C</strong>onsistency \u2014 because synchronous replication for consistency costs round trips even when nothing is broken. DynamoDB/Cassandra defaults lean PA/EL; Spanner and single-primary SQL with sync replication lean PC/EC.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th></th><th>Relational (Postgres, MySQL)</th><th>Document (MongoDB)</th><th>Key-value / wide-column (DynamoDB, Cassandra)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Data model</td><td>Normalised tables, joins, schema enforced</td><td>Nested JSON documents, flexible schema</td><td>Items accessed by partition key (+ sort key)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Queries</td><td>Arbitrary ad-hoc SQL</td><td>Rich queries and aggregation pipeline within a collection; <code>$lookup</code> joins are limited</td><td>Must design tables around known access patterns; scans are expensive; no joins</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Transactions</td><td>Full ACID, multi-row</td><td>Single-document atomic; multi-document ACID since 4.0 (with overhead)</td><td>Single-item atomic; DynamoDB TransactWriteItems up to 100 items</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Strong on primary</td><td>Tunable (read/write concern)</td><td>DynamoDB: eventually consistent reads by default, strongly consistent reads optional (not on global secondary indexes)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Scaling writes</td><td>Vertical, then manual sharding</td><td>Built-in sharding</td><td>Built-in, effectively unlimited with a good partition key</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>When to choose NoSQL instead</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>DynamoDB:</strong> massive, predictable key-based access (sessions, carts, user feeds, IoT ingest) needing single-digit-ms latency at any scale, serverless operations, and access patterns known up front.</li>\n    <li><strong>MongoDB:</strong> data naturally shaped as self-contained documents read/written as a unit (product catalogue with varying attributes, CMS content), fast-evolving schemas.</li>\n    <li><strong>Cassandra:</strong> write-heavy time series across regions with tolerance for eventual consistency.</li>\n    <li><strong>Stay relational</strong> when you need ad-hoc analytical queries, multi-entity transactions (money, inventory), strong integrity constraints, or you don't yet know your access patterns \u2014 and remember Postgres <code>jsonb</code> covers many \"we need flexible schema\" cases.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"NoSQL scales, SQL doesn't\"</span>\n    <p>A single well-tuned Postgres/MySQL instance with read replicas handles far more than most products ever need, and distributed SQL exists. The real trade is <em>query flexibility and integrity</em> vs <em>predictable horizontal scale for known access patterns</em>. In interviews, justify the choice from access patterns, consistency needs and scale numbers \u2014 not from fashion.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"st-qa\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Explain ACID and the mechanism behind each property.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><strong>Atomicity:</strong> all-or-nothing \u2014 implemented by the write-ahead log plus a commit record; crash recovery treats transactions without a commit record as aborted (Postgres marks them aborted; InnoDB applies undo). <strong>Consistency:</strong> invariants hold \u2014 enforced by declared constraints (PK, FK, UNIQUE, CHECK) and application logic. <strong>Isolation:</strong> concurrent transactions don't interfere beyond what the isolation level permits \u2014 implemented with locks and/or MVCC snapshots. <strong>Durability:</strong> committed data survives a crash \u2014 WAL is fsynced before COMMIT returns; data pages are flushed later at checkpoints; replication protects against machine loss.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What's the difference between a non-repeatable read and a phantom read?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">A non-repeatable read is an <em>existing row</em> changing value between two reads in the same transaction (someone UPDATEd and committed it). A phantom is the <em>set of rows matching a predicate</em> changing \u2014 new rows inserted (or deleted) that satisfy your WHERE. Row locks on what you read prevent the first but not the second, because the phantom row didn't exist to be locked; preventing phantoms needs predicate/gap (next-key) locks or a transaction-wide snapshot. Postgres Repeatable Read prevents both via the snapshot; the SQL standard only requires RR to prevent non-repeatable reads.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Two users click \"Buy\" on the last item at the same time and both succeed. Fix it.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">It's a lost update / check-then-act race: both read <code>stock = 1</code>, both decrement. Simplest fix \u2014 make the check and the write one atomic statement:<div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> products <span class=\"k\">SET</span> stock = stock - <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> product_id = :pid <span class=\"k\">AND</span> stock &gt;= <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"k\">RETURNING</span> stock;   <span class=\"c\">-- 0 rows \u2192 sold out</span></div>The second UPDATE blocks on the row lock, then re-evaluates <code>stock &gt;= 1</code> on the committed version (0) and updates nothing. Add <code>CHECK (stock &gt;= 0)</code> as a safety net. Alternatives: <code>SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE</code> then decide in the app, or optimistic locking with a version column. Insert the order row in the same transaction as the decrement.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What is write skew, and which isolation levels prevent it?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Two transactions read an overlapping set of rows, each makes a decision based on that read, and each writes a <em>different</em> row \u2014 so there is no write-write conflict \u2014 but together they violate an invariant (classic: two on-call doctors both go off call because each saw the other still on call). Snapshot isolation (Postgres RR, Oracle \"Serializable\", SQL Server SNAPSHOT) allows it. True Serializable prevents it: Postgres SSI detects the read-write dependency cycle and aborts one with 40001; InnoDB Serializable turns plain reads into shared locks so the second writer blocks/deadlocks. At lower levels, fix it by locking what you read (<code>SELECT \u2026 FOR UPDATE</code>) or by expressing the invariant as a constraint.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>How do Postgres and MySQL InnoDB differ at Repeatable Read?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Postgres RR is snapshot isolation: every statement in the transaction sees the snapshot taken at its first statement, including UPDATEs; if you try to update a row someone else changed after your snapshot, you get a serialization error (so lost updates are prevented, phantoms don't appear, write skew is possible). InnoDB RR (the MySQL default) gives plain SELECTs a consistent snapshot, but locking reads and UPDATE/DELETE operate on the <em>latest committed</em> data and use next-key locks \u2014 no serialization error is raised, so a read-then-write lost update can happen and a SELECT can disagree with an UPDATE in the same transaction. Postgres's default is Read Committed; MySQL's is Repeatable Read.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Explain MVCC. Why does a long-running transaction hurt a Postgres database?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">MVCC keeps multiple versions of each row; an UPDATE writes a new version (tagged <code>xmin</code> = creating txn) and stamps the old one with <code>xmax</code>. Each transaction reads with a snapshot and sees only versions committed in it, so readers and writers don't block each other. Old versions stay in the table until VACUUM removes those no snapshot can see. A long-running (or idle-in-transaction) session holds the oldest snapshot, so VACUUM cannot remove <em>any</em> dead version newer than it anywhere in the database \u2014 tables and indexes bloat, index-only scans degrade (visibility map not set), and in extreme cases transaction-ID freezing falls behind toward wraparound. It also holds its locks the whole time.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Write a query for multiple workers to safely pull jobs from a table without double-processing.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">BEGIN</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> id, payload <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> jobs\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'pending'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> created_at\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">10</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- process the batch, then:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> jobs <span class=\"k\">SET</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'done'</span> <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"k\">ANY</span>(:claimed_ids);\n<span class=\"k\">COMMIT</span>;</div>FOR UPDATE locks the claimed rows; SKIP LOCKED makes other workers silently skip them instead of waiting, so each worker gets a disjoint batch. If a worker crashes, its transaction rolls back and the jobs become claimable again. For long processing, don't hold the transaction open: instead atomically flip <code>status = 'running'</code> with a <code>locked_until</code> timestamp (UPDATE \u2026 FROM the SKIP LOCKED CTE \u2026 RETURNING), commit, and have a reaper reset expired leases. Index: <code>CREATE INDEX ON jobs (created_at) WHERE status = 'pending'</code>.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>How do deadlocks happen, and how do you prevent them in a money-transfer service?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Transfer A\u2192B locks A then B; a concurrent transfer B\u2192A locks B then A. Each holds one row and waits for the other: a cycle. The database detects the cycle (Postgres after <code>deadlock_timeout</code>, InnoDB immediately) and aborts one with a deadlock error. Prevention: acquire locks in a canonical order regardless of transfer direction \u2014 <code>SELECT id FROM accounts WHERE id IN (:a, :b) ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE</code> \u2014 then do both UPDATEs. Keep the transaction short, avoid lock upgrades, and still wrap it in a retry loop for 40P01/40001 since some deadlocks (e.g. via FK checks or index gaps) are hard to rule out entirely.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>A user updates their profile, refreshes, and sees the old value. What happened and how do you fix it?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">The write went to the primary but the read was served by an asynchronous replica that hadn't yet replayed the change \u2014 replication lag violating read-your-writes consistency. Fixes: route a user's reads of their own recently-written data to the primary (e.g. for a few seconds after any write, tracked in the session or a cookie); or record the commit LSN and only read from a replica that has replayed past it; or use <code>synchronous_commit = remote_apply</code> for that write (costly). Sticky routing to one replica additionally guarantees monotonic reads. Also monitor lag and alert when it exceeds the window you assume.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>You need to shard an orders database. What shard key do you choose and what breaks?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Typically <code>user_id</code> (or tenant_id in B2B): high cardinality, present in most queries (\"my orders\"), and it co-locates a user's orders, payments and addresses on one shard so those joins and transactions stay single-shard. Not <code>order_date</code> (all new writes hit one shard) and not <code>country</code> (skewed). What breaks: queries by other keys (look up an order by order_id \u2192 embed the shard in the ID or keep a lookup table/global secondary index); cross-user analytics (scatter-gather, or better, CDC into a warehouse); global uniqueness and sequences (use UUIDv7/Snowflake IDs); multi-shard transactions (2PC or sagas); hot users (dedicated shard or key splitting); and rebalancing (pre-split into many virtual shards).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Explain CAP and PACELC. Where do Postgres and DynamoDB sit?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">CAP: when a network partition splits a replicated system, each side must either refuse requests to stay consistent (CP) or keep serving possibly-stale data (AP). PACELC adds the normal case: even without partitions, stronger consistency costs latency (waiting for replicas). A single-primary Postgres with async replicas: writes are CP-ish (only the primary accepts them; if it's unreachable, writes stop until failover), reads from replicas are eventually consistent \u2014 PC/EL-ish if you read replicas, EC if you read the primary or use synchronous replication. DynamoDB: by default PA/EL \u2014 eventually consistent reads, highly available; you can request strongly consistent reads (EC) at higher cost/latency, and global tables use last-writer-wins across regions.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>When would you pick DynamoDB or MongoDB over Postgres for a new service?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">DynamoDB: the access patterns are few, known and key-based (get session by id, list a user's items by time), scale is very high or spiky, you want serverless operation with predictable single-digit-ms latency, and you can live without ad-hoc joins and with limited transactions \u2014 model the table around queries (single-table design, partition + sort keys, GSIs). MongoDB: entities are naturally self-contained documents with variable structure that are read and written whole. Postgres when: relationships and joins matter, you need multi-row ACID (payments, inventory), strong constraints, ad-hoc analytics, or the access patterns are still evolving. Many teams start on Postgres (with <code>jsonb</code> for flexible fields) and move specific hot paths to a key-value store once the patterns are proven.</div></details>\n</main>",
  "sql_modeling": "<main>\n  <h1>Data Modeling &amp; Normalization</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-keys\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Keys</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Primary, candidate, surrogate vs natural, FKs &amp; referential actions</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-relationships\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Relationships &amp; ER Thinking</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">1:1, 1:N, M:N via junction tables</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-fd\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Functional Dependencies</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">The logic underneath every normal form</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-anomalies\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Anomalies</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Insert, update, delete \u2014 what redundancy breaks</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-normal-forms\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">1NF \u2192 2NF \u2192 3NF \u2192 BCNF</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Each with a before/after table</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-denorm\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Denormalization</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">When breaking the rules is the right call</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-oltp-olap\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">OLTP vs OLAP</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Two workloads, two modeling philosophies</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-dimensional\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Dimensional Modeling</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Facts, dimensions, grain, star vs snowflake, additivity</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-scd\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Slowly Changing Dimensions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Type 1/2/3 with Type 2 SQL</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-modern\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Modern Warehouse Patterns</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">One Big Table, medallion, dbt</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-integrity\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Constraints, Integrity &amp; Views</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Let the database enforce the rules</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-design\"><span class=\"rm-num\">12</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Schema Design Interview</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Ride-sharing walk-through, end to end</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"sm-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">13</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Say it out loud, then reveal</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Normalization is dependency bookkeeping:</strong> every normal form is a rule about functional dependencies (X \u2192 Y). 2NF removes dependencies on <em>part</em> of a composite key, 3NF removes dependencies between non-key columns, BCNF demands every determinant be a superkey. Phrase it as \"each fact is stored exactly once, keyed by the thing it is a fact about\" and you can derive all of them.</li>\n    <li><strong>Why normalize \u2014 the three anomalies:</strong> redundancy means one real-world fact lives in many rows. Update anomaly: you change some copies and not others. Insert anomaly: you cannot record a fact (a new product) without an unrelated one (an order). Delete anomaly: deleting the last order silently deletes the only record of the product.</li>\n    <li><strong>Surrogate vs natural keys:</strong> natural keys (email, SSN, ISBN) carry business meaning and change; surrogate keys (identity/UUID) are meaningless and stable. Standard practice: surrogate primary key for joins + a UNIQUE constraint on the natural key so duplicates are still impossible. In warehouses, surrogate keys on dimensions are mandatory for SCD Type 2.</li>\n    <li><strong>M:N always becomes a junction table:</strong> students\u2194courses becomes <code>enrollments(student_id, course_id)</code> with a composite PK and two FKs. Attributes of the relationship itself (grade, enrolled_at) live on the junction table, not on either side.</li>\n    <li><strong>OLTP normalizes, OLAP denormalizes:</strong> OLTP optimizes many small concurrent writes where integrity matters (3NF). OLAP optimizes few huge scans over columnar storage where joins and repeated reads are the cost (star schema, wide tables). Same data, opposite trade-off.</li>\n    <li><strong>Declare the grain first:</strong> a fact table's grain (\"one row per order line\", \"one row per account per day\") is the first decision in dimensional modeling. Mixing grains in one fact table causes double counting. Every dimension must be single-valued at that grain.</li>\n    <li><strong>Additivity decides how measures aggregate:</strong> additive (revenue) sums across every dimension; semi-additive (account balance, inventory) sums across entities but not across time \u2014 use last/average over time; non-additive (ratios, unit price) never sum \u2014 store numerator and denominator and compute the ratio after aggregating.</li>\n    <li><strong>SCD Type 2 = history via new rows:</strong> on change, expire the current row (set valid_to, is_current = false) and insert a new version with a new surrogate key. Facts point at the surrogate key that was current when the event happened, so old reports stay reproducible.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. KEYS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-keys\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>Keys \u2014 Identity and References</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 the key vocabulary</span>\n    <p><strong>Superkey:</strong> any set of columns whose values uniquely identify a row. <strong>Candidate key:</strong> a <em>minimal</em> superkey \u2014 remove any column and it stops being unique. A table can have several (users: <code>user_id</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>(country, national_id)</code>). <strong>Primary key:</strong> the one candidate key you nominate as the row's identity; implies NOT NULL + UNIQUE, and in most engines builds a unique index. <strong>Alternate key:</strong> the candidate keys you didn't pick \u2014 enforce them with UNIQUE. <strong>Composite key:</strong> a key made of more than one column. <strong>Foreign key:</strong> a column (set) whose values must exist as a key (PK or UNIQUE) in another table.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Surrogate vs natural keys</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Natural key (email, ISBN, country_code)</th><th>Surrogate key (identity, UUID)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Meaning</strong></td><td>Comes from the business domain</td><td>None \u2014 generated by the system</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Stability</strong></td><td>Can change (people change email) \u2192 cascading updates through every FK</td><td>Never changes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Size / join cost</strong></td><td>Often wide strings, sometimes composite</td><td>Narrow integer (8 bytes) or 16-byte UUID</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Duplicate protection</strong></td><td>Built in</td><td>None on its own \u2014 two identical customers get two ids unless you add UNIQUE on the natural key</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Warehouses</strong></td><td>Kept as the \"business/durable key\"</td><td>Required for SCD Type 2 (several rows per business key)</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 use both</span>\n    <p>The surrogate key answers \"which row?\"; the natural key answers \"which real-world thing?\". Use a surrogate PK for joins and stability, and keep the natural key with a UNIQUE constraint so the database still rejects duplicates. A surrogate key without a uniqueness rule on the natural key is how you end up with three \"Acme Corp\" customers.<br><br>\n    Sequential integers vs UUIDs: integers are compact and index-friendly (inserts append to the right edge of the B-tree). Random UUIDv4 values scatter inserts across the index (page splits, poor cache locality) but can be generated anywhere without coordination. UUIDv7 (time-ordered) gets most of both.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Foreign keys and referential actions</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE TABLE departments (\n  id         BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,   -- surrogate\n  name       TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE                                -- natural / alternate key\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE employees (\n  id          BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  email       TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  name        TEXT NOT NULL,\n  dept_id     BIGINT REFERENCES departments(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,  -- keep employee, orphan dept\n  manager_id  BIGINT REFERENCES employees(id)   ON DELETE RESTRICT,  -- self-reference\n  salary      NUMERIC(12,2) CHECK (salary &gt; 0),\n  hire_date   DATE NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE order_items (\n  order_id    BIGINT REFERENCES orders(order_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,  -- items die with the order\n  line_no     INT,\n  product_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES products(product_id),     -- default NO ACTION\n  qty         INT NOT NULL CHECK (qty &gt; 0),\n  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, line_no)                                  -- composite key\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX ON employees (dept_id);      -- Postgres does NOT index the referencing side for you\nCREATE INDEX ON order_items (product_id);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Action (ON DELETE / ON UPDATE)</th><th>What happens when the parent row is deleted/updated</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>NO ACTION</strong> (default)</td><td>Error if children exist; checked at end of statement (can be DEFERRABLE to end of transaction)</td><td>Most references</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>RESTRICT</strong></td><td>Error immediately; never deferrable</td><td>\"Never delete a customer who has orders\"</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>CASCADE</strong></td><td>Children are deleted/updated too</td><td>Composition: order \u2192 order_items, post \u2192 comments</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>SET NULL</strong></td><td>Child FK becomes NULL (column must be nullable)</td><td>Optional relationships: employee's department dissolved</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>SET DEFAULT</strong></td><td>Child FK set to its default (which must itself exist in the parent)</td><td>Reassign to an \"unassigned\" bucket</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 cascades and unindexed FKs</span>\n    <p>CASCADE is convenient and dangerous: one <code>DELETE FROM customers WHERE id = 7</code> can silently remove orders, items, payments \u2014 a chain you never see in the statement. Use it only for true ownership (the child has no meaning without the parent).<br><br>\n    Second trap: PostgreSQL indexes the referenced side (the PK) but <em>not</em> the referencing column. Deleting a parent then requires a sequential scan of the child table to check for/cascade to children, and holds locks while it does. MySQL InnoDB auto-creates an index on FK columns; Postgres and SQL Server do not. Also: many warehouses (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift) accept PK/FK declarations but <strong>don't enforce them</strong> \u2014 they are hints for the optimizer, so your pipeline must test uniqueness itself.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. RELATIONSHIPS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-relationships\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>Relationships &amp; ER Thinking</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 cardinality and optionality</span>\n    <p>An ER (entity\u2013relationship) model has <strong>entities</strong> (nouns that have identity: user, order, product), <strong>attributes</strong> (their properties) and <strong>relationships</strong>. Each relationship has a <strong>cardinality</strong> (how many on each side: 1:1, 1:N, M:N) and an <strong>optionality</strong> (must a row participate? \u2014 mandatory maps to NOT NULL FK, optional to nullable FK).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">users \u2500\u25001\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25000..1\u2500\u2500 user_profiles     1:1   FK on profiles, UNIQUE (or FK = PK)\nusers \u2500\u25001\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25000..N\u2500\u2500 orders            1:N   FK on the \"many\" side (orders.user_id)\norders \u25001\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25001..N\u2500\u2500 order_items       1:N   composition, ON DELETE CASCADE\nstudents \u2500M\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500N\u2500 courses           M:N   needs junction table enrollments\nemployees \u25001\u2500\u2500\u2500\u25000..N\u2500\u2500 employees         1:N   self-reference via manager_id</div>\n\n  <h3>The three shapes in DDL</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- 1:1 \u2014 the FK is also the PK, so at most one profile per user\nCREATE TABLE user_profiles (\n  user_id  BIGINT PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES users(user_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  bio      TEXT,\n  avatar   TEXT\n);\n\n-- 1:N \u2014 FK lives on the many side; NOT NULL makes the relationship mandatory\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  order_id    BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  user_id     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(user_id),\n  order_date  DATE NOT NULL,\n  amount      NUMERIC(12,2) NOT NULL,\n  status      TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\n-- M:N \u2014 junction (bridge / associative) table with a composite PK\nCREATE TABLE enrollments (\n  student_id   BIGINT REFERENCES students(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  course_id    BIGINT REFERENCES courses(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  enrolled_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  grade        TEXT,                              -- attribute of the RELATIONSHIP\n  PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id)             -- no duplicate enrollment\n);\nCREATE INDEX ON enrollments (course_id);          -- PK index serves student_id-first lookups only</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why 1:1 tables exist at all</span>\n    <p>If the relationship is truly 1:1 and mandatory, the columns could live in the same table. You split anyway when (a) the extra columns are optional and sparse (most users have no profile), (b) they are large or rarely read (keep the hot table narrow), (c) they have different access control (PII in a locked-down table), or (d) they are owned by a different service.<br><br>\n    For M:N, ask \"is the relationship itself a thing?\" Usually yes: an enrollment has a date and a grade; a friendship has a created_at; a playlist entry has a position. Once it has attributes it is an entity in its own right, which is why the junction table is natural, not a hack.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 the junction table is a guest list</span>\n    <p>Parties and people are M:N: a person attends many parties, a party has many people. You don't write every guest's name on the party invitation card, nor every party on the person's ID. You keep a guest list \u2014 one line per (party, person) \u2014 and that line is also where \"arrived at 9pm, brought wine\" goes.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 the comma-list and the polymorphic FK</span>\n    <p>Storing <code>course_ids = '3,7,12'</code> on a student is the classic M:N mistake: no FK enforcement, no index, every query needs string splitting, and updating one membership rewrites the whole value. A Postgres array has the same integrity problem.<br><br>\n    Polymorphic associations \u2014 <code>comments(commentable_type, commentable_id)</code> pointing at posts <em>or</em> photos \u2014 cannot have a real FK because the target table varies. Prefer one nullable FK per target with a CHECK that exactly one is set, or a separate junction table per target.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. FUNCTIONAL DEPENDENCIES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-fd\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>Functional Dependencies \u2014 the Basis of Normalization</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 functional dependency (FD)</span>\n    <p><strong>X \u2192 Y</strong> (\"X determines Y\") holds in a relation if any two rows that agree on X must agree on Y. In <code>employees</code>: <code>id \u2192 name, dept_id, salary</code>; <code>dept_id \u2192 dept_name</code>. X is the <em>determinant</em>.<br><br>\n    FDs are statements about the <strong>business rules</strong>, not about the current data. A sample where every city happens to have one zip code does not prove <code>city \u2192 zip</code>; the domain says it's false.<br><br>\n    A set of columns K is a <strong>superkey</strong> exactly when K \u2192 (all columns). The <strong>closure</strong> X\u207a is every column derivable from X using Armstrong's axioms (reflexivity: if Y \u2286 X then X \u2192 Y; augmentation: X \u2192 Y \u21d2 XZ \u2192 YZ; transitivity: X \u2192 Y, Y \u2192 Z \u21d2 X \u2192 Z). Compute closures to find candidate keys.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">Relation: order_lines(order_id, product_id, order_date, customer_id, customer_city,\n                         product_name, unit_price, qty)\n\nFDs (business rules):\n  order_id              \u2192 order_date, customer_id\n  customer_id           \u2192 customer_city\n  product_id            \u2192 product_name, unit_price\n  (order_id, product_id) \u2192 qty\n\nClosure of {order_id, product_id}:\n  start            {order_id, product_id}\n  + order_id FD    {.., order_date, customer_id}\n  + customer_id FD {.., customer_city}\n  + product_id FD  {.., product_name, unit_price}\n  + composite FD   {.., qty}           = all columns  \u2192 candidate key</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Dependency type</th><th>Shape</th><th>Example above</th><th>Violates</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Full</strong></td><td>Non-key column depends on the whole key</td><td>(order_id, product_id) \u2192 qty</td><td>Nothing \u2014 this is what you want</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Partial</strong></td><td>Non-key column depends on part of a composite key</td><td>product_id \u2192 product_name</td><td>2NF</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Transitive</strong></td><td>Key \u2192 non-key \u2192 non-key</td><td>order_id \u2192 customer_id \u2192 customer_city</td><td>3NF</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Non-superkey determinant</strong></td><td>Any X \u2192 Y where X isn't a superkey (even if Y is a key column)</td><td>tutor \u2192 subject (see BCNF)</td><td>BCNF</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Multi-valued</strong></td><td>X \u2192\u2192 Y: X determines a <em>set</em> of Y independent of other columns</td><td>employee \u2192\u2192 skill, employee \u2192\u2192 language</td><td>4NF</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 every FD wants its own table</span>\n    <p>An FD X \u2192 Y says \"Y is a fact about X\". If X is the table's key, the fact is stored once per X \u2014 perfect. If X is anything else, the fact gets repeated on every row that shares an X value \u2014 redundancy. Normalization is just: for each FD whose determinant isn't a key, move (X, Y) into a table keyed by X. That's the entire algorithm; the numbered forms are milestones along it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. ANOMALIES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-anomalies\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>Anomalies Normalization Prevents</h2>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>order_id</th><th>product_id</th><th>customer_id</th><th>customer_city</th><th>product_name</th><th>unit_price</th><th>qty</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>101</td><td>P1</td><td>C1</td><td>Pune</td><td>Kettle</td><td>40</td><td>1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>101</td><td>P2</td><td>C1</td><td>Pune</td><td>Mug</td><td>8</td><td>4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>102</td><td>P1</td><td>C2</td><td>Delhi</td><td>Kettle</td><td>40</td><td>2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>103</td><td>P3</td><td>C1</td><td>Pune</td><td>Toaster</td><td>55</td><td>1</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Anomaly</th><th>What goes wrong in the table above</th><th>Root cause</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Update</strong></td><td>C1 moves to Mumbai. You must update 3 rows; update 2 and the data now says C1 lives in two cities at once.</td><td>customer_city is repeated per order line (transitive dependency)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Insert</strong></td><td>You want to add product P4 \"Blender\" to the catalog. You can't \u2014 the key includes order_id, and no order exists yet (NULL in a PK is illegal).</td><td>Product facts are stored inside an order-line table (partial dependency)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Delete</strong></td><td>Order 103 is cancelled and deleted. The only record that the Toaster exists, and its price, is gone with it.</td><td>Two independent facts share one row</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 the photocopied phone number</span>\n    <p>If your friend's phone number is written on 30 sticky notes around the house, when it changes you'll update most of them and miss a few \u2014 and you'll never know which note is right. Store it once in a contacts book and point to it from everywhere else. Normalization is the contacts book; a foreign key is \"see contacts book, entry 42\".</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. NORMAL FORMS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-normal-forms\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF \u2014 Before and After</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 the ladder (each includes the previous)</span>\n    <p><strong>1NF:</strong> every column holds a single atomic value of one type; no repeating groups; rows identifiable by a key. <strong>2NF:</strong> 1NF + no non-key column depends on <em>part</em> of a candidate key. <strong>3NF:</strong> 2NF + no non-key column depends on another non-key column (for every X \u2192 Y, X is a superkey or Y is part of some candidate key). <strong>BCNF:</strong> for every non-trivial X \u2192 Y, X is a superkey \u2014 no exception for key columns.<br><br>\n    Memory hook: every non-key attribute depends on \"the key (1NF), the whole key (2NF), and nothing but the key (3NF)\". BCNF extends \"nothing but the key\" to key columns too.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>1NF \u2014 atomic values, no repeating groups</h3>\n  <p>Before: a customer's phones packed into one cell, and repeated phone columns.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>customer_id</th><th>name</th><th>phones</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>C1</td><td>Asha</td><td>98200-11111, 98200-22222</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>C2</td><td>Ravi</td><td>99300-33333</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>After: one row per (customer, phone). Now \"find the customer with phone X\" is an indexed equality lookup instead of a LIKE scan, and adding a third phone needs no schema change.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>customers: customer_id (PK)</th><th>name</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>C1</td><td>Asha</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>C2</td><td>Ravi</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>customer_phones: customer_id (FK)</th><th>phone \u2014 PK is (customer_id, phone)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>C1</td><td>98200-11111</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>C1</td><td>98200-22222</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>C2</td><td>99300-33333</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>2NF \u2014 no partial dependencies</h3>\n  <p>Before: <code>order_items(order_id, product_id, qty, order_date, product_name)</code> with key (order_id, product_id). <code>order_date</code> depends only on order_id; <code>product_name</code> only on product_id.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>order_id</th><th>product_id</th><th>qty</th><th>order_date</th><th>product_name</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>101</td><td>P1</td><td>1</td><td>2024-03-01</td><td>Kettle</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>101</td><td>P2</td><td>4</td><td>2024-03-01</td><td>Mug</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>102</td><td>P1</td><td>2</td><td>2024-03-02</td><td>Kettle</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>After: split each partial dependency into the table keyed by its determinant.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\">orders(order_id PK, order_date)              101 | 2024-03-01 ; 102 | 2024-03-02\nproducts(product_id PK, product_name)        P1 | Kettle ; P2 | Mug\norder_items(order_id FK, product_id FK, qty, PK(order_id, product_id))\n                                             101 | P1 | 1 ; 101 | P2 | 4 ; 102 | P1 | 2</div>\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 2NF only bites composite keys</span>\n    <p>If a table's only candidate key is a single column, there is no \"part of the key\", so any 1NF table with single-column keys is automatically 2NF. 2NF problems show up in junction/line-item tables, where it's tempting to copy descriptive columns of one parent onto the line.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>3NF \u2014 no transitive dependencies</h3>\n  <p>Before: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id, dept_name, dept_location)</code>. Key is id; but <code>dept_id \u2192 dept_name, dept_location</code>, so those columns depend on the key only <em>through</em> dept_id.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>id</th><th>name</th><th>dept_id</th><th>dept_name</th><th>dept_location</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>1</td><td>Asha</td><td>10</td><td>Data</td><td>Bangalore</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>2</td><td>Ravi</td><td>10</td><td>Data</td><td>Bangalore</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3</td><td>Meera</td><td>20</td><td>Sales</td><td>Mumbai</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>After: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id FK)</code> + <code>departments(dept_id PK, dept_name, dept_location)</code>. Moving the Data team to Hyderabad is now a one-row update.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>departments: dept_id</th><th>dept_name</th><th>dept_location</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>10</td><td>Data</td><td>Bangalore</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>20</td><td>Sales</td><td>Mumbai</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>BCNF \u2014 every determinant is a superkey</h3>\n  <p>Rules: each tutor teaches exactly one subject (<code>tutor \u2192 subject</code>); a student has one tutor per subject (<code>(student, subject) \u2192 tutor</code>). Candidate keys: (student, subject) and (student, tutor). Every column is part of some key, so the table is in 3NF \u2014 yet <code>tutor \u2192 subject</code> has a non-superkey determinant.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>student</th><th>subject</th><th>tutor</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Asha</td><td>Math</td><td>Dr. Rao</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ravi</td><td>Math</td><td>Dr. Rao</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Asha</td><td>Physics</td><td>Dr. Iyer</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>Anomaly: \"Dr. Rao teaches Math\" is stored once per student; you can't record a new tutor's subject until they have a student. After (BCNF):</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\">tutor_subjects(tutor PK, subject)              Dr. Rao | Math ; Dr. Iyer | Physics\nstudent_tutors(student, tutor FK, PK(student, tutor))\n                                               Asha | Dr. Rao ; Ravi | Dr. Rao ; Asha | Dr. Iyer</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 BCNF can lose a dependency</span>\n    <p>After the split, the rule \"(student, subject) \u2192 tutor\" (a student can't have two Math tutors) spans two tables; no single UNIQUE constraint enforces it \u2014 you'd need a trigger or a join check. That's why 3NF is the usual practical target: a 3NF decomposition that is both lossless and dependency-preserving always exists; a BCNF one may not. Say this in interviews \u2014 it shows you know normalization is a trade-off, not a religion.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Form</th><th>Removes</th><th>One-line test</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>1NF</strong></td><td>Lists in cells, repeating column groups</td><td>Can every cell be compared with = to one value?</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>2NF</strong></td><td>Partial dependencies</td><td>Does any non-key column depend on just part of a composite key?</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>3NF</strong></td><td>Transitive dependencies</td><td>Does any non-key column depend on another non-key column?</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>BCNF</strong></td><td>Any non-superkey determinant</td><td>Is the left side of every FD a superkey?</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>4NF</strong></td><td>Independent multi-valued facts in one table</td><td>Are two unrelated lists (skills, languages) cross-producted together?</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. DENORMALIZATION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-denorm\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>Denormalization \u2014 When and Why</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 denormalization</span>\n    <p>Deliberately storing redundant data (copied columns, precomputed aggregates, pre-joined tables) to make reads cheaper, accepting more expensive and riskier writes. It is a conscious decision taken <em>after</em> designing the normalized model, not a substitute for designing it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Technique</th><th>Example</th><th>Read win</th><th>Write cost / risk</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Copied attribute</strong></td><td>orders.customer_country copied from users</td><td>Filter orders by country without a join</td><td>Must decide: snapshot at order time (often correct!) or keep in sync</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Stored aggregate</strong></td><td>posts.comment_count, users.follower_count</td><td>O(1) instead of COUNT over millions</td><td>Hot-row contention on popular posts; drift if a write path forgets to increment</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Materialized view</strong></td><td>daily_revenue_by_country</td><td>Dashboards hit a tiny table</td><td>Staleness between refreshes; refresh cost</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Pre-joined wide table</strong></td><td>fact_orders with customer + product attributes inlined</td><td>Single-table scans in columnar engines</td><td>Rebuild/backfill when a dimension attribute changes</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Embedded documents / arrays</strong></td><td>order JSON with line items</td><td>One fetch per order</td><td>Hard to query across documents; no FK</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 when it pays</span>\n    <p>Denormalize when (1) the read:write ratio is very high, (2) the join or aggregate is on the hot path and measured to be the bottleneck, (3) the copied data changes rarely or <em>should</em> be frozen (the shipping address on an order must NOT change when the user edits their profile \u2014 that's a historical fact, not redundancy), and (4) there is one owner of the sync logic (trigger, CDC pipeline, or a dbt model rebuilt from source).<br><br>\n    In analytics it's the default: storage is cheap, columnar engines read only the columns you touch, writes are batch, and the source of truth stays normalized upstream \u2014 so the risk of drift is bounded by \"rebuild from source\".</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 \"it's faster\" without measuring</span>\n    <p>Indexes, covering indexes and materialized views often get the read speed without duplicating data in the base tables. Reach for denormalization in OLTP only after EXPLAIN shows the join is the cost. And always name the mechanism that keeps the copy correct \u2014 \"we'll remember to update it\" is not a mechanism.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. OLTP vs OLAP \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-oltp-olap\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>OLTP vs OLAP Modeling</h2>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>OLTP (operational)</th><th>OLAP (analytical)</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Workload</strong></td><td>Many small reads/writes by key: \"place order 9123\"</td><td>Few large scans + aggregations: \"revenue by country by month\"</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td>Normalized (3NF) \u2014 integrity, no anomalies</td><td>Dimensional (star) or wide denormalized tables</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Storage</strong></td><td>Row-oriented (whole row together \u2014 cheap to insert/fetch one row)</td><td>Column-oriented (each column compressed together \u2014 cheap to scan 3 columns of 1B rows)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Indexes</strong></td><td>B-tree on PKs/FKs/lookup columns</td><td>Few or none; partitioning, clustering/sort keys, zone maps</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>History</strong></td><td>Current state; updates in place</td><td>Full history; append, snapshots, SCDs</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Consistency</strong></td><td>ACID transactions, enforced constraints</td><td>Batch/stream loads; constraints often unenforced, tested in pipeline</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Engines</strong></td><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle</td><td>Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift, Databricks, ClickHouse, DuckDB</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the cost model flips</span>\n    <p>In OLTP the expensive thing is <em>writing the same fact twice</em> (anomalies, lock contention), so you normalize. In OLAP the expensive thing is <em>joining and scanning</em> billions of rows for every dashboard query, while writes are a nightly or streaming batch you control \u2014 so you pay redundancy to save joins. Data flows OLTP \u2192 (CDC / ELT) \u2192 warehouse; the warehouse model is derived, so it can be rebuilt if it drifts.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. DIMENSIONAL MODELING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-dimensional\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>Dimensional Modeling \u2014 Facts, Dimensions, Grain</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 facts and dimensions (Kimball)</span>\n    <p><strong>Fact table:</strong> one row per measurable business event or state at a declared grain; columns are foreign keys to dimensions plus numeric <em>measures</em> (amount, qty, duration). Tall and narrow. <strong>Dimension table:</strong> the descriptive context \u2014 who, what, where, when (customer, product, store, date); columns are attributes you filter and group by. Short and wide. <strong>Grain:</strong> the exact meaning of one fact row, stated in business terms, decided before choosing any columns.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>The four-step design process</h3>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Pick the business process</strong> \u2014 orders, shipments, page views, subscriptions.</li>\n    <li><strong>Declare the grain</strong> \u2014 \"one row per order line item\". Atomic grain is preferred; you can always roll up, never drill down.</li>\n    <li><strong>Identify dimensions</strong> \u2014 everything single-valued at that grain: date, customer, product, promotion.</li>\n    <li><strong>Identify facts (measures)</strong> \u2014 numbers true at that grain: qty, gross_amount, discount_amount.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Fact table type</th><th>Grain</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Transaction</strong></td><td>One row per event</td><td>fact_order_items, fact_page_views</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Periodic snapshot</strong></td><td>One row per entity per period</td><td>fact_account_balance_daily, fact_inventory_weekly</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Accumulating snapshot</strong></td><td>One row per process instance, updated as milestones occur</td><td>fact_order_fulfillment (ordered_at, shipped_at, delivered_at)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Factless</strong></td><td>One row per event with no measure</td><td>fact_class_attendance (student, class, date) \u2014 you COUNT rows</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Star vs snowflake</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">                 dim_date\n                    \u2502\n dim_customer \u2500\u2500 fact_order_items \u2500\u2500 dim_product          STAR: dims are flat (category_name\n                    \u2502                                      lives inside dim_product)\n                 dim_store\n\n dim_product \u2500\u2500 dim_subcategory \u2500\u2500 dim_category           SNOWFLAKE: dims normalized into\n                                                           hierarchies (more joins)</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Star schema</th><th>Snowflake schema</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Dimension shape</strong></td><td>Denormalized, one table per dimension</td><td>Normalized into sub-dimension tables</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Joins per query</strong></td><td>Fact + 1 per dimension</td><td>More (walk the hierarchy)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Storage</strong></td><td>Some repetition in dims (cheap \u2014 dims are small)</td><td>Less repetition</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Usability for BI</strong></td><td>Simple for analysts and BI tools</td><td>Harder to navigate</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>When</strong></td><td>Default choice</td><td>Very large dims, shared hierarchies maintained separately</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE TABLE dim_date (\n  date_key     INT PRIMARY KEY,          -- 20240301 (smart key is acceptable for dates)\n  full_date    DATE NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  year         INT, quarter INT, month INT, day_of_week INT,\n  is_weekend   BOOLEAN, is_holiday BOOLEAN\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE fact_order_items (           -- grain: one row per order line\n  date_key       INT    NOT NULL REFERENCES dim_date(date_key),\n  customer_sk    BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES dim_customer(customer_sk),\n  product_sk     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES dim_product(product_sk),\n  order_id       BIGINT NOT NULL,         -- degenerate dimension (no dim table)\n  line_no        INT    NOT NULL,\n  qty            INT,                     -- additive\n  gross_amount   NUMERIC(12,2),           -- additive\n  discount_amt   NUMERIC(12,2),           -- additive\n  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, line_no)\n);\n\n-- Typical star query: constrain dims, aggregate facts\nSELECT d.year, d.month, p.category, SUM(f.gross_amount - f.discount_amt) AS net_revenue\nFROM fact_order_items f\nJOIN dim_date d     ON d.date_key = f.date_key\nJOIN dim_product p  ON p.product_sk = f.product_sk\nWHERE d.year = 2024\nGROUP BY d.year, d.month, p.category;</div>\n\n  <h3>Additive, semi-additive, non-additive measures</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Can SUM across</th><th>Examples</th><th>How to aggregate</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Additive</strong></td><td>Every dimension</td><td>revenue, qty, clicks</td><td>SUM anywhere</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Semi-additive</strong></td><td>Some dimensions, not time</td><td>account balance, inventory on hand, headcount</td><td>SUM across accounts on one date; over time use end-of-period or AVG</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Non-additive</strong></td><td>None</td><td>conversion rate, unit price, margin %, distinct users</td><td>Store components (numerator, denominator) and divide after summing; distinct counts must be recomputed from atomic data</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 summing a balance, averaging a ratio</span>\n    <p>Summing daily balances over a month gives 30\u00d7 the money. Averaging per-store conversion rates gives every store equal weight regardless of traffic (average of ratios \u2260 ratio of sums). And daily distinct users cannot be summed into monthly distinct users \u2014 a user active on 20 days is counted 20 times. Store <code>orders</code> and <code>sessions</code> as additive facts, compute <code>SUM(orders) / SUM(sessions)</code> at query time.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 conformed dimensions are the glue</span>\n    <p>If fact_orders and fact_returns both use the <em>same</em> dim_product and dim_date (same keys, same attribute definitions), you can compute \"return rate by category\" by aggregating each fact to category separately and joining the results (\"drill-across\"). Never join two fact tables directly at row level \u2014 their grains differ and you'll fan out.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. SCD \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-scd\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>Slowly Changing Dimensions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 SCD types</span>\n    <p>Dimension attributes change (a customer moves city, a product changes category). The SCD type defines what history you keep. <strong>Type 0:</strong> never changes (original signup date). <strong>Type 1:</strong> overwrite \u2014 no history; old facts now report under the new value. <strong>Type 2:</strong> add a new row per version with validity dates and a new surrogate key \u2014 full history. <strong>Type 3:</strong> add a \"previous value\" column \u2014 one step of history. (Type 4 = separate history table; Type 6 = 1+2+3 hybrid: Type 2 rows plus a current-value column on every row.)</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Asha moves Pune \u2192 Mumbai on 2024-06-01</th><th>\"Revenue by city\" for Asha's 2023 orders</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>city overwritten to Mumbai</td><td>Mumbai (history rewritten)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>old row expired; new row (new customer_sk) with Mumbai</td><td>Pune (facts point at old surrogate key)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>city = Mumbai, previous_city = Pune</td><td>Choose either column; only one prior value kept</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <h3>Type 2 table and load SQL</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE TABLE dim_customer (\n  customer_sk  BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,  -- surrogate: one per VERSION\n  customer_id  BIGINT NOT NULL,                                   -- business/durable key\n  name         TEXT,\n  city         TEXT,\n  valid_from   DATE NOT NULL,\n  valid_to     DATE NOT NULL DEFAULT DATE '9999-12-31',           -- half-open: [from, to)\n  is_current   BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE\n);\n-- at most one current row per customer\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON dim_customer (customer_id) WHERE is_current;\n\n-- Nightly load from stg_customer(customer_id, name, city), in ONE transaction\nBEGIN;\n\n-- Step 1: expire current rows whose tracked attributes changed\nUPDATE dim_customer d\nSET    valid_to = CURRENT_DATE,\n       is_current = FALSE\nFROM   stg_customer s\nWHERE  d.customer_id = s.customer_id\n  AND  d.is_current\n  AND  (d.name IS DISTINCT FROM s.name OR d.city IS DISTINCT FROM s.city);\n\n-- Step 2: insert a new current version for changed AND brand-new customers\n-- (after step 1, both kinds have no current row)\nINSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id, name, city, valid_from, valid_to, is_current)\nSELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, DATE '9999-12-31', TRUE\nFROM   stg_customer s\nLEFT JOIN dim_customer d\n       ON d.customer_id = s.customer_id AND d.is_current\nWHERE  d.customer_id IS NULL;\n\nCOMMIT;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">customer_sk | customer_id | city   | valid_from | valid_to   | is_current\n          7 |          42 | Pune   | 2022-01-10 | 2024-06-01 | false\n         93 |          42 | Mumbai | 2024-06-01 | 9999-12-31 | true</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Point-in-time (\"as-of\") lookup when facts carry only the business key\nSELECT o.order_id, o.order_date, d.city\nFROM orders o\nJOIN dim_customer d\n  ON  d.customer_id = o.user_id\n  AND o.order_date &gt;= d.valid_from\n  AND o.order_date &lt;  d.valid_to;        -- half-open interval: exactly one match\n\n-- Type 3: one step of history in a column\nALTER TABLE dim_product ADD COLUMN previous_category TEXT;\nUPDATE dim_product\nSET previous_category = category, category = 'Kitchen'\nWHERE product_id = 501 AND category IS DISTINCT FROM 'Kitchen';</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 the details that break Type 2</span>\n    <p>(1) Use <code>IS DISTINCT FROM</code>, not <code>&lt;&gt;</code>: <code>NULL &lt;&gt; 'Pune'</code> is NULL, so a change to/from NULL would never be detected. (2) Use a half-open interval [valid_from, valid_to) consistently; mixing inclusive end dates with \"new row starts the same day\" double-matches on the boundary date. (3) Facts must store the surrogate key that was current at event time (lookup during fact load) \u2014 joining facts to dims on the business key with <code>is_current</code> silently turns your Type 2 into Type 1. (4) Run both steps in one transaction or a MERGE (Postgres 15+, Snowflake, BigQuery) so readers never see a customer with zero current rows. In dbt, <code>snapshots</code> implement exactly this pattern (dbt_valid_from / dbt_valid_to).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. MODERN PATTERNS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-modern\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>Modern Warehouse Patterns</h2>\n\n  <h3>Wide tables / One Big Table (OBT)</h3>\n  <p>Flatten the star into one very wide table at the fact grain: every dimension attribute you commonly filter on becomes a column (<code>order_id, order_date, customer_country, customer_segment, product_category, revenue, ...</code>). Columnar storage makes unused columns free to carry, compression makes repeated values cheap, and the absence of joins makes queries fast and analyst-proof.</p>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>No joins; easy for BI tools and ad-hoc SQL; predictable performance</td><td>Dimension changes require rebuilding/backfilling the table; SCD history is frozen into rows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Works well with partitioning by date + clustering</td><td>Only one grain per table \u2014 multiple processes need multiple OBTs, and metric definitions can diverge</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <p>Common practice: keep a star (or normalized core) as the governed layer and generate OBTs from it as consumption marts.</p>\n\n  <h3>Medallion architecture \u2014 bronze / silver / gold</h3>\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Contents</th><th>Guarantees</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Bronze (raw)</strong></td><td>Source data as landed: CDC logs, JSON events, API dumps; append-only</td><td>Replayable; nothing lost; schema may be loose</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Silver (cleaned)</strong></td><td>Deduplicated, typed, conformed entities; joined keys; often near-3NF or cleaned event tables</td><td>One row per entity/event, tested keys, consistent types/timezones</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Gold (business)</strong></td><td>Star schemas, aggregates, OBTs, feature tables for ML</td><td>Metric definitions agreed; optimized for consumption</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"callout anlg\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy \u2014 a restaurant kitchen</span>\n    <p>Bronze is the delivery dock (crates exactly as the supplier sent them, kept in case you need to check what arrived). Silver is prep (washed, chopped, labeled ingredients any chef can use). Gold is the plated dish for a specific customer. You can always re-plate from prep, and re-prep from the dock \u2014 never the other way.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>dbt in one paragraph</h3>\n  <p>dbt turns the transformation layer into version-controlled SELECT statements. Each model is a SELECT; <code>ref()</code> declares dependencies so dbt builds a DAG and runs models in order; materializations choose view / table / incremental; tests (<code>unique</code>, <code>not_null</code>, <code>relationships</code>, <code>accepted_values</code>) assert the key/FK rules warehouses don't enforce; snapshots implement SCD Type 2.</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- models/marts/fct_orders.sql\nSELECT o.order_id, o.user_id, o.order_date, o.amount, u.country\nFROM {{ ref('stg_orders') }} o\nJOIN {{ ref('stg_users') }}  u USING (user_id)\nWHERE o.status = 'completed'</div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. INTEGRITY \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-integrity\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>Constraints, Integrity &amp; Views</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 constraints are executable documentation</span>\n    <p>Every rule enforced in application code alone will eventually be violated \u2014 by a backfill script, a second service, or a manual fix at 2am. A constraint in the database is checked on every write, by every client, forever. It also gives the optimizer facts it can use (a UNIQUE column lets it drop a DISTINCT or prove a join won't fan out).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Enforces</th><th>Gotcha</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>NOT NULL</strong></td><td>Value must be present</td><td>Default should be NOT NULL; nullable only when \"unknown\" is a real state</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>UNIQUE</strong></td><td>No duplicate values</td><td>NULLs are distinct by default \u2014 many NULL emails allowed. PG15+: <code>UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>PRIMARY KEY</strong></td><td>UNIQUE + NOT NULL, row identity</td><td>One per table</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>FOREIGN KEY</strong></td><td>Referenced row exists</td><td>NULL FK values are not checked; index the child column</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>CHECK</strong></td><td>Row-level predicate</td><td>Passes when the expression is NULL (unknown is not a violation)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>EXCLUDE</strong> (Postgres)</td><td>No two rows satisfy an operator pairwise, e.g. overlapping ranges</td><td>Needs btree_gist for equality on scalar columns</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE TABLE bookings (\n  booking_id  BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  room_id     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES rooms(room_id),\n  stay        DATERANGE NOT NULL,\n  status      TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('held', 'confirmed', 'cancelled')),\n  -- a room cannot be double-booked (requires: CREATE EXTENSION btree_gist)\n  EXCLUDE USING gist (room_id WITH =, stay WITH &amp;&amp;) WHERE (status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled')\n);\n\n-- Partial unique index: one active subscription per user\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX one_active_sub ON subscriptions (user_id) WHERE status = 'active';</div>\n\n  <h3>Views as an abstraction layer</h3>\n  <p>A <strong>view</strong> is a stored query, expanded into the calling query at plan time \u2014 no data is stored, so it is always current and the optimizer can push predicates into it. Uses: hide joins behind a stable interface (rename/split tables without breaking consumers), restrict columns/rows for security (expose <code>users_public</code> without PII), and encode one agreed metric definition. A <strong>materialized view</strong> stores the result: fast reads, stale until <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code> (add <code>CONCURRENTLY</code>, which requires a unique index, to avoid blocking readers).</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE VIEW active_customers AS\nSELECT u.user_id, u.country, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders_90d\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.user_id\nWHERE o.order_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - 90\n  AND o.status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.country;\n\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW daily_revenue AS\nSELECT order_date, SUM(amount) AS revenue\nFROM orders WHERE status = 'completed'\nGROUP BY order_date;\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON daily_revenue (order_date);\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY daily_revenue;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 views are not caches</span>\n    <p>A plain view does not make a slow query faster \u2014 it is re-executed every time. Stacking views on views hides enormous plans. Also, <code>SELECT *</code> in a view definition is expanded at creation time in Postgres: adding a column to the base table does not add it to the view.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 12. SCHEMA DESIGN INTERVIEW \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-design\"><span class=\"num\">12</span>Designing a Schema in an Interview \u2014 Ride-Sharing</h2>\n\n  <h3>Step 1 \u2014 clarify scope and the questions the data must answer</h3>\n  <p>Ask: riders and drivers only, or also pooling/deliveries? Multiple vehicles per driver? Surge pricing? Which metrics matter \u2014 trips per day, cancellation rate, driver earnings, ETA accuracy? Real-time (OLTP) schema, analytics schema, or both? State assumptions out loud: one rider per trip, a driver can switch vehicles, payments may be retried.</p>\n\n  <h3>Step 2 \u2014 entities, relationships, keys</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">riders   1 \u2500\u2500 N  trips  N \u2500\u2500 1  drivers  1 \u2500\u2500 N  vehicles (driver may own several)\ntrips    1 \u2500\u2500 N  trip_events    (status history: requested, accepted, arrived, started, completed, cancelled)\ntrips    1 \u2500\u2500 N  payments       (retries, refunds)\ntrips    1 \u2500\u2500 0..2 ratings      (rider rates driver, driver rates rider)\ncities   1 \u2500\u2500 N  trips</div>\n\n  <h3>Step 3 \u2014 OLTP DDL (3NF)</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE TABLE riders (\n  rider_id    BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  phone       TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  name        TEXT NOT NULL,\n  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE drivers (\n  driver_id   BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  phone       TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  license_no  TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  status      TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('active', 'suspended', 'offboarded'))\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE vehicles (\n  vehicle_id  BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  driver_id   BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES drivers(driver_id),\n  plate       TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  vehicle_type TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE trips (\n  trip_id        BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  rider_id       BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES riders(rider_id),\n  driver_id      BIGINT REFERENCES drivers(driver_id),     -- NULL until accepted\n  vehicle_id     BIGINT REFERENCES vehicles(vehicle_id),   -- snapshot: vehicle used THIS trip\n  city_id        INT NOT NULL REFERENCES cities(city_id),\n  requested_at   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n  pickup_point   POINT, dropoff_point POINT,\n  status         TEXT NOT NULL,                            -- current state (denormalized from events)\n  fare_amount    NUMERIC(10,2),                            -- frozen at completion\n  surge_multiplier NUMERIC(4,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0\n);\nCREATE INDEX ON trips (rider_id, requested_at);\nCREATE INDEX ON trips (driver_id, requested_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE trip_events (                                 -- append-only history\n  trip_id     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES trips(trip_id),\n  event_type  TEXT NOT NULL,\n  event_at    TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (trip_id, event_type, event_at)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE ratings (\n  trip_id     BIGINT REFERENCES trips(trip_id),\n  rated_by    TEXT CHECK (rated_by IN ('rider', 'driver')),\n  stars       SMALLINT NOT NULL CHECK (stars BETWEEN 1 AND 5),\n  PRIMARY KEY (trip_id, rated_by)                          -- at most one rating per side\n);</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 the decisions worth narrating</span>\n    <p><strong>fare_amount and vehicle_id on trips</strong> are historical facts, frozen at trip time \u2014 not redundancy. Recomputing fare from today's price table would be wrong. <strong>trips.status</strong> duplicates the latest trip_event: a deliberate denormalization so \"find active trips\" doesn't need a MAX-per-group over the events table; the event table stays the audit log. <strong>ratings</strong> uses a composite PK (trip_id, rated_by) to make \"rate twice\" impossible rather than checked in code. <strong>Indexes</strong> follow access paths: rider history, driver earnings.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Step 4 \u2014 the analytics layer</h3>\n  <p>Declare the grain: <code>fact_trips</code> = one row per trip request (including cancellations). Dimensions: dim_date, dim_time_of_day, dim_rider, dim_driver (SCD2 on city/tier), dim_city. Measures: fare (additive), distance_km (additive), wait_seconds (additive; average = SUM/COUNT), is_completed / is_cancelled flags (sum them to get counts \u2192 rates). Then show it answers a real question:</p>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Cancellation rate by city and week\nSELECT c.city_name,\n       DATE_TRUNC('week', f.requested_at)::date AS week,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(f.is_cancelled::int) / COUNT(*), 2) AS cancel_pct\nFROM fact_trips f\nJOIN dim_city c ON c.city_sk = f.city_sk\nGROUP BY c.city_name, DATE_TRUNC('week', f.requested_at)::date\nORDER BY c.city_name, week;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 jumping to DDL</span>\n    <p>Interviewers grade the process: clarify \u2192 entities \u2192 relationships/cardinality \u2192 keys \u2192 grain \u2192 access patterns/indexes \u2192 how metrics are computed \u2192 trade-offs (what you denormalized and how you keep it correct). Silent table-writing, no stated grain, and no mention of how history (status changes, price changes) is preserved are the common fails.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 13. Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"sm-qa\"><span class=\"num\">13</span>Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What's the difference between a candidate key, a primary key and a superkey?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">A superkey is any column set that uniquely identifies rows (it can contain extra columns). A candidate key is a minimal superkey \u2014 removing any column breaks uniqueness. A table may have several candidate keys (user_id, email). The primary key is the single candidate key chosen as the row's identity; it is NOT NULL and UNIQUE. The other candidate keys should still be enforced with UNIQUE constraints, otherwise the database will happily accept two users with the same email.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Surrogate or natural primary key \u2014 which do you choose and why?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Usually a surrogate (identity or UUID) as the PK, plus a UNIQUE constraint on the natural key. Natural keys change (emails, phone numbers, even \"immutable\" codes get reissued), and a changing PK must cascade through every referencing table. Surrogates are narrow and stable, making joins and indexes cheaper. The natural key is kept for duplicate prevention and lookups. Exceptions: small stable code tables (ISO country codes) and junction tables, where the composite of the two FKs is the natural key and is the right PK. In warehouses surrogate keys on dimensions are required because SCD Type 2 stores several versions of the same business key.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>How do you model a many-to-many relationship? Write the DDL for users following users.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">With a junction table holding two FKs and a composite PK. For a self-referential M:N like \"follows\", both FKs point at users:\n  <div class=\"fx\">CREATE TABLE follows (\n  follower_id  BIGINT REFERENCES users(user_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  followee_id  BIGINT REFERENCES users(user_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  PRIMARY KEY (follower_id, followee_id),\n  CHECK (follower_id &lt;&gt; followee_id)            -- no self-follow\n);\nCREATE INDEX ON follows (followee_id);           -- \"who follows me\" lookups</div>\n  The PK index supports \"whom do I follow\"; the second index supports the reverse direction. For a symmetric relation (friendship) either store both directions or store one canonical row with CHECK (user_a &lt; user_b).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Explain 2NF vs 3NF with an example.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">2NF forbids partial dependencies: a non-key column depending on part of a composite key. In <code>order_items(order_id, product_id, qty, product_name)</code>, product_name depends only on product_id, so it repeats for every order containing the product \u2014 move it to products. 3NF forbids transitive dependencies: non-key \u2192 non-key. In <code>employees(id, dept_id, dept_name)</code>, dept_name depends on dept_id, not on id directly \u2014 move it to departments. Both are the same idea: every fact belongs in the table keyed by its determinant. 2NF matters only for composite keys; 3NF applies to any table.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Give a table that is in 3NF but not BCNF.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Tutoring: <code>(student, subject, tutor)</code> where each tutor teaches one subject (tutor \u2192 subject) and each student has one tutor per subject ((student, subject) \u2192 tutor). Candidate keys are (student, subject) and (student, tutor), so every column is prime and 3NF is satisfied (3NF allows X \u2192 Y when Y is part of a key). But tutor is not a superkey, so tutor \u2192 subject violates BCNF; \"Dr. Rao teaches Math\" repeats per student. Decompose into tutor_subjects(tutor, subject) and student_tutors(student, tutor). The cost: the rule \"one tutor per student per subject\" now spans two tables and can't be enforced with a single constraint \u2014 BCNF isn't always dependency-preserving.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>When would you denormalize, and how do you keep the copy correct?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">When reads vastly outnumber writes, a join/aggregate on the hot path has been measured as the bottleneck, and the copied data rarely changes or is meant to be frozen. Examples: a comment_count on posts, the current status on trips, a pre-joined fact table in the warehouse. Name the sync mechanism: a trigger or the same transaction that writes the source row (strong consistency), a CDC/stream consumer (eventual), or a scheduled rebuild from the normalized source (warehouse). Also distinguish true redundancy from historical snapshots \u2014 an order's shipping address and price must be copied and frozen; that's correctness, not denormalization.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>What is the grain of a fact table and why declare it first?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">The grain is the business meaning of one row \u2014 \"one row per order line\", \"one row per user per day\". It determines which dimensions are valid (each must be single-valued at the grain) and which measures are true at that level. Declaring it first prevents mixed-grain tables: if some rows are order lines and others are order-level shipping charges, summing revenue double-counts or misattributes. Prefer the atomic grain because you can aggregate up but never drill below what you stored.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Star vs snowflake \u2014 which would you choose in Snowflake/BigQuery today?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Star by default. Dimensions are small relative to facts, so the redundancy of flattening hierarchies into one dimension table is cheap, and fewer joins makes queries simpler and BI-friendly. Columnar engines compress repeated category names well. Snowflaking makes sense when a hierarchy is large, shared and maintained independently (a product taxonomy used by several dims), or for governance reasons. Many modern teams go further and publish wide OBT marts derived from the star.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>A customer changes city. How do you keep reports by historical city correct? Write the core SQL.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">SCD Type 2: version rows with a surrogate key, valid_from/valid_to and is_current. On change, expire the current row and insert a new one; fact rows reference the surrogate key current at event time.\n  <div class=\"fx\">UPDATE dim_customer d\nSET valid_to = CURRENT_DATE, is_current = FALSE\nFROM stg_customer s\nWHERE d.customer_id = s.customer_id AND d.is_current\n  AND d.city IS DISTINCT FROM s.city;\n\nINSERT INTO dim_customer (customer_id, name, city, valid_from, valid_to, is_current)\nSELECT s.customer_id, s.name, s.city, CURRENT_DATE, DATE '9999-12-31', TRUE\nFROM stg_customer s\nLEFT JOIN dim_customer d ON d.customer_id = s.customer_id AND d.is_current\nWHERE d.customer_id IS NULL;</div>\n  Use IS DISTINCT FROM for NULL-safe change detection, a half-open [valid_from, valid_to) interval, and run both statements in one transaction (or one MERGE).</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Why can't you sum account balances across days? How do you report them?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">A balance is a semi-additive measure: it's a level (state), not a flow. Summing across accounts on one date is meaningful (total deposits held), but summing the same account across 30 daily snapshots counts the same money 30 times. Over time, report the period-end value (balance on the last day of the month, via a filter on the last date or a window like LAST_VALUE), or the average daily balance (SUM / number of days). Flows like deposits and withdrawals are additive and can be summed anywhere.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Your warehouse declares a primary key on dim_product but you find duplicate product_sk values. How is that possible and what do you do?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Snowflake, BigQuery and Redshift accept PK/UNIQUE/FK declarations but do not enforce them (except NOT NULL); they're metadata, sometimes used by the optimizer \u2014 which can then return wrong results if the declared uniqueness is false. Duplicates typically come from a non-idempotent incremental load or a reprocessed batch. Fix: dedupe with ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_sk ORDER BY loaded_at DESC) = 1, make the load idempotent (MERGE on the key), and add pipeline tests (dbt unique / not_null / relationships) that fail the run before bad data reaches gold.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Walk me through designing an e-commerce schema in 5 minutes.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Clarify: marketplace or single seller, variants (size/colour), multi-currency, returns. Entities: users, addresses (1:N), products, product_variants (1:N, the SKU you actually sell, with price and stock), orders (N:1 users, with frozen shipping address and currency), order_items (N:1 orders, N:1 variants, with unit_price frozen at purchase, PK (order_id, line_no)), payments (N:1 orders, retries/refunds), categories with product_categories junction (M:N). Keys: surrogate PKs, UNIQUE on email and SKU code, FKs with CASCADE only for order \u2192 order_items. Constraints: CHECK qty &gt; 0, status enums. Indexes: orders(user_id, created_at), order_items(variant_id). Analytics: fact_order_items at line grain with dim_date, dim_customer (SCD2 on segment/country), dim_product; revenue additive, AOV computed as SUM(revenue)/COUNT(DISTINCT order_id).</div></details>\n\n</main>",
  "sql_interview": "<main>\n  <h1>SQL Interview Patterns</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-framework\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Approach Framework</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Grain, NULLs, ties, duplicates \u2192 CTEs \u2192 edge cases</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-nth\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Nth Highest &amp; Top-N per Group</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">DENSE_RANK, OFFSET, the NULL-on-empty requirement</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-dupes\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Duplicates</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Find them, delete all but one</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-running\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Running Totals &amp; Moving Averages</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Frames: ROWS vs RANGE</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-growth\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">MoM / YoY Growth</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">LAG and the missing-period trap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-streaks\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Streaks \u2014 Gaps &amp; Islands</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">date \u2212 ROW_NUMBER trick</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-retention\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Retention &amp; Cohorts</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Day-N retention, cohort matrix</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-funnel\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Funnels</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Conditional aggregation, ordered steps</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-dau\"><span class=\"rm-num\">09</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">DAU / MAU &amp; Stickiness</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Rolling distinct counts</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-sessions\"><span class=\"rm-num\">10</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Sessionization</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">LAG + flag + running SUM</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-percentiles\"><span class=\"rm-num\">11</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Median &amp; Percentiles</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">PERCENTILE_CONT vs DISC, manual median</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-pivot\"><span class=\"rm-num\">12</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Pivot / Unpivot</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">FILTER, CASE, crosstab, LATERAL VALUES</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-ratios\"><span class=\"rm-num\">13</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Ratios &amp; Percent of Total</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Integer division, NULLIF, SUM(SUM()) OVER</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-spine\"><span class=\"rm-num\">14</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Date Spine</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">generate_series + LEFT JOIN + COALESCE</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-antijoin\"><span class=\"rm-num\">15</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Did X but not Y</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Anti-joins and the NOT IN trap</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-pairs\"><span class=\"rm-num\">16</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Pairs &amp; Mutual Friends</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Self-joins, symmetric edges, a &lt; b</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-recursive\"><span class=\"rm-num\">17</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Hierarchies</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Recursive CTEs, depth, cycles</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\" data-target=\"si-qa\"><span class=\"rm-num\">18</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Interview Q&amp;A \u2014 Practice Problems</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">14 classic problems with full solutions</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>Clarify before typing:</strong> state the grain of every input table and of the output, how NULLs should be treated, what to do with ties, and whether duplicates exist. Half the \"wrong answers\" in SQL rounds are correct queries for a different question. Then build in CTEs, one transformation per step, so you can sanity-check each.</li>\n    <li><strong>ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK:</strong> for 100, 100, 90 they return 1,2,3 / 1,1,3 / 1,1,2. \"Nth highest distinct salary\" \u2192 DENSE_RANK; \"exactly one row per group\" \u2192 ROW_NUMBER with a deterministic tie-breaker; \"top 3 including ties\" \u2192 RANK or DENSE_RANK depending on whether ties consume slots.</li>\n    <li><strong>Default window frame trap:</strong> with ORDER BY and no frame, the frame is RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW \u2014 peers (equal ORDER BY values) are included together, so running totals jump on ties and LAST_VALUE returns the current row's peer group end, not the partition end. Write ROWS frames explicitly.</li>\n    <li><strong>LAG looks at the previous row, not the previous period:</strong> if a month or day is missing, LAG silently compares to the wrong period. Build a date spine (generate_series) or join on <code>date - INTERVAL '1 day'</code> when the question says \"previous day\".</li>\n    <li><strong>Gaps &amp; islands:</strong> for consecutive dates, <code>date \u2212 ROW_NUMBER()</code> is constant within a run. Group by that value to get each streak's start, end and length. Deduplicate dates first, or ROW_NUMBER will break the arithmetic.</li>\n    <li><strong>Ratios:</strong> integer / integer truncates in Postgres, SQL Server and SQLite (5/10 = 0) \u2014 multiply by 100.0 or cast. Wrap the denominator in NULLIF(x, 0) to return NULL instead of a division error. AVG(condition::int) or AVG(CASE ... THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END) is a clean rate.</li>\n    <li><strong>Anti-joins:</strong> use NOT EXISTS or LEFT JOIN \u2026 WHERE right.key IS NULL. Never NOT IN against a column that can contain NULL \u2014 a single NULL makes the predicate unknown for every row and returns nothing.</li>\n    <li><strong>Filters on the right side of a LEFT JOIN go in ON:</strong> putting <code>WHERE e.event_type = 'purchase'</code> after a LEFT JOIN discards the NULL-extended rows and silently turns it into an inner join \u2014 retention, funnels and date spines all lose their zero rows.</li>\n  </ol>\n</div>\n\n  <p>Running schema used throughout: <code>employees(id, name, dept_id, manager_id, salary, hire_date)</code>, <code>departments(id, name)</code>, <code>orders(order_id, user_id, order_date, amount, status)</code>, <code>users(user_id, signup_date, country)</code>, <code>events(user_id, event_time, event_type)</code>.</p>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. FRAMEWORK \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-framework\"><span class=\"num\">01</span>Approach Framework</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Definition \u2014 the four questions to ask out loud</span>\n    <p><strong>Grain:</strong> what is one row of each input, and what must one row of the output be? (\"one row per user per month\"). <strong>NULLs:</strong> can the key/measure be NULL; should NULL users count; should a missing value be 0 or absent? <strong>Ties:</strong> if two employees share the top salary, return both or one? <strong>Duplicates:</strong> can a user have two events at the same timestamp, or two rows per day? Then confirm the time window, timezone, and whether \"active\" means any event or a specific event type.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Skeleton: one CTE per idea, named for what it contains\nWITH base AS (            -- 1. filter + fix grain (dedupe, cast timestamps to dates)\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d\n  FROM events\n  WHERE event_time &gt;= DATE '2024-01-01'\n),\nper_user AS (             -- 2. aggregate to the grain you need\n  SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS active_days\n  FROM base\n  GROUP BY user_id\n)\nSELECT active_days, COUNT(*) AS users   -- 3. final shape\nFROM per_user\nGROUP BY active_days\nORDER BY active_days;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 verify like a tester</span>\n    <p>After writing it, run the query in your head on edge cases: a user with zero rows (do they disappear? should they?), a group where every value is NULL, a tie at the cut-off, a single-row table, an empty table (does a scalar question need NULL rather than no rows?), a duplicate event, a month with no data. Saying \"if a user has no orders the INNER JOIN drops them, so I'll LEFT JOIN from users\" is exactly what interviewers are listening for.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 join fan-out before aggregation</span>\n    <p>Joining <code>users \u2192 orders \u2192 events</code> and then summing <code>orders.amount</code> multiplies each order by its user's event count. Aggregate each child table to the join key in its own CTE first, then join one-row-per-key results. Quick check: <code>COUNT(*)</code> vs <code>COUNT(DISTINCT key)</code> after each join.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. NTH HIGHEST \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-nth\"><span class=\"num\">02</span>Nth Highest Value &amp; Top-N per Group</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 second highest distinct salary; NULL if none exists</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"second highest\", \"Nth largest\", \"return null if there is no such value\". The NULL requirement means the result must be one row even when nothing qualifies \u2014 a scalar subquery or an aggregate over the filtered set provides that.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- A) LIMIT/OFFSET inside a scalar subquery: an empty subquery yields NULL\nSELECT (\n  SELECT DISTINCT salary\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE salary IS NOT NULL\n  ORDER BY salary DESC\n  LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1          -- OFFSET N-1 for the Nth\n) AS second_highest;\n\n-- B) DENSE_RANK + MAX: MAX over zero rows returns NULL\nSELECT MAX(salary) AS second_highest\nFROM (\n  SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE salary IS NOT NULL\n) t\nWHERE rnk = 2;\n\n-- C) Classic no-window version\nSELECT MAX(salary) AS second_highest\nFROM employees\nWHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);</div>\n\n  <table class=\"tbl\">\n    <tr><th>salary</th><th>ROW_NUMBER</th><th>RANK</th><th>DENSE_RANK</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>100</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>100</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>90</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>80</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>3</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 DISTINCT, NULL sort order, and empty results</span>\n    <p>Without DISTINCT, <code>LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1</code> on (100, 100, 90) returns 100. In PostgreSQL and Oracle, <code>ORDER BY salary DESC</code> puts NULLs <strong>first</strong> (NULL sorts as larger than any value), so a NULL salary becomes \"the highest\" \u2014 filter it or write <code>DESC NULLS LAST</code>. MySQL and SQL Server sort NULLs last on DESC. Finally, a bare <code>SELECT ... WHERE rnk = 2</code> returns zero rows, not a NULL row \u2014 the wrapper is the point.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Top-N per group</h3>\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 top 3 earners in each department</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"per\", \"in each\", \"for every\" + \"top/highest/latest\". Window function partitioned by the group, then filter in an outer query (window results can't be referenced in WHERE of the same level because WHERE runs first).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT e.dept_id, e.name, e.salary,\n         DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees e\n  WHERE e.salary IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT d.name AS department, r.name AS employee, r.salary\nFROM ranked r\nJOIN departments d ON d.id = r.dept_id\nWHERE r.rnk &lt;= 3\nORDER BY d.name, r.salary DESC;\n\n-- Latest order per user: exactly one row, deterministic tie-break\nSELECT DISTINCT ON (user_id) user_id, order_id, order_date, amount\nFROM orders\nORDER BY user_id, order_date DESC, order_id DESC;   -- Postgres-only shorthand</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 which ranking function?</span>\n    <p>\"Top 3 salaries\" with DENSE_RANK can return more than 3 people (all ties at each of the 3 distinct values); ROW_NUMBER returns exactly 3 but drops a tied employee arbitrarily unless you add a tie-breaker. Ask which the interviewer wants. DISTINCT ON is Postgres-only; the portable version is ROW_NUMBER() = 1. Snowflake/BigQuery/Databricks support <code>QUALIFY rnk &lt;= 3</code> to filter windows without a subquery.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. DUPLICATES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-dupes\"><span class=\"num\">03</span>Duplicates \u2014 Find and Delete Keeping One</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 users_raw(id, email, created_at) has duplicate emails</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"duplicate\", \"appears more than once\", \"keep the earliest/latest\". Find with GROUP BY \u2026 HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1; delete with a self-join or ROW_NUMBER over the duplicate key.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Find duplicates and how many copies\nSELECT LOWER(TRIM(email)) AS email_norm, COUNT(*) AS copies\nFROM users_raw\nGROUP BY LOWER(TRIM(email))\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;\n\n-- See the full duplicate rows\nSELECT *\nFROM (\n  SELECT u.*, COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY email) AS copies\n  FROM users_raw u\n) t\nWHERE copies &gt; 1;\n\n-- Delete, keeping the smallest id per email (self-join form)\nDELETE FROM users_raw a\nUSING users_raw b\nWHERE a.email = b.email\n  AND a.id &gt; b.id;\n\n-- Delete, keeping the EARLIEST created row (ROW_NUMBER form; any rule you like)\nDELETE FROM users_raw\nWHERE id IN (\n  SELECT id\n  FROM (\n    SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY created_at, id) AS rn\n    FROM users_raw\n  ) t\n  WHERE rn &gt; 1\n);\n\n-- No primary key at all? Postgres physical row id\nDELETE FROM users_raw a\nUSING users_raw b\nWHERE a.email = b.email AND a.ctid &gt; b.ctid;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 NULLs and dialects</span>\n    <p>The self-join uses <code>=</code>, so rows with NULL email are never considered duplicates of each other; ROW_NUMBER's PARTITION BY puts all NULLs in one partition and <em>will</em> delete all but one. Decide which you want. MySQL forbids selecting from the table you're deleting from in a subquery (error 1093) \u2014 wrap it in a derived table or use the multi-table <code>DELETE a FROM t a JOIN t b ...</code> syntax. In a warehouse, often the safer move is <code>CREATE TABLE clean AS SELECT ... WHERE rn = 1</code> and swap.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. RUNNING TOTALS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-running\"><span class=\"num\">04</span>Running Totals &amp; Moving Averages</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 cumulative revenue per user; 7-day moving average of daily revenue</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"cumulative\", \"to date\", \"running\", \"rolling N-day\", \"trailing\". Window aggregate with ORDER BY and an explicit frame.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Running total per user (explicit ROWS frame)\nSELECT user_id, order_id, order_date, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id\n                         ORDER BY order_date, order_id\n                         ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS cum_amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'completed';\n\n-- 7-day moving average on a daily series\nWITH daily AS (\n  SELECT order_date AS d, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY order_date\n)\nSELECT d, revenue,\n       AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY d\n                          RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL '6 days' PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS ma7_calendar,\n       AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY d\n                          ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)                 AS ma7_rows,\n       COUNT(*)     OVER (ORDER BY d\n                          RANGE BETWEEN INTERVAL '6 days' PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS days_in_window\nFROM daily\nORDER BY d;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\">order_date | amount | SUM OVER (ORDER BY order_date)  | with ROWS frame\n2024-03-01 |     10 |   10                            |   10\n2024-03-02 |     20 |   50   (both 03-02 rows are     |   30\n2024-03-02 |     20 |   50    peers under RANGE)      |   50\n2024-03-03 |      5 |   55                            |   55</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 default frame and missing days</span>\n    <p>Omitting the frame gives RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING, which includes all peers with the same ORDER BY value \u2014 duplicate dates produce a \"running\" total that jumps two rows at once. Add a unique tie-breaker or use ROWS.<br><br>\n    <code>ROWS 6 PRECEDING</code> means \"6 previous rows\", which equals 7 calendar days only if no day is missing. Either use a value-based RANGE with an interval (Postgres 11+, Oracle; not SQL Server) or fill missing days with a date spine first. The first 6 days average fewer than 7 points \u2014 show <code>days_in_window</code> or set them to NULL with <code>CASE WHEN days_in_window = 7 ...</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. GROWTH \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-growth\"><span class=\"num\">05</span>MoM / YoY Growth with LAG</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 month-over-month revenue growth %</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"vs previous month/year\", \"growth\", \"change\", \"delta\". Aggregate to the period, then LAG.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)::date AS month,\n         SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY 1\n)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n       LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev_revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n             / NULLIF(LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month), 0), 2) AS mom_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month;\n\n-- YoY robust to missing months: join on the exact prior period\nWITH monthly AS (\n  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)::date AS month, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders WHERE status = 'completed' GROUP BY 1\n)\nSELECT cur.month, cur.revenue, prev.revenue AS revenue_last_year,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (cur.revenue - prev.revenue) / NULLIF(prev.revenue, 0), 2) AS yoy_pct\nFROM monthly cur\nLEFT JOIN monthly prev\n  ON prev.month = cur.month - INTERVAL '1 year'\nORDER BY cur.month;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 LAG(x, 12) is not \"last year\"</span>\n    <p>LAG(revenue, 12) is \"12 rows back\". One month with no orders and every YoY comparison after it is shifted. Use the self-join on <code>month - INTERVAL '1 year'</code> or a month spine. Also: growth from a negative or zero base is undefined \u2014 NULLIF handles zero; mention negatives. The first period's growth is NULL, not 0.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. STREAKS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-streaks\"><span class=\"num\">06</span>Consecutive Days &amp; Streaks \u2014 Gaps and Islands</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 each user's longest login streak; users with 3+ consecutive days</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"consecutive\", \"in a row\", \"streak\", \"uninterrupted\", \"periods of\". Islands = runs of consecutive values; gaps = breaks between them.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 why date \u2212 ROW_NUMBER works</span>\n    <p>Within a run of consecutive days, the date increases by 1 and ROW_NUMBER increases by 1, so their difference is constant. When a day is skipped, the date jumps by 2+ while ROW_NUMBER still jumps by 1, so the difference changes. That constant is a group id for the island.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">login_date | rn | login_date - rn\n2024-03-01 |  1 | 2024-02-29     \u2510\n2024-03-02 |  2 | 2024-02-29     \u251c island A, 3 days\n2024-03-03 |  3 | 2024-02-29     \u2518\n2024-03-05 |  4 | 2024-03-01     \u2510 island B, 2 days\n2024-03-06 |  5 | 2024-03-01     \u2518</div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH days AS (                               -- dedupe: one row per user per day\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d\n  FROM events\n),\ngrp AS (\n  SELECT user_id, d,\n         d - (ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d))::int AS island\n  FROM days\n),\nstreaks AS (\n  SELECT user_id, island,\n         MIN(d) AS streak_start, MAX(d) AS streak_end, COUNT(*) AS streak_len\n  FROM grp\n  GROUP BY user_id, island\n)\nSELECT user_id, MAX(streak_len) AS longest_streak\nFROM streaks\nGROUP BY user_id\nHAVING MAX(streak_len) &gt;= 3;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 duplicates and types</span>\n    <p>Two events on the same day give the same date two different row numbers and split the island \u2014 always dedupe to one row per day first. In Postgres, <code>date - integer</code> returns a date, but ROW_NUMBER returns BIGINT and there's no <code>date - bigint</code> operator, hence the <code>::int</code> cast. For integer sequences (ids) the same trick is <code>id - ROW_NUMBER()</code>; for \"same value repeated consecutively\" use <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id) - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY value ORDER BY id)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. RETENTION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-retention\"><span class=\"num\">07</span>User Retention &amp; Cohort Analysis</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 day-1 / day-7 retention by signup date</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"came back\", \"returned on day N\", \"retention\", \"cohort\". Day-N retention = users of a cohort active exactly N days after signup \u00f7 cohort size. Start from the cohort table and LEFT JOIN activity so the denominator keeps users who never returned.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH cohort AS (\n  SELECT user_id, signup_date\n  FROM users\n  WHERE signup_date &lt;= CURRENT_DATE - 7          -- only users old enough to be measured\n),\nactivity AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d\n  FROM events\n)\nSELECT c.signup_date,\n       COUNT(*)                                        AS cohort_size,\n       COUNT(a1.user_id)                               AS d1_retained,\n       COUNT(a7.user_id)                               AS d7_retained,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(a1.user_id) / COUNT(*), 1)  AS d1_pct,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(a7.user_id) / COUNT(*), 1)  AS d7_pct\nFROM cohort c\nLEFT JOIN activity a1 ON a1.user_id = c.user_id AND a1.d = c.signup_date + 1\nLEFT JOIN activity a7 ON a7.user_id = c.user_id AND a7.d = c.signup_date + 7\nGROUP BY c.signup_date\nORDER BY c.signup_date;</div>\n  <p>Because <code>activity</code> is deduped to one row per user per day, each LEFT JOIN matches at most one row, so <code>COUNT(*)</code> is still the cohort size. That's why the dedupe matters.</p>\n\n  <h3>Monthly cohort matrix</h3>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH cohorts AS (\n  SELECT user_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', signup_date)::date AS cohort_month\n  FROM users\n),\nactivity AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', event_time)::date AS active_month\n  FROM events\n),\njoined AS (\n  SELECT c.cohort_month, a.user_id,\n         (EXTRACT(YEAR FROM a.active_month) - EXTRACT(YEAR FROM c.cohort_month)) * 12\n       + (EXTRACT(MONTH FROM a.active_month) - EXTRACT(MONTH FROM c.cohort_month)) AS month_n\n  FROM cohorts c\n  JOIN activity a ON a.user_id = c.user_id\n),\nsizes AS (\n  SELECT cohort_month, COUNT(*) AS cohort_size FROM cohorts GROUP BY cohort_month\n)\nSELECT s.cohort_month, s.cohort_size,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT j.user_id) FILTER (WHERE j.month_n = 1) / s.cohort_size, 1) AS m1,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT j.user_id) FILTER (WHERE j.month_n = 2) / s.cohort_size, 1) AS m2,\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(DISTINCT j.user_id) FILTER (WHERE j.month_n = 3) / s.cohort_size, 1) AS m3\nFROM sizes s\nLEFT JOIN joined j ON j.cohort_month = s.cohort_month\nGROUP BY s.cohort_month, s.cohort_size\nORDER BY s.cohort_month;</div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">cohort_month | cohort_size |  m1  |  m2  |  m3\n2024-01-01   |        1200 | 41.5 | 33.0 | 29.8\n2024-02-01   |        1350 | 43.2 | 34.1 | NULL-ish (immature: shows 0.0 until data exists)</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 immature cohorts, ON vs WHERE, exact vs rolling</span>\n    <p>Recent cohorts haven't had N days yet, so they show artificially low retention \u2014 exclude them or display NULL. Put the day condition in the LEFT JOIN's ON clause; in WHERE it removes non-returning users and every cohort shows 100%. Clarify \"day-7 retention\": active exactly on day 7 (classic), or on any day 1\u20137 (\"rolling\"/\"unbounded\"), or in week 2? Each gives very different numbers. Also confirm whether events on the signup day itself count as activity.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. FUNNEL \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-funnel\"><span class=\"num\">08</span>Funnel Conversion</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 view \u2192 add_to_cart \u2192 checkout \u2192 purchase: users at each step and step conversion</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"funnel\", \"drop-off\", \"how many reached step X\", \"conversion from A to B\". Pivot one row per user with conditional aggregation, then count. Decide whether steps must happen in order.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Unordered: did the user ever do each step?\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'view')        AS viewed,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'add_to_cart') AS carted,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'checkout')    AS checked_out,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'purchase')    AS purchased\nFROM events\nWHERE event_time &gt;= DATE '2024-03-01' AND event_time &lt; DATE '2024-04-01';\n\n-- Ordered: each step must come after the previous one\nWITH per_user AS (\n  SELECT user_id,\n         MIN(event_time) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'view')        AS t_view,\n         MIN(event_time) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'add_to_cart') AS t_cart,\n         MIN(event_time) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'checkout')    AS t_checkout,\n         MIN(event_time) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'purchase')    AS t_purchase\n  FROM events\n  GROUP BY user_id\n),\nsteps AS (\n  SELECT COUNT(t_view)                                                  AS s1_view,\n         COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE t_cart &gt; t_view)                         AS s2_cart,\n         COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE t_checkout &gt; t_cart AND t_cart &gt; t_view)  AS s3_checkout,\n         COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE t_purchase &gt; t_checkout\n                            AND t_checkout &gt; t_cart AND t_cart &gt; t_view) AS s4_purchase\n  FROM per_user\n)\nSELECT *,\n       ROUND(100.0 * s2_cart     / NULLIF(s1_view, 0), 1)     AS view_to_cart_pct,\n       ROUND(100.0 * s3_checkout / NULLIF(s2_cart, 0), 1)     AS cart_to_checkout_pct,\n       ROUND(100.0 * s4_purchase / NULLIF(s3_checkout, 0), 1) AS checkout_to_purchase_pct,\n       ROUND(100.0 * s4_purchase / NULLIF(s1_view, 0), 1)     AS overall_pct\nFROM steps;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 counting events instead of users, and the first-occurrence approximation</span>\n    <p><code>COUNT(*)</code> of events counts a user who viewed 30 times as 30 viewers; use COUNT(DISTINCT user_id). NULL comparisons help you: <code>t_cart &gt; t_view</code> is NULL (not counted) when either is missing. The ordered version compares <em>first</em> occurrences \u2014 a user whose first cart predates their first view but who later views and carts again is excluded; for strict session-level funnels, sessionize first and compute per session. In MySQL/BigQuery without FILTER, use <code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type = 'view' THEN user_id END)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 09. DAU/MAU \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-dau\"><span class=\"num\">09</span>DAU / MAU &amp; Stickiness</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 daily DAU, trailing-30-day MAU, and DAU/MAU</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"active users\", \"engagement\", \"stickiness\". DAU/MAU \u2248 the fraction of a month the average monthly user is active (0.5 \u2192 ~15 days). Rolling distinct counts are the hard part: <code>COUNT(DISTINCT ...) OVER (...)</code> is not supported in PostgreSQL, SQL Server, or Snowflake windows with frames.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH days AS (\n  SELECT generate_series(DATE '2024-01-01', DATE '2024-03-31', INTERVAL '1 day')::date AS d\n),\nuser_days AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d\n  FROM events\n  WHERE event_time &gt;= DATE '2024-01-01' - 29          -- include lookback for early days\n    AND event_time &lt;  DATE '2024-04-01'\n)\nSELECT days.d,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT ud.user_id) FILTER (WHERE ud.d = days.d) AS dau,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT ud.user_id)                              AS mau_30d,\n       ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT ud.user_id) FILTER (WHERE ud.d = days.d)::numeric\n             / NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT ud.user_id), 0), 3)       AS stickiness\nFROM days\nLEFT JOIN user_days ud\n  ON ud.d BETWEEN days.d - 29 AND days.d\nGROUP BY days.d\nORDER BY days.d;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 summing DAUs, calendar vs trailing month</span>\n    <p>MAU is not SUM(DAU) \u2014 that counts a user once per active day. Calendar-month MAU (<code>COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC('month', ...)</code>) and trailing-30-day MAU give different numbers; say which. The range self-join is O(days \u00d7 rows-in-window); at scale use HyperLogLog sketches (BigQuery HLL_COUNT, Snowflake HLL, Postgres hll extension) or a per-user \"last active date\" table. Don't forget the lookback: without it, early January MAU only sees a few days of data.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 10. SESSIONIZATION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-sessions\"><span class=\"num\">10</span>Sessionization</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 split events into sessions with a 30-minute inactivity timeout</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"session\", \"visit\", \"idle for more than X minutes\", \"time spent\". Three steps: LAG the previous timestamp, flag a new session when the gap exceeds the threshold, running SUM of flags = session number.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH ordered AS (\n  SELECT user_id, event_time, event_type,\n         LAG(event_time) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY event_time) AS prev_time\n  FROM events\n),\nflagged AS (\n  SELECT *,\n         CASE WHEN prev_time IS NULL\n                OR event_time - prev_time &gt; INTERVAL '30 minutes'\n              THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS is_new_session\n  FROM ordered\n),\nnumbered AS (\n  SELECT *,\n         SUM(is_new_session) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY event_time\n                                   ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS session_no\n  FROM flagged\n)\nSELECT user_id, session_no,\n       MIN(event_time)                   AS session_start,\n       MAX(event_time)                   AS session_end,\n       MAX(event_time) - MIN(event_time) AS duration,\n       COUNT(*)                          AS events_in_session\nFROM numbered\nGROUP BY user_id, session_no\nORDER BY user_id, session_no;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 flag-and-cumsum is a general tool</span>\n    <p>\"Start a new group whenever condition X holds, then running-sum the starts\" solves any \"segment a sequence\" problem: trips separated by &gt;1 hour, price regimes that start on each change (<code>price IS DISTINCT FROM LAG(price)</code>), sensor episodes above a threshold. It's gaps-and-islands for arbitrary break conditions.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 duration and timestamp ties</span>\n    <p>A one-event session has duration 0, which drags down average session length \u2014 some definitions add the timeout or the time to the next event. Equal timestamps under the default RANGE frame would get the same running sum (fine here, but use ROWS to be explicit). Gap is computed after sorting per user, so make sure event_time is in one timezone.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 11. PERCENTILES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-percentiles\"><span class=\"num\">11</span>Median and Percentiles</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 median salary per department; p90 order value</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"median\", \"percentile\", \"p95\", \"typical\" (when data is skewed). PostgreSQL ordered-set aggregates: <code>PERCENTILE_CONT(f)</code> interpolates between values; <code>PERCENTILE_DISC(f)</code> returns an actual value from the data (the first whose cumulative distribution \u2265 f).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT dept_id,\n       PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median_salary,\n       PERCENTILE_DISC(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS median_disc,\n       PERCENTILE_CONT(0.9) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY salary) AS p90\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY dept_id;\n\n-- Portable median without percentile functions (works for odd and even counts)\nWITH r AS (\n  SELECT dept_id, salary,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary) AS rn,\n         COUNT(*)     OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id)                 AS cnt\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE salary IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) AS median_salary\nFROM r\nWHERE rn IN ((cnt + 1) / 2, (cnt + 2) / 2)      -- integer division: cnt=5 \u2192 3,3 ; cnt=4 \u2192 2,3\nGROUP BY dept_id;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 dialects and semantics</span>\n    <p>In Postgres, PERCENTILE_CONT is an aggregate only \u2014 it can't be used with OVER. In BigQuery it's the opposite: <code>PERCENTILE_CONT(x, 0.5) OVER (PARTITION BY ...)</code> is analytic-only (use APPROX_QUANTILES for aggregates). MySQL has neither (use the ROW_NUMBER method). Snowflake has MEDIAN(). For [1, 2, 3, 4], CONT median = 2.5, DISC median = 2. NULLs are ignored by the percentile aggregates; the manual version must filter them or they'll get row numbers.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 12. PIVOT \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-pivot\"><span class=\"num\">12</span>Pivot / Unpivot</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 revenue per user with one column per order status; and back again</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"one column per\", \"turn rows into columns\", \"wide format\", \"report by month as columns\". Conditional aggregation is the portable pivot; the categories must be known when writing the query.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- Pivot with FILTER (Postgres, SQLite 3.30+) or CASE (everywhere)\nSELECT user_id,\n       COALESCE(SUM(amount) FILTER (WHERE status = 'completed'), 0) AS completed,\n       COALESCE(SUM(amount) FILTER (WHERE status = 'cancelled'), 0) AS cancelled,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'refunded' THEN amount ELSE 0 END)    AS refunded\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY user_id;\n\n-- crosstab (tablefunc extension): use the 2-argument form\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS tablefunc;\nSELECT *\nFROM crosstab(\n  $$ SELECT user_id, status, SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 1, 2 $$,\n  $$ VALUES ('cancelled'), ('completed'), ('refunded') $$\n) AS ct(user_id BIGINT, cancelled NUMERIC, completed NUMERIC, refunded NUMERIC);\n\n-- Unpivot: quarterly_sales(region, q1, q2, q3, q4) \u2192 (region, quarter, sales)\nSELECT s.region, v.quarter, v.sales\nFROM quarterly_sales s\nCROSS JOIN LATERAL (\n  VALUES ('Q1', s.q1), ('Q2', s.q2), ('Q3', s.q3), ('Q4', s.q4)\n) AS v(quarter, sales);</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 CASE without ELSE, one-argument crosstab</span>\n    <p><code>SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN amount END)</code> returns NULL (not 0) for users with no such rows \u2014 add ELSE 0 or COALESCE. With COUNT, use <code>COUNT(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 END)</code>; <code>ELSE 0</code> would count every row because COUNT counts non-NULLs. The one-argument crosstab fills columns left to right, so a user missing a category gets values shifted into the wrong column \u2014 always pass the category list. Snowflake, SQL Server and BigQuery have native PIVOT/UNPIVOT syntax; the UNION ALL of one SELECT per column is the portable unpivot.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 13. RATIOS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-ratios\"><span class=\"num\">13</span>Ratios &amp; Percent of Total</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 cancellation rate per country; each country's share of revenue</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"rate\", \"percentage\", \"share\", \"fraction\", \"% of total\". Numerator and denominator at the same grain, decimal arithmetic, guarded denominator.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT u.country,\n       COUNT(*)                                                     AS orders,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE o.status = 'cancelled')               AS cancelled,\n       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE o.status = 'cancelled') / COUNT(*)    AS wrong_rate,  -- integer division \u2192 0\n       ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE o.status = 'cancelled')\n             / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0), 2)                              AS cancel_pct,\n       ROUND(AVG((o.status = 'cancelled')::int), 4)                 AS cancel_rate, -- same thing, 0..1\n       SUM(o.amount) FILTER (WHERE o.status = 'completed')          AS revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(o.amount) FILTER (WHERE o.status = 'completed')\n             / SUM(SUM(o.amount) FILTER (WHERE o.status = 'completed')) OVER (), 2) AS revenue_share_pct\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.user_id = o.user_id\nGROUP BY u.country\nORDER BY revenue DESC NULLS LAST;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Intuition \u2014 SUM(SUM(x)) OVER ()</span>\n    <p>Window functions run <em>after</em> GROUP BY, on the grouped rows. The inner SUM is the per-country aggregate; the outer SUM \u2026 OVER () adds those up across all countries without collapsing them. It's the cleanest percent-of-total, with no second scan or subquery. Use <code>OVER (PARTITION BY region)</code> for share within a parent group.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 integer division, ratio of averages, division by zero</span>\n    <p>In Postgres, SQL Server and SQLite, <code>7 / 10 = 0</code>. MySQL's <code>/</code> always returns a decimal (its <code>DIV</code> is integer), and BigQuery's <code>/</code> returns FLOAT64 (SAFE_DIVIDE returns NULL on zero). Write <code>100.0 *</code> first or cast to numeric. <code>NULLIF(den, 0)</code> turns a division-by-zero error into NULL. Finally, the average of per-day rates \u2260 the overall rate: a day with 2 orders and 1 cancel (50%) gets the same weight as a day with 10,000 orders. Compute SUM(numerator) / SUM(denominator) unless the question asks for an unweighted mean.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 14. DATE SPINE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-spine\"><span class=\"num\">14</span>Date Spine \u2014 Fill Missing Dates</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 daily order counts including days with zero orders</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"every day\", \"including days with no activity\", \"continuous time series\", \"for charting\", and silently inside any rolling-window or LAG question. GROUP BY can only output dates that exist in the data; you need a table of all dates to LEFT JOIN from.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH spine AS (\n  SELECT generate_series(DATE '2024-03-01', DATE '2024-03-31', INTERVAL '1 day')::date AS d\n),\ndaily AS (\n  SELECT order_date AS d, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders\n  WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY order_date\n)\nSELECT s.d,\n       COALESCE(dl.orders, 0)  AS orders,\n       COALESCE(dl.revenue, 0) AS revenue\nFROM spine s\nLEFT JOIN daily dl ON dl.d = s.d\nORDER BY s.d;\n\n-- Per user x per day (every user gets every day): CROSS JOIN the spine with users first\nSELECT u.user_id, s.d, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders\nFROM users u\nCROSS JOIN generate_series(DATE '2024-03-01', DATE '2024-03-07', INTERVAL '1 day') AS s(d)\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.user_id AND o.order_date = s.d::date\nGROUP BY u.user_id, s.d\nORDER BY u.user_id, s.d;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 COUNT(*) on a LEFT JOIN, WHERE on the right table</span>\n    <p>Aggregating directly after a LEFT JOIN, <code>COUNT(*)</code> returns 1 for an empty day (the spine row itself); count a right-side column (<code>COUNT(o.order_id)</code>). A filter like <code>WHERE o.status = 'completed'</code> eliminates NULL-extended rows and undoes the spine \u2014 put it in ON or in the pre-aggregated CTE. Dialects: BigQuery <code>GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY</code> + UNNEST; Snowflake <code>GENERATOR</code> + ROW_NUMBER or a calendar table; MySQL 8 recursive CTE (default depth limit 1000).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 15. ANTI-JOIN \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-antijoin\"><span class=\"num\">15</span>\"Users Who Did X but Not Y\" \u2014 Anti-Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 users who added to cart but never purchased</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"but not\", \"never\", \"without\", \"only\", \"haven't\". Semi-join for the positive condition, anti-join for the negative.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- 1) NOT EXISTS \u2014 preferred: NULL-safe, stops at first match\nSELECT DISTINCT e.user_id\nFROM events e\nWHERE e.event_type = 'add_to_cart'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n        SELECT 1 FROM events p\n        WHERE p.user_id = e.user_id AND p.event_type = 'purchase');\n\n-- 2) LEFT JOIN / IS NULL\nSELECT DISTINCT c.user_id\nFROM events c\nLEFT JOIN events p\n  ON p.user_id = c.user_id AND p.event_type = 'purchase'\nWHERE c.event_type = 'add_to_cart'\n  AND p.user_id IS NULL;\n\n-- 3) One pass with GROUP BY + HAVING (great for \"did A and B but not C\")\nSELECT user_id\nFROM events\nGROUP BY user_id\nHAVING BOOL_OR(event_type = 'add_to_cart')\n   AND NOT BOOL_OR(event_type = 'purchase');\n\n-- 4) Set difference\nSELECT user_id FROM events WHERE event_type = 'add_to_cart'\nEXCEPT\nSELECT user_id FROM events WHERE event_type = 'purchase';</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 NOT IN with NULLs</span>\n    <p><code>WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM purchases)</code> expands to <code>user_id &lt;&gt; a AND user_id &lt;&gt; b AND user_id &lt;&gt; NULL ...</code>; the last comparison is NULL, so the whole AND is never TRUE and the query returns <strong>zero rows</strong> if any purchase row has a NULL user_id. NOT EXISTS doesn't have this problem. EXCEPT removes duplicates (EXCEPT ALL keeps them). BOOL_OR is Postgres; elsewhere use <code>MAX(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) = 1</code> or <code>COUNTIF</code> in BigQuery.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 16. PAIRS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-pairs\"><span class=\"num\">16</span>Mutual Friends &amp; Pair Problems \u2014 Self-Joins</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 mutual friends of two users; the pairs of users with the most mutual friends</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"pairs\", \"both\", \"in common\", \"mutual\", \"compared to their manager\", \"bought together\". Join a table to itself; impose <code>a &lt; b</code> to avoid self-pairs and mirrored duplicates.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">-- friendships(user_a, user_b) stores each friendship ONCE: symmetrize first\nWITH edges AS (\n  SELECT user_a AS user_id, user_b AS friend_id FROM friendships\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT user_b, user_a FROM friendships\n)\n-- Mutual friends of users 1 and 2\nSELECT f1.friend_id\nFROM edges f1\nJOIN edges f2 ON f2.friend_id = f1.friend_id\nWHERE f1.user_id = 1 AND f2.user_id = 2;\n\n-- Top 10 pairs by number of mutual friends\nWITH edges AS (\n  SELECT user_a AS user_id, user_b AS friend_id FROM friendships\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT user_b, user_a FROM friendships\n)\nSELECT f1.user_id AS u1, f2.user_id AS u2, COUNT(*) AS mutual_friends\nFROM edges f1\nJOIN edges f2\n  ON  f1.friend_id = f2.friend_id      -- share a friend\n  AND f1.user_id &lt; f2.user_id          -- each unordered pair once, no self-pairs\nGROUP BY f1.user_id, f2.user_id\nORDER BY mutual_friends DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n\n-- Products frequently bought together (same order)\nSELECT a.product_id AS p1, b.product_id AS p2, COUNT(DISTINCT a.order_id) AS together\nFROM order_items a\nJOIN order_items b ON b.order_id = a.order_id AND a.product_id &lt; b.product_id\nGROUP BY a.product_id, b.product_id\nORDER BY together DESC\nLIMIT 20;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 asymmetric storage and quadratic blow-up</span>\n    <p>If friendships are stored once per pair, querying only <code>user_a = 1</code> misses half of user 1's friends \u2014 symmetrize with UNION ALL. Using <code>&lt;&gt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;</code> returns every pair twice. A self-join on a shared key is quadratic per key: a celebrity with 1M friends generates ~5\u00d710\u00b9\u00b9 pairs. Mention filtering hubs or sampling at scale.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 17. RECURSIVE \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-recursive\"><span class=\"num\">17</span>Hierarchical Data \u2014 Recursive CTEs</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Problem \u2014 full org chart with depth and path; everyone under a given manager</span>\n    <p><strong>Cue:</strong> \"all levels\", \"reports directly or indirectly\", \"chain of command\", \"ancestors\", \"bill of materials\", \"category tree\". A recursive CTE = anchor query UNION ALL a recursive member that joins the CTE to the base table; it iterates until the recursive step returns no rows.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH RECURSIVE org AS (\n  -- anchor: the root(s)\n  SELECT id, name, manager_id, 1 AS depth, ARRAY[id] AS path, name::text AS chain\n  FROM employees\n  WHERE manager_id IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  -- recursive step: direct reports of the previous level\n  SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id, o.depth + 1, o.path || e.id, o.chain || ' &gt; ' || e.name\n  FROM employees e\n  JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id\n  WHERE NOT e.id = ANY(o.path)                    -- cycle guard\n)\nSELECT id, name, depth, chain\nFROM org\nORDER BY path;\n\n-- All direct + indirect reports of employee 7, and the count\nWITH RECURSIVE reports AS (\n  SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 7\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT e.id, e.name FROM employees e JOIN reports r ON e.manager_id = r.id\n)\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS total_reports FROM reports;</div>\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trap \u2014 cycles, depth limits, UNION vs UNION ALL</span>\n    <p>Bad data (A manages B, B manages A) makes the recursion infinite. Guard with a visited path array (as above), or the Postgres 14+ <code>CYCLE id SET is_cycle USING path</code> clause. UNION instead of UNION ALL also deduplicates and can stop simple cycles, at extra cost. Limits: SQL Server stops at 100 levels by default (<code>OPTION (MAXRECURSION n)</code>); MySQL at 1000 (<code>cte_max_recursion_depth</code>). The recursive member can't use aggregates or reference the CTE twice in Postgres.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 18. Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"si-qa\"><span class=\"num\">18</span>Interview Q&amp;A \u2014 Practice Problems</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><h3>Say your answer out loud before revealing</h3><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Department top three: list every employee whose salary is among the three highest distinct salaries in their department, with the department name.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">\"Three highest distinct salaries\" means ties share a rank and don't consume extra slots \u2192 DENSE_RANK. Filter in an outer query.\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT e.name, e.salary, e.dept_id,\n         DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY e.dept_id ORDER BY e.salary DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM employees e\n)\nSELECT d.name AS department, r.name AS employee, r.salary\nFROM ranked r\nJOIN departments d ON d.id = r.dept_id\nWHERE r.rnk &lt;= 3\nORDER BY department, r.salary DESC;</div>\n  Edge cases: a department with only two distinct salaries returns everyone; employees with NULL dept_id are dropped by the inner join (ask if they should appear); NULL salaries would rank first under DESC in Postgres \u2014 add <code>NULLS LAST</code> or filter them.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Ride cancellation rate: trips(id, client_id, driver_id, status, request_date) and accounts(account_id, is_banned, role). For each day between 2024-10-01 and 2024-10-03, compute the share of trips cancelled (by rider or driver), counting only trips where neither the client nor the driver is banned. Round to 2 decimals.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Join accounts twice (once per role), filter banned in the join, and average a 0/1 flag. Statuses: 'completed', 'cancelled_by_driver', 'cancelled_by_client'.\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT t.request_date AS day,\n       ROUND(AVG(CASE WHEN t.status &lt;&gt; 'completed' THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END), 2) AS cancellation_rate\nFROM trips t\nJOIN accounts c ON c.account_id = t.client_id AND NOT c.is_banned\nJOIN accounts d ON d.account_id = t.driver_id AND NOT d.is_banned\nWHERE t.request_date BETWEEN DATE '2024-10-01' AND DATE '2024-10-03'\nGROUP BY t.request_date\nORDER BY day;</div>\n  Why AVG of 1.0/0: it's numerator/denominator at the same grain with no integer-division risk. A day where every trip involves a banned user disappears entirely \u2014 if the spec wants it with 0 or NULL, LEFT JOIN from a date spine. Using <code>LIKE 'cancelled%'</code> instead of <code>&lt;&gt; 'completed'</code> is safer if new statuses (e.g. 'in_progress') can exist.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Next-day return: activity(player_id, event_date, games_played) has one row per player per login day. What fraction of players logged in again on the day immediately after their first login? Round to 2 decimals.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Find each player's first day, LEFT JOIN to activity on first_day + 1, divide matches by players.\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH firsts AS (\n  SELECT player_id, MIN(event_date) AS first_day\n  FROM activity\n  GROUP BY player_id\n)\nSELECT ROUND(COUNT(a.player_id)::numeric / COUNT(*), 2) AS fraction\nFROM firsts f\nLEFT JOIN activity a\n  ON a.player_id = f.player_id\n AND a.event_date = f.first_day + 1;</div>\n  COUNT(*) counts all players (each has exactly one firsts row and at most one match, since (player_id, event_date) is unique); COUNT(a.player_id) counts only matches. The <code>::numeric</code> cast avoids integer division. Common wrong answer: \"logged in on any two consecutive days\" \u2014 the question is specifically day 1 relative to the <em>first</em> login.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Repeated readings: readings(id, num) with consecutive ids. Return every number that appears at least three times in a row.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Compare each row to its neighbours with LAG/LEAD; DISTINCT because a run of 5 would output the number 3 times.\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT DISTINCT num AS consecutive_num\nFROM (\n  SELECT num,\n         LAG(num)  OVER (ORDER BY id) AS prev_num,\n         LEAD(num) OVER (ORDER BY id) AS next_num\n  FROM readings\n) t\nWHERE num = prev_num AND num = next_num;</div>\n  For \"at least K in a row\" generalize with gaps-and-islands: <code>id - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY num ORDER BY id)</code> is constant within a run of the same number (if ids are gap-free; otherwise use <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY id)</code> in place of id), then <code>GROUP BY num, grp HAVING COUNT(*) &gt;= K</code>. Old self-join solutions on id+1, id+2 break when ids have gaps.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Warmer than yesterday: weather(id, record_date, temperature). Return the ids of days that were warmer than the previous calendar day.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">\"Previous calendar day\", not previous row \u2014 so join on the date arithmetic rather than LAG.\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT w.id\nFROM weather w\nJOIN weather y ON y.record_date = w.record_date - 1\nWHERE w.temperature &gt; y.temperature;</div>\n  With LAG you'd need an extra check: <code>record_date - LAG(record_date) OVER (ORDER BY record_date) = 1</code>. If dates are missing, LAG alone would compare to two days ago \u2014 the classic trap. MySQL equivalent: <code>DATEDIFF(w.record_date, y.record_date) = 1</code>; SQL Server: <code>DATEADD(day, -1, w.record_date)</code>.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Second highest salary, returning NULL when it doesn't exist. Then generalize to the Nth.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT (\n  SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM employees\n  WHERE salary IS NOT NULL\n  ORDER BY salary DESC\n  LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1\n) AS second_highest;\n\n-- Nth as a function (Postgres)\nCREATE FUNCTION nth_highest_salary(n INT) RETURNS NUMERIC AS $$\n  SELECT MAX(salary) FROM (\n    SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk\n    FROM employees WHERE salary IS NOT NULL\n  ) t\n  WHERE rnk = n;\n$$ LANGUAGE sql STABLE;</div>\n  The scalar subquery returns NULL when empty; MAX over zero rows also returns NULL. DISTINCT/DENSE_RANK make duplicates of the top salary not count as \"second\". OFFSET can't be an expression like N-1 in MySQL functions without a variable \u2014 compute it first.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Find employees who earn more than their own manager.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Self-join: one alias for the employee, one for the manager.\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT e.name AS employee, e.salary, m.name AS manager, m.salary AS manager_salary\nFROM employees e\nJOIN employees m ON m.id = e.manager_id\nWHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;</div>\n  Inner join excludes employees with no manager (the CEO), which is correct here. If either salary is NULL the comparison is NULL and the row is excluded \u2014 mention it.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Customers who never ordered: list users with no rows in orders. Then: users who signed up in 2024 and have no completed order.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">\n  <div class=\"fx\">SELECT u.user_id\nFROM users u\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.user_id = u.user_id);\n\nSELECT u.user_id\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o\n  ON o.user_id = u.user_id AND o.status = 'completed'   -- condition in ON, not WHERE\nWHERE u.signup_date &gt;= DATE '2024-01-01' AND u.signup_date &lt; DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND o.order_id IS NULL;</div>\n  In the second query, moving <code>o.status = 'completed'</code> to WHERE would make the query return nothing (status is NULL for the anti-joined rows). Avoid <code>NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders)</code> \u2014 one NULL user_id in orders returns zero rows.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Login streaks: from logins(user_id, login_ts), return users who logged in on at least 5 consecutive calendar days, with the start and end of their longest streak.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Dedupe to dates, gaps-and-islands, then pick the longest island per user.\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH d AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT user_id, login_ts::date AS day FROM logins\n),\ng AS (\n  SELECT user_id, day,\n         day - (ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY day))::int AS grp\n  FROM d\n),\ns AS (\n  SELECT user_id, MIN(day) AS start_day, MAX(day) AS end_day, COUNT(*) AS len,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, MIN(day) DESC) AS pick\n  FROM g\n  GROUP BY user_id, grp\n)\nSELECT user_id, start_day, end_day, len\nFROM s\nWHERE pick = 1 AND len &gt;= 5;</div>\n  The window in <code>s</code> runs after GROUP BY, so it can rank aggregated islands; the tie-break picks the most recent of equally long streaks. Timezone matters: casting a UTC timestamp to date may split a local day.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Month-over-month growth: for each month, total completed revenue and the % change from the previous month, rounded to 2 decimals, with months that had no orders shown as 0.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Spine of months \u2192 LEFT JOIN monthly revenue \u2192 LAG. The spine is what makes LAG mean \"previous month\".\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH months AS (\n  SELECT generate_series(DATE_TRUNC('month', MIN(order_date)),\n                         DATE_TRUNC('month', MAX(order_date)),\n                         INTERVAL '1 month')::date AS month\n  FROM orders\n),\nrev AS (\n  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)::date AS month, SUM(amount) AS revenue\n  FROM orders WHERE status = 'completed'\n  GROUP BY 1\n),\nfilled AS (\n  SELECT m.month, COALESCE(r.revenue, 0) AS revenue\n  FROM months m LEFT JOIN rev r USING (month)\n)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n             / NULLIF(LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month), 0), 2) AS mom_pct\nFROM filled\nORDER BY month;</div>\n  Growth after a zero month is NULL (undefined), not infinity \u2014 state that choice.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Most-connected person: requests_accepted(requester_id, accepter_id) \u2014 each row is a friendship. Who has the most friends, and how many?</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">A user's friends come from both columns, so stack them with UNION ALL before counting.\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH ids AS (\n  SELECT requester_id AS user_id FROM requests_accepted\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT accepter_id FROM requests_accepted\n)\nSELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS num_friends\nFROM ids\nGROUP BY user_id\nORDER BY num_friends DESC\nFETCH FIRST 1 ROWS WITH TIES;          -- Postgres 13+: returns all tied leaders</div>\n  UNION (without ALL) would collapse a user's multiple friendships into one row and undercount. If the same pair can appear twice (A\u2192B and B\u2192A), dedupe pairs first with <code>LEAST/GREATEST</code>.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Median order value per country \u2014 without using PERCENTILE_CONT.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH r AS (\n  SELECT u.country, o.amount,\n         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY u.country ORDER BY o.amount) AS rn,\n         COUNT(*)     OVER (PARTITION BY u.country)                  AS cnt\n  FROM orders o\n  JOIN users u ON u.user_id = o.user_id\n  WHERE o.status = 'completed' AND o.amount IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT country, AVG(amount) AS median_amount\nFROM r\nWHERE rn BETWEEN cnt / 2.0 AND cnt / 2.0 + 1\nGROUP BY country;</div>\n  Check: cnt = 4 \u2192 rn in [2, 3] \u2192 average of the 2nd and 3rd; cnt = 5 \u2192 rn in [2.5, 3.5] \u2192 only the 3rd. With PERCENTILE_CONT available: <code>PERCENTILE_CONT(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY amount)</code> grouped by country.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Delete duplicate emails: person(id, email). Delete rows so each email keeps only the row with the smallest id.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">\n  <div class=\"fx\">DELETE FROM person p\nUSING person q\nWHERE p.email = q.email\n  AND p.id &gt; q.id;</div>\n  Every row that has a same-email row with a smaller id is deleted; the minimum id per email has no such partner and survives. MySQL syntax: <code>DELETE p FROM person p JOIN person q ON p.email = q.email AND p.id &gt; q.id;</code>. Case/whitespace variants (<code>A@x.com</code> vs <code>a@x.com </code>) are not equal \u2014 normalize if the spec says emails are case-insensitive.</div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qmark\">Q</span>Price as of a date: price_changes(product_id, new_price, change_date). Every product starts at price 10. Return each product's price on 2024-08-16.</summary><div class=\"qa-body\">Latest change on or before the date per product; products with no such change default to 10. Start from all products so none disappear.\n  <div class=\"fx\">WITH latest AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT ON (product_id) product_id, new_price\n  FROM price_changes\n  WHERE change_date &lt;= DATE '2024-08-16'\n  ORDER BY product_id, change_date DESC\n)\nSELECT p.product_id, COALESCE(l.new_price, 10) AS price\nFROM (SELECT DISTINCT product_id FROM price_changes) p\nLEFT JOIN latest l USING (product_id)\nORDER BY p.product_id;</div>\n  Portable version: ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY change_date DESC) = 1 in place of DISTINCT ON. The trap is filtering the date in the outer WHERE, which drops products whose every change is after the date \u2014 they must still appear with 10.</div></details>\n\n</main>",
  "sql": "<main>\n  <h1>SQL for Data Science</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"roadmap-wrap\">\n    <h2 class=\"roadmap-title\">What we'll cover</h2>\n    <div class=\"roadmap-grid\">\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">01</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Query Foundations</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">SELECT, WHERE, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">02</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Aggregation & GROUP BY</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">The pipeline \u2014 and HAVING vs WHERE</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">03</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">JOINs</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">INNER, LEFT, anti-join, self-join</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">04</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Subqueries & CTEs</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Correlated cost, EXISTS vs IN, recursion</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">05</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Window Functions</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Why rows don't collapse \u2014 frames, ranking</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">06</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Indexes & Optimization</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">B-tree, the sargability trap, EXPLAIN</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">07</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Transactions & ACID</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Isolation levels \u2014 what each prevents</span></div></div>\n      <div class=\"rm-item rm-done\"><span class=\"rm-num\">08</span><div class=\"rm-text\"><span class=\"rm-label\">Data Modeling</span><span class=\"rm-sub\">Normalization as dependency logic</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Revision</h2>\n  <ol>\n    <li><strong>The logical execution order is not the written order:</strong> SQL is written SELECT&#8594;FROM&#8594;WHERE&#8594;GROUP BY&#8594;HAVING&#8594;ORDER BY, but it executes FROM&#8594;WHERE&#8594;GROUP BY&#8594;HAVING&#8594;SELECT&#8594;ORDER BY&#8594;LIMIT. This single fact explains why you can't reference a SELECT alias in WHERE (WHERE runs before SELECT computes it), why WHERE can't filter on an aggregate (aggregates don't exist until GROUP BY runs), and why HAVING can. Memorise this order; half of all SQL confusion dissolves once you internalise it.</li>\n    <li><strong>WHERE filters rows, HAVING filters groups:</strong> WHERE runs before aggregation on individual rows &#8212; it cannot see SUM/COUNT/AVG because those don't exist yet. HAVING runs after GROUP BY on the aggregated result &#8212; it exists specifically to filter on aggregates. <code>WHERE amount &gt; 100</code> keeps individual sales over 100 then sums them; <code>HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 100</code> sums every group then keeps groups whose total exceeds 100. Different pipeline stages, different results.</li>\n    <li><strong>INNER keeps matches, LEFT keeps all left rows:</strong> INNER JOIN returns only rows with a match on both sides. LEFT JOIN returns every left row, filling NULLs where the right has no match. The anti-join pattern &#8212; <code>LEFT JOIN ... WHERE right.id IS NULL</code> &#8212; finds left rows with no right match (customers who never ordered, users who churned). This is the single most useful non-obvious JOIN pattern in analytics.</li>\n    <li><strong>Correlated subqueries are O(n&#178;):</strong> a correlated subquery references the outer query and re-executes once per outer row. On a 1M-row table that's 1M subquery executions. EXISTS short-circuits (stops at first match) so it's cheaper than IN when the subquery is large; IN materialises the entire subquery result set first. The general fix for a slow correlated subquery is to rewrite it as a JOIN or a window function &#8212; both let the engine process the set once.</li>\n    <li><strong>Window functions compute across rows without collapsing them:</strong> GROUP BY collapses N rows into one per group. A window function (<code>OVER (PARTITION BY ...)</code>) computes the same aggregate but attaches it to every original row &#8212; you keep all N rows AND get the group-level value on each. This is why \"show each sale alongside its region's total\" needs a window, not GROUP BY. ROW_NUMBER/RANK/LAG/LEAD plus running totals via <code>SUM() OVER (ORDER BY ...)</code> cover ~90% of real window use.</li>\n    <li><strong>An index is a sorted B-tree; a function on the column destroys it:</strong> a B-tree index stores column values sorted, giving O(log n) lookup instead of O(n) scan. But <code>WHERE YEAR(date) = 2024</code> applies a function to every stored value, so the sorted order is useless and the engine falls back to a full scan. Rewrite as <code>WHERE date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND date &lt; '2025-01-01'</code> &#8212; now the range maps directly onto the sorted index. This property is called sargability, and it's the most common real-world query-performance bug.</li>\n    <li><strong>ACID isolation levels trade correctness for concurrency:</strong> READ UNCOMMITTED allows dirty reads (seeing another transaction's uncommitted data). READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads (same query, different result within one transaction). REPEATABLE READ prevents that but allows phantoms (new rows appearing). SERIALIZABLE prevents all three but serialises conflicting transactions, killing throughput. Each level up costs concurrency. Know which anomaly each level permits &#8212; it's a guaranteed senior-interview question.</li>\n    <li><strong>Normalization is about functional dependencies, not memorised rules:</strong> 1NF = atomic values (no lists in a cell). 2NF = no partial dependency (in a composite key, no non-key column depends on only part of the key). 3NF = no transitive dependency (no non-key column depends on another non-key column). The point is eliminating update anomalies: if a fact lives in two places, the two copies can disagree. Star schemas in warehouses deliberately denormalise for read speed &#8212; know both directions and when each applies.</li>\n  </ol>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 01. FOUNDATIONS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"foundations\">01 &#183; Query Foundations</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The one mental model that fixes everything: logical execution order</span>\n    <p>You <strong>write</strong> SQL in this order:<br>\n    <strong>SELECT &#8594; FROM &#8594; WHERE &#8594; GROUP BY &#8594; HAVING &#8594; ORDER BY &#8594; LIMIT</strong><br><br>\n    The database <strong>executes</strong> it in a completely different order:<br>\n    <strong>FROM &#8594; WHERE &#8594; GROUP BY &#8594; HAVING &#8594; SELECT &#8594; ORDER BY &#8594; LIMIT</strong><br><br>\n    Almost every \"why doesn't this work\" in SQL traces back to this mismatch. The engine builds a working set of rows (FROM), filters them (WHERE), groups them (GROUP BY), filters groups (HAVING), <em>then</em> computes your output columns (SELECT), sorts (ORDER BY), and truncates (LIMIT).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Why this immediately explains three \"gotchas\"</span>\n    <p><strong>1. You can't use a SELECT alias in WHERE.</strong> <code>SELECT price*qty AS total FROM t WHERE total &gt; 100</code> fails &#8212; WHERE runs before SELECT computes <code>total</code>. It doesn't exist yet.<br><br>\n    <strong>2. You can't filter an aggregate in WHERE.</strong> <code>WHERE SUM(x) &gt; 100</code> fails &#8212; SUM doesn't exist until GROUP BY runs, which is after WHERE.<br><br>\n    <strong>3. You CAN use a SELECT alias in ORDER BY.</strong> <code>ORDER BY total</code> works &#8212; ORDER BY runs after SELECT, so the alias exists by then.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>The core clauses, as fast reference:</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> region, product        <span class=\"c\">-- DISTINCT dedupes the final rows</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sales\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> amount &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>                    <span class=\"c\">-- row filter, runs early</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> region <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (<span class=\"g\">'North'</span>, <span class=\"g\">'South'</span>)   <span class=\"c\">-- IN = shorthand for OR chain</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> product <span class=\"k\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"g\">'A%'</span>            <span class=\"c\">-- % = any chars, _ = one char</span>\n  <span class=\"k\">AND</span> ship_date <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NOT</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>       <span class=\"c\">-- never use = NULL, always IS NULL</span>\n<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> amount <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>\n<span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">10</span> <span class=\"k\">OFFSET</span> <span class=\"y\">20</span>;             <span class=\"c\">-- skip 20, take next 10 (pagination)</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The NULL trap that catches everyone</span>\n    <p><code>NULL</code> is not a value &#8212; it's \"unknown.\" Any comparison with NULL returns NULL (not TRUE, not FALSE). So <code>WHERE x = NULL</code> matches nothing, ever. You must use <code>IS NULL</code> / <code>IS NOT NULL</code>. Worse: <code>WHERE x != 5</code> silently <em>excludes</em> rows where x is NULL, because <code>NULL != 5</code> is NULL, not TRUE. If you want those rows, you need <code>WHERE x != 5 OR x IS NULL</code>. This is the single most common source of silently-wrong query results.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) &#8212; a real distinction</span>\n    <p><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows, including rows that are all NULL. <code>COUNT(col)</code> counts non-NULL values in that column &#8212; NULLs are skipped. So <code>COUNT(*) - COUNT(email)</code> tells you how many rows have a missing email. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</code> counts unique non-NULL values. Interviewers ask this to check whether you actually understand NULL semantics.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 02. AGGREGATION \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"aggregation\">02 &#183; Aggregation, GROUP BY &amp; the HAVING/WHERE distinction</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">What GROUP BY actually does</span>\n    <p>GROUP BY collapses rows that share a value into a single output row, and every column in SELECT must then be either (a) a column you grouped by, or (b) wrapped in an aggregate function. This rule follows directly from the collapse: if you group 10 sales rows into one \"North\" row, and you ask for <code>amount</code>, the engine has 10 different amounts and no way to pick one &#8212; so it forces you to aggregate (SUM, AVG, MAX) or group by it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>Here is the distinction that gets asked in nearly every SQL screen. These two queries both \"filter on 100\" and return <strong>completely different results</strong>:</p>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Query A: filter rows FIRST, then aggregate what survives</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> region, <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sales\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> amount &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>          <span class=\"c\">-- keeps individual sales over 100</span>\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> region;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Query B: aggregate ALL rows, then filter the groups</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> region, <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sales\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> region\n<span class=\"k\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(amount) &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- keeps regions whose TOTAL exceeds 100</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Trace the pipeline and the difference is obvious</span>\n    <p>Say the North region has three sales: 60, 60, 60.<br><br>\n    <strong>Query A</strong>: WHERE runs first and throws out all three (none exceeds 100). North contributes nothing. Total for North: it doesn't even appear.<br><br>\n    <strong>Query B</strong>: no row filter. All three sum to 180. HAVING checks 180 &gt; 100 &#8594; true. North appears with total 180.<br><br>\n    Same \"100,\" opposite outcomes &#8212; because WHERE filters the <em>inputs</em> to the sum and HAVING filters the <em>output</em> of the sum. This is the whole point of having two filter clauses.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Use both together &#8212; and know why the order is efficient</span>\n    <p>Real queries often use both: <code>WHERE</code> to cut rows before the expensive grouping, then <code>HAVING</code> to filter the result. <code>WHERE region != 'Test' ... GROUP BY region HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 1000</code>. Putting the cheap row-filter in WHERE first means the engine groups fewer rows &#8212; filtering early is almost always faster than filtering late. Putting a non-aggregate condition in HAVING when it could go in WHERE is a performance mistake, even though it returns the same answer.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\"># Conditional aggregation \u2014 count/sum with a condition inside the aggregate</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> region,\n       <span class=\"k\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total_orders,\n       <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'returned'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">ELSE</span> <span class=\"y\">0</span> <span class=\"k\">END</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> returns,\n       <span class=\"k\">AVG</span>(<span class=\"k\">CASE</span> <span class=\"k\">WHEN</span> status = <span class=\"g\">'completed'</span> <span class=\"k\">THEN</span> amount <span class=\"k\">END</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> avg_completed\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> region;</div>\n  <p>Conditional aggregation (<code>SUM(CASE WHEN ...)</code>) is how you pivot and compute segmented metrics in one pass &#8212; a heavily-used real-world pattern.</p>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 03. JOINS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"joins\">03 &#183; JOINs</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The four joins, precisely</span>\n    <p><strong>INNER JOIN</strong>: rows that match on both sides. No match = row disappears.<br>\n    <strong>LEFT JOIN</strong>: all rows from the left table; right-side columns are NULL where there's no match.<br>\n    <strong>RIGHT JOIN</strong>: mirror of LEFT (rarely used &#8212; people flip the table order and use LEFT).<br>\n    <strong>FULL OUTER JOIN</strong>: all rows from both sides, NULLs filled where either side is missing.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy: two guest lists</span>\n    <p>You have a list of invited guests (left) and a list of people who showed up (right). <strong>INNER</strong> = people both invited and present. <strong>LEFT</strong> = everyone invited, marked present or absent. <strong>Anti-join</strong> (LEFT + <code>WHERE present.name IS NULL</code>) = invited but didn't show. That last one &#8212; finding what's missing &#8212; is the pattern you'll reach for constantly and it's not obvious to beginners.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Anti-join: customers who have never placed an order</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> c.customer_id, c.name\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> customers c\n<span class=\"k\">LEFT JOIN</span> orders o <span class=\"k\">ON</span> c.customer_id = o.customer_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> o.customer_id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span>;      <span class=\"c\">-- no matching order = churned/never-active</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Self-join: find employees who earn more than their manager</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> e.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> employee, m.name <span class=\"k\">AS</span> manager\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">JOIN</span> employees m <span class=\"k\">ON</span> e.manager_id = m.employee_id\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> e.salary &gt; m.salary;         <span class=\"c\">-- same table aliased twice</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The JOIN that silently multiplies your rows</span>\n    <p>If the join key is not unique on the right side, each left row matches multiple right rows and your result set <em>multiplies</em>. Join <code>orders</code> to <code>order_items</code> (one order, many items) and then <code>SUM(orders.total)</code> &#8212; you've now counted each order's total once per item, inflating the sum. This \"fan-out\" is the most common cause of wrong aggregate numbers after a join. Fix: aggregate to the right grain first (in a CTE), then join. Always know the cardinality of your join keys before you trust a post-join SUM.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 04. SUBQUERIES & CTEs \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"subqueries\">04 &#183; Subqueries, CTEs &amp; the correlated-subquery cost</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">CTE vs subquery</span>\n    <p>A <strong>CTE</strong> (Common Table Expression, the <code>WITH</code> clause) is a named temporary result you define once and reference by name &#8212; readable, and chainable (each CTE can reference the previous). A <strong>subquery</strong> is an inline query nested inside another. Functionally similar; CTEs win on readability and on avoiding repetition when you need the same intermediate result twice.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">WITH</span> regional_totals <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> region, <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> total\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sales\n    <span class=\"k\">GROUP BY</span> region\n),\nranked <span class=\"k\">AS</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> region, total,\n           <span class=\"k\">RANK</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> total <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rk\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> regional_totals            <span class=\"c\">-- second CTE references the first</span>\n)\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> ranked <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> rk &lt;= <span class=\"y\">3</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- top 3 regions by revenue</span></div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Why a correlated subquery can be O(n&#178;) &#8212; and EXISTS vs IN</span>\n    <p>A <strong>correlated</strong> subquery references a column from the outer query, so it must re-run <em>once for every outer row</em>. Outer table has 1M rows &#8594; the subquery executes 1M times. That's the O(n&#178;) blow-up.<br><br>\n    <strong>EXISTS</strong> stops at the first matching row (short-circuit) &#8212; it only needs to know if <em>any</em> match exists, so it's cheap even on a large subquery.<br><br>\n    <strong>IN</strong> materialises the <em>entire</em> subquery result into a set, then checks membership. On a large subquery that set can be huge.<br><br>\n    Rule of thumb: <code>EXISTS</code> when the subquery is big and you only need existence; <code>IN</code> when the subquery returns a small, fixed list. And a NULL inside an <code>IN</code> list breaks <code>NOT IN</code> entirely (it returns no rows) &#8212; another reason to prefer <code>NOT EXISTS</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- Correlated (slow on big tables): re-runs per outer row</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees e\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; (<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> dept = e.dept);\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Same result via window function: engine scans the set ONCE</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> name, salary,\n           <span class=\"k\">AVG</span>(salary) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> dept) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> dept_avg\n    <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> employees\n) t\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> salary &gt; dept_avg;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Recursive CTEs exist &#8212; know they're the tool for hierarchies</span>\n    <p>When you need to walk a tree (org chart, category hierarchy, bill-of-materials), a <code>WITH RECURSIVE</code> CTE is the tool: an anchor query (the starting rows) UNION ALL a recursive query that references the CTE itself. You don't need to memorise the syntax cold, but you must recognise that \"traverse arbitrary-depth hierarchy in SQL\" &#8594; recursive CTE, because interviewers ask how you'd handle it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 05. WINDOW FUNCTIONS \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"windows\">05 &#183; Window Functions</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The defining property: aggregate without collapsing</span>\n    <p>GROUP BY takes N rows and returns one row per group &#8212; you lose the individual rows. A <strong>window function</strong> computes a value <em>across a set of rows</em> (the \"window\") but returns it <em>alongside every original row</em>. You keep all N rows AND get the group-level computation on each one. That's the entire reason window functions exist, and it's what GROUP BY fundamentally cannot do.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The question that forces a window function</span>\n    <p>\"Show each sale, and next to it, that region's total sales.\" With GROUP BY you'd get one row per region &#8212; the individual sales are gone. With a window: <code>SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY region)</code> puts the region total on every sale row, no collapse. The moment a question says \"each row, alongside a group-level number,\" you need a window, not GROUP BY.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- PARTITION BY = the grouping; ORDER BY inside OVER = ordering within each partition</span>\n<span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> region, sale_date, amount,\n       <span class=\"k\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> region <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> amount <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> rn,\n       <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> region)                       <span class=\"k\">AS</span> region_total,\n       <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> region <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> sale_date)      <span class=\"k\">AS</span> running_total,\n       <span class=\"k\">LAG</span>(amount)  <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> region <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> sale_date)      <span class=\"k\">AS</span> prev_sale\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span> sales;</div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK &#8212; the tie behaviour</span>\n    <p>On values 100, 90, 90, 80:<br>\n    <strong>ROW_NUMBER</strong> &#8594; 1, 2, 3, 4 (arbitrary tiebreak, always unique).<br>\n    <strong>RANK</strong> &#8594; 1, 2, 2, 4 (ties share, then a gap).<br>\n    <strong>DENSE_RANK</strong> &#8594; 1, 2, 2, 3 (ties share, no gap).<br><br>\n    \"Latest row per user\" &#8594; <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user ORDER BY ts DESC) = 1</code>. This dedup-keep-latest pattern is the #1 real-world window use.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The frame clause changes the answer silently</span>\n    <p>Adding <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER()</code> changes the default frame from \"whole partition\" to \"start of partition through current row\" &#8212; which is how you get a running total instead of a grand total. To make a proper N-row moving average you specify the frame explicitly: <code>AVG(x) OVER (ORDER BY date ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)</code> for a 7-day window. And <code>ROWS</code> (physical rows) vs <code>RANGE</code> (value range, ties grouped) give different results when the ORDER BY column has duplicates. Getting the frame wrong is a subtle, common bug &#8212; the query runs fine and returns the wrong number.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- The six classic patterns interviewers expect you to write from memory</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- 1. Nth highest</span>       <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"k\">DISTINCT</span> salary <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> salary <span class=\"k\">DESC</span> <span class=\"k\">LIMIT</span> <span class=\"y\">1</span> <span class=\"k\">OFFSET</span> N-<span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"c\">-- 2. Dedup keep latest</span>  <span class=\"k\">ROW_NUMBER</span>() <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">PARTITION BY</span> id <span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> ts <span class=\"k\">DESC</span>) = <span class=\"y\">1</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- 3. MoM growth</span>         (rev - <span class=\"k\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> month)) / <span class=\"k\">LAG</span>(rev) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (...) * <span class=\"y\">100</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- 4. 7-day rolling avg</span>  <span class=\"k\">AVG</span>(x) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> d <span class=\"k\">ROWS BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"y\">6</span> <span class=\"k\">PRECEDING</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> <span class=\"k\">CURRENT ROW</span>)\n<span class=\"c\">-- 5. Running total</span>      <span class=\"k\">SUM</span>(x) <span class=\"k\">OVER</span> (<span class=\"k\">ORDER BY</span> d)\n<span class=\"c\">-- 6. Churn (anti-join)</span>  <span class=\"k\">LEFT JOIN</span> next_month ... <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> next.id <span class=\"k\">IS</span> <span class=\"k\">NULL</span></div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 06. INDEXES \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"indexes\">06 &#183; Indexes &amp; Query Optimization</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">What an index is, mechanically</span>\n    <p>An index is a separate sorted data structure (almost always a <strong>B-tree</strong>) that stores the indexed column's values in order, each pointing back to its table row. Because it's sorted, the engine finds a value in O(log n) steps instead of scanning all n rows. The cost: every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must also update the index, so indexes speed reads and slow writes. You index the columns you filter and join on, not every column.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Analogy: the index at the back of a textbook</span>\n    <p>To find every mention of \"gradient descent\" you don't read all 800 pages (full table scan) &#8212; you flip to the alphabetical index and jump straight to the page numbers (index lookup). But if you ask \"every term that <em>contains</em> the letters 'grad',\" the alphabetical index is useless &#8212; it's sorted by whole word, not by substring. That's exactly why a leading-wildcard <code>LIKE '%grad%'</code> can't use an index, while <code>LIKE 'grad%'</code> can.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Sargability: the #1 real-world performance bug</span>\n    <p>Wrapping an indexed column in a function or expression destroys the index, because the stored sorted values no longer match what you're comparing. Every one of these forces a full scan:</p>\n    <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- BAD: function on the column \u2192 index unusable \u2192 full scan</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">YEAR</span>(order_date) = <span class=\"y\">2024</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"k\">UPPER</span>(email) = <span class=\"g\">'A@B.COM'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> amount * <span class=\"y\">1.1</span> &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span>\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- GOOD: rewrite so the bare column meets a constant \u2192 index used</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> order_date &gt;= <span class=\"g\">'2024-01-01'</span> <span class=\"k\">AND</span> order_date &lt; <span class=\"g\">'2025-01-01'</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> email = <span class=\"g\">'a@b.com'</span>          <span class=\"c\">-- store normalised, or use a functional index</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> amount &gt; <span class=\"y\">100</span> / <span class=\"y\">1.1</span></div>\n    <p>A query that is \"sargable\" (Search-ARGument-able) keeps the indexed column bare on one side of the comparison. This one idea fixes more slow queries in practice than any other.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Composite index column order matters</span>\n    <p>An index on <code>(region, date)</code> is sorted by region first, then date within region. It can serve <code>WHERE region = 'North'</code> and <code>WHERE region = 'North' AND date &gt; '...'</code> &#8212; but it <em>cannot</em> efficiently serve <code>WHERE date &gt; '...'</code> alone, because the dates aren't globally sorted, only within each region. Rule: put the column you filter on <em>equality</em> first, the <em>range</em> column second. This is the \"leftmost prefix\" rule and it's a frequent interview probe.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"c\">-- EXPLAIN shows the plan. What you're looking for:</span>\n<span class=\"k\">EXPLAIN</span> <span class=\"k\">ANALYZE</span> <span class=\"k\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"k\">FROM</span> orders <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> customer_id = <span class=\"y\">42</span>;\n\n<span class=\"c\">-- Seq Scan     \u2192 reading every row. Bad on large tables. Missing/unused index.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Index Scan   \u2192 using the index. Good.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Index Only Scan \u2192 answered entirely from the index, never touched the table. Best.</span>\n<span class=\"c\">-- Nested Loop vs Hash Join \u2192 how tables are joined; Hash usually better for big joins.</span></div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 07. TRANSACTIONS & ACID \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"acid\">07 &#183; Transactions &amp; ACID</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">ACID, one real example per letter</span>\n    <p><strong>Atomicity</strong> &#8212; all statements in a transaction succeed or all roll back. Transfer $100: debit A <em>and</em> credit B both happen, or neither does. No state where money left A but never reached B.<br>\n    <strong>Consistency</strong> &#8212; the transaction moves the DB from one valid state to another, respecting all constraints (foreign keys, checks). A transaction that would orphan a row is rejected.<br>\n    <strong>Isolation</strong> &#8212; concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-finished work (degree controlled by isolation level, below).<br>\n    <strong>Durability</strong> &#8212; once committed, the data survives a crash/power loss (written to disk, not just memory).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Isolation levels: what each one actually prevents</span>\n    <p>Higher isolation = more correctness, less concurrency. Each level permits a specific anomaly:</p>\n    <table class=\"tbl\">\n      <tr><th>Level</th><th>Dirty read</th><th>Non-repeatable read</th><th>Phantom</th></tr>\n      <tr><td>READ UNCOMMITTED</td><td>possible</td><td>possible</td><td>possible</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>READ COMMITTED</td><td>prevented</td><td>possible</td><td>possible</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>REPEATABLE READ</td><td>prevented</td><td>prevented</td><td>possible</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>SERIALIZABLE</td><td>prevented</td><td>prevented</td><td>prevented</td></tr>\n    </table>\n    <p style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><strong>Dirty read</strong>: you read another transaction's uncommitted change that later rolls back &#8212; you acted on data that never really existed. <strong>Non-repeatable read</strong>: you read a row twice in one transaction and get different values because someone committed an UPDATE in between. <strong>Phantom</strong>: you run the same <code>WHERE</code> twice and new rows appear because someone committed an INSERT that matches your filter.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">The tradeoff is throughput, and it's the interview point</span>\n    <p>SERIALIZABLE is the safest and the slowest &#8212; it effectively serialises conflicting transactions, so under high concurrency you get lock contention and reduced throughput. Most production systems run at <strong>READ COMMITTED</strong> (the default in Postgres and Oracle) as the pragmatic balance. The senior-level answer is never \"always use the strongest level\" &#8212; it's \"match the level to the anomaly the workload actually cares about, because each step up costs concurrency.\"</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"fx\"><span class=\"k\">BEGIN</span>;\n  <span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = balance - <span class=\"y\">100</span> <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">1</span>;\n  <span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class=\"k\">SET</span> balance = balance + <span class=\"y\">100</span> <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = <span class=\"y\">2</span>;\n<span class=\"k\">COMMIT</span>;   <span class=\"c\">-- both updates durable together; ROLLBACK undoes both if anything failed</span></div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 08. DATA MODELING \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"modeling\">08 &#183; Data Modeling &amp; Normalization</h2>\n\n  <div class=\"callout def\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Normalization is functional-dependency management, not memorised rules</span>\n    <p>The three normal forms each remove a class of redundancy, and the real goal underneath all of them is eliminating <strong>update anomalies</strong> &#8212; situations where one fact is stored in multiple places and the copies can drift out of sync.<br><br>\n    <strong>1NF</strong>: every cell holds a single atomic value (no comma-separated lists, no repeating groups).<br>\n    <strong>2NF</strong>: 1NF, plus no non-key column depends on only <em>part</em> of a composite key. (Only relevant when the primary key is multiple columns.)<br>\n    <strong>3NF</strong>: 2NF, plus no non-key column depends on <em>another non-key</em> column (no transitive dependency).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout intu\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Why the abstract rules matter: a concrete anomaly</span>\n    <p>Store <code>(order_id, product_id, product_name, product_category)</code> with the order. <code>product_name</code> depends on <code>product_id</code>, not on the order &#8212; a transitive dependency (violates 3NF). Now the product's name lives in every order row that contains it. Rename the product and you must update thousands of order rows; miss one and your data contradicts itself. Normalising &#8212; moving product attributes to a <code>products</code> table keyed by <code>product_id</code> &#8212; means the name lives in exactly one place. That's the point: one fact, one location.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"callout trap\">\n    <span class=\"label\">Warehouses deliberately denormalise &#8212; know both directions</span>\n    <p>Everything above optimises for <em>write</em> integrity. Analytical warehouses do the opposite: <strong>star schemas</strong> denormalise into a central fact table plus wide dimension tables, accepting redundancy to avoid expensive joins on huge read-heavy queries. OLTP (transactional apps) &#8594; normalise for write consistency. OLAP (analytics) &#8594; denormalise for read speed. The senior answer knows normalization is a spectrum you choose along based on read/write profile, not a law you always maximise. Also know SCD Type 2 (add a new row with validity dates) as the standard way warehouses preserve history instead of overwriting.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 Q&A \u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550 -->\n  <h2 id=\"qa\">Interview Q&amp;A</h2>\n  <div class=\"qa-head\"><button class=\"reveal-all\" id=\"revealAll\">Reveal all</button></div>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">Why can't you use a column alias defined in SELECT inside your WHERE clause?</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Because of logical execution order: WHERE runs before SELECT. The engine filters rows (WHERE) before it computes the output columns (SELECT), so the alias literally doesn't exist yet when WHERE runs. ORDER BY, which runs after SELECT, <em>can</em> use the alias. The fix is to repeat the expression in WHERE, or wrap the query in a subquery/CTE and filter on the alias in the outer query.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">HAVING vs WHERE &#8212; explain with an example where they give different answers.</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation. Take a region with sales 60, 60, 60. <code>WHERE amount &gt; 100</code> removes all three rows before summing, so the region contributes nothing. <code>HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 100</code> sums to 180 first, then keeps the region because 180 &gt; 100. Same threshold, opposite results, because one filters the sum's inputs and the other filters its output. Use WHERE for cheap row cuts before grouping (faster), HAVING only for conditions on aggregates.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">Why is a correlated subquery slow, and how do you fix it?</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A correlated subquery references the outer query, so it re-executes once per outer row &#8212; O(n&#178;) on an n-row table. The fix is almost always to rewrite it as a JOIN or a window function, both of which let the engine process the data set in a single pass. Example: \"employees earning above their department average\" as a correlated subquery re-runs the AVG per employee; rewritten with <code>AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept)</code> it computes once. For existence checks specifically, <code>EXISTS</code> short-circuits at the first match and beats a correlated <code>IN</code>.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">EXISTS vs IN &#8212; when do you pick each?</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>EXISTS</code> stops at the first matching row, so it's efficient when the subquery is large and you only need to know whether any match exists. <code>IN</code> materialises the entire subquery result into a set before checking membership, which is fine for a small fixed list but expensive for a large subquery. Critically, <code>NOT IN</code> breaks if the subquery can return NULL (it yields no rows at all), so prefer <code>NOT EXISTS</code> for negation. Default: EXISTS for large correlated checks, IN for small static lists.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">A window function and GROUP BY can both compute a group average. When must you use the window?</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When you need to keep the individual rows. GROUP BY collapses each group into one row, discarding the detail. A window function computes the group value and attaches it to every original row without collapsing. So \"show each transaction next to its account's average transaction\" requires <code>AVG(amt) OVER (PARTITION BY account)</code> &#8212; GROUP BY would leave you with one row per account and no individual transactions. Any \"each row alongside a group-level metric\" requirement forces a window.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">You have <code>WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2024</code> and the query is slow despite an index on order_date. Why?</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Wrapping the indexed column in <code>YEAR()</code> makes the predicate non-sargable: the index stores raw <code>order_date</code> values in sorted order, but the engine now has to compute <code>YEAR()</code> on every row to test the condition, so it can't use the sorted structure and falls back to a full scan. Rewrite as a range on the bare column: <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-01-01'</code>. Now the comparison maps directly onto the sorted index and becomes an index range scan. Any function or arithmetic on an indexed column kills the index.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">Walk through the isolation levels and the anomaly each one permits.</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>READ UNCOMMITTED allows dirty reads (reading uncommitted data that may roll back). READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads (a row's value changes between two reads in the same transaction because someone committed an UPDATE). REPEATABLE READ prevents non-repeatable reads but allows phantoms (new rows matching your WHERE appear because someone committed an INSERT). SERIALIZABLE prevents all three by effectively serialising conflicting transactions, at the cost of concurrency/throughput. Most systems default to READ COMMITTED as the practical balance; you raise the level only for the specific anomaly the workload can't tolerate.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">Explain 3NF through the anomaly it prevents, not the definition.</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>3NF removes transitive dependencies &#8212; a non-key column depending on another non-key column. Concretely: storing <code>product_name</code> in an orders table, where name depends on <code>product_id</code> (itself a non-key attribute of the order). The consequence is redundancy: the product name is duplicated across every order line for that product. Renaming the product now requires updating many rows, and any missed row makes the data self-contradictory &#8212; an update anomaly. Moving product attributes into a <code>products</code> table keyed by product_id stores each fact once. Warehouses knowingly violate this (star schemas) to trade write-integrity for read speed.</p></div></details>\n\n  <details class=\"qa\"><summary class=\"qmark\">You join orders to order_items and your SUM(order_total) is inflated. What happened?</summary>\n  <div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Join fan-out. <code>order_items</code> has multiple rows per order, so the LEFT/INNER join multiplies each order row once per item. Summing <code>order_total</code> now counts each order's total once per line item, inflating the result by the number of items. The fix is to aggregate to the correct grain before joining: compute item-level aggregates in a CTE keyed by order_id, then join one row per order. The general principle: always know the cardinality of your join keys, because a one-to-many join silently duplicates the \"one\" side's columns.</p></div></details>\n\n</main>"
});
